  <row Id="79709" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-09-17T03:39:16.913" Score="5" ViewCount="974" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a function inside a loop inside a function. The inner function acquires and stores a large vector of data in memory (as a global variable... I'm using &quot;R&quot; which is like &quot;S-Plus&quot;). The loop loops through a long list of data to be acquired. The outer function starts the process and passes in the list of datasets to be acquired.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;for (dataset in list_of_datasets) {&#xA;  for (datachunk in dataset) {&#xA;    &amp;lt;process datachunk&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;store result? as vector? where?&amp;gt;&#xA;  }&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I programmed the inner function to store each dataset before moving to the next, so all the work of the outer function occurs as side effects on global variables... a big no-no. Is this better or worse than collecting and returning a giant, memory-hogging vector of vectors? Is there a superior third approach?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Would the answer change if I were storing the data vectors in a database rather than in memory? Ideally, I'd like to be able to terminate the function (or have it fail due to network timeouts) without losing all the information processed prior to termination.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerDisplayName="James Lavin" LastEditorUserId="202229" LastEditDate="2016-04-28T13:21:33.607" LastActivityDate="2016-04-28T13:21:33.607" Title="Worse sin: side effects or passing massive objects?" Tags="&lt;memory&gt;&lt;r&gt;&lt;function&gt;&lt;global-variables&gt;&lt;side-effects&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
