  <row Id="582653" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="587944" CreationDate="2009-02-24T17:21:30.490" Score="14" ViewCount="2963" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am working with a survey dataset. It has two string &lt;code&gt;vectors&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;start&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;finish&lt;/code&gt;, indicating the time of the day when the interview was started, and finished, respectively.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;They are &lt;code&gt;character&lt;/code&gt; strings that look like: &lt;code&gt;&quot;9:24 am&quot;&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;&quot;12:35 pm&quot;&lt;/code&gt;, and so forth. I am trying to calculate the duration of the interview based on these two. What is the best way of doing this?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I know that, for dates, there are lots of &lt;code&gt;classe&lt;/code&gt;s or &lt;code&gt;function&lt;/code&gt;s like &lt;code&gt;as.date()&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;as.Date()&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;chron()&lt;/code&gt;, or &lt;code&gt;as.POSIXct()&lt;/code&gt;. So I was looking for something like &lt;code&gt;as.time()&lt;/code&gt;, but could not find it. Should I just append a made-up date and convert the whole thing into a &lt;code&gt;POSIX()&lt;/code&gt; date-time &lt;code&gt;class&lt;/code&gt;, then use &lt;code&gt;difftime()&lt;/code&gt;? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What is the best practice of handling time in &lt;code&gt;R&lt;/code&gt;?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="69117" OwnerDisplayName="Chang Chung" LastEditorUserId="4137985" LastEditDate="2017-12-14T10:09:25.690" LastActivityDate="2017-12-14T10:09:25.690" Title="what is the best practice of handling time in R?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;datetime&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="5" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="234" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="12202" CreationDate="2008-08-01T22:13:34.100" Score="88" ViewCount="46985" Body="&lt;p&gt;MySQL has this incredibly useful yet proprietary &lt;code&gt;REPLACE INTO&lt;/code&gt; SQL Command. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Can this easily be emulated in SQL Server 2005?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Starting a new Transaction, doing a &lt;code&gt;Select()&lt;/code&gt; and then either &lt;code&gt;UPDATE&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;INSERT&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;COMMIT&lt;/code&gt; is always a little bit of a pain, especially when doing it in the application and therefore always keeping 2 versions of the statement.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I wonder if there is an easy and &lt;strong&gt;universal&lt;/strong&gt; way to implement such a function into SQL Server 2005?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="91" LastEditorUserId="7014431" LastEditorDisplayName="user1873471" LastEditDate="2020-02-24T11:06:06.070" LastActivityDate="2020-07-27T01:42:16.993" Title="SQL Server 2005 implementation of MySQL REPLACE INTO?" Tags="&lt;mysql&gt;&lt;sql-server&gt;&lt;sql-server-2005&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="16" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
