  <row Id="371" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="396" CreationDate="2008-08-02T08:19:18.260" Score="453" ViewCount="111601" Body="&lt;p&gt;This is a tricky one and I've always relied on techniques, such as permission-based emails (i.e. only sending to people you have permission to send to) and not using blatantly &lt;em&gt;spamish&lt;/em&gt; terminology.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Of late, some of the emails I send out programmatically have started being shuffled into people's spam folder automatically and I'm wondering what I can do about it.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This is despite the fact that these particular emails are not ones that humans would mark as spam, specifically, they are emails that contain license keys that people have paid good money for, so I don't think they're going to consider them spam&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I figure this is a big topic in which I am essentially an ignorant simpleton.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="49" LastEditorUserId="1039608" LastEditorDisplayName="dbr" LastEditDate="2012-05-04T09:47:31.763" LastActivityDate="2021-05-20T15:46:45.717" Title="How do you make sure email you send programmatically is not automatically marked as spam?" Tags="&lt;email&gt;&lt;email-spam&gt;" AnswerCount="24" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="317" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="382" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="384" CreationDate="2008-08-02T08:58:27.540" Score="80" ViewCount="9657" Body="&lt;p&gt;What is the meaning of the &lt;em&gt;Java warning&lt;/em&gt;?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;Type safety: The cast from Object to List&amp;lt;Integer&amp;gt; is actually checking against the erased type List&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I get this warning when I try to cast an Object to a type with generic information, such as in the following code:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;Object object = getMyList();&#xA;List&amp;lt;Integer&amp;gt; list = (List&amp;lt;Integer&amp;gt;) object;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="122" OwnerDisplayName="Mike Stone" LastEditorUserId="1707091" LastEditDate="2019-04-17T19:47:56.127" LastActivityDate="2021-11-02T07:46:01.727" Title="What is the meaning of the type safety warning in certain Java generics casts?" Tags="&lt;java&gt;&lt;generics&gt;&lt;warnings&gt;&lt;casting&gt;&lt;type-safety&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="9" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="387" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-02T09:28:17.683" Score="44" ViewCount="3932" Body="&lt;p&gt;In Lucene if you had multiple indexes that covered only one partition each. Why does the same search on different indexes return results with different scores? The results from different servers match exactly. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;i.e. if I searched for :&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Name - John Smith&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;DOB - 11/11/1934&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Partition 0&lt;/strong&gt; would return a score of 0.345&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Partition 1&lt;/strong&gt; would return a score of 0.337&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Both match exactly on name and DOB.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="134" LastEditorUserId="1997093" LastEditorDisplayName="Stephen Hendry" LastEditDate="2016-12-20T07:04:01.190" LastActivityDate="2016-12-20T10:38:13.847" Title="Lucene Score results" Tags="&lt;search&gt;&lt;lucene&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
