  <row Id="419" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="17396" CreationDate="2008-08-02T11:52:01.543" Score="37" ViewCount="2918" Body="&lt;p&gt;Part of my everyday work is maintaining and extending legacy VB6 applications. A common engine is written in C/C++ and VB6 uses these functions in order to improve performance. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;When it comes to asynchronous programming, a C interface is not enough and we rely on COM controls to fire events to VB6.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;My problem is that when I register the control in VB6, VB loads this control in memory and does not unload it until I quit the VB6 IDE. As the control is loaded the whole time, I am unable to recompile it in VC6, because the DLL file is locked.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;A solution I found is not to enable the control in VB but use the &lt;code&gt;CreateObject()&lt;/code&gt; with the full name of my control. The problem then is that I must declare my control as an Object because VB6 knows nothing of the interface I am using and I do not have access to IntelliSense, which is a pain.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Any idea how I can tell VB6 to unload controls after quitting the application or directly in the IDE ?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="268" OwnerDisplayName="Vincent Robert" LastEditorUserId="1997093" LastEditorDisplayName="Joel Spolsky" LastEditDate="2016-12-22T03:56:15.020" LastActivityDate="2017-11-11T02:39:28.627" Title="Unload a COM control when working in VB6 IDE" Tags="&lt;com&gt;&lt;vb6&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
