  <row Id="1163640" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1164111" CreationDate="2009-07-22T07:34:10.200" Score="5" ViewCount="5002" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a histogram with several hundred items, for which I do a Q-Q plot. This results in EPS that is 2.5 megabytes large. This is too much for a figure that is only going to be included in a publication and is not going to be viewed at 100x magnification.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there any option in R to somehow output smaller EPS? I have searched docs to no avail. Or is my best option to, say, rasterize it afterwards at 300 dpi? If that's the case, any recommendations for the tool for this job?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The R code for the plot is nothing fancy:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;postscript(filename)&#xA;qqnorm(n, main=title))&#xA;qqline(n)&#xA;dev.off()&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;EDIT:&lt;/strong&gt; Doh! My question mentioned outputting EPS, and then converting it to some raster format. When of course I could just generate PNG in the first place from R.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="80458" LastEditorUserId="680068" LastEditDate="2017-12-22T11:07:21.737" LastActivityDate="2017-12-22T11:07:21.737" Title="Reduce size of plots in EPS format" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;postscript&gt;&lt;eps&gt;" AnswerCount="6" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
