  <row Id="1169388" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1169399" CreationDate="2009-07-23T03:10:59.123" Score="24" ViewCount="42989" Body="&lt;p&gt;Suppose I have the following vector:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;x &amp;lt;- c(8, 6, 9, 9, 7, 3, 2, 5, 5, 1, 6, 8, 5, 2, 9, 3, 5, 10, 8, 2)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How can I find which elements are either 8 or 9?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="84458" LastEditorUserId="2204410" LastEditDate="2018-08-17T08:01:05.920" LastActivityDate="2020-10-11T10:38:43.350" Title="Finding All Positions for Multiple Elements in a Vector" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;vector&gt;&lt;r-faq&gt;" AnswerCount="6" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="12" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="1323" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-04T15:14:47.517" Score="23" ViewCount="2749" Body="&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://stackoverflow.com/questions/180/function-for-creating-color-wheels&quot;&gt;Original Question&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If you are given N maximally distant colors (and some associated distance metric), can you come up with a way to sort those colors into some order such that the first M are also reasonably close to being a maximally distinct set?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In other words, given a bunch of distinct colors, come up with an ordering so I can use as many colors as I need starting at the beginning and be reasonably assured that they are all distinct and that nearby colors are also very distinct (e.g., bluish red isn't next to reddish blue).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Randomizing is OK but certainly not optimal.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Clarification: Given some large and visually distinct set of colors (say 256, or 1024), I want to sort them such that when I use the first, say, 16 of them that I get a relatively visually distinct subset of colors. This is equivalent, roughly, to saying I want to sort this list of 1024 so that the closer individual colors are visually, the farther apart they are on the list.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2089740" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditDate="2017-05-23T12:26:00.663" LastActivityDate="2012-09-03T20:50:02.030" Title="Followup: &quot;Sorting&quot; colors by distinctiveness" Tags="&lt;algorithm&gt;&lt;language-agnostic&gt;&lt;colors&gt;" AnswerCount="9" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="6" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="1169426" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1169451" CreationDate="2009-07-23T03:24:25.917" Score="10" ViewCount="2829" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a data frame detailing edge weights among N nodes.  Is there a package for working with this sort of data?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;For example, I would like to plot the following information as a network:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;  p1 p2 counts&#xA;1  a  b    100&#xA;2  a  c    200&#xA;3  a  d    100&#xA;4  b  c     80&#xA;5  b  d     90&#xA;6  b  e    100&#xA;7  c  d    100&#xA;8  c  e     40&#xA;9  d  e     60&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="84458" LastEditorUserId="164901" LastEditDate="2010-03-07T17:47:47.697" LastActivityDate="2010-03-07T17:47:47.697" Title="Manipulating Network Data in R" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;networking&gt;&lt;social-networking&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="7" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
