  <row Id="1476" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="13107" CreationDate="2008-08-04T18:20:36.487" Score="384" ViewCount="286835" Body="&lt;p&gt;How do you express an integer as a binary number with Python literals?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I was easily able to find the answer for hex:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt;&amp;gt;&amp;gt; 0x12AF&#xA;4783&#xA;&amp;gt;&amp;gt;&amp;gt; 0x100&#xA;256&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;and octal:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt;&amp;gt;&amp;gt; 01267&#xA;695&#xA;&amp;gt;&amp;gt;&amp;gt; 0100&#xA;64&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How do you use literals to express binary in Python?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Summary of Answers&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Python 2.5 and earlier: can express binary using &lt;code&gt;int('01010101111',2)&lt;/code&gt; but not with a literal.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Python 2.5 and earlier: there is &lt;em&gt;no way&lt;/em&gt; to express binary literals.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Python 2.6 beta: You can do like so: &lt;code&gt;0b1100111&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;0B1100111&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Python 2.6 beta: will also allow &lt;code&gt;0o27&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;0O27&lt;/code&gt; (second character is the letter O) to represent an octal.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Python 3.0 beta: Same as 2.6, but will no longer allow the older &lt;code&gt;027&lt;/code&gt; syntax for octals.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="92" LastEditorUserId="4952130" LastEditDate="2017-03-13T13:47:17.753" LastActivityDate="2020-11-29T04:40:27.877" Title="How do you express binary literals in Python?" Tags="&lt;python&gt;&lt;syntax&gt;&lt;binary&gt;&lt;integer&gt;&lt;literals&gt;" AnswerCount="8" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="45" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="1496" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1517" CreationDate="2008-08-04T18:51:38.543" Score="35" ViewCount="9670" Body="&lt;p&gt;For those of us that like to use the graphical version of Vim or Emacs, instead of the console version, which version do you recommend?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;For Vim, there's &lt;a href=&quot;http://sourceforge.net/projects/macosxvim/&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;Mac OS X Vim&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://code.google.com/p/macvim/&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;MacVim&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://code.google.com/p/vim-cocoa/&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;Vim-Cocoa&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;For Emacs, &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.apple.com/downloads/macosx/unix_open_source/carbonemacspackage.html&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;CarbonEmacs&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.xemacs.org/&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;XEmacs&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a href=&quot;http://aquamacs.org/&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;Aquamacs&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Are there more? Which of these are ready for prime-time? If it's a tough call, what are the trade-offs? Are all of these still being maintained?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;No discussion of Vim versus Emacs, if you don't mind, or comparisons with other editors.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="136" LastEditorUserId="10106580" LastEditDate="2019-07-24T14:44:27.110" LastActivityDate="2021-10-25T11:47:42.603" Title="What are the preferred versions of Vim and Emacs on Mac OS X?" Tags="&lt;macos&gt;&lt;vim&gt;&lt;emacs&gt;&lt;editor&gt;" AnswerCount="13" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="6" ClosedDate="2012-08-03T16:38:59.173" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
