  <row Id="1669" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1672" CreationDate="2008-08-04T22:46:36.900" Score="697" ViewCount="337192" Body="&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Preferred languages&lt;/em&gt;: C/C++, Java, and Ruby.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I am looking for some helpful books/tutorials on how to write your own compiler simply for educational purposes. I am most familiar with C/C++, Java, and Ruby, so I prefer resources that involve one of those three, but any good resource is acceptable.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="340" LastEditorUserId="63550" LastEditorDisplayName="Patrick McElhaney" LastEditDate="2014-02-28T23:45:33.330" LastActivityDate="2021-07-30T22:04:27.037" Title="Learning to write a compiler" Tags="&lt;compiler-construction&gt;&lt;language-agnostic&gt;" AnswerCount="38" CommentCount="4" FavoriteCount="2021" ClosedDate="2014-06-26T12:41:32.190" CommunityOwnedDate="2008-10-01T09:30:26.387" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="1679" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1704" CreationDate="2008-08-04T22:56:45.540" Score="16" ViewCount="10607" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm writing an app to help facilitate some research, and part of this involves doing some statistical calculations. Right now, the researchers are using a program called &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spss&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;SPSS&lt;/a&gt;. Part of the output that they care about looks like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://i.stack.imgur.com/m0swl.png&quot; alt=&quot;[Part of the SPSS output&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;They're really only concerned about the &lt;code&gt;F&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;Sig.&lt;/code&gt; values. My problem is that I have no background in statistics, and I can't figure out what the tests are called, or how to calculate them.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I thought the &lt;code&gt;F&lt;/code&gt; value might be the result of the &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-test#Table_on_F-test&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;F-test&lt;/a&gt;, but after following the steps given on Wikipedia, I got a result that was different from what &lt;code&gt;SPSS&lt;/code&gt; gives.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="55" LastEditorUserId="1219121" LastEditorDisplayName="Ryan Fox" LastEditDate="2012-03-28T16:47:35.633" LastActivityDate="2015-11-02T15:51:48.007" Title="How do I calculate these statistics?" Tags="&lt;statistics&gt;&lt;spss&gt;" AnswerCount="6" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="1683" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3020" CreationDate="2008-08-04T23:06:23.443" Score="24" ViewCount="3499" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am looking for guidance regarding the best practice around the use of the Profile feature in ASP.NET.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How do you decide what should be kept in the built-in user Profile, or if you should create your own database table and add a column for the desired fields? For example, a user has a zip code, should I save the zip code in my own table, or should I add it to the web.config xml profile and access it via the user profile ASP.NET mechanism? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The pros/cons I can think of right now are that since I don't know the profile very well (it is a bit of a &lt;em&gt;Matrix&lt;/em&gt; right now), I probably can do whatever I want if I go the table route (e.g., SQL to get all the users in the same zip code as the current user). I don't know if I can do the same if I use the ASP.NET profile.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="350" LastEditorUserId="1753960" LastEditDate="2017-12-13T15:25:12.563" LastActivityDate="2017-12-13T15:25:12.563" Title="ASP.NET built in user profile vs. old style user class/tables" Tags="&lt;asp.net&gt;&lt;profile&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="1709" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1724" CreationDate="2008-08-04T23:43:34.757" Score="24" ViewCount="17612" Body="&lt;p&gt;My dilemma is, basically, how to share an enumeration between two applications.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The users upload documents through a front-end application that is on the web. This application calls a web service of the back-end application and passes the document to it. The back-end app saves the document and inserts a row in the &lt;em&gt;Document&lt;/em&gt; table.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The document type (7 possible document types: &lt;em&gt;Invoice&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;Contract&lt;/em&gt; etc.) is passed as a parameter to the web service's &lt;em&gt;UploadDocument&lt;/em&gt; method. The question is, what should the type (and possible values) of this parameter be?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Since you need to hardcode these values in both applications, I think it is O.K. to use a descriptive string (&lt;em&gt;Invoice&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;Contract&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;WorkOrder&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;SignedWorkOrder&lt;/em&gt;). &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is it maybe a better approach to create a &lt;em&gt;DocumentTypes&lt;/em&gt; enumeration in the first application, and to reproduce it also in the second application, and then pass the corresponding integer value to the web service between them?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="349" LastEditorUserId="2134" LastEditorDisplayName="Chris Fournier" LastEditDate="2008-08-23T18:44:03.343" LastActivityDate="2008-09-08T12:35:03.563" Title="How to pass enumerated values to a web service" Tags="&lt;web-services&gt;&lt;application-integration&gt;" AnswerCount="8" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="1711" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-04T23:50:22.933" Score="1438" ViewCount="1094695" Body="&lt;p&gt;If you could go back in time and tell yourself to read a specific book at the beginning of your career as a developer, which book would it be?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I expect this list to be varied and to cover a wide range of things.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;To search:&lt;/strong&gt; Use the search box in the upper-right corner. To search the answers of the current question, use &lt;code&gt;inquestion:this&lt;/code&gt;.  For example:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;inquestion:this &quot;Code Complete&quot;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="303" LastEditorUserId="102937" LastEditorDisplayName="Roger Pate" LastEditDate="2011-09-26T15:39:12.017" LastActivityDate="2012-02-21T21:02:44.073" Title="What is the single most influential book every programmer should read?" Tags="&lt;resources&gt;" AnswerCount="214" CommentCount="28" FavoriteCount="7486" ClosedDate="2012-01-11T13:32:24.477" CommunityOwnedDate="2008-09-19T11:11:13.237" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="1734" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1780" CreationDate="2008-08-05T00:12:55.290" Score="35" ViewCount="2359" Body="&lt;p&gt;I was just looking through some information about Google's &lt;a href=&quot;http://code.google.com/apis/protocolbuffers/&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;protocol buffers&lt;/a&gt; data interchange format.  Has anyone played around with the code or even created a project around it?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I'm currently using XML in a Python project for structured content created by hand in a text editor, and I was wondering what the general opinion was on Protocol Buffers as a user-facing input format.  The speed and brevity benefits definitely seem to be there, but there are so many factors when it comes to actually generating and processing the data.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="59" LastEditorUserId="1675954" LastEditDate="2013-02-02T14:04:50.913" LastActivityDate="2013-02-02T14:04:50.913" Title="Any experiences with Protocol Buffers?" Tags="&lt;python&gt;&lt;xml&gt;&lt;database&gt;&lt;protocol-buffers&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="1174799" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1174826" CreationDate="2009-07-23T22:17:24.527" Score="159" ViewCount="130049" Body="&lt;p&gt;How do you pause an R script for a specified number of seconds or miliseconds? In many languages, there is a &lt;code&gt;sleep&lt;/code&gt; function, but &lt;code&gt;?sleep&lt;/code&gt; references a data set. And &lt;code&gt;?pause&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;?wait&lt;/code&gt; don't exist.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The intended purpose is for self-timed animations. The desired solution works without asking for user input.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="23929" LastEditorUserId="684229" LastEditDate="2014-01-17T15:03:00.207" LastActivityDate="2018-06-06T17:18:12.443" Title="How to make execution pause, sleep, wait for X seconds in R?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;animation&gt;&lt;statistics&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="29" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
