  <row Id="2043" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="2044" CreationDate="2008-08-05T09:34:49.073" Score="33" ViewCount="9485" Body="&lt;p&gt;A Windows process created from an exe file has access to the command string which invoked it, including its file's path and filename. eg. &lt;code&gt;C:\MyApp\MyApp.exe --help&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;But this is not so for a dll invoked via &lt;code&gt;LoadLibrary&lt;/code&gt;. Does anyone know of a way for a function loaded via dll to find out what its path and filename is?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Specifically I'm interested in a Delphi solution, but I suspect that the answer would be pretty much the same for any language.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="369" LastEditorUserId="369" LastEditorDisplayName="Vaibhav" LastEditDate="2021-11-19T22:09:05.343" LastActivityDate="2021-11-19T22:09:05.343" Title="Can a Windows dll retrieve its own filename?" Tags="&lt;windows&gt;&lt;delphi&gt;&lt;winapi&gt;&lt;dll&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="2046" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="7641" CreationDate="2008-08-05T09:43:01.257" Score="50" ViewCount="8142" Body="&lt;p&gt;As a novice in practicing test-driven development, I often end up in a quandary as to how to unit test persistence to a database.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I know that technically this would be an integration test (not a unit test), but I want to find out the best strategies for the following:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Testing queries. &lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Testing inserts. How do I know that the insert that has gone wrong if it fails? I can test it by inserting and then querying, but how can I know that the query wasn't wrong? &lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Testing updates and deletes -- same as testing inserts&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What are the best practices for doing these?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Regarding testing SQL: I am aware that this could be done, but if I use an O/R Mapper like NHibernate, it attaches some naming warts in the aliases used for the output queries, and as that is somewhat unpredictable I'm not sure I could test for that.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Should I just, abandon everything and simply trust NHibernate? I'm not sure that's prudent.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="372" LastEditorUserId="521799" LastEditorDisplayName="Chris Fournier" LastEditDate="2012-08-14T14:05:52.823" LastActivityDate="2020-07-09T15:12:11.730" Title="How do I unit test persistence?" Tags="&lt;sql-server&gt;&lt;unit-testing&gt;&lt;tdd&gt;" AnswerCount="10" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="13" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="2048" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="2080" CreationDate="2008-08-05T09:45:41.377" Score="13" ViewCount="3688" Body="&lt;p&gt;For all the RSS feeds I subscribe to I use &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.google.com/reader/&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;Google Reader&lt;/a&gt;, which I love. I do however have a couple of specific RSS feeds that I'd like to be notified of as soon as they get updated (say, for example, an RSS feed for a forum I like to monitor and respond to as quickly as possible). &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Are there any tools out there for this kind of monitoring which also have some kind of alert functionality (for example, a prompt window)? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I've tried &lt;a href=&quot;http://web.archive.org/web/20110615171752/http://www.simbolic.net:80/software/rssalert/&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;Simbolic RSS Alert&lt;/a&gt; but I found it a bit buggy and couldn't get it to alert me as often as I liked.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Suggestions? Or perhaps a different experience with Simbolic? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="298" LastEditorUserId="9780149" LastEditDate="2020-06-18T19:30:44.177" LastActivityDate="2021-10-25T11:20:51.307" Title="Monitor a specific RSS" Tags="&lt;rss&gt;" AnswerCount="9" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="2056" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="101561" CreationDate="2008-08-05T10:06:33.020" Score="2274" ViewCount="538642" Body="&lt;p&gt;When looking beyond the &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rapid_application_development&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;RAD&lt;/a&gt; (drag-drop and configure) way of building user interfaces that many tools encourage you are likely to come across three design patterns called &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Model%E2%80%93view%E2%80%93controller&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;Model-View-Controller&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Model%E2%80%93view%E2%80%93presenter&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;Model-View-Presenter&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Model_View_ViewModel&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;Model-View-ViewModel&lt;/a&gt;. My question has three parts to it:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;What issues do these patterns address?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;How are they similar?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;How are they different?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="358" LastEditorUserId="1595451" LastEditorDisplayName="Steve M" LastEditDate="2020-08-29T01:23:49.180" LastActivityDate="2022-01-27T06:54:23.653" Title="What are MVP and MVC and what is the difference?" Tags="&lt;user-interface&gt;&lt;model-view-controller&gt;&lt;design-patterns&gt;&lt;terminology&gt;&lt;mvp&gt;" AnswerCount="24" CommentCount="5" FavoriteCount="1336" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="1213783" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1214401" CreationDate="2009-07-31T17:17:08.837" Score="6" ViewCount="1068" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am writing a function that takes two variables and separately regresses each of them on a set of controls expressed as a one-sided formula. Right now I'm using the following to make the formula for one of the regressions, but it feels a bit hacked-up:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;foo &amp;lt;- function(x, y, controls) {&#xA;    cl &amp;lt;- match.call()&#xA;    xn &amp;lt;- cl[[&quot;x&quot;]]&#xA;    xf &amp;lt;- as.formula(paste(xn, deparse(controls)))&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I'd prefer to do this using &lt;code&gt;update.formula()&lt;/code&gt;, but of course &lt;code&gt;update.formula(controls, x ~ .)&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;update.formula(controls, as.name(x) ~ .)&lt;/code&gt; don't work. What should I be doing?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="143383" LastActivityDate="2009-07-31T19:25:47.080" Title="Using function arguments in update.formula" Tags="&lt;r&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
