  <row Id="1296646" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1296745" CreationDate="2009-08-18T21:33:18.473" Score="1435" ViewCount="1297732" Body="&lt;p&gt;I want to sort a data frame by multiple columns. For example, with the data frame below I would like to sort by column 'z' (descending) then by column 'b' (ascending):&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;dd &amp;lt;- data.frame(b = factor(c(&amp;quot;Hi&amp;quot;, &amp;quot;Med&amp;quot;, &amp;quot;Hi&amp;quot;, &amp;quot;Low&amp;quot;), &#xA;      levels = c(&amp;quot;Low&amp;quot;, &amp;quot;Med&amp;quot;, &amp;quot;Hi&amp;quot;), ordered = TRUE),&#xA;      x = c(&amp;quot;A&amp;quot;, &amp;quot;D&amp;quot;, &amp;quot;A&amp;quot;, &amp;quot;C&amp;quot;), y = c(8, 3, 9, 9),&#xA;      z = c(1, 1, 1, 2))&#xA;dd&#xA;    b x y z&#xA;1  Hi A 8 1&#xA;2 Med D 3 1&#xA;3  Hi A 9 1&#xA;4 Low C 9 2&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="84458" LastEditorUserId="1851712" LastEditDate="2021-12-07T17:45:34.007" LastActivityDate="2021-12-07T17:45:34.007" Title="Sort (order) data frame rows by multiple columns" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;sorting&gt;&lt;dataframe&gt;&lt;r-faq&gt;" AnswerCount="19" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="587" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="1297698" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1299539" CreationDate="2009-08-19T03:35:02.050" Score="3" ViewCount="5020" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have fit a regression using lme4 thanks to a &lt;a href=&quot;https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1169539/linear-regression-and-group-by-in-r&quot;&gt;previous answer&lt;/a&gt;. Now that I have a regression fit for each state I'd like to use lattice to plot QQ plots for each state. I would also like to plot error plots for each state in a lattice format. How do I make a lattice plot using the results of a lme4 regression? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Below is a simple sample (yeah, I like a good alliteration) using two states. I would like to make a two panel lattice made from the object fits. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;library(lme4)&#xA;d &amp;lt;- data.frame(state=rep(c('NY', 'CA'), c(10, 10)), year=rep(1:10, 2), response=c(rnorm(10), rnorm(10)))&#xA;fits &amp;lt;- lmList(response ~ year | state, data=d)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="37751" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditDate="2017-05-23T12:26:43.993" LastActivityDate="2010-02-14T07:06:50.270" Title="Plotting Regression results from lme4 in R using Lattice (or something else)" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;linear-regression&gt;&lt;lme4&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="1298100" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1300563" CreationDate="2009-08-19T06:27:14.043" Score="53" ViewCount="46236" Body="&lt;p&gt;Is there an easy way to create a &quot;movie&quot; by stitching together several plots, within R?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="144278" LastActivityDate="2020-04-04T19:00:07.343" Title="Creating a Movie from a Series of Plots in R" Tags="&lt;graphics&gt;&lt;video&gt;&lt;r&gt;&lt;movieclip&gt;&lt;movies&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="41" ClosedDate="2017-01-03T10:29:49.607" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="2649" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="2653" CreationDate="2008-08-05T18:18:39.373" Score="21" ViewCount="3436" Body="&lt;p&gt;When writing a T-SQL script that I plan on re-running, often times I use temporary tables to store temporary data.  Since the temp table is created on the fly, I'd like to be able to drop that table only if it exists (before I create it).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I'll post the method that I use, but I'd like to see if there is a better way.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="434" LastEditorUserId="434" LastEditorDisplayName="Nathan Bedford" LastEditDate="2009-01-13T16:03:30.267" LastActivityDate="2017-01-30T17:05:01.413" Title="What's the best way to determine if a temporary table exists in SQL Server?" Tags="&lt;sql-server&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="1299871" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1300618" CreationDate="2009-08-19T13:18:11.250" Score="1441" ViewCount="1574677" Body="&lt;p&gt;Given two data frames:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;df1 = data.frame(CustomerId = c(1:6), Product = c(rep(&quot;Toaster&quot;, 3), rep(&quot;Radio&quot;, 3)))&#xA;df2 = data.frame(CustomerId = c(2, 4, 6), State = c(rep(&quot;Alabama&quot;, 2), rep(&quot;Ohio&quot;, 1)))&#xA;&#xA;df1&#xA;#  CustomerId Product&#xA;#           1 Toaster&#xA;#           2 Toaster&#xA;#           3 Toaster&#xA;#           4   Radio&#xA;#           5   Radio&#xA;#           6   Radio&#xA;&#xA;df2&#xA;#  CustomerId   State&#xA;#           2 Alabama&#xA;#           4 Alabama&#xA;#           6    Ohio&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How can I do database style, i.e., &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Join_%28SQL%29&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;sql style, joins&lt;/a&gt;? That is, how do I get:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;An &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Join_%28SQL%29#Inner_join&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;inner join&lt;/a&gt; of &lt;code&gt;df1&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;df2&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;&#xA;Return only the rows in which the left table have matching keys in the right table.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;An &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Join_%28SQL%29#Outer_join&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;outer join&lt;/a&gt; of &lt;code&gt;df1&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;df2&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;&#xA;Returns all rows from both tables, join records from the left which have matching keys in the right table.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;A &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Join_%28SQL%29#Left_outer_join&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;left outer join (or simply left join)&lt;/a&gt; of &lt;code&gt;df1&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;df2&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;Return all rows from the left table, and any rows with matching keys from the right table.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;A &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Join_%28SQL%29#Right_outer_join&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;right outer join&lt;/a&gt; of &lt;code&gt;df1&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;df2&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;Return all rows from the right table, and any rows with matching keys from the left table.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Extra credit:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How can I do a SQL style select statement?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="23929" LastEditorUserId="792066" LastEditDate="2021-10-13T19:44:50.000" LastActivityDate="2022-01-10T13:33:18.660" Title="How to join (merge) data frames (inner, outer, left, right)" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;r-faq&gt;" AnswerCount="13" CommentCount="3" FavoriteCount="779" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="2658" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="2668" CreationDate="2008-08-05T18:29:14.283" Score="76" ViewCount="9482" Body="&lt;p&gt;I need to implement version control, even for just the developing I do at home.  I have read about how great subversion is for the past couple of years and was about to dedicate myself to learning this on the side until I heard about Git being the up and coming version control system.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Given the situation, should I hold off and see which one comes out on top? What are their relative advantages?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;One issue I noticed with Git is, there are not many full featured GUIs, which is important to many users on my team.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Also, wouldn't mind suggestions on how to get started with one or the other. (tutorials, etc.)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="406" LastEditorUserId="5452687" LastEditorDisplayName="Ryan Fox" LastEditDate="2022-02-23T12:27:23.230" LastActivityDate="2022-02-23T12:27:23.230" Title="Getting started with Version Control System" Tags="&lt;git&gt;&lt;svn&gt;&lt;version-control&gt;&lt;language-agnostic&gt;&lt;dvcs&gt;" AnswerCount="34" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="29" CommunityOwnedDate="2008-09-23T06:50:43.100" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="2688" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="2741" CreationDate="2008-08-05T18:45:46.747" Score="21" ViewCount="3365" Body="&lt;p&gt;When you execute a SQL query, you have to clean your strings or users can execute malicious SQL on your website.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I usually just have a function escape_string(blah), which:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Replaces escapes (&lt;code&gt;\&lt;/code&gt;) with double escapes (&lt;code&gt;\\&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Replaces single quotes (&lt;code&gt;'&lt;/code&gt;) with an escaped single quote (&lt;code&gt;\'&lt;/code&gt;). &lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is this adequate? Is there a hole in my code? Is there a library which can do this quickly and reliably for me?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I'd like to see graceful solutions in Perl, Java, and PHP.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="432" LastEditorUserId="116" LastEditorDisplayName="Andy Lester" LastEditDate="2016-09-30T04:32:43.200" LastActivityDate="2021-10-25T11:34:30.053" Title="What do I need to escape when sending a query?" Tags="&lt;mysql&gt;&lt;oracle&gt;&lt;security&gt;" AnswerCount="11" CommentCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="2702" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="2736" CreationDate="2008-08-05T18:55:35.963" Score="32" ViewCount="48936" Body="&lt;p&gt;I know I need to have (although I don't know why) a &lt;code&gt;GROUP BY&lt;/code&gt; clause on the end of a SQL query that uses any aggregate functions like &lt;code&gt;count&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;sum&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;avg&lt;/code&gt;, etc:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;SELECT count(userID), userName&#xA;FROM users&#xA;GROUP BY userName&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;When else would &lt;code&gt;GROUP BY&lt;/code&gt; be useful, and what are the performance ramifications?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="357" LastEditorUserId="73226" LastEditDate="2016-12-01T20:57:37.357" LastActivityDate="2016-12-01T20:57:37.357" Title="How do I use T-SQL Group By" Tags="&lt;sql&gt;&lt;sql-server&gt;&lt;group-by&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="3" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="2709" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-05T19:06:40.510" Score="9" ViewCount="671" Body="&lt;p&gt;In a &lt;strong&gt;Win32&lt;/strong&gt; environment, you can use the &lt;strong&gt;GetLastInputInfo API&lt;/strong&gt; call in &lt;a href=&quot;https://docs.microsoft.com/windows/desktop/api/winuser/nf-winuser-getlastinputinfo&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;Microsoft documentation&lt;/a&gt;.  Basically, this method returns the last tick that corresponds with when the user last provided input, and you have to compare that to the current tick to determine how long ago that was.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Xavi23cr has a good example for C# at &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.codeproject.com/KB/cs/GetIdleTimeWithCS.aspx&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;codeproject&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Any suggestions for other environments?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="434" LastEditorUserId="5044304" LastEditorDisplayName="Vaibhav" LastEditDate="2019-12-16T11:51:05.903" LastActivityDate="2019-12-16T11:51:05.903" Title="How can you tell when a user last pressed a key (or moved the mouse)?" Tags="&lt;language-agnostic&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="1300575" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1300949" CreationDate="2009-08-19T15:09:30.767" Score="11" ViewCount="3055" Body="&lt;p&gt;Formulas are a very useful feature of R's statistical and graphical functions. Like everyone, I am a user of these functions. However, I have never written a function that takes a formula object as an argument. I was wondering if someone could help me, by either linking to a readable introduction to this side of R programming, or by giving a self-contained example.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="143813" LastEditorUserId="1953718" LastEditDate="2021-01-14T08:43:52.037" LastActivityDate="2021-01-14T08:43:52.037" Title="Formulas in user-defined functions in R" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;r-formula&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="3" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="1301759" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1302334" CreationDate="2009-08-19T18:24:23.177" Score="4" ViewCount="1678" Body="&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Note:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;I changed the example from when I first posted. My first example was too simplified to capture the real problem.&lt;/em&gt; &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I have two data frames which are sorted differently in one column. I want to match one column and then merge in the value from the second column. The second column needs to stay in the same order. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So I have this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;state&amp;lt;-c(&quot;IA&quot;,&quot;IA&quot;,&quot;IA&quot;,&quot;IL&quot;,&quot;IL&quot;,&quot;IL&quot;)&#xA;value1&amp;lt;-c(1,2,3,4,5,6)&#xA;s1&amp;lt;-data.frame(state,value1)&#xA;state&amp;lt;-c(&quot;IL&quot;,&quot;IL&quot;,&quot;IL&quot;,&quot;IA&quot;,&quot;IA&quot;,&quot;IA&quot;)&#xA;value2&amp;lt;-c(3,4,5,6,7,8)&#xA;s2&amp;lt;-data.frame(state,value2)&#xA;&#xA;s1&#xA;s2&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;which returns this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; s1&#xA;  state value1&#xA;1    IA      1&#xA;2    IA      2&#xA;3    IA      3&#xA;4    IL      4&#xA;5    IL      5&#xA;6    IL      6&#xA;&amp;gt; s2&#xA;  state value2&#xA;1    IL      3&#xA;2    IL      4&#xA;3    IL      5&#xA;4    IA      6&#xA;5    IA      7&#xA;6    IA      8&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;and I want this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;  state value1 value2&#xA;1    IA      1      6&#xA;2    IA      2      7&#xA;3    IA      3      8&#xA;4    IL      4      3&#xA;5    IL      5      4&#xA;6    IL      6      5&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I'm about to drive myself silly trying to solve this. Seems like it should be a simple subscript problem. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="37751" LastEditorUserId="37751" LastEditDate="2009-08-19T19:37:48.850" LastActivityDate="2011-03-21T03:04:44.390" Title="Mixed Merge in R - Subscript solution?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;merge&gt;&lt;subscript&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="5" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="2711" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="2763" CreationDate="2008-08-05T19:08:47.543" Score="26" ViewCount="6827" Body="&lt;p&gt;Let's aggregate a list of free quality web site design templates. There are a million of these sites out there, but most are repetitive and boring. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I'll start with &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.freecsstemplates.org/&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;freeCSStemplates.org&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I also think other sites should follow some sort of standards, for example here are freeCSStemplates standards&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Released for FREE under the Creative Commons Attribution 2.5 license &lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Very lightweight in terms of images &lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Tables-free (ie. they use no tables for layout purposes) &lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;W3C standards compliant and valid (XHTML Strict) &lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Provided with public domain photos, generously provided by PDPhoto.org and Wikimedia Commons &lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="45" LastEditorUserId="397817" LastEditorDisplayName="Jason Baker" LastEditDate="2018-11-10T23:41:21.557" LastActivityDate="2018-11-10T23:41:21.557" Title="What sites offer free, quality web site design templates?" Tags="&lt;css&gt;&lt;templates&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="14" ClosedDate="2012-12-26T21:46:55.497" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="1309263" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2009-08-20T22:50:43.847" Score="124" ViewCount="55518" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am currently working on an algorithm to implement a rolling median filter (analogous to a rolling mean filter) in C. From my search of the literature, there appear to be two reasonably efficient ways to do it. The first is to sort the initial window of values, then perform a binary search to insert the new value and remove the existing one at each iteration.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The second (from Hardle and Steiger, 1995, JRSS-C, Algorithm 296) builds a double-ended heap structure, with a maxheap on one end, a minheap on the other, and the median in the middle. This yields a linear-time algorithm instead of one that is O(n log n).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Here is my problem: implementing the former is doable, but I need to run this on millions of time series, so efficiency matters a lot. The latter is proving very difficult to implement. I found code in the Trunmed.c file of the code for the stats package of R, but it is rather indecipherable.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Does anyone know of a well-written C implementation for the linear time rolling median algorithm?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Edit: Link to Trunmed.c code &lt;a href=&quot;http://google.com/codesearch/p?hl=en&amp;amp;sa=N&amp;amp;cd=1&amp;amp;ct=rc#mYw3h_Lb_e0/R-2.2.0/src/library/stats/src/Trunmed.c&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;http://google.com/codesearch/p?hl=en&amp;amp;sa=N&amp;amp;cd=1&amp;amp;ct=rc#mYw3h_Lb_e0/R-2.2.0/src/library/stats/src/Trunmed.c&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="143141" LastEditorUserId="376138" LastEditDate="2017-07-13T21:25:36.083" LastActivityDate="2021-02-05T10:04:07.480" Title="Rolling median algorithm in C" Tags="&lt;c&gt;&lt;algorithm&gt;&lt;r&gt;&lt;statistics&gt;&lt;median&gt;" AnswerCount="13" CommentCount="7" FavoriteCount="65" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="1310247" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1311620" CreationDate="2009-08-21T05:45:34.667" Score="26" ViewCount="13270" Body="&lt;p&gt;Many intro R books and guides start off with the practice of attaching a &lt;code&gt;data.frame&lt;/code&gt; so that you can call the variables by name. I have always found it favorable to call variables with &lt;code&gt;$&lt;/code&gt; notation or square bracket slicing &lt;code&gt;[,2]&lt;/code&gt;. That way I can use multiple &lt;code&gt;data.frame&lt;/code&gt;s without confusing them and/or use iteration to successively call columns of interest. I noticed Google recently posted &lt;a href=&quot;http://google-styleguide.googlecode.com/svn/trunk/google-r-style.html&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;coding guidelines for R&lt;/a&gt; which included the line&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;1) attach: avoid using it &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How do people feel about this practice?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="76235" LastEditorUserId="4205652" LastEditDate="2016-03-07T21:27:30.307" LastActivityDate="2016-09-27T17:57:10.157" Title="Do you use attach() or call variables by name or slicing?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;coding-style&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="11" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
