  <row Id="1395136" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1395423" CreationDate="2009-09-08T17:11:03.747" Score="4" ViewCount="418" Body="&lt;p&gt;Most looping code looks like this&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;retVal=NULL&#xA;for i {&#xA;  for j {&#xA;    result &amp;lt;- *some function of vector[i] and vector[j]* &#xA;    retVal = rbind(retVal,result)&#xA;  }&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Since this is so common, is there a systematic way of translating this idiom?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Can this be extended to most loops?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="23929" LastActivityDate="2009-09-08T18:05:25.010" Title="Is there a systematic way to convert R code with loops to vectorized code?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;loops&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="1395147" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1395210" CreationDate="2009-09-08T17:12:31.810" Score="17" ViewCount="34010" Body="&lt;p&gt;In an effort to help populate the R tag here, I am posting a few questions I have often received from students. I have developed my own answers to these over the years, but perhaps there are better ways floating around that I don't know about.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The question: I just ran a regression with continuous &lt;code&gt;y&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;x&lt;/code&gt; but factor &lt;code&gt;f&lt;/code&gt; (where &lt;code&gt;levels(f)&lt;/code&gt; produces &lt;code&gt;c(&quot;level1&quot;,&quot;level2&quot;)&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt; thelm &amp;lt;- lm(y~x*f,data=thedata)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Now I would like to plot the predicted values of &lt;code&gt;y&lt;/code&gt; by &lt;code&gt;x&lt;/code&gt; broken down by groups defined by &lt;code&gt;f&lt;/code&gt;. All of the plots I get are ugly and show too many lines.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;My answer: Try the &lt;code&gt;predict()&lt;/code&gt; function.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;##restrict prediction to the valid data &#xA;##from the model by using thelm$model rather than thedata&#xA;&#xA; thedata$yhat &amp;lt;- predict(thelm,&#xA;      newdata=expand.grid(x=range(thelm$model$x),&#xA;                          f=levels(thelm$model$f)))&#xA;&#xA; plot(yhat~x,data=thethedata,subset=f==&quot;level1&quot;)&#xA; lines(yhat~x,data=thedata,subset=f==&quot;level2&quot;)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Are there other ideas out there that are (1) easier to understand for a newcomer and/or (2) better from some other perspective?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="161808" LastEditorUserId="190277" LastEditDate="2015-02-08T17:00:54.510" LastActivityDate="2017-08-22T14:46:09.307" Title="Best way to plot interaction effects from a linear model" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;regression&gt;&lt;interaction&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="19" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="3654" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3655" CreationDate="2008-08-06T16:40:23.130" Score="23" ViewCount="1554" Body="&lt;p&gt;When developing a new web based application which version of html should you aim for?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;EDIT:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;cool I was just attempting to get a feel from others I tend to use XHTML 1.0 Strict in my own work and Transitional when others are involved in the content creation.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I marked the first XHTML 1.0 Transitional post as the 'correct answer' but believe strongly that all the answers given at that point where equally valid.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="269" LastEditorUserId="269" LastEditorDisplayName="sparkes" LastEditDate="2008-08-06T17:00:01.077" LastActivityDate="2016-06-21T08:51:04.627" Title="HTML version choice" Tags="&lt;html&gt;&lt;xml&gt;&lt;xhtml&gt;" AnswerCount="14" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="3" ClosedDate="2012-08-01T16:00:12.863" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
