  <row Id="5024" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="5041" CreationDate="2008-08-07T17:34:27.857" Score="22" ViewCount="6463" Body="&lt;p&gt;Does anyone know where online copies of the old &lt;em&gt;The Perl Journal&lt;/em&gt; articles can be found?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I know they are now owned by Dr. Dobb's, just the main page for it says they are part of whatever section the subject matter is relevant too, rather than being indexed together. That said, I have never been able to find any of them online on that site.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I know Mark Jason Dominus has a few of his articles &lt;a href=&quot;http://perl.plover.com/&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;on his site&lt;/a&gt;, any one know of any other good places? Or even what search terms to use at Dr. Dobb's?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="277" LastEditorUserId="223226" LastEditorDisplayName="brian d foy" LastEditDate="2014-12-25T19:54:47.547" LastActivityDate="2014-12-25T19:54:47.547" Title="Is The Perl Journal available online?" Tags="&lt;perl&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="6" ClosedDate="2013-11-05T04:38:20.910" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="5025" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="5031" CreationDate="2008-08-07T17:36:23.873" Score="16" ViewCount="1163" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm wanting extra security for a particular point in my web app. So I want to lock the database (SQL Server 2005). Any suggestions or is this even necessary with SQL Server?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Edit on question:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The query is failing silently with no errors messages logged, and does not occur inside of a transaction.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Final Solution:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I never was able to solve the problem, however what I wound up doing was switching to MySQL and using a transactional level query here. This was not the main or even a primary reason to switch. I had been having problems with SQL Server and it allowed me to have our CMS and various other tools all running on the same database. Previous we had a SQL Server and a MySQL database running to run our site. The port was a bit on the time consuming however in the long run I feel it will work much better for the site and the business.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="657" LastEditorUserId="1631551" LastEditorDisplayName="jtyost2" LastEditDate="2016-04-19T13:29:52.280" LastActivityDate="2016-04-19T13:29:52.280" Title="Locking a SQL Server Database with PHP" Tags="&lt;php&gt;&lt;sql-server&gt;&lt;database&gt;&lt;sql-server-2005&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
