  <row Id="1419136" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2009-09-13T23:42:28.203" Score="3" ViewCount="165" Body="&lt;p&gt;Here's my situation. I have an object that I've created using &lt;code&gt;read.spss&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; a &amp;lt;- read.spss(...)&#xA;&amp;gt; attach(a)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Now in this object &lt;code&gt;a&lt;/code&gt; is a set questions that I would like to pull out that follows a sequence of question numbers:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; q3 &amp;lt;- data.frame(q3_1, q3_2, q3_4, ... q3_27)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there a way to automate it such that it pulls out all questions starting with &lt;code&gt;q3_&lt;/code&gt; from the original object into the new &lt;code&gt;q3 data.frame&lt;/code&gt;?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I've tried using the &lt;code&gt;paste&lt;/code&gt; function to no avail.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; q3 &amp;lt;- data.frame(paste(&quot;q3_&quot;,1:27,sep=&quot;&quot;))&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;That just returns a &lt;code&gt;data.frame&lt;/code&gt; with the pasted sequence. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Ideally, I would like something that pulls in everything from a question beginning with a &lt;code&gt;qX_&lt;/code&gt;, as some values are missing or outdated. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerDisplayName="1beb" LastEditorUserId="3392044" LastEditDate="2016-02-29T09:05:47.223" LastActivityDate="2016-02-29T09:05:47.223" Title="Automating multiple variable withdrawal from an object" Tags="&lt;r&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="1419526" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1419625" CreationDate="2009-09-14T03:12:33.847" Score="2" ViewCount="288" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have two random variables:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;X ~ binom(n, p1)&#xA;Y ~ binom(n, p2)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;n is a known parameter (the total number of trials), while p1 and p2 are unknown.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I have one sample from each distribution (x from X, and y from Y). To give some context, x and y are numbers of true positives from two different classifiers, at a fixed selectivity.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I would like to use R to test the null hypothesis p1=p2 against p1 &gt; p2.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In particular, I would like to be able to find the p-value P(X-Y=x-y | p1=p2), and if possible, a confidence interval for the difference between p1 and p2.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What is the best way to go about this?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="169462" LastActivityDate="2009-09-14T04:03:43.063" Title="Difference of binomial parameters in R" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;statistics&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="5374" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="5469" CreationDate="2008-08-07T21:53:58.440" Score="16" ViewCount="20035" Body="&lt;p&gt;I would have thought this would be an easy one to Google, but I've been unsucessful.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I want to assign a variable the value out of an attribute (easy so far) then use that variable to select another node based on the value of that attribute.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Example:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;xsl:variable name=&quot;myId&quot; select=&quot;@id&quot; /&amp;gt;&#xA;&amp;lt;xsl value-of select=&quot;//Root/Some/Other/Path/Where[@id='{@myId}']/@Name /&amp;gt;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;That does not work.  If I replace the {@myId} with the value that is in the variable then it does find the right node, but doign it this way produces nothing.  I'm sure I'm missing something, or perhaps there is a different way to do it.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The context is that there is related data under different top-level nodes that share the same id value so I need to get the related nodes in my template.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="312" LastEditorUserId="1584286" LastEditDate="2013-07-04T12:24:18.877" LastActivityDate="2013-08-14T23:28:46.837" Title="How do you use a variable in xsl when trying to select a node?" Tags="&lt;xslt&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="3" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="1422247" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2009-09-14T15:22:22.277" Score="6" ViewCount="1843" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a density object dd created like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;x1 &amp;lt;- rnorm(1000) &#xA;x2 &amp;lt;- rnorm(1000, 3, 2) &#xA;x &amp;lt;- rbind(x1, x2)&#xA;dd &amp;lt;- density(x) &#xA;plot(dd)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Which produces this very non-Gaussian distribution:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.cerebralmastication.com/wp-content/uploads/2009/09/nongaus.png&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;alt text http://www.cerebralmastication.com/wp-content/uploads/2009/09/nongaus.png&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I would ultimately like to get random deviates from this distribution similar to how rnorm gets deviates from a normal distribution. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The way I am trying to crack this is to get the CDF of my kernel and then get it to tell me the variate if I pass it a cumulative probability (inverse CDF). That way I can turn a vector of uniform random variates into draws from the density. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;It seems like what I am trying to do should be something basic that others have done before me. Is there a simple way or a simple function to do this? I hate reinventing the wheel. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;FWIW I found &lt;a href=&quot;http://tolstoy.newcastle.edu.au/R/help/01a/1957.html&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;this R Help article&lt;/a&gt; but I can't grok what they are doing and the final output does not seem to produce what I am after. But it could be a step along the way that I just don't understand. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I've considered just going with a &lt;a href=&quot;http://hosho.ees.hokudai.ac.jp/~kubo/Rdoc/library/SuppDists/html/Johnson.html&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;Johnson distribution from the suppdists package&lt;/a&gt; but Johnson won't give me the nice bimodal hump which my data has. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="37751" LastEditorUserId="1140748" LastEditDate="2012-05-06T17:01:16.883" LastActivityDate="2012-05-06T17:01:16.883" Title="Generate stochastic random deviates from a density object with R" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;probability&gt;&lt;stochastic&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="5396" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="5481" CreationDate="2008-08-07T22:10:05.663" Score="8" ViewCount="1406" Body="&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I've been developing a site using ASP.NET MVC, and have decided to use the new SQL Server 2008 &lt;code&gt;FILESTREAM&lt;/code&gt; facility to store files 'within' the database rather than as separate entities.  While initially working within VS2008 (using a trusted connection to the database), everything was fine and dandy.  Issues arose, however, when I shifted the site to IIS7 and changed over to SQL authentication on the database.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;It seems that streaming a &lt;code&gt;FILESTREAM&lt;/code&gt; doesn't work with SQL authentication, only with Windows authentication.  Given this, what is the best practice to follow?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Is there a way to force this sort of thing to work under SQL authentication?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Should I add &lt;code&gt;NETWORK SERVICE&lt;/code&gt; as a database user and then use Trusted authentication?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Should I create another user, and run both the IIS site and the database connection under this?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Any other suggestions?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="691" LastEditorUserId="2641576" LastEditDate="2014-11-25T16:52:46.813" LastActivityDate="2014-11-25T16:52:46.813" Title="SQL Server 2008 FileStream on a Web Server" Tags="&lt;sql-server&gt;&lt;sql-server-2008&gt;&lt;iis&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
