  <row Id="6173" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="6183" CreationDate="2008-08-08T17:24:19.100" Score="17" ViewCount="11665" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm looking for a .NET regular expression extract all the URLs from a webpage but haven't found one to be comprehensive enough to cover all the different ways you can specify a link. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;And a side question:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there &lt;strong&gt;one regex to rule them all&lt;/strong&gt;? Or am I better off using a series of less complicated regular expressions and just using mutliple passes against the raw HTML? (Speed vs. Maintainability)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="322" LastEditorUserId="858913" LastEditorDisplayName="Jerub" LastEditDate="2017-12-16T22:04:58.370" LastActivityDate="2019-04-10T16:22:20.287" Title="Regular expression for parsing links from a webpage?" Tags="&lt;.net&gt;&lt;html&gt;&lt;regex&gt;" AnswerCount="9" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="5" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="1439513" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1439843" CreationDate="2009-09-17T15:15:26.193" Score="45" ViewCount="102635" Body="&lt;p&gt;I would like to be able to create a sequence of letters in R (to assist in importing data from a SPSS file)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;It's quite easy to create a sequence of numbers, for example:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;seq(1,1000)&#xA;[1] 1 2 3 4 5 6 ... 1000&#xA;&#xA;paste(&quot;something_&quot;,1:12,sep=&quot;&quot;)&#xA;[1] something1 something2 ... something12&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;But is there any functionality for appending, pasting, or creating sequences of letters like this? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;paste(&quot;This_&quot;,a:z,sep=&quot;&quot;)&#xA;[1]This_a This_b This_c ... This_4z&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Thanks in advance! &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="170352" LastActivityDate="2020-03-21T15:52:49.787" Title="Creating a sequential list of letters with R" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;alphabet&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="9" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="6184" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="6211" CreationDate="2008-08-08T17:32:41.783" Score="39" ViewCount="32841" Body="&lt;p&gt;When you subscribe to an event on an object from within a form, you are essentially handing over control of your callback method to the event source. You have no idea whether that event source will choose to trigger the event on a different thread.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The problem is that when the callback is invoked, you cannot assume that you can make update controls on your form because sometimes those controls will throw an exception if the event callback was called on a thread different than the thread the form was run on.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="756" LastEditorUserId="567854" LastEditorDisplayName="Alex Miller" LastEditDate="2019-01-20T13:58:49.230" LastActivityDate="2019-01-20T13:58:49.230" Title="How do I make event callbacks into my win forms thread safe?" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;.net&gt;&lt;winforms&gt;&lt;multithreading&gt;&lt;events&gt;" AnswerCount="6" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="12" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="1441717" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1446144" CreationDate="2009-09-17T22:50:42.917" Score="33" ViewCount="43579" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have some US demographic and firmographic data.&lt;br&gt;&#xA;I would like to plot zipcode areas in a state or a smaller region (e.g. city). Each area would be annotated by color and/or text specific to that area. The output would be similar to &lt;a href=&quot;http://maps.huge.info/&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;http://maps.huge.info/&lt;/a&gt; but a) with annotated text; b) pdf output; c) scriptable in R or Python.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there any package and code that allows me to do this?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="143813" LastEditorUserId="100297" LastEditDate="2016-09-01T15:04:21.403" LastActivityDate="2019-06-03T15:16:12.227" Title="Plotting color map with zip codes in R or Python" Tags="&lt;python&gt;&lt;r&gt;&lt;graphics&gt;&lt;zipcode&gt;" AnswerCount="8" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="23" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
