  <row Id="6547" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="6565" CreationDate="2008-08-09T02:23:27.890" Score="39" ViewCount="18473" Body="&lt;p&gt;One of those classic programming interview questions...&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;You are given two marbles, and told that they will break when dropped from some certain height (and presumably suffer no damage if dropped from below that height). You’re then taken to a 100 story building (presumably higher than the certain height), and asked to find the highest floor your can drop a marble from without breaking it as efficiently as possible.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Extra info&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;You must find the correct floor (not a possible range)&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;The marbles are both guaranteed to break at the same floor&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Assume it takes zero time for you to change floors - only the number of marble drops counts&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Assume the correct floor is randomly distributed in the building&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="797" LastEditorUserId="1401975" LastEditorDisplayName="Matt Sheppard" LastEditDate="2015-08-27T10:54:42.120" LastActivityDate="2019-03-27T11:15:10.377" Title="Two marbles and a 100 story building" Tags="&lt;algorithm&gt;&lt;puzzle&gt;" AnswerCount="10" CommentCount="5" FavoriteCount="32" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="6557" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="6567" CreationDate="2008-08-09T02:32:01.507" Score="86" ViewCount="76305" Body="&lt;p&gt;It seems that a List object cannot be stored in a List variable in C#, and can't even be explicitly cast that way.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;List&amp;lt;string&amp;gt; sl = new List&amp;lt;string&amp;gt;();&#xA;List&amp;lt;object&amp;gt; ol;&#xA;ol = sl;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;results in Cannot implicitly convert type &lt;code&gt;System.Collections.Generic.List&amp;lt;string&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;System.Collections.Generic.List&amp;lt;object&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;And then...&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;List&amp;lt;string&amp;gt; sl = new List&amp;lt;string&amp;gt;();&#xA;List&amp;lt;object&amp;gt; ol;&#xA;ol = (List&amp;lt;object&amp;gt;)sl;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;results in Cannot convert type &lt;code&gt;System.Collections.Generic.List&amp;lt;string&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;System.Collections.Generic.List&amp;lt;object&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Of course, you can do it by pulling everything out of the string list and putting it back in one at a time, but it is a rather convoluted solution.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="797" LastEditorUserId="512251" LastEditorDisplayName="Constantin" LastEditDate="2012-09-27T04:25:55.163" LastActivityDate="2017-04-27T09:52:30.337" Title="In C#, why can't a List&lt;string&gt; object be stored in a List&lt;object&gt; variable" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;.net&gt;&lt;generics&gt;&lt;covariance&gt;&lt;type-safety&gt;" AnswerCount="14" CommentCount="4" FavoriteCount="16" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="6578" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="6614" CreationDate="2008-08-09T03:13:04.000" Score="123" ViewCount="35452" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm just beginning to have a look at Objective-C and Cocoa with a view to playing with the iPhone SDK. I'm reasonably comfortable with C's &lt;code&gt;malloc&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;free&lt;/code&gt; concept, but Cocoa's references counting scheme has me rather confused. I'm told it's very elegant once you understand it, but I'm just not over the hump yet.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How do &lt;code&gt;release&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;retain&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;autorelease&lt;/code&gt; work and what are the conventions about their use?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;(Or failing that, what did you read which helped you get it?)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="797" LastEditorUserId="63550" LastEditorDisplayName="Paul" LastEditDate="2011-04-06T09:33:43.243" LastActivityDate="2016-12-19T03:41:31.810" Title="Understanding reference counting with Cocoa and Objective-C" Tags="&lt;iphone&gt;&lt;objective-c&gt;&lt;cocoa&gt;&lt;memory&gt;" AnswerCount="14" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="90" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
