  <row Id="1523298" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1523316" CreationDate="2009-10-06T02:12:38.657" Score="8" ViewCount="2237" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have three data sets of different lengths and I would like to plot density functions of all three on the same plot. This is straight forward with base graphics:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;n &amp;lt;- c(rnorm(10000), rnorm(10000))&#xA;a &amp;lt;- c(rnorm(10001), rnorm(10001, 0, 2))&#xA;p &amp;lt;- c(rnorm(10002), rnorm(10002, 2, .5))&#xA;&#xA;plot(density(n))&#xA;lines(density(a))&#xA;lines(density(p))&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Which gives me something like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.cerebralmastication.com/wp-content/uploads/2009/10/density.png&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;alt text http://www.cerebralmastication.com/wp-content/uploads/2009/10/density.png&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;But I really want to do this with GGPLOT2 because I want to add other features that are only available with GGPLOT2. It seems that GGPLOT really wants to take my empirical data and calculate the density for me. And it gives me a bunch of lip because my data sets are of different lengths. So how do I get these three densities to plot in GGPLOT2?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="37751" LastEditorUserId="1140748" LastEditDate="2012-05-06T17:00:45.573" LastActivityDate="2012-05-06T17:00:45.573" Title="Plotting predefined density functions using ggplot and R" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;ggplot2&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="7237" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="178509" CreationDate="2008-08-10T16:46:12.640" Score="11" ViewCount="5919" Body="&lt;p&gt;Our software must be able to run on SQL Server 2000 and 2005.  To simplify development, we're running our SQL Server 2005 databases in compatibility level 80.  However, database performance seems slower on SQL 2005 than on SQL 2000 in some cases (we have not confirmed this using benchmarks yet).  Would upgrading the compatibility level to 90 improve performance on the SQL 2005 servers?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="799" LastEditorUserId="116" LastEditorDisplayName="Mark Harrison" LastEditDate="2008-08-18T16:15:50.593" LastActivityDate="2010-05-06T06:44:49.897" Title="Does running a SQL Server 2005 database in compatibility level 80 have a negative impact on performance?" Tags="&lt;sql-server&gt;&lt;database&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="7244" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1409873" CreationDate="2008-08-10T17:14:10.260" Score="92" ViewCount="22132" Body="&lt;p&gt;What I want to do is something like this: I have enums with combined flagged values.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;public static class EnumExtension&#xA;{&#xA;    public static bool IsSet&amp;lt;T&amp;gt;( this T input, T matchTo ) &#xA;        where T:enum //the constraint I want that doesn't exist in C#3&#xA;    {    &#xA;        return (input &amp;amp; matchTo) != 0;&#xA;    }&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So then I could do:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;MyEnum tester = MyEnum.FlagA | MyEnum.FlagB&#xA;&#xA;if( tester.IsSet( MyEnum.FlagA ) )&#xA;    //act on flag a&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Unfortunately, C#'s generic where constraints have no enum restriction, only class and struct. C# doesn't see enums as structs (even though they are value types) so I can't add extension types like this.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Does anyone know a workaround?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="905" LastEditorUserId="567854" LastEditorDisplayName="Keith" LastEditDate="2019-01-20T13:55:37.633" LastActivityDate="2019-01-20T13:55:37.633" Title="Anyone know a good workaround for the lack of an enum generic constraint?" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;.net&gt;&lt;enums&gt;&lt;flags&gt;" AnswerCount="12" CommentCount="7" FavoriteCount="24" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="7245" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="7258" CreationDate="2008-08-10T17:16:55.137" Score="41" ViewCount="30953" Body="&lt;p&gt;What's the most efficient algorithm to find the rectangle with the largest area which will fit in the empty space?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Let's say the screen looks like this ('#' represents filled area):&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;....................&#xA;..............######&#xA;##..................&#xA;.................###&#xA;.................###&#xA;#####...............&#xA;#####...............&#xA;#####...............&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;A probable solution is:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;....................&#xA;..............######&#xA;##...++++++++++++...&#xA;.....++++++++++++###&#xA;.....++++++++++++###&#xA;#####++++++++++++...&#xA;#####++++++++++++...&#xA;#####++++++++++++...&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Normally I'd enjoy figuring out a solution. Although this time I'd like to avoid wasting time fumbling around on my own since this has a practical use for a project I'm working on. Is there a well-known solution?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Shog9&lt;/strong&gt; wrote:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;Is your input an array (as implied by the other responses), or a list of occlusions in the form of arbitrarily sized, positioned rectangles (as might be the case in a windowing system when dealing with window positions)?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Yes, I have a structure which keeps track of a set of windows placed on the screen. I also have a grid which keeps track of all the areas between each edge, whether they are empty or filled, and the pixel position of their left or top edge. I think there is some modified form which would take advantage of this property. Do you know of any?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="758" LastEditorUserId="758" LastEditorDisplayName="tweakt" LastEditDate="2008-08-11T06:26:39.427" LastActivityDate="2021-12-21T17:53:09.243" Title="Puzzle: Find largest rectangle (maximal rectangle problem)" Tags="&lt;algorithm&gt;&lt;language-agnostic&gt;&lt;math&gt;&lt;geometry&gt;" AnswerCount="6" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="24" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="7252" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="7274" CreationDate="2008-08-10T17:34:46.630" Score="109" ViewCount="10015" Body="&lt;p&gt;We have a junior programmer that simply doesn't write enough tests.&lt;br&gt;&#xA;I have to nag him every two hours, &quot;have you written tests?&quot;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;We've tried:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Showing that the design becomes simpler&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Showing it prevents defects&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Making it an ego thing saying only bad programmers don't&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;This weekend 2 team members had to come to work because his code had a NULL reference and he didn't test it&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;My work requires top quality stable code, and usually everyone 'gets it' and there's no need to push tests through. We know we can make him write tests, but we all know the useful tests are those written when you're into it.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Do you know of more motivations?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="573" LastEditorUserId="2134" LastEditorDisplayName="Chris Fournier" LastEditDate="2008-08-23T17:53:57.660" LastActivityDate="2015-07-01T14:40:36.170" Title="How to make junior programmers write tests?" Tags="&lt;unit-testing&gt;&lt;testing&gt;" AnswerCount="24" CommentCount="8" FavoriteCount="61" ClosedDate="2014-12-18T09:30:03.477" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
