  <row Id="7642" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="7644" CreationDate="2008-08-11T09:41:11.373" Score="23" ViewCount="13061" Body="&lt;p&gt;Is there an official convention for naming private fields in VB.NET?  For example, if I have a property called 'Foo', I normally call the private field '_Foo'.  This seems to be frowned upon in the &lt;a href=&quot;http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/ms229012.aspx&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;Offical Guidelines&lt;/a&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&quot;Do not use a prefix for field names. For example, do not use g_ or s_ to distinguish static versus non-static fields.&quot;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In C#, you could call the private field 'foo', the property 'Foo', and refer to the private field as 'this.foo' in the constructor.  As VB.NET is case insensitive you can't do this - any suggestions?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="133" LastActivityDate="2021-03-13T07:08:58.023" Title="Naming convention for VB.NET private fields" Tags="&lt;vb.net&gt;&lt;convention&gt;" AnswerCount="10" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="8" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
