  <row Id="1660124" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1661144" CreationDate="2009-11-02T09:01:28.513" Score="452" ViewCount="817704" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a data frame with two columns. First column contains categories such as &quot;First&quot;, &quot;Second&quot;, &quot;Third&quot;, and the second column has numbers that represent the number of times I saw the specific groups from &quot;Category&quot;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;Category     Frequency&#xA;First        10&#xA;First        15&#xA;First        5&#xA;Second       2&#xA;Third        14&#xA;Third        20&#xA;Second       3&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I want to sort the data by Category and sum all the Frequencies:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;Category     Frequency&#xA;First        30&#xA;Second       5&#xA;Third        34&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How would I do this in R?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="177541" LastEditorUserId="1953718" LastEditDate="2020-04-28T11:16:55.733" LastActivityDate="2020-12-17T03:33:03.310" Title="How to sum a variable by group" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;dataframe&gt;&lt;aggregate&gt;&lt;r-faq&gt;" AnswerCount="15" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="177" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="8517" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="36791" CreationDate="2008-08-12T05:51:01.947" Score="4" ViewCount="2754" Body="&lt;p&gt;I've had this long term issue in not quite understanding how to implement a decent Lucene sort or ranking.  Say I have a list of cities and their populations. If someone searches &quot;new&quot; or &quot;london&quot; I want the list of prefix matches ordered by population, and I have that working with a prefix search and an sort by field reversed, where there is a population field, IE New Mexico, New York; or London, Londonderry.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;However I also always want the exact matching name to be at the top.  So in the case of &quot;London&quot; the list should show &quot;London, London, Londonderry&quot; where the first London is in the UK and the second London is in Connecticut, even if Londonderry has a higher population than London CT.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Does anyone have a single query solution?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="459" LastEditorUserId="459" LastEditorDisplayName="dlamblin" LastEditDate="2008-08-22T16:56:04.280" LastActivityDate="2012-04-01T09:30:45.367" Title="Lucene exact ordering" Tags="&lt;lucene&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="8532" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="9085" CreationDate="2008-08-12T06:04:48.940" Score="8" ViewCount="6568" Body="&lt;p&gt;I've had an app doing prefix searches for a while. Recently the index size was increased and it turned out that some prefixes were too darned numerous for lucene to handle.  It kept throwing me a &lt;a href=&quot;http://web.archive.org/web/20080901205009/http://lucene.apache.org:80/java/2_3_2/api/core/org/apache/lucene/search/BooleanQuery.TooManyClauses.html&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;Too Many Clauses&lt;/a&gt; error, which was very frustrating as I kept looking at my JARs and confirming that none of the included code actually used a boolean query.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Why doesn't it throw something like a Too Many Hits exception? And why does increasing the boolean query's static max clauses integer actually make this error go away, when I'm definitely only using a prefix query? Is there something fundamental to how queries are run that I'm not understanding; is it that they secretly become Boolean queries?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="459" LastEditorUserId="9780149" LastEditDate="2020-06-29T17:46:53.970" LastActivityDate="2020-06-29T18:09:27.367" Title="With Lucene: Why do I get a Too Many Clauses error if I do a prefix search?" Tags="&lt;exception&gt;&lt;lucene&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="1661479" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1662207" CreationDate="2009-11-02T14:01:35.420" Score="11" ViewCount="10514" Body="&lt;p&gt;I regularly make figures (the exploratory data analysis type) in R. I also program in Python and was wondering if there are features or concepts in matplotlib that would be worth learning. For instance, I am quite happy with R - but its image() function will produce large files with pixelated output, whereas Matlab's equivalent figure (I also program regularly in Matlab) seems to be manageable in file size and also 'smoothed' - does matplotlib also provide such reductions...? But more generally, I wonder what other advantages matplotlib might confer. I don't mean this to be a trolling question. Thanks.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="143476" LastActivityDate="2009-11-02T16:19:23.747" Title="matplotlib for R user?" Tags="&lt;python&gt;&lt;r&gt;&lt;matplotlib&gt;&lt;scipy&gt;&lt;data-visualization&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="6" ClosedDate="2020-06-04T15:27:09.783" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
