  <row Id="9734" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="9747" CreationDate="2008-08-13T12:53:20.550" Score="7" ViewCount="2645" Body="&lt;p&gt;Why does C#.Net allow the declaration of the string object to be case-insensitive?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;String sHello = &quot;Hello&quot;;&#xA;string sHello = &quot;Hello&quot;;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Both the lower-case and upper-case S of the word String are acceptable and this seems to be the only object that allows this.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Can anyone explain why?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="383" OwnerDisplayName="GateKiller" LastEditorUserId="2376156" LastEditDate="2013-12-16T01:26:06.543" LastActivityDate="2013-12-16T01:26:06.543" Title="C#.Net case-insensitive string" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;.net&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="3" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
