  <row Id="1828206" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1829052" CreationDate="2009-12-01T19:09:13.427" Score="5" ViewCount="4462" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm getting a result I don't understand in R.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If I use &lt;code&gt;strptime&lt;/code&gt; with a year and day formatted %Y-%m (like &quot;2009-12&quot;), I get an NA result. But if I add a day, like &quot;2009-12-01&quot;, and change the format string accordingly, I do get a result. Example:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; strptime(&quot;2009-12&quot;,format=&quot;%Y-%m&quot;)&#xA;[1] NA&#xA;&amp;gt; strptime(&quot;2009-12-03&quot;,format=&quot;%Y-%m-%d&quot;)&#xA;[1] &quot;2009-12-03&quot;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Why is that?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Update:&lt;/strong&gt; The thing I'm curious about is why strptime doesn't parse a year and a month, and the reason it seems weird that it wouldn't do so is because it &lt;strong&gt;does&lt;/strong&gt; parse a year only, or a year-and-a-day:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; strptime(&quot;2009&quot;,format=&quot;%Y&quot;) # year only. Works. Uses current month and day as defaults.&#xA;[1] &quot;2009-12-02&quot;&#xA;&amp;gt; strptime(&quot;2009-03&quot;,format=&quot;%Y-%d&quot;) # year and day. Works. Uses current month as default.&#xA;[1] &quot;2009-12-03&quot;&#xA;&amp;gt; strptime(&quot;2009-03&quot;,format=&quot;%Y-%m&quot;) # year and month. Doesn't work. ?&#xA;[1] NA&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Update to explain why this is not a duplicate&lt;/strong&gt;&#xA;The possible duplicate was asked a few years after this question and it is concerned with a separate API in &lt;code&gt;R&lt;/code&gt;: the &lt;code&gt;asDate&lt;/code&gt; function. This question is about a quirk of the &lt;code&gt;strptime&lt;/code&gt; function that as of &lt;code&gt;R&lt;/code&gt; 3.1.3 still applies.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="137784" LastEditorUserId="137784" LastEditDate="2015-06-25T18:31:36.833" LastActivityDate="2015-06-25T18:31:36.833" Title="Why is parsing &quot;%Y-%m&quot; using strptime in R giving an NA result, but &quot;%Y-%m-%d&quot; works?" Tags="&lt;datetime&gt;&lt;r&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="4" FavoriteCount="1" ClosedDate="2015-06-16T20:13:26.540" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="10661" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="11290" CreationDate="2008-08-14T04:16:18.610" Score="6" ViewCount="323" Body="&lt;p&gt;We deployed a live, fresh, swanky site using preview 3 including rigorous stress testing. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Page Output caching was our saviour and afforded us the ability to meet our performance contractual requirements.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;My question is, is there a difference between action &lt;code&gt;OutputCacheFilter&lt;/code&gt; and page output caching?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is the action output caching faster than page output caching?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="209" OwnerDisplayName="CVertex" LastEditorUserId="5270744" LastEditorDisplayName="Haacked" LastEditDate="2017-03-09T18:36:07.093" LastActivityDate="2017-03-09T18:36:07.093" Title="Does the OutputCacheFilter in the Microsoft MVC Preview 4 actually save on action invocations?" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;asp.net&gt;&lt;asp.net-mvc&gt;&lt;outputcache&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
