  <row Id="10668" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-14T04:31:32.903" Score="25" ViewCount="18044" Body="&lt;p&gt;I've been trying to understand how to read the memory of other processes on Mac OS X, but I'm not having much luck. I've seen many examples online using &lt;code&gt;ptrace&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;PEEKDATA&lt;/code&gt; and such, however it doesn't have that option on BSD &lt;sup&gt;[&lt;a href=&quot;http://developer.apple.com/documentation/Darwin/Reference/ManPages/man2/ptrace.2.html&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;&lt;code&gt;man ptrace&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt;]&lt;/sup&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;int pid = fork();&#xA;if (pid &amp;gt; 0) {&#xA;    // mess around with child-process's memory&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How is it possible to read from and write to the memory of another process on Mac OS X?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1114" OwnerDisplayName="Jeremy Banks" LastEditorUserId="4332828" LastEditorDisplayName="Chris Hanson" LastEditDate="2016-11-24T23:37:41.430" LastActivityDate="2016-11-25T00:15:05.313" Title="Reading Other Process' Memory in OS X?" Tags="&lt;c&gt;&lt;macos&gt;&lt;memory&gt;&lt;ptrace&gt;" AnswerCount="8" CommentCount="6" FavoriteCount="13" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="10670" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="10714" CreationDate="2008-08-14T04:35:16.810" Score="9" ViewCount="2443" Body="&lt;p&gt;We have a remoting singleton server running in a separate windows service (let's call her RemotingService). The clients of the RemotingService are ASP.NET instances (many many).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Currently, the clients remoting call RemotingService and blocks while the RemotingService call is serviced. However, the remoting service is getting complicated enough (with more RPC calls and complex algorithms) that the asp.net worker threads are blocked for a significantly long time (4-5 seconds).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;According to &lt;a href=&quot;http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/magazine/cc164128.aspx&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;this msdn article&lt;/a&gt;, doing this will not scale well because an asp.net worker thread is blocked for each remoting RPC. It advises switching to async handlers to free up asp.net worker threads.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;The purpose of an asynchronous handler&#xA;  is to free up an ASP.NET thread pool&#xA;  thread to service additional requests&#xA;  while the handler is processing the&#xA;  original request.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This seems fine, except the remoting call still takes up a thread from the thread pool.&#xA;Is this the same thread pool as the asp.net worker threads? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How should I go about turning my remoting singleton server into an async system such that I free up my asp.net worker threads?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I've probably missed out some important information, please let me know if there is anything else you need to know to answer the question.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="209" OwnerDisplayName="CVertex" LastActivityDate="2012-08-11T15:44:43.557" Title="Asynchronous Remoting calls" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;.net-3.5&gt;&lt;.net-2.0&gt;&lt;remoting&gt;&lt;rpc&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="10680" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="10686" CreationDate="2008-08-14T04:53:45.620" Score="21" ViewCount="11314" Body="&lt;p&gt;Is there any difference between&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;   int on_exit(void (*function)(int , void *), void *arg);&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;and&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;   int atexit(void (*function)(void));&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;other than the fact that the function used by on_exit gets the exit status?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;That is, if I don't care about the exit status, is there any reason to use one or the other?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Edit:&lt;/strong&gt; Many of the answers warned against &lt;code&gt;on_exit&lt;/code&gt; because it's non-standard. If I'm developing an app that is for internal corporate use and guaranteed to run on specific configurations, should I worry about this? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1084" OwnerDisplayName="Nathan Fellman" LastEditorUserId="25507" LastEditDate="2011-09-27T13:33:11.823" LastActivityDate="2011-09-27T13:33:11.823" Title="Is there a difference between the on_exit() and atexit() functions?" Tags="&lt;c&gt;&lt;linux&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="10727" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="10818" CreationDate="2008-08-14T06:07:53.347" Score="3" ViewCount="566" Body="&lt;p&gt;In your applications, what's a &quot;long time&quot; to keep a transaction open before committing or rolling back?  Minutes?  Seconds?  Hours?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;and on which database?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="116" OwnerDisplayName="Mark Harrison" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditDate="2008-09-09T21:13:53.247" LastActivityDate="2009-02-13T00:54:40.213" Title="What is a &quot;reasonable&quot; length of time to keep a SQL cursor open?" Tags="&lt;sql&gt;&lt;cursors&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="10731" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="10820" CreationDate="2008-08-14T06:10:25.013" Score="8" ViewCount="5354" Body="&lt;p&gt;So our web server apps need to connect to the database, and some other apps have startup scripts that execute at boot time.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What's the best way to store the name/password for these applications, in terms of&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;security, e.g. perhaps we don't want sysadmins to know the database password&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;maintainability, e.g. making the configuration easy to change when the password changes, etc.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;both windows and linux solutions appreciated!&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="116" OwnerDisplayName="Mark Harrison" LastEditorUserId="13302" LastEditDate="2010-11-04T05:57:28.910" LastActivityDate="2010-11-04T05:57:28.910" Title="Best way to store a database password in a startup script / config file?" Tags="&lt;sql-server&gt;&lt;security&gt;&lt;passwords&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="5" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="1829429" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1833211" CreationDate="2009-12-01T22:35:50.457" Score="26" ViewCount="46289" Body="&lt;p&gt;Is there a function to get an index (row number and column number) for a matrix?  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Suppose that I have a simple matrix:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;a &amp;lt;- matrix(1:50, nrow=5)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there an easy way to get back something like c(3, 5) for the number &quot;23&quot;, for instance?  In this case, saying &lt;code&gt;which(a==23)&lt;/code&gt; just returns 23.  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This seems to work but I'm sure that there's a better way:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;matrix.index &amp;lt;- function(a, value) {&#xA;  idx &amp;lt;- which(data.frame(a)==value)&#xA;  col.num &amp;lt;- ceiling(idx/nrow(a))&#xA;  row.num &amp;lt;- idx - (col.num-1) * nrow(a)&#xA;  return(c(row.num, col.num))&#xA;}&#xA;&amp;gt; matrix.index(a, 23)&#xA;[1] 3 5&#xA;&amp;gt; matrix.index(a, 50)&#xA;[1]  5 10&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="163053" LastActivityDate="2009-12-02T14:24:50.610" Title="Index value for matrix in R?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;matrix&gt;&lt;filtering&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="6" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="1831245" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1831937" CreationDate="2009-12-02T07:42:55.297" Score="3" ViewCount="3086" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have the following setup:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;emp &amp;lt;- structure(list(s = structure(c(1L, 2L, 2L, 2L, 7L, 7L, 3L, 4L, 4L, 4L, 4L, 8L, 8L, 8L, 9L, 9L, 9L, 9L, 10L, 5L, 5L, 6L), .Label = c(&quot;8&quot;, &quot;24&quot;, &quot;31&quot;, &quot;78&quot;, &quot;135&quot;, &quot;142&quot;, &quot;30&quot;, &quot;98&quot;, &quot;117&quot;, &quot;123&quot;), class = &quot;factor&quot;, scores = structure(c(1, 2, 14, 3, 5, 17, 18, 20, 11, 13), .Dim = 10L, .Dimnames = list(c(&quot;8&quot;, &quot;24&quot;, &quot;30&quot;, &quot;31&quot;, &quot;78&quot;, &quot;98&quot;, &quot;117&quot;, &quot;123&quot;, &quot;135&quot;, &quot;142&quot;)))), t = structure(c(6L, 1L, 2L, 4L, 7L, 9L, 3L, 1L,  2L, 4L, 5L, 8L, 9L, 10L, 7L, 8L, 9L, 11L, 9L, 1L, 4L, 1L), .Label = c(&quot;8&quot;, &quot;24&quot;, &quot;40&quot;, &quot;78&quot;, &quot;135&quot;, &quot;142&quot;, &quot;30&quot;, &quot;98&quot;, &quot;117&quot;, &quot;119&quot;, &quot;123&quot; ), class = &quot;factor&quot;, scores = structure(c(1, 2, 14, 4, 5, 17, 18, 19, 20, 11, 13), .Dim = 11L, .Dimnames = list(c(&quot;8&quot;, &quot;24&quot;, &quot;30&quot;, &quot;40&quot;, &quot;78&quot;, &quot;98&quot;, &quot;117&quot;, &quot;119&quot;, &quot;123&quot;, &quot;135&quot;, &quot;142&quot;)))), V1 = c(3L, 1L, 9L, 4L, 1L, 107L, 2L, 5L, 3L, 1L, 2L, 1L,  6L, 14L, 20L, 1L, 4L, 1L, 2L, 3L, 2L, 2L)), .Names = c(&quot;s&quot;, &quot;t&quot;, &quot;V1&quot;), row.names = c(NA, -22L), class = &quot;data.frame&quot;)&#xA;o &amp;lt;- c(8L, 24L, 31L, 40L, 78L, 80L, 85L, 94L, 104L, 113L, 135L, 136L, 142L, 30L, 54L, 91L, 98L, 117L, 119L, 123L, 9L, 34L, 97L, 126L,  140L, 13L, 75L, 92L, 134L, 138L, 141L, 6L, 12L, 22L, 44L, 48L, 51L, 57L, 64L, 79L, 84L, 88L, 93L, 100L, 115L, 124L, 129L, 132L,  143L, 144L, 2L, 10L, 14L, 15L, 16L, 17L, 19L, 35L, 39L, 41L, 50L, 52L, 53L, 58L, 61L, 66L, 67L, 68L, 71L, 72L, 73L, 76L, 96L, 99L, 101L, 106L, 109L, 114L, 121L, 127L, 128L, 131L, 137L, 145L, 146L, 148L, 150L, 4L, 18L, 23L, 28L, 29L, 32L, 37L, 38L, 65L, 82L, 90L, 102L, 105L, 107L, 111L, 122L, 130L, 133L, 139L, 147L, 3L, 5L, 7L, 11L, 21L, 27L, 33L, 43L, 45L, 46L, 47L, 49L, 55L, 56L, 59L, 60L, 62L, 63L, 69L, 70L, 77L, 83L, 87L, 89L, 103L, 108L, 112L, 116L, 118L, 120L, 125L, 149L, 151L, 1L, 20L, 25L, 26L, 36L, 42L, 74L, 81L, 86L, 95L, 110L)&#xA;&#xA;emp$s &amp;lt;- reorder(factor(emp$s),match(emp$s,o))&#xA;emp$t &amp;lt;- reorder(factor(emp$t),match(emp$t,o))&#xA;qq &amp;lt;- ggplot(emp,aes(x=s,y=t))&#xA;qq + geom_tile(aes(fill=log(V1)))+theme_bw()+&#xA;scale_fill_gradient(low=&quot;white&quot;,high=&quot;black&quot;)+&#xA;opts(axis.text.x=theme_text(angle=-90, hjust=0, size=5),&#xA;     axis.text.y=theme_text(vjust=0, size=5),&#xA;     panel.grid.minor = theme_blank(),&#xA;     panel.grid.major = theme_blank())&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This produces the following:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://i.stack.imgur.com/Ly2ha.png&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://i.stack.imgur.com/Ly2ha.png&quot; alt=&quot;alt text&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I would like to include a vertical line just after 142 and before 30.  (Note, I need to keep these values as a factor.) I've been considering two options: &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;vline: I only am able to put the line on 142 and on 30, but not between them.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;grid stuff: I feel like the ideal solution is to introduce a grid.major or something.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Any ideas?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="84458" LastEditorUserId="4751173" LastEditDate="2019-05-14T13:10:52.940" LastActivityDate="2019-05-14T13:10:52.940" Title="Vertical gridlines in ggplot with discrete x-axis" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;ggplot2&gt;&lt;gridlines&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="10752" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-14T06:27:57.207" Score="335" ViewCount="58131" Body="&lt;p&gt;I know the so-called textbook definition of unit tests and integration tests.  What I am curious about is when it is time to write unit tests... I will write them to cover as many sets of classes as possible.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;For example, if I have a &lt;code&gt;Word&lt;/code&gt; class, I will write some unit tests for the &lt;code&gt;Word&lt;/code&gt; class.  Then, I begin writing my &lt;code&gt;Sentence&lt;/code&gt; class, and when it needs to interact with the &lt;code&gt;Word&lt;/code&gt; class, I will often write my unit tests such that they test both &lt;code&gt;Sentence&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;Word&lt;/code&gt;... at least in the places where they interact.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Have these tests essentially become integration tests because they now test the integration of these 2 classes, or is it just a unit test that spans 2 classes?&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In general, because of this uncertain line, I will rarely actually write integration tests... or is my using the finished product to see if all the pieces work properly the actual integration tests, even though they are manual and rarely repeated beyond the scope of each individual feature?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Am I misunderstanding integration tests, or is there really just very little difference between integration and unit tests?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="122" OwnerDisplayName="Mike Stone" LastEditorUserId="2756409" LastEditorDisplayName="Chris Fournier" LastEditDate="2019-09-09T16:05:25.957" LastActivityDate="2019-12-05T20:05:54.460" Title="What is the difference between integration and unit tests?" Tags="&lt;unit-testing&gt;&lt;integration-testing&gt;" AnswerCount="20" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="170" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="1832761" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1837105" CreationDate="2009-12-02T13:01:00.887" Score="4" ViewCount="450" Body="&lt;p&gt;let's say, I have this xml file:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;?xml version=&quot;1.0&quot; encoding=&quot;UTF-8&quot; ?&amp;gt;&#xA;&amp;lt;TimeSeries&amp;gt;&#xA;  &amp;lt;timeZone&amp;gt;1.0&amp;lt;/timeZone&amp;gt;&#xA;  &amp;lt;series&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;header/&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;event date=&quot;2009-09-30&quot; time=&quot;10:00:00&quot; value=&quot;0.0&quot; flag=&quot;2&quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/event&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;event date=&quot;2009-09-30&quot; time=&quot;10:15:00&quot; value=&quot;0.0&quot; flag=&quot;2&quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/event&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;event date=&quot;2009-09-30&quot; time=&quot;10:30:00&quot; value=&quot;0.0&quot; flag=&quot;2&quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/event&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;event date=&quot;2009-09-30&quot; time=&quot;10:45:00&quot; value=&quot;0.0&quot; flag=&quot;2&quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/event&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;event date=&quot;2009-09-30&quot; time=&quot;11:00:00&quot; value=&quot;0.0&quot; flag=&quot;2&quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/event&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;event date=&quot;2009-09-30&quot; time=&quot;11:15:00&quot; value=&quot;0.0&quot; flag=&quot;2&quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/event&amp;gt;&#xA;  &amp;lt;/series&amp;gt;&#xA;  &amp;lt;series&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;header/&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;event date=&quot;2009-09-30&quot; time=&quot;08:00:00&quot; value=&quot;1.0&quot; flag=&quot;2&quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/event&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;event date=&quot;2009-09-30&quot; time=&quot;08:15:00&quot; value=&quot;2.6&quot; flag=&quot;2&quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/event&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;event date=&quot;2009-09-30&quot; time=&quot;09:00:00&quot; value=&quot;6.3&quot; flag=&quot;2&quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/event&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;event date=&quot;2009-09-30&quot; time=&quot;09:15:00&quot; value=&quot;4.4&quot; flag=&quot;2&quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/event&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;event date=&quot;2009-09-30&quot; time=&quot;09:30:00&quot; value=&quot;3.9&quot; flag=&quot;2&quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/event&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;event date=&quot;2009-09-30&quot; time=&quot;09:45:00&quot; value=&quot;2.0&quot; flag=&quot;2&quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/event&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;event date=&quot;2009-09-30&quot; time=&quot;10:00:00&quot; value=&quot;1.7&quot; flag=&quot;2&quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/event&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;event date=&quot;2009-09-30&quot; time=&quot;10:15:00&quot; value=&quot;2.3&quot; flag=&quot;2&quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/event&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;event date=&quot;2009-09-30&quot; time=&quot;10:30:00&quot; value=&quot;2.0&quot; flag=&quot;2&quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/event&amp;gt;&#xA;  &amp;lt;/series&amp;gt;&#xA;  &amp;lt;series&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;header/&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;event date=&quot;2009-09-30&quot; time=&quot;10:00:00&quot; value=&quot;0.0&quot; flag=&quot;2&quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/event&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;event date=&quot;2009-09-30&quot; time=&quot;10:15:00&quot; value=&quot;0.0&quot; flag=&quot;2&quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/event&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;event date=&quot;2009-09-30&quot; time=&quot;10:30:00&quot; value=&quot;0.0&quot; flag=&quot;2&quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/event&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;event date=&quot;2009-09-30&quot; time=&quot;10:45:00&quot; value=&quot;0.0&quot; flag=&quot;2&quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/event&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;event date=&quot;2009-09-30&quot; time=&quot;11:00:00&quot; value=&quot;0.0&quot; flag=&quot;2&quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/event&amp;gt;&#xA;  &amp;lt;/series&amp;gt;&#xA;&amp;lt;/TimeSeries&amp;gt;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;and let's say I want to do something with its series elements and that I would like to put in practice the advice 'vectorize the vectorizable'...  I import the XML library and do the following:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;R&amp;gt; library(&quot;XML&quot;)&#xA;R&amp;gt; doc &amp;lt;- xmlTreeParse('/home/mario/Desktop/sample.xml')&#xA;R&amp;gt; TimeSeriesNode &amp;lt;- xmlRoot(doc)&#xA;R&amp;gt; seriesNodes &amp;lt;- xmlElementsByTagName(TimeSeriesNode, &quot;series&quot;)&#xA;R&amp;gt; length(seriesNodes)&#xA;[1] 3&#xA;R&amp;gt; (function(x){length(xmlElementsByTagName(x[['series']], 'event'))}&#xA;+ )(seriesNodes)&#xA;[1] 6&#xA;R&amp;gt; &#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;and I don't understand why I should only get the result of applying the function to the first element: I had expected three values, just as the length of seriesNodes, something like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;R&amp;gt; mapply(length, seriesNodes)&#xA;series series series &#xA;     7     10      6 &#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;oops!  I already came with the answer: &quot;use &lt;code&gt;mapply&lt;/code&gt;&quot;:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;R&amp;gt; mapply(function(x){length(xmlElementsByTagName(x, 'event'))}, seriesNodes)&#xA;series series series &#xA;     6      9      5 &#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;but then I see the following problem: the R-inferno tells me that I'm &quot;loop-hiding&quot;, not &quot;vectorizing&quot;!  can I avoid looping at all?  ...&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="78912" LastEditorUserId="78912" LastEditDate="2009-12-02T15:58:24.100" LastActivityDate="2009-12-03T15:37:04.230" Title="how to vectorize with xml data?" Tags="&lt;xml&gt;&lt;r&gt;&lt;language-features&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
