  <row Id="1875795" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1875842" CreationDate="2009-12-09T18:21:38.537" Score="25" ViewCount="7729" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm working with a large data frame, and have run up against RAM limits. At this point, I probably need to work with a serialized version on the disk. There are &lt;a href=&quot;http://cran.r-project.org/web/views/HighPerformanceComputing.html&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;a few packages&lt;/a&gt; to support out-of-memory operations, but I'm not sure which one will suit my needs. I'd prefer to keep everything in data frames, so the &lt;code&gt;ff&lt;/code&gt; package looks encouraging, but there are still compatibility problems that I can't work around.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What's the first tool to reach for when you realize that your data has reached out-of-memory scale?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="197321" LastEditorUserId="3001626" LastEditDate="2015-12-22T18:48:17.653" LastActivityDate="2019-09-17T05:48:21.670" Title="Best practices for storing and using data frames too large for memory?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;out-of-memory&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="17" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
