  <row Id="11804" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="920922" CreationDate="2008-08-15T00:08:15.533" Score="9" ViewCount="3609" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm working on a web service at the moment and there is the potential that the returned results could be quite large ( &gt; 5mb). &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;It's perfectly valid for this set of data to be this large and the web service can be called either sync or async, but I'm wondering what people's thoughts are on the following:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;If the connection is lost, the&#xA;entire resultset will have to be&#xA;regenerated and sent again. Is there&#xA;any way I can do any sort of&#xA;&quot;resume&quot; if the connection is lost&#xA;or reset?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Is sending a result set this large even appropriate? Would it be better to implement some sort of &quot;paging&quot; where the resultset is generated and stored on the server and the client can then download chunks of the resultset in smaller amounts and re-assemble the set at their end?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="493" OwnerDisplayName="lomaxx" LastEditorUserId="2134" LastEditorDisplayName="Chris Fournier" LastEditDate="2008-08-23T19:09:16.730" LastActivityDate="2013-04-11T08:24:05.390" Title="Returning Large Results Via a Webservice" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;.net&gt;&lt;web-services&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="1899243" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1899627" CreationDate="2009-12-14T06:27:47.423" Score="7" ViewCount="10018" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a stationary time series to which I want to fit a linear model with an autoregressive term to correct for serial correlation, i.e. using the formula At = c1*Bt + c2*Ct + ut, where ut = r*ut-1 + et&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;(ut is an AR(1) term to correct for serial correlation in the error terms)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Does anyone know what to use in R to model this?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Thanks&#xA;Karl&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="141789" LastActivityDate="2014-07-17T23:34:36.613" Title="GLM with autoregressive term to correct for serial correlation" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;model&gt;&lt;statistics&gt;&lt;correlation&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="11806" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="448051" CreationDate="2008-08-15T00:09:16.407" Score="12" ViewCount="14860" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm trying to run powershell commands through a web interface (ASP.NET/C#) in order to create mailboxes/etc on Exchange 2007. When I run the page using Visual Studio (Cassini), the page loads up correctly. However, when I run it on IIS (v5.1), I get the error &quot;unknown user name or bad password&quot;. The biggest problem that I noticed was that Powershell was logged in as ASPNET instead of my Active Directory Account. How do I force my Powershell session to be authenticated with another Active Directory Account?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Basically, the script that I have so far looks something like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;RunspaceConfiguration rc = RunspaceConfiguration.Create();&#xA;PSSnapInException snapEx = null;&#xA;rc.AddPSSnapIn(&quot;Microsoft.Exchange.Management.PowerShell.Admin&quot;, out snapEx);&#xA;&#xA;Runspace runspace = RunspaceFactory.CreateRunspace(rc);&#xA;runspace.Open();&#xA;&#xA;Pipeline pipeline = runspace.CreatePipeline();&#xA;using (pipeline)&#xA;{&#xA;   pipeline.Commands.AddScript(&quot;Get-Mailbox -identity 'user.name'&quot;);&#xA;   pipeline.Commands.Add(&quot;Out-String&quot;);&#xA;&#xA;   Collection&amp;lt;PSObject&amp;gt; results = pipeline.Invoke();&#xA;&#xA;   if (pipeline.Error != null &amp;amp;&amp;amp; pipeline.Error.Count &amp;gt; 0)&#xA;   {&#xA;       foreach (object item in pipeline.Error.ReadToEnd())&#xA;          resultString += &quot;Error: &quot; + item.ToString() + &quot;\n&quot;;&#xA;   }&#xA;&#xA;   runspace.Close();&#xA;&#xA;   foreach (PSObject obj in results)&#xA;      resultString += obj.ToString();&#xA;}&#xA;&#xA;return resultString;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="889" OwnerDisplayName="Eldila" LastEditorUserId="5640" LastEditorDisplayName="Rich B" LastEditDate="2009-03-10T02:24:30.793" LastActivityDate="2009-09-30T18:35:51.107" Title="How do you impersonate an Active Directory user in Powershell?" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;asp.net&gt;&lt;powershell&gt;&lt;active-directory&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="5" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="11809" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="11815" CreationDate="2008-08-15T00:12:20.923" Score="33" ViewCount="28819" Body="&lt;p&gt;The only thing I've found has been;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre class=&quot;lang-css prettyprint-override&quot;&gt;&lt;code&gt;.hang {&#xA;    text-indent: -3em;&#xA;    margin-left: 3em;&#xA;} &#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The only way for this to work is putting text in a paragraph, which causes those horribly unsightly extra lines. I'd much rather just have them in a &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;span class=&quot;hang&quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; type of thing.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I'm also looking for a way to further indent than just a single-level of hanging. Using paragraphs to stack the indentions doesn't work.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="362" OwnerDisplayName="Armentia" LastEditorUserId="362" LastEditorDisplayName="Armentia" LastEditDate="2015-04-29T13:52:03.417" LastActivityDate="2021-04-15T14:26:08.830" Title="How to create an all browser-compatible hanging indent style in CSS in a span" Tags="&lt;html&gt;&lt;css&gt;&lt;indentation&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="11820" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="11832" CreationDate="2008-08-15T00:29:57.563" Score="4" ViewCount="1418" Body="&lt;p&gt;This &lt;a href=&quot;https://stackoverflow.com/questions/11782/file-uploads-via-web-services&quot;&gt;question and answer&lt;/a&gt; shows how to send a file as a byte array through an XML web service. How much overhead is generated by using this method for file transfer?  I assume the data looks something like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;?xml version=&quot;1.0&quot; encoding=&quot;UTF-8&quot; ?&amp;gt;&#xA;&amp;lt;bytes&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;byte&amp;gt;16&amp;lt;/byte&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;byte&amp;gt;28&amp;lt;/byte&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;byte&amp;gt;127&amp;lt;/byte&amp;gt;&#xA;    ...&#xA;&amp;lt;/bytes&amp;gt;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If this format is correct, the bytes must first be converted to UTF-8 characters. Each of these characters allocates 8 bytes. Are the bytes stored in base 10, hex, or binary characters? How much larger does the file appear as it is being sent due to the XML data and character encoding? Is compression built into web services?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="26" OwnerDisplayName="modesty" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditorDisplayName="Chris Fournier" LastEditDate="2017-05-23T10:32:50.377" LastActivityDate="2015-06-30T03:43:19.153" Title="How much extra overhead is generated when sending a file over a web service as a byte array?" Tags="&lt;xml&gt;&lt;web-services&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="1905258" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1907855" CreationDate="2009-12-15T04:58:52.337" Score="9" ViewCount="2185" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm new to R and having a hard time piecing together information from various sources online related to what is considered a &quot;good&quot; practice with writing R code.  I've read basic guides but I've been having a hard time finding information that is definitely up to date.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;What are some examples of well written/documented S3 classes?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;How about corresponding S4 classes?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;What conventions do you use when commenting .R classes/functions?  Do you put all of your comments in both .Rd files and .R files?  Is synchronization of these files tiresome?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="15050" LastEditorUserId="2679518" LastEditDate="2014-05-01T02:42:03.400" LastActivityDate="2014-05-01T02:42:03.400" Title="R code examples/best practices" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;coding-style&gt;&lt;comments&gt;&lt;s4&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="9" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="1907129" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1907188" CreationDate="2009-12-15T12:33:15.077" Score="3" ViewCount="366" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am following the hints to &lt;a href=&quot;https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1879843/distributing-r-package-containing-unit-tests&quot;&gt;this question&lt;/a&gt;, but I'm impatient and I would like to run my tests more quickly, without having to wait for the 30+ checks that &lt;code&gt;R CMD check src&lt;/code&gt; invokes  before &lt;code&gt;checking tests&lt;/code&gt;.  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;what I thought I could do was to add a &lt;code&gt;--standalone&lt;/code&gt; option to the &lt;code&gt;doRUnit.R&lt;/code&gt; suggested in &lt;a href=&quot;http://wiki.r-project.org/rwiki/doku.php?id=developers:runit&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;that R-wiki page&lt;/a&gt; so that I could run the unit tests independently of &lt;code&gt;R CMD&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I added these lines to the script:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;  opt &amp;lt;- list(standalone=NULL)&#xA;  if(require(&quot;getopt&quot;, quietly=TRUE)) {&#xA;    ## path to unit tests may be given on command line, in which case&#xA;    ## we also want to move the cwd to this script&#xA;    opt &amp;lt;- getopt(matrix(c('standalone', 's', 0, &quot;logical&quot;),&#xA;                         ncol=4, byrow=TRUE))&#xA;    if(!is.null(opt$standalone)) {&#xA;      ## switch the cwd to the dir of this script&#xA;      args &amp;lt;- commandArgs()&#xA;      script.name &amp;lt;- substring(args[substring(args, 1, 7)==&quot;--file=&quot;], 8, 1000)&#xA;      if(!is.null(script.name))&#xA;        setwd(dirname(script.name))&#xA;    }&#xA;  }&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;with this change, the script finds the &lt;code&gt;test.*\.R&lt;/code&gt; files, independently from the directory from which I invoke the script.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;the remaining problem now is that the &lt;code&gt;doRUnit.R&lt;/code&gt; script loads the &lt;strong&gt;installed&lt;/strong&gt; library, it does not &lt;code&gt;source()&lt;/code&gt; the files that compose the library.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;assuming that I want to load each and every file in the &lt;code&gt;R&lt;/code&gt; directory, how would I do that?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;assuming you have a better testing schema (satisfying the requirements &quot;quick&quot;, &quot;uninstalled&quot;), what is it?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="78912" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditDate="2017-05-23T11:48:36.327" LastActivityDate="2014-01-01T17:41:21.873" Title="RUnit testing uninstalled package" Tags="&lt;unit-testing&gt;&lt;r&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="11831" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-15T00:39:01.150" Score="68" ViewCount="19369" Body="&lt;p&gt;Singletons are a hotly debated design pattern, so I am interested in what the Stack Overflow community thought about them.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Please provide reasons for your opinions, not just &quot;Singletons are for lazy programmers!&quot;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Here is a fairly good article on the issue, although it is against the use of Singletons:&#xA;&lt;a href=&quot;http://web.archive.org/web/20090303174418/http://scientificninja.com/advice/performant-singletons&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;scientificninja.com: performant-singletons&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Does anyone have any other good articles on them? Maybe in support of Singletons?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1366" OwnerDisplayName="Adam" LastEditorUserId="637987" LastEditorDisplayName="Chris Fournier" LastEditDate="2019-06-06T20:19:06.977" LastActivityDate="2019-06-06T20:19:06.977" Title="Singletons: good design or a crutch?" Tags="&lt;language-agnostic&gt;&lt;design-patterns&gt;&lt;singleton&gt;" AnswerCount="23" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="52" ClosedDate="2014-06-13T12:31:43.843" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="11854" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="11935" CreationDate="2008-08-15T01:00:47.690" Score="6" ViewCount="1584" Body="&lt;p&gt;In a project our team is using object lists to perform mass operations on sets of data that should all be processed in a similar way.  In particular, different objects would ideally act the same, which would be very easily achieved with polymorphism.  The problem I have with it is that inheritance implies the &lt;em&gt;is a&lt;/em&gt; relationship, rather than the &lt;em&gt;has a&lt;/em&gt; relationship.  For example, several objects &lt;em&gt;have a&lt;/em&gt; damage counter, but to make this easy to use in an object list, polymorphism could be used - except that would imply an &lt;em&gt;is a&lt;/em&gt; relationship which wouldn't be true.  (A person &lt;em&gt;is not a&lt;/em&gt; damage counter.)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The only solution I can think of is to have a member of the class return the proper object type when implicitly casted instead of relying on inheritance.  Would it be better to forgo the &lt;em&gt;is a&lt;/em&gt; / &lt;em&gt;has a&lt;/em&gt; ideal in exchange for ease of programming?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Edit:&#xA;To be more specific, I am using C++, so using polymorphism would allow the different objects to &quot;act the same&quot; in the sense that the derived classes could reside within a single list and be operated upon by a virtual function of the base class.  The use of an interface (or imitating them via inheritance) seems like a solution I would be willing to use.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1256" OwnerDisplayName="ImPerfection" LastEditorUserId="2193" LastEditorDisplayName="Jason Etheridge" LastEditDate="2008-09-21T07:38:09.493" LastActivityDate="2010-01-18T21:29:48.753" Title="Inheritance and Polymorphism - Ease of use vs Purity" Tags="&lt;c++&gt;&lt;inheritance&gt;&lt;oop&gt;&lt;polymorphism&gt;" AnswerCount="10" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="1908010" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1908095" CreationDate="2009-12-15T14:58:56.497" Score="43" ViewCount="11118" Body="&lt;p&gt;In packages like marray and &lt;a href=&quot;http://bioinf.wehi.edu.au/limma/&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;limma&lt;/a&gt;, when complex objects are loaded, they contain &quot;members variables&quot; that are accessed using the @ symbol. What does this mean and how does it differ from the $ symbol?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="30911" LastEditorUserId="602276" LastEditDate="2011-08-25T13:16:10.960" LastActivityDate="2019-01-23T16:20:17.493" Title="What does the @ symbol mean in R?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;s4&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="11" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="11857" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-15T01:05:30.520" Score="28" ViewCount="7501" Body="&lt;p&gt;I would like to gauge what solutions other people put in place to get Team System functionality.  We all know that Team System can be pricey for some of us.  I know they offer a small team edition with five licenses with a MSDN subscription, but what if your team is bigger than five or you don't want to use Team System?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1117" OwnerDisplayName="Dale Ragan" LastEditorUserId="781754" LastEditorDisplayName="Dale Ragan" LastEditDate="2016-02-18T17:08:56.010" LastActivityDate="2016-02-18T17:08:56.010" Title="What do you use as a good alternative to Team System?" Tags="&lt;svn&gt;&lt;tfs&gt;&lt;cruisecontrol.net&gt;&lt;fogbugz&gt;" AnswerCount="12" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="10" CommunityOwnedDate="2009-09-04T18:11:44.720" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="1909357" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1909396" CreationDate="2009-12-15T18:18:11.573" Score="2" ViewCount="420" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm working on a big Sweave document/script on a Mac OS X system, R version 2.9.2. Under some circumstances, it appears as if Sweave is redirecting stdout, so that &lt;code&gt;x &amp;lt;- 1; print(x)&lt;/code&gt; gives nothing at all. (The console is still running, as &lt;code&gt;plot(x)&lt;/code&gt; pops up a plot as normal.) So, two questions:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;How do I force stdout to go back to the console, and,&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Why does Sweave do this, and how?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="135944" LastActivityDate="2009-12-15T18:25:11.247" Title="losing stdout in R console on Mac OS X" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;interactive&gt;&lt;sweave&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="11878" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="11932" CreationDate="2008-08-15T01:50:02.473" Score="8" ViewCount="845" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am currently architecting a small CRUD applicaton. Their database is a huge mess and will be changing frequently over the course of the next 6 months to a year. What would you recommend for my data layer:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;1) ORM (if so, which one?)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;2) Linq2Sql&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;3) Stored Procedures&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;4) Parametrized Queries&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I really need a solution that will be dynamic enough (both fast and easy) where I can replace tables and add/delete columns frequently.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Note: I do not have much experience with ORM (only a little SubSonic) and generally tend to use stored procedures so maybe that would be the way to go. I would love to learn Ling2Sql or NHibernate if either would allow for the situation I've described above.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="285" OwnerDisplayName="sestocker" LastEditorUserId="3" LastEditorDisplayName="sestocker" LastEditDate="2012-03-22T00:27:07.337" LastActivityDate="2012-03-22T00:27:07.337" Title="Best .NET Solution for Frequently Changed Database" Tags="&lt;.net&gt;&lt;database&gt;&lt;change-management&gt;" AnswerCount="12" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="4" ClosedDate="2017-10-24T11:58:47.670" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="1915001" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1915361" CreationDate="2009-12-16T14:32:10.197" Score="12" ViewCount="6059" Body="&lt;p&gt;On a generic plot, with time on the x-axis, I would like to highlight a period of some specific years.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How can I bestly do this? My idea is for example a light yellow bar for the highlighted years, behind the plot of course.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The plot code I have now:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;pdf(&quot;temperature_imfs_big_interm5.pdf&quot;, width=6, height=8);&#xA;par(mfrow=c(temperature$bigEmdIm5$nimf+1,1), mar=c(2,1,2,1))&#xA;for(i in 1:temperature$bigEmdIm5$nimf) {&#xA;    plot(timeline$big, temperature$bigEmdIm5$imf[,i], type=&quot;l&quot;, xlab=&quot;&quot;, ylab=&quot;&quot;, ylim=range(temperature$bigEmdIm5$imf[,i]), axes=FALSE, main=paste(i, &quot;-th IMF&quot;, sep=&quot;&quot;))#; abline(h=0)&#xA;  axis.POSIXct(side=1, at=tickpos$big)&#xA;}&#xA;plot(timeline$big, temperature$bigEmdIm5$residue, xlab=&quot;&quot;, ylab=&quot;&quot;, axes=FALSE, main=&quot;residue&quot;, type=&quot;l&quot;)&#xA;axis.POSIXct(side=1, at=tickpos$big)&#xA;dev.off();&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Where temperature$bigEmdIm5 is the output of emperical mode decompostion. The data is in months, so I would like to higlight 01/1950 until 12/1950 for example.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="85514" LastEditorUserId="1851712" LastEditDate="2018-03-25T12:34:20.163" LastActivityDate="2019-05-21T13:04:14.190" Title="Highlight (shade) plot background in specific time range" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;plot&gt;&lt;highlight&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="4" FavoriteCount="8" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="11879" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="11899" CreationDate="2008-08-15T01:51:48.663" Score="6" ViewCount="4928" Body="&lt;p&gt;Instead of returning a common string, is there a way to return classic objects?&#xA;If not: what are the best practices? Do you transpose your object to xml and rebuild the object on the other side? What are the other possibilities? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="391" OwnerDisplayName="Paul Celi" LastEditorUserId="2134" LastEditorDisplayName="Chris Fournier" LastEditDate="2008-08-23T18:41:27.453" LastActivityDate="2009-01-27T12:21:09.750" Title="Is it possible to return objects from a WebService?" Tags="&lt;web-services&gt;" AnswerCount="11" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="11887" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="11898" CreationDate="2008-08-15T02:01:13.217" Score="15" ViewCount="7735" Body="&lt;p&gt;I get an Access is Denied error message when I use the strong name tool to create a new key to sign a .NET assembly.  This works just fine on a Windows XP machine but it does not work on my Vista machine.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;PS C:\users\brian\Dev\Projects\BELib\BELib&amp;gt; sn -k keypair.snk&#xA;&#xA;Microsoft (R) .NET Framework Strong Name Utility  Version 3.5.21022.8&#xA;Copyright (c) Microsoft Corporation.  All rights reserved.&#xA;&#xA;Failed to generate a strong name key pair -- Access is denied.&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What causes this problem and how can I fix it?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;Are you running your PowerShell or&#xA;  Command Prompt as an Administrator? I&#xA;  found this to be the first place to&#xA;  look until you get used to User Access&#xA;  Control or by turning User Access&#xA;  Control off.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Yes I have tried running PS and the regular command prompt as administrator.  The same error message comes up.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1254" OwnerDisplayName="Brian Ensink" LastEditorUserId="1288" LastEditDate="2012-06-29T11:06:48.107" LastActivityDate="2021-08-23T19:45:30.507" Title="sn.exe fails with Access Denied error message" Tags="&lt;.net&gt;&lt;strongname&gt;&lt;sn.exe&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="3" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
