  <row Id="1973360" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1974151" CreationDate="2009-12-29T07:21:28.330" Score="0" ViewCount="765" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am new to R language. I have successfully loaded a query's resultset into a variable. Now I want to access the resultset data by column name and row number. And I need to verify if it is Null (it is shown as &amp;lt; NA &gt; in result) then send a mail by bat file with PHP. My sample code is below.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;library(RODBC)&#xA;newconn = odbcConnect(&quot;db&quot;, uid=&quot;uid&quot;, pwd=&quot;pwd&quot;) &#xA;new &amp;lt;- sqlQuery(newconn,&quot;SELECT COL1, COL2 FROM TABLE1;&quot;, errors = TRUE, 1)&#xA;if(new$COL1[3] == &quot;&amp;lt;NA&amp;gt;&quot;){&#xA;system(&quot;sendmail.bat&quot;)&#xA;}else{&#xA;print (&quot;failed&quot;)&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Also I would like to compare a string result like below.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;if(new$COL2[10] == 'MYSTRING'){&#xA;print(&quot;success&quot;)&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;But I think I am using wrong syntax. Please help as I am not able to get the correct syntax for doing these comparisons. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="79358" LastEditorUserId="4370109" LastEditDate="2017-01-20T23:42:51.557" LastActivityDate="2017-01-20T23:42:51.557" Title="R accessing DB query results by column and row number" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;syntax&gt;&lt;comparison&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="12569" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="12571" CreationDate="2008-08-15T18:14:28.327" Score="6" ViewCount="1153" Body="&lt;p&gt;Let's say we have a simple function defined in a pseudo language.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;List&amp;lt;Numbers&amp;gt; SortNumbers(List&amp;lt;Numbers&amp;gt; unsorted, bool ascending);&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;We pass in an unsorted list of numbers and a boolean specifying ascending or descending sort order. In return, we get a sorted list of numbers.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In my experience, some people are better at capturing boundary conditions than others. The question is, &quot;How do you know when you are 'done' capturing test cases&quot;?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;We can start listing cases now and some clever person will undoubtedly think of 'one more' case that isn't covered by any of the previous.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="12081" OwnerDisplayName="Brad Barker" LastEditorUserId="1190" LastEditorDisplayName="Gaius" LastEditDate="2008-08-23T20:02:59.393" LastActivityDate="2008-09-26T01:42:25.813" Title="Rigor in capturing test cases for unit testing" Tags="&lt;unit-testing&gt;&lt;testing&gt;&lt;sorting&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="4" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
