  <row Id="12890" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="12950" CreationDate="2008-08-15T23:36:30.207" Score="21" ViewCount="10983" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a large database of normalized order data that is becoming very slow to query for reporting.  Many of the queries that I use in reports join five or six tables and are having to examine tens or hundreds of thousands of lines.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;There are lots of queries and most have been optimized as much as possible to reduce server load and increase speed.  I think it's time to start keeping a copy of the data in a denormalized format.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Any ideas on an approach?  Should I start with a couple of my worst queries and go from there?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1430" OwnerDisplayName="Eric G" LastEditorUserId="2967572" LastEditDate="2014-01-15T08:01:49.050" LastActivityDate="2014-01-15T08:01:49.050" Title="What is a good way to denormalize a mysql database?" Tags="&lt;mysql&gt;&lt;database&gt;&lt;denormalization&gt;" AnswerCount="8" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="3" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
