  <row Id="14422" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="14507" CreationDate="2008-08-18T10:23:22.113" Score="17" ViewCount="4267" Body="&lt;p&gt;For example, &lt;a href=&quot;http://developer.apple.com/cocoa/pyobjc.html&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;http://developer.apple.com/cocoa/pyobjc.html&lt;/a&gt; is still for OS X 10.4 Tiger, not 10.5 Leopard.. And that's the official Apple documentation for it..&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The official PyObjC page is equally bad, &lt;a href=&quot;http://pyobjc.sourceforge.net/&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;http://pyobjc.sourceforge.net/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;It's so bad it's baffling.. I'm considering learning Ruby primarily because the RubyCocoa stuff is so much better documented, and there's lots of decent tutorials (&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.rubycocoa.com/&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;http://www.rubycocoa.com/&lt;/a&gt; for example), and because of the Shoes GUI toolkit..&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Even &lt;a href=&quot;http://66.163.168.225/babelfish/translate_url_content?lp=ja_en&amp;amp;url=http%3A%2F%2Fblog.monospace.jp%2F2007%2F11%2F05%2Fxcode3_cocoa_python%2F&amp;amp;fr=avbbf-us&amp;amp;.intl=us&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;this badly-auto-translated Japanese tutorial&lt;/a&gt; is more useful than the rest of the documentation I could find..&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;All I want to do is create fairly simple Python applications with Cocoa GUI's..&lt;br&gt;&#xA;Can anyone shed light on the horrible documentation, or point me at some tutorials that don't just give you huge blocks of code and assume you know what &lt;code&gt;NSThread.detachNewThreadSelector_toTarget_withObject_(&quot;queryController&quot;, self, None)&lt;/code&gt; does..?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="745" OwnerDisplayName="dbr" LastEditorDisplayName="user1228" LastEditDate="2013-08-20T12:56:44.553" LastActivityDate="2013-08-20T12:56:44.553" Title="Why is the PyObjC documentation so bad?" Tags="&lt;python&gt;&lt;macos&gt;&lt;cocoa&gt;&lt;pyobjc&gt;" AnswerCount="9" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="4" ClosedDate="2013-08-20T14:46:02.913" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="14432" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="14462" CreationDate="2008-08-18T10:35:14.913" Score="10" ViewCount="3829" Body="&lt;p&gt;I know that &lt;code&gt;.NET&lt;/code&gt; is &lt;code&gt;JIT&lt;/code&gt; compiled to the architecture you are running on just before the app runs, but does the JIT compiler optimize for 64bit architecture at all?  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there anything that needs to be done or considered when programming an app that will run on a &lt;code&gt;64bit system&lt;/code&gt;? (i.e. Will using Int64 improve performance and will the JIT compiler automatically make Int64 work on 32bit systems?)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="194" OwnerDisplayName="Adam Haile" LastEditorUserId="4311889" LastEditDate="2016-08-01T14:41:19.210" LastActivityDate="2016-08-01T14:41:19.210" Title="64bit .NET Performance tuning" Tags="&lt;.net&gt;&lt;performance&gt;&lt;optimization&gt;&lt;64-bit&gt;&lt;jit&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="3" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
