  <row Id="2169118" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="2170161" CreationDate="2010-01-30T19:28:33.760" Score="16" ViewCount="36689" Body="&lt;p&gt;The following R commands will install all CRAN packages:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;availablePackages &amp;lt;- available.packages()[,1]&#xA;install.packages(availablePackages)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;And the following command will list all installed packages:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;installedPackages &amp;lt;- .packages(all.available = TRUE)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;My question is: &lt;b&gt;How do I instruct R to install all CRAN packages that are not already installed?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="58394" LastActivityDate="2019-10-07T16:57:20.760" Title="Installing all CRAN packages that are not already installed?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;cran&gt;" AnswerCount="6" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="7" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="14451" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="14472" CreationDate="2008-08-18T10:56:42.623" Score="20" ViewCount="31909" Body="&lt;p&gt;What is the best way to make a delphi application (delphi 2007 for win32 here) go completely full screen, removing the application border and covering windows task bar ?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I am looking for something similar to what IE does when you hit F11.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I wish this to be a run time option for the user not a design time decision by my good self.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;As Mentioned in the accepted answer &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;BorderStyle := bsNone; &#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;was part of the way to do it.  Strangely I kept getting a &lt;code&gt;E2010 Incompatible types: 'TFormBorderStyle' and 'TBackGroundSymbol'&lt;/code&gt; error when using that line (another type had &lt;code&gt;bsNone&lt;/code&gt; defined).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;To overcome this I had to use : &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;BorderStyle := Forms.bsNone;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1724" LastEditorUserId="1737" LastEditDate="2014-05-19T08:52:08.853" LastActivityDate="2018-11-09T13:46:27.903" Title="What is the best way to make a Delphi Application completely full screen?" Tags="&lt;delphi&gt;&lt;delphi-2007&gt;" AnswerCount="9" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="14" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="14453" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="19672" CreationDate="2008-08-18T10:58:45.007" Score="5" ViewCount="573" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm needing to access Excel workbooks from .Net. I know all about the different ways of doing it (I've written them up in a &lt;a href=&quot;http://blog.functionalfun.net/2008/06/reading-and-writing-excel-files-with.html&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot; title=&quot;Reading and Writing Excel files in .Net&quot;&gt;blog post&lt;/a&gt;), and I know that using a native .Net component is going to be the fastest. But the question is, which of the components wins? Has anybody benchmarked them? I've been using Syncfusion XlsIO, but that's very slow for some key operations (like deleting rows in a workbook containing thousands of Named ranges).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1727" LastActivityDate="2017-11-06T10:55:06.500" Title="Does anyone have .Net Excel IO component benchmarks?" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;excel&gt;&lt;components&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="2170043" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="2170103" CreationDate="2010-01-31T00:14:04.650" Score="33" ViewCount="9188" Body="&lt;p&gt;When installing R packages (say &lt;code&gt;mcmcpack&lt;/code&gt; in this example) under Ubuntu I have the choice between the following two methods of installation:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;# Let the distribution's packaging system take care of installation/upgrades&#xA;apt-get install r-cran-mcmcpack&#xA;&#xA;# Let R take care of installation/upgrades&#xA;install.packages(&quot;mcmcpack&quot;)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Questions:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Is any of the two ways of installing R packages considered &quot;best practice&quot;?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Assume that I first &lt;code&gt;install.packages(&quot;mcmcpack&quot;)&lt;/code&gt; and later on &lt;code&gt;apt-get install r-cran-mcmcpack&lt;/code&gt; - should I expect trouble?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Assume that I first &lt;code&gt;apt-get install r-cran-mcmcpack&lt;/code&gt; and later on &lt;code&gt;install.packages(&quot;mcmcpack&quot;)&lt;/code&gt; - should I expect trouble?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="58394" LastEditorUserId="58394" LastEditDate="2010-02-01T00:26:39.760" LastActivityDate="2015-03-08T10:46:23.887" Title="R: apt-get install r-cran-foo vs. install.packages(&quot;foo&quot;)" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;ubuntu&gt;&lt;debian&gt;&lt;cran&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="16" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="14464" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="14591" CreationDate="2008-08-18T11:19:28.367" Score="83" ViewCount="107238" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a structure which I need to populate and write to disk (several actually).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;An example is:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;byte-6    &#xA;bit0 - original_or_copy  &#xA;bit1 - copyright  &#xA;bit2 - data_alignment_indicator  &#xA;bit3 - PES_priority  &#xA;bit4-bit5 - PES_scrambling control.  &#xA;bit6-bit7 - reserved  &#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In C I might do something like the following:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;struct PESHeader  {&#xA;    unsigned reserved:2;&#xA;    unsigned scrambling_control:2;&#xA;    unsigned priority:1;&#xA;    unsigned data_alignment_indicator:1;&#xA;    unsigned copyright:1;&#xA;    unsigned original_or_copy:1;&#xA;};&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there any way to do this in C# that would enable me to access the bits using the struct dereferencing dot operator? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;For a couple of structures, I can just do bit shifting wrapped in an accessor function.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I have loads of structures to handle in this way, so I'm looking for something that's easier to read and quicker to write.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1006" OwnerDisplayName="Rob" LastEditorUserId="15168" LastEditDate="2015-12-17T06:53:34.677" LastActivityDate="2021-08-21T07:04:05.053" Title="Bit fields in C#" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;bit-fields&gt;" AnswerCount="12" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="38" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
