  <row Id="16891" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="17187" CreationDate="2008-08-19T20:32:42.413" Score="9" ViewCount="2109" Body="&lt;p&gt;As you may know, in &lt;code&gt;VS 2008&lt;/code&gt; &lt;kbd&gt;ctrl&lt;/kbd&gt;+&lt;kbd&gt;tab&lt;/kbd&gt; brings up a nifty navigator window with a thumbnail of each file. I love it, but there is one tiny thing that is annoying to me about this feature: &lt;em&gt;the window stays around after releasing the &lt;kbd&gt;ctrl&lt;/kbd&gt; key&lt;/em&gt;. When doing an &lt;kbd&gt;alt&lt;/kbd&gt;+&lt;kbd&gt;tab&lt;/kbd&gt; in windows, you can hit tab to get to the item you want (while still holding down the &lt;kbd&gt;alt&lt;/kbd&gt; key), and then when you find what you want, &lt;em&gt;lifting up&lt;/em&gt; on the &lt;kbd&gt;alt&lt;/kbd&gt; key selects that item.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I wish &lt;code&gt;VS 2008&lt;/code&gt; would do the same. For me, when I lift off of &lt;kbd&gt;ctrl&lt;/kbd&gt;, the window is still there. I have to hit &lt;kbd&gt;enter&lt;/kbd&gt; to actually select the item. I find this annoying.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Does anyone know how to make &lt;code&gt;VS 2008&lt;/code&gt; dismiss the window on the &lt;em&gt;release&lt;/em&gt; of the &lt;kbd&gt;ctrl&lt;/kbd&gt; key?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1789" OwnerDisplayName="Jon" LastEditorUserId="2901002" LastEditorDisplayName="hop" LastEditDate="2015-07-17T10:46:00.583" LastActivityDate="2018-06-27T02:47:26.067" Title="VS 2008 - ctrl-tab behavior" Tags="&lt;visual-studio-2008&gt;&lt;ide&gt;&lt;keyboard-shortcuts&gt;" AnswerCount="10" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="4" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="16897" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="17642" CreationDate="2008-08-19T20:34:13.177" Score="9" ViewCount="5734" Body="&lt;p&gt;In the company that i work, we develop all the GUI in C#, but the application kernel is mainly developed in Delphi 5 (for historical reasons), with a lot of components made in COM+. Related to this very specific sort of application a I two questions:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Experienced guys in Delphi and/or COM, do you have any workrounds to work with the buggy TLB interface ? &#xA;Some of the bugs are: IDE crashing during edition of a large TLB, lost of methods IDs, TLB corruption, etc.&#xA;Here, we haven't found any good solution. Actually we tried do upgrade do the new 2007 version. But the new IDE TLB interface has the same bugs that we found before.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;How do you control TLBs versions ? The TLB file is in a binary format and conflict resolutions are very hard to do. We tried to do it exporting the interfaces descriptions to IDL and commiting into CVS, but we didn't found any good way to generate TLBs from IDL using Delphi. Additionaly, the MIDL tool provided by Microsoft, didn't parse correctly the IDL files that we exported from delphi.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2015" OwnerDisplayName="Veronese" LastEditorUserId="3218692" LastEditorDisplayName="Charles Faiga" LastEditDate="2015-11-20T11:40:18.913" LastActivityDate="2015-11-20T11:40:18.913" Title="Delphi and COM: TLB and maintenance issues" Tags="&lt;delphi&gt;&lt;com&gt;&lt;typelib&gt;&lt;com-hell&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="2351744" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="2352006" CreationDate="2010-02-28T16:48:51.293" Score="30" ViewCount="23691" Body="&lt;p&gt;Here is a function I wrote to break a long string into lines not longer than a given length&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;strBreakInLines &amp;lt;- function(s, breakAt=90, prepend=&quot;&quot;) {&#xA;  words &amp;lt;- unlist(strsplit(s, &quot; &quot;))&#xA;  if (length(words)&amp;lt;2) return(s)&#xA;  wordLen &amp;lt;- unlist(Map(nchar, words))&#xA;  lineLen &amp;lt;- wordLen[1]&#xA;  res &amp;lt;- words[1]&#xA;  lineBreak &amp;lt;- paste(&quot;\n&quot;, prepend, sep=&quot;&quot;)&#xA;  for (i in 2:length(words)) {&#xA;    lineLen &amp;lt;- lineLen+wordLen[i]&#xA;    if (lineLen &amp;lt; breakAt) &#xA;      res &amp;lt;- paste(res, words[i], sep=&quot; &quot;)&#xA;    else {&#xA;      res &amp;lt;- paste(res, words[i], sep=lineBreak)&#xA;      lineLen &amp;lt;- 0&#xA;    }&#xA;  }&#xA;  return(res)&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;It works for the problem I had; but I wonder if I can learn something here. Is there a shorter or more efficient solution, especially can I get rid of the for loop?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="216064" LastActivityDate="2015-07-23T11:28:20.610" Title="Insert line breaks in long string -- word wrap" Tags="&lt;string&gt;&lt;r&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="9" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="16918" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="16951" CreationDate="2008-08-19T20:44:48.897" Score="122" ViewCount="21818" Body="&lt;p&gt;I've been looking for a decent guide to Haskell for some time, but haven't been able to find one that seems interesting enough to read through and/or makes sense.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I've had prior exposure to Haskell a few years back, but I can't remember much about it. I remember the &quot;Aha!&quot;-feeling was incredible when I finally got it, and it was actually fun to play with, so I'm looking to rediscover the lost art of Haskell.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I'm familiar with Ruby and its functional programming tricks, so I think I'm not completely in the dark. Any links?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2018" OwnerDisplayName="wvdschel" LastEditorUserId="2134" LastEditorDisplayName="Chris Fournier" LastEditDate="2008-08-23T14:48:44.437" LastActivityDate="2021-05-17T11:04:45.050" Title="Beginners Guide to Haskell?" Tags="&lt;haskell&gt;&lt;functional-programming&gt;" AnswerCount="16" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="97" ClosedDate="2012-05-01T23:08:09.210" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
