  <row Id="17373" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="27791" CreationDate="2008-08-20T03:20:02.090" Score="12" ViewCount="18172" Body="&lt;p&gt;How do I open the default mail program with a Subject and Body in a cross-platform way?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Unfortunately, this is for a a client app written in Java, not a website.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I would like this to work in a cross-platform way (which means Windows and Mac, sorry Linux). I am happy to execute a VBScript in Windows, or AppleScript in OS X. But I have no idea what those scripts should contain. I would love to execute the user's default program vs. just searching for Outlook or whatever.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In OS X, I have tried executing the command:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;open mailto:?subject=MySubject&amp;amp;body=TheBody&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;URL escaping is needed to replace spaces with &lt;code&gt;%20&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Updated&lt;/strong&gt; On Windows, you have to play all sorts of games to get &lt;code&gt;start&lt;/code&gt; to run correctly. Here is the proper Java incantation:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;class Win32 extends OS {&#xA;    public void email(String subject, String body) throws Exception {&#xA;        String cmd = &quot;cmd.exe /c start \&quot;\&quot; \&quot;&quot; + formatMailto(subject, body) + &quot;\&quot;&quot;;&#xA;        Runtime.getRuntime().exec(cmd);&#xA;    }&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="338" OwnerDisplayName="Frank Krueger" LastEditorUserId="338" LastEditorDisplayName="Frank Krueger" LastEditDate="2008-08-26T02:56:18.230" LastActivityDate="2013-01-22T03:35:58.493" Title="How do I open the default mail program with a Subject and Body in a cross-platform way?" Tags="&lt;java&gt;&lt;windows&gt;&lt;macos&gt;&lt;email&gt;" AnswerCount="9" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="3" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="17376" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="17414" CreationDate="2008-08-20T03:27:08.343" Score="6" ViewCount="3482" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a PHP script that runs as a CGI program and the HTTP &lt;code&gt;Authenticate&lt;/code&gt; header gets eaten and spit out. So I would like to implement some kind of FORM based authentication. As an added constraint, there is no database so no session data can be stored.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I am very open to having a master username and password. I just need to protect the application from an intruder who doesn't know these credentials.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So how would you implement this?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Cookies?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I could present the form and if it validates, I can send back a cookie that is a hash of the IP address come secret code. Then I can prevent pages from rendering unless the thing decrypts correctly. But I have no idea how to implement that in PHP.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="338" OwnerDisplayName="Frank Krueger" LastEditorUserId="2961" LastEditorDisplayName="Stu Thompson" LastEditDate="2008-09-17T07:56:49.710" LastActivityDate="2019-08-24T16:08:46.053" Title="How would you implement FORM based authentication without a backing database?" Tags="&lt;php&gt;&lt;http&gt;&lt;authentication&gt;&lt;cookies&gt;&lt;cgi&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
