  <row Id="17715" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="17730" CreationDate="2008-08-20T10:38:45.653" Score="5" ViewCount="287" Body="&lt;p&gt;So, you're writing a web application and you have several areas of the site where the user can upload files. My basic working method for this is to store the actual file on the server, and have a database table that connects the stored filename to the record it relates to.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;My question is this: Should there be a different table for each &quot;type&quot; of file? Also, should the files be stored in context-related locations on the server, or all together?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Some examples: user profile photos, job application CVs, related documents on CMS pages, etc.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1951" OwnerDisplayName="mercutio" LastEditorUserId="2134" LastEditorDisplayName="Chris Fournier" LastEditDate="2008-08-23T15:21:52.690" LastActivityDate="2015-10-08T00:52:19.917" Title="When is a file just a file?" Tags="&lt;database-design&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="2397097" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2010-03-07T17:12:48.107" Score="21" ViewCount="21050" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have an R function which produces 95% confidence ellipses for scatterplots. The output looks like this, having a default of 50 points for each ellipse (50 rows):&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;           [,1]         [,2]&#xA; [1,]  0.097733810  0.044957994&#xA; [2,]  0.084433494  0.050337990&#xA; [3,]  0.069746783  0.054891438&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I would like to superimpose a number of such ellipses for each level of a factor called 'site' on a &lt;code&gt;ggplot2&lt;/code&gt; scatterplot, produced from this command:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; plat1 &amp;lt;- ggplot(mapping=aes(shape=site, size=geom), shape=factor(site)); plat1 + geom_point(aes(x=PC1.1,y=PC2.1))&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This is run on a dataset, called &lt;code&gt;dflat&lt;/code&gt; which looks like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;site      geom         PC1.1        PC2.1       PC3.1        PC1.2       PC2.2&#xA;1 Buhlen 1259.5649 -0.0387975838 -0.022889782  0.01355317  0.008705276  0.02441577&#xA;2 Buhlen  653.6607 -0.0009398704 -0.013076251  0.02898955 -0.001345149  0.03133990&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The result is fine, but when I try to add the ellipse (let's say for this one site, called &quot;Buhlen&quot;):&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; plat1 + geom_point(aes(x=PC1.1,y=PC2.1)) + geom_path(data=subset(dflat, site=&quot;Buhlen&quot;),mapping=aes(x=ELLI(PC1.1,PC2.1)[,1],y=ELLI(PC1.1,PC2.1)[,2]))&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I get an error message: &lt;code&gt;&quot;Error in data.frame(x = c(0.0977338099339815, 0.0844334944904515, 0.0697467834016782,  : &#xA;  arguments imply differing number of rows: 50, 211&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I've managed to fix this in the past, but I cannot remember how. It seems that geom_path is relying on the same points rather than plotting new ones. Any help would be appreciated.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="288281" LastActivityDate="2018-06-03T00:14:31.607" Title="How can a data ellipse be superimposed on a ggplot2 scatterplot?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;ggplot2&gt;&lt;ellipse&gt;&lt;scatter-plot&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="6" FavoriteCount="15" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="17717" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-20T10:40:38.723" Score="26" ViewCount="16426" Body="&lt;p&gt;We are currently using MySQL for a product we are building, and are keen to move to PostgreSQL as soon as possible, primarily for licensing reasons.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Has anyone else done such a move? Our database is the lifeblood of the application and will eventually be storing TBs of data, so I'm keen to hear about experiences of performance improvements/losses, major hurdles in converting SQL and stored procedures, etc.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Edit: Just to clarify to those who have asked why we don't like MySQL's licensing. We are developing a commercial product which (currently) depends on MySQL as a database back-end. Their license states we need to pay them a percentage of our list price per installation, and not a flat fee. As a startup, this is less than appealing.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1693" OwnerDisplayName="Steve" LastEditorUserId="305" LastEditorDisplayName="Mark Biek" LastEditDate="2008-08-31T00:46:43.493" LastActivityDate="2012-05-06T22:46:40.563" Title="Migrating from MySQL to PostgreSQL" Tags="&lt;mysql&gt;&lt;database&gt;&lt;postgresql&gt;&lt;licensing&gt;&lt;migration&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="12" ClosedDate="2012-01-31T16:13:40.443" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
