  <row Id="18197" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="18263" CreationDate="2008-08-20T15:15:06.333" Score="12" ViewCount="1162" Body="&lt;p&gt;How do you test the usability of the user interfaces of your applications - be they web or desktop?  Do you just throw it all together and then tweak it based on user experience once the application is live?  Or do you pass it to a specific usability team for testing prior to release?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;We are a small software house, but I am interested in the best practices of how to measure usability.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Any help appreciated.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="770" OwnerDisplayName="Martin" LastEditorUserId="770" LastEditorDisplayName="Martin" LastEditDate="2008-12-10T09:27:07.920" LastActivityDate="2020-07-27T16:34:41.250" Title="How do you test the usability of your user interfaces" Tags="&lt;user-interface&gt;&lt;testing&gt;&lt;usability&gt;" AnswerCount="11" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="3" ClosedDate="2020-07-27T18:04:38.827" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="2436688" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="2436960" CreationDate="2010-03-13T00:14:09.813" Score="253" ViewCount="307868" Body="&lt;p&gt;If I have some R list &lt;code&gt;mylist&lt;/code&gt;, you can append an item &lt;code&gt;obj&lt;/code&gt; to it like so:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;mylist[[length(mylist)+1]] &amp;lt;- obj&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;But surely there is some more compact way.  When I was new at R, I  tried writing &lt;code&gt;lappend()&lt;/code&gt; like so:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;lappend &amp;lt;- function(lst, obj) {&#xA;    lst[[length(lst)+1]] &amp;lt;- obj&#xA;    return(lst)&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;but of course that doesn't work due to R's call-by-name semantics (&lt;code&gt;lst&lt;/code&gt; is effectively copied upon call, so changes to &lt;code&gt;lst&lt;/code&gt; are not visible outside the scope of &lt;code&gt;lappend()&lt;/code&gt;.  I know you can do environment hacking in an R function to reach outside the scope of your function and mutate the calling environment, but that seems like a large hammer to write a simple append function.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Can anyone suggest a more beautiful way of doing this? Bonus points if it works for both vectors and lists.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="5222" LastEditorUserId="202229" LastEditDate="2016-04-28T18:22:43.240" LastActivityDate="2018-11-26T11:23:48.910" Title="Append an object to a list in R in amortized constant time, O(1)?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;performance&gt;&lt;list&gt;&lt;append&gt;&lt;big-o&gt;" AnswerCount="17" CommentCount="8" FavoriteCount="74" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="18199" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="18221" CreationDate="2008-08-20T15:15:25.177" Score="3" ViewCount="2257" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a SQL Server 2005 database, that I would like to be able to recreate at a moment's notice. I want to be able to point to my database and generate a complete set of scripts that will not only create all the &lt;em&gt;tables / views / sprocs / functions&lt;/em&gt; that are in the database, but will also populate all the tables with data.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Are there any tools that do this? Are there any open-source or free tools that do this?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2059" LastEditorUserId="7750640" LastEditDate="2017-07-10T19:19:58.100" LastActivityDate="2017-07-10T19:19:58.100" Title="How do I generate scripts that will rebuild my MS SQL Server 2005 database with data?" Tags="&lt;sql-server&gt;&lt;scripting&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
