  <row Id="18505" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="18756" CreationDate="2008-08-20T18:22:55.320" Score="7" ViewCount="5576" Body="&lt;p&gt;In an application that I am currently working on, a requirement is to bring a window of an external application to the foreground.  Making Win32 API calls such as BringWindowToTop and SetForeground window do not work all the time.  This is due to some restrictions within Windows XP.  What I would like to do instead is send simulate a mouse click the window's button on the taskbar which I am hoping will bring the window to the front.  Does anyone know how this is possible?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="324" OwnerDisplayName="adeel825" LastEditorUserId="611" LastEditorDisplayName="OJ" LastEditDate="2008-08-22T06:13:03.843" LastActivityDate="2013-01-23T05:09:55.427" Title="Sending a mouse click to a button in the taskbar using C#" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;.net&gt;&lt;windows&gt;&lt;winapi&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="18524" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="18529" CreationDate="2008-08-20T18:29:48.410" Score="115" ViewCount="216572" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a list of integers, &lt;code&gt;List&amp;lt;Integer&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; and I'd like to convert all the integer objects into Strings, thus finishing up with a new &lt;code&gt;List&amp;lt;String&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Naturally, I could create a new &lt;code&gt;List&amp;lt;String&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; and loop through the list calling &lt;code&gt;String.valueOf()&lt;/code&gt; for each integer, but I was wondering if there was a better (read: &lt;em&gt;more automatic&lt;/em&gt;) way of doing it?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="916" OwnerDisplayName="ChrisThomas123" LastEditorUserId="6074376" LastEditorDisplayName="ChrisThomas123" LastEditDate="2017-11-02T22:27:53.883" LastActivityDate="2021-12-17T12:31:15.580" Title="Converting List&lt;Integer&gt; to List&lt;String&gt;" Tags="&lt;java&gt;&lt;string&gt;&lt;collections&gt;&lt;integer&gt;" AnswerCount="21" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="26" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="18533" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="18539" CreationDate="2008-08-20T18:36:02.310" Score="21" ViewCount="9205" Body="&lt;p&gt;I've found myself increasingly unsatisfied with the DataSet/DataTable/DataRow paradigm in .Net, mostly because it's often a couple of steps more complicated than what I really want to do.  In cases where I'm binding to controls, DataSets are fine.  But in other cases, there seems to be a fair amount of mental overhead.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I've played a bit with SqlDataReader, and that seems to be good for simple jaunts through a select, but I feel like there may be some other models lurking in .Net that are useful to learn more about.  I feel like all of the help I find on this just uses DataSet by default.  Maybe that and DataReader really are the best options.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I'm not looking for a best/worst breakdown, just curious what my options are and what experiences you've had with them.  Thanks!&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;-Eric Sipple&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="111" OwnerDisplayName="saalon" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditDate="2008-09-09T22:13:42.683" LastActivityDate="2021-07-06T15:48:31.517" Title="C#: What Else Do You Use Besides DataSet" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;.net&gt;&lt;sql&gt;&lt;dataset&gt;" AnswerCount="13" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="3" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="18538" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="5645413" CreationDate="2008-08-20T18:37:51.370" Score="116" ViewCount="69120" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'd like some sorthand for this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;Map rowToMap(row) {&#xA;    def rowMap = [:];&#xA;    row.columns.each{ rowMap[it.name] = it.val }&#xA;    return rowMap;&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;given the way the GDK stuff is, I'd expect to be able to do something like:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;Map rowToMap(row) {&#xA;    row.columns.collectMap{ [it.name,it.val] }&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;but I haven't seen anything in the docs... am I missing something? or am I just way too lazy?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2031" OwnerDisplayName="danb" LastEditorUserId="2031" LastEditorDisplayName="danb" LastEditDate="2008-08-20T22:27:36.357" LastActivityDate="2017-03-09T08:06:40.983" Title="shortcut for creating a Map from a List in groovy?" Tags="&lt;java&gt;&lt;collections&gt;&lt;groovy&gt;&lt;expandometaclass&gt;" AnswerCount="8" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="17" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="18584" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="18718" CreationDate="2008-08-20T18:59:03.377" Score="1" ViewCount="6968" Body="&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Possible Duplicate:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;a href=&quot;https://stackoverflow.com/questions/9/how-do-i-calculate-someones-age-in-c&quot;&gt;How do I calculate someone&amp;#39;s age in C#?&lt;/a&gt;  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Maybe this could be silly but and I don't have issues with my age but sometimes it is good to calculate the exact age of someone, I have introduced my birthdate in my profile (01/12/1975) &quot;dd/mm/yyyy&quot; and it calculated 33 and I'm 32 actually still, isn't it better to calculate the exact age? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Maybe&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;DateTime dt1 = DateTime.Now;&#xA;TimeSpan dt2;&#xA;dt2 = dt1.Subtract(new DateTime(1975, 12, 01));&#xA;double year = dt2.TotalDays / 365;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The result of year is 32.77405678074&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Could this code be OK?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1130097" OwnerDisplayName="nmiranda" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditDate="2017-05-23T12:28:00.033" LastActivityDate="2012-05-26T23:50:05.027" Title="Exact age calculation" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="1" ClosedDate="2012-05-28T00:39:18.857" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="18585" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="19056" CreationDate="2008-08-20T18:59:35.187" Score="4" ViewCount="4843" Body="&lt;h3&gt;Update: Solved, with code&lt;/h3&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://stackoverflow.com/questions/18585/why-cant-you-bind-the-size-of-a-windows-form-to-applicationsettings#19056&quot;&gt;I got it working, see my answer below for the code...&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h3&gt;Original Post&lt;/h3&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;As Tundey pointed out in &lt;a href=&quot;https://stackoverflow.com/questions/18421/best-way-to-bind-windows-forms-properties-to-applicationsettings-in-c#18456&quot;&gt;his answer&lt;/a&gt; to my &lt;a href=&quot;https://stackoverflow.com/questions/18421/best-way-to-bind-windows-forms-properties-to-applicationsettings-in-c&quot;&gt;last question&lt;/a&gt;, you can bind nearly everything about a windows forms control to ApplicationSettings pretty effortlessly. So is there really no way to do this with form Size? &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.codeproject.com/KB/cs/UserSettings.aspx&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;This tutorial&lt;/a&gt; says you need to handle Size explicitly so you can save RestoreBounds instead of size if the window is maximized or minimized. However, I hoped I could just use a property like:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;public Size RestoreSize&#xA;{&#xA;    get&#xA;    {&#xA;        if (this.WindowState == FormWindowState.Normal)&#xA;        {&#xA;            return this.Size;&#xA;        }&#xA;        else&#xA;        {&#xA;            return this.RestoreBounds.Size;&#xA;        }&#xA;    }&#xA;    set&#xA;    {&#xA;        ...&#xA;    }&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;But I can't see a way to bind this in the designer (Size is notably missing from the PropertyBinding list).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="229" OwnerDisplayName="Brian Jorgensen" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditorDisplayName="mark" LastEditDate="2020-06-20T09:12:55.060" LastActivityDate="2010-12-11T21:24:47.210" Title="Why can't you bind the Size of a windows form to ApplicationSettings?" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;visual-studio&gt;&lt;data-binding&gt;&lt;.net-2.0&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="2458013" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="2458377" CreationDate="2010-03-16T20:47:50.973" Score="53" ViewCount="41801" Body="&lt;p&gt;Let's say we have the following function:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;foo &amp;lt;- function(x)&#xA;{&#xA;    line1 &amp;lt;- x&#xA;    line2 &amp;lt;- 0&#xA;    line3 &amp;lt;- line1 + line2&#xA;    return(line3)&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;And that we want to change the second line to be:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;    line2 &amp;lt;- 2&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How would you do that?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;One way is to use&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;fix(foo)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;And change the function.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Another way is to just write the function again.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there another way? (Remember, the task was to change just the second line)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What I would like is for some way to represent the function as a vector of strings (well, characters), then change one of it's values, and then turn it into a function again.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="256662" LastEditorUserId="256662" LastEditDate="2010-03-16T21:29:06.150" LastActivityDate="2022-02-07T18:12:21.820" Title="What ways are there to edit a function in R?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;function&gt;&lt;editing&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="3" FavoriteCount="21" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
