  <row Id="18696" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="18714" CreationDate="2008-08-20T20:03:28.840" Score="3" ViewCount="2370" Body="&lt;p&gt;Our company is currently writing a GUI automation testing tool for compact framework applications. We have initially searched many tools but none of them was right for us. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;By using the tool you can record test-cases and group them together to test-suites. For every test-suite there is generated an application, which launches the application-under-test and simulates user-input.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In general the tool works fine, but as we are using &lt;strong&gt;window handles&lt;/strong&gt; for simulation user input, you can't do very many things. For example it is impossible for us to get the name of a control (we just get the caption). &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Another problem using window handles is checking for a change. At the moment we simulate a click on a control and depending on the result we know if the application has gone to the next step.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there any other (simpler) way for doing such things (for example the message queue or anything else)?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2078" LastEditorUserId="3619" LastEditorDisplayName="Chris Fournier" LastEditDate="2009-12-04T18:13:37.587" LastActivityDate="2009-12-04T18:13:37.587" Title="GUI Automation testing - Window handle questions" Tags="&lt;ui-automation&gt;&lt;window-handles&gt;&lt;gui-testing&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="4" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="18702" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="18750" CreationDate="2008-08-20T20:07:34.057" Score="1" ViewCount="276" Body="&lt;p&gt;I often use webservice this way&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;public void CallWebservice()&#xA;&#xA;{&#xA;&#xA;  mywebservice web = new mywebservice();&#xA;&#xA;  web.call();&#xA;&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;but sometimes I do this&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;private mywebservice web;&#xA;&#xA;public Constructor()&#xA;{&#xA;&#xA;  web = new mywebservice();&#xA;&#xA;}&#xA;&#xA;public void CallWebservice()&#xA;{&#xA;&#xA;  web.call();&#xA;&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The second approach likes me very much but sometimes it times out and I had to start the application again, the first one I think it brings overhead and it is not very efficient, in fact, sometimes the first call returns a WebException - ConnectFailure (I don't know why). &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I found an article (&lt;a href=&quot;http://addressof.com/blog/archive/2004/04/27/Web-Service-Woes-_2800_A-light-at-the-end-of-the-tunnel_3F002900_.aspx&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;Web Service Woes (A light at the end of the tunnel?)&lt;/a&gt;) that exceeds the time out turning the KeepAlive property to false in the overriden function GetWebRequest, here's is the code:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;Protected Overrides Function GetWebRequest(ByVal uri As System.Uri) As System.Net.WebRequest&#xA;&#xA;  Dim webRequest As Net.HttpWebRequest = CType(MyBase.GetWebRequest(uri), Net.HttpWebRequest)&#xA;&#xA;  webRequest.KeepAlive = False&#xA;&#xA;  Return webRequest&#xA;&#xA;End Function&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The question is, is it possible to extend forever the webservice time out and finally, how do you implement your webservices to handle this issue?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1130097" OwnerDisplayName="nmiranda" LastEditorUserId="1448360" LastEditorDisplayName="Chris Fournier" LastEditDate="2013-02-16T14:52:14.073" LastActivityDate="2013-02-16T14:52:14.073" Title="Webservice alive forever" Tags="&lt;web-services&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="18705" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="19071" CreationDate="2008-08-20T20:09:45.393" Score="0" ViewCount="308" Body="&lt;p&gt;I need to be able to create basic MS Project items (tasks, projects, resources, etc.) programmatically from my app to my Project Server 2003 install, and haven't found any good examples. Can anyone point me to some good references or have some sample code of connecting to the server and creating these items?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="829" OwnerDisplayName="SkullDuggerT" LastEditorUserId="16587746" LastEditDate="2021-09-16T07:09:22.513" LastActivityDate="2021-09-16T07:09:22.513" Title="Anyone know how to create Projects/Tasks for Project Server 2003 via C#?" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;project-server&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="18717" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="18723" CreationDate="2008-08-20T20:18:08.757" Score="127" ViewCount="41258" Body="&lt;p&gt;As far as I know, foreign keys (FK) are used to aid the programmer to manipulate data in the correct way. Suppose a programmer is actually doing this in the right manner already, then do we really need the concept of foreign keys?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Are there any other uses for foreign keys? Am I missing something here?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="184" OwnerDisplayName="Niyaz" LastEditorUserId="116" LastEditorDisplayName="chills42" LastEditDate="2016-09-30T04:34:45.633" LastActivityDate="2020-02-05T17:16:59.333" Title="Are foreign keys really necessary in a database design?" Tags="&lt;database&gt;&lt;oracle&gt;&lt;foreign-keys&gt;" AnswerCount="24" CommentCount="15" FavoriteCount="54" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="18719" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-20T20:18:32.470" Score="9" ViewCount="21690" Body="&lt;p&gt;As part of our databuild run a 3rd party program (3D Studio Max) to export a number of assets. Unfortunately if a user is not currently logged in, or the machine is locked, then Max does not run correctly.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This can be solved for freshly booted machines by using a method such as TweakUI for automatic login. However when a user connects via Remote Desktop (to initiate a non-scheduled build, change a setting, whatever) then after the session ends the machine is left in a locked state with Max unable to run.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I'm looking for a way to configure windows (via fair means or foul) so either it does not lock when the remote session ends, or it &quot;unlocks&quot; itself a short while after. I'm aware of a method under XP where you can run a batchfile on the machine which kicks the remote user off, but this does not appear to work on Windows Server.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1043" OwnerDisplayName="Andrew" LastEditorUserId="1043" LastEditorDisplayName="Andrew Grant" LastEditDate="2009-03-09T17:30:37.097" LastActivityDate="2009-03-09T17:30:37.097" Title="How can I prevent a server from becoming locked after a Remote Desktop session" Tags="&lt;windows-server-2008&gt;&lt;build-automation&gt;&lt;windows-server-2003&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="18754" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="18831" CreationDate="2008-08-20T20:33:28.423" Score="34" ViewCount="28010" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm writing some documentation in Markdown, and creating a separate file for each section of the doc.  I would like to be able to convert all the files to HTML in one go, but I can't find anyone else who has tried the same thing.  I'm on a Mac, so I would think a simple bash script should be able to handle it, but I've never done anything in bash and haven't had any luck.  It seems like it should be simple to write something so I could just run:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;markdown-batch ./*.markdown&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Any ideas?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2185" OwnerDisplayName="Brock" LastEditorUserId="488657" LastEditDate="2014-05-11T16:24:14.987" LastActivityDate="2022-02-05T13:06:01.210" Title="How can I convert Markdown documents to HTML en masse?" Tags="&lt;bash&gt;&lt;markdown&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="12" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="18757" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-20T20:34:12.540" Score="13" ViewCount="1354" Body="&lt;p&gt;The Add view and the Edit view are often incredibly similar that it is unwarranted to write 2 views. As the app evolves you would be making the same changes to both.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;However, there are usually subtle differences. For instance, a field might be read-only once it's been added, and if that field is a DropDownList you no longer need that List in the ViewData.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So, should I create a view data class which contains all the information for both views, where, depending on the operation you're performing, certain properties will be null?&lt;br&gt;&#xA;Should I include the operation in the view data as an enum?&lt;br&gt;&#xA;Should I surround all the subtle differences with &lt;em&gt;&amp;lt;% if( ViewData.Model.Op == Ops.Editing ) { %&gt;&lt;/em&gt; ?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Or is there a better way?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1851" OwnerDisplayName="tgmdbm" LastEditorUserId="727208" LastEditDate="2012-07-13T06:39:05.240" LastActivityDate="2012-07-13T06:39:05.240" Title="Using ASP.NET MVC, how to best avoid writing both the Add View and Edit View?" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;asp.net-mvc&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="6" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="18764" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="18906" CreationDate="2008-08-20T20:38:50.283" Score="74" ViewCount="42956" Body="&lt;p&gt;Since both a &lt;code&gt;Table Scan&lt;/code&gt; and a &lt;code&gt;Clustered Index Scan&lt;/code&gt; essentially scan all records in the table, why is a Clustered Index Scan supposedly better?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;As an example - what's the performance difference between the following when there are many records?:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;declare @temp table(&#xA;    SomeColumn varchar(50)&#xA;)&#xA;&#xA;insert into @temp&#xA;select 'SomeVal'&#xA;&#xA;select * from @temp&#xA;&#xA;-----------------------------&#xA;&#xA;declare @temp table(&#xA;    RowID int not null identity(1,1) primary key,&#xA;    SomeColumn varchar(50)&#xA;)&#xA;&#xA;insert into @temp&#xA;select 'SomeVal'&#xA;&#xA;select * from @temp&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="357" OwnerDisplayName="Terrapin" LastEditorUserId="2124004" LastEditorDisplayName="Terrapin" LastEditDate="2013-12-19T18:37:14.807" LastActivityDate="2016-10-15T18:49:21.750" Title="What's the difference between a Table Scan and a Clustered Index Scan?" Tags="&lt;sql&gt;&lt;sql-server&gt;&lt;indexing&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="24" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
