  <row Id="18787" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="31726" CreationDate="2008-08-20T20:46:23.573" Score="6" ViewCount="5235" Body="&lt;p&gt;When a controller renders a view based on a model you can get the properties from the ViewData collection using the indexer (ie. ViewData[&quot;Property&quot;]).  However, I have a shared user control that I tried to call using the following:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;return View(&quot;Message&quot;, new { DisplayMessage = &quot;This is a test&quot; });&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;and on my Message control I had this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;%= ViewData[&quot;DisplayMessage&quot;] %&amp;gt;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I would think this would render the DisplayMessage correctly, however, null is being returned.  After a heavy dose of tinkering around, I finally created a &quot;MessageData&quot; class in order to strongly type my user control:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;public class MessageControl : ViewUserControl&amp;lt;MessageData&amp;gt;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;and now this call works:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;return View(&quot;Message&quot;, new MessageData() { DisplayMessage = &quot;This is a test&quot; });&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;and can be displayed like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;%= ViewData.Model.DisplayMessage %&amp;gt;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Why wouldn't the DisplayMessage property be added to the ViewData (ie. ViewData[&quot;DisplayMessage&quot;]) collection without strong typing the user control?  Is this by design?  Wouldn't it make sense that ViewData would contain a key for &quot;DisplayMessage&quot;?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="105" OwnerDisplayName="Ryan Eastabrook" LastEditorUserId="100297" LastEditorDisplayName="Haacked" LastEditDate="2019-04-01T20:07:27.827" LastActivityDate="2019-04-01T20:07:27.827" Title="Asp.net MVC User Control ViewData" Tags="&lt;asp.net&gt;&lt;asp.net-mvc&gt;&lt;viewdata&gt;&lt;viewusercontrol&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="18803" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="18839" CreationDate="2008-08-20T20:53:05.040" Score="122" ViewCount="32741" Body="&lt;p&gt;In college I've had numerous design and &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unified_Modeling_Language&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;UML&lt;/a&gt; oriented courses, and I recognize that UML can be used to benefit a software project, especially &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Use_case&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;use-case&lt;/a&gt; mapping, but is it really practical?  I've done a few co-op work terms, and it appears that UML is not used heavily in the industry.  Is it worth the time during a project to create UML diagrams?  Also, I find that class diagrams are generally not useful, because it's just faster to look at the header file for a class.  Specifically which diagrams are the most useful?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Edit:&lt;/strong&gt; My experience is limited to small, under 10 developer projects.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Edit:&lt;/strong&gt; Many good answers, and though not the most verbose, I belive the one selected is the most balanced.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2134" OwnerDisplayName="Chris Fournier" LastEditorUserId="2134" LastEditorDisplayName="Chris Fournier" LastEditDate="2008-08-23T17:55:57.093" LastActivityDate="2019-09-07T04:08:57.767" Title="Is UML practical?" Tags="&lt;uml&gt;&lt;class-design&gt;&lt;diagram&gt;" AnswerCount="31" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="46" ClosedDate="2012-02-27T03:42:11.630" CommunityOwnedDate="2011-06-03T08:39:55.867" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="18836" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="18837" CreationDate="2008-08-20T21:03:54.543" Score="17" ViewCount="3347" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm looking for shell scripts files installed on my system, but &lt;strong&gt;find&lt;/strong&gt; doesn't work:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;$ find /usr -name *.sh&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;But I know there are a ton of scripts out there.  For instance:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;$ ls /usr/local/lib/*.sh&#xA;/usr/local/lib/tclConfig.sh  &#xA;/usr/local/lib/tkConfig.sh&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Why doesn't &lt;strong&gt;find&lt;/strong&gt; work?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1438" OwnerDisplayName="Jon Ericson" LastEditorUserId="116" LastEditorDisplayName="Mark Harrison" LastEditDate="2008-08-25T15:00:54.533" LastActivityDate="2014-09-05T04:09:31.820" Title="Why doesn't **find** find anything?" Tags="&lt;bash&gt;&lt;unix&gt;&lt;shell&gt;&lt;ksh&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
