  <row Id="2619618" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="2640533" CreationDate="2010-04-12T02:48:08.180" Score="18" ViewCount="13866" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm using the &quot;by&quot; function in R to chop up a data frame and apply a function to different parts, like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;pairwise.compare &amp;lt;- function(x) {&#xA;Nright &amp;lt;- ...&#xA;Nwrong &amp;lt;- ...&#xA;Ntied &amp;lt;- ...&#xA;return(c(Nright=Nright, Nwrong=Nwrong, Ntied=Ntied))&#xA;}&#xA;Z.by &amp;lt;- by(rankings, INDICES=list(rankings$Rater, rankings$Class), FUN=pairwise.compare)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The result (Z.by) looks something like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;: 4 &#xA;: 357 &#xA;Nright Nwrong Ntied&#xA;     3      0     0&#xA;------------------------------------------------------------&#xA;: 8 &#xA;: 357 &#xA;NULL&#xA;------------------------------------------------------------&#xA;: 10 &#xA;: 470 &#xA;Nright Nwrong Ntied&#xA;     3      4     1 &#xA;------------------------------------------------------------ &#xA;: 11 &#xA;: 470 &#xA;Nright Nwrong Ntied&#xA;    12      4     1&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What I would like is to have this result converted into a data frame (with the NULL entries not present) so it looks like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;  Rater Class Nright Nwrong Ntied&#xA;1     4   357      3      0     0&#xA;2    10   470      3      4     1&#xA;3    11   470     12      4     1&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How do I do that? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="742" LastEditorUserId="5414452" LastEditDate="2016-01-23T10:50:43.357" LastActivityDate="2020-04-10T14:08:46.597" Title="Convert a &quot;by&quot; object to a data frame in R" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;dataframe&gt;&lt;aggregate&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="10" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="21574" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="21621" CreationDate="2008-08-22T01:32:54.937" Score="102" ViewCount="42005" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm not clear on the differences between the &quot;current&quot; version of Ruby (1.8) and the &quot;new&quot; version (1.9). Is there an &quot;easy&quot; or a &quot;simple&quot; explanation of the differences and why it is so different?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="757" OwnerDisplayName="salt.racer" LastEditorUserId="19750" LastEditorDisplayName="Prakash" LastEditDate="2010-11-09T14:09:42.810" LastActivityDate="2014-04-08T15:13:32.627" Title="What is the difference between Ruby 1.8 and Ruby 1.9" Tags="&lt;ruby&gt;&lt;ruby-1.9&gt;&lt;ruby-1.8&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="4" FavoriteCount="45" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="21583" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="21603" CreationDate="2008-08-22T01:35:33.773" Score="32" ViewCount="8258" Body="&lt;p&gt;This past summer I was developing a basic ASP.NET/SQL Server CRUD app, and unit testing was one of the requirements.  I ran into some trouble when I tried to test against the database.  To my understanding, unit tests should be:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;stateless&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;independent from each other&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;repeatable with the same results i.e. no persisting changes&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;These requirements seem to be at odds with each other when developing for a database.  For example, I can't test Insert() without making sure the rows to be inserted aren't there yet, thus I need to call the Delete() first.  But, what if they aren't already there?  Then I would need to call the Exists() function first.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;My eventual solution involved very large setup functions (yuck!) and an empty test case which would run first and indicate that the setup ran without problems.  This is sacrificing on the independence of the tests while maintaining their statelessness.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Another solution I found is to wrap the function calls in a transaction which can be easily rolled back, like &lt;a href=&quot;http://weblogs.asp.net/rosherove/archive/2004/10/05/238201.aspx&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;Roy Osherove's XtUnit&lt;/a&gt;.  This work, but it involves another library, another dependency, and it seems a little too heavy of a solution for the problem at hand.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So, what has the SO community done when confronted with this situation?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;tgmdbm said:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;You typically use your favourite&#xA;  automated unit testing framework to&#xA;  perform integration tests, which is&#xA;  why some people get confused, but they&#xA;  don't follow the same rules. You are&#xA;  allowed to involve the concrete&#xA;  implementation of many of your classes&#xA;  (because they've been unit tested).&#xA;  You are testing &lt;strong&gt;how your concrete&#xA;  classes interact with each other and&#xA;  with the database&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So if I read this correctly, there is really no way to &lt;em&gt;effectively&lt;/em&gt; unit-test a Data Access Layer.  Or, would a &quot;unit test&quot; of a Data Access Layer involve testing, say, the SQL/commands generated by the classes, independent of actual interaction with the database?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1266" OwnerDisplayName="pbh101" LastEditorUserId="16587" LastEditorDisplayName="Chris Fournier" LastEditDate="2013-06-17T11:44:44.400" LastActivityDate="2014-04-25T15:24:00.510" Title="Unit-Testing Databases" Tags="&lt;database&gt;&lt;unit-testing&gt;&lt;transactions&gt;&lt;xtunit&gt;" AnswerCount="9" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="16" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="21589" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="66513" CreationDate="2008-08-22T01:39:05.920" Score="4" ViewCount="1396" Body="&lt;p&gt;I did some tests a while ago and never figured out how to make this work.  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The ingredients:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;COM+ transactional object (developed in VB6)  &lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;.Net web application (with transaction) in IIS that...&lt;br&gt;&#xA;makes a call to the COM+ component&lt;br&gt;&#xA;updates a row in a SQL database&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Testing:&lt;/strong&gt;  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Run the .Net application and force an exception.  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Result:&lt;/strong&gt;  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The update made from the .Net application rolls back.&lt;br&gt;&#xA;The update made by the COM+ object does not roll back.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If I call the COM+ object from an old ASP page the rollback works.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I know some people may be thinking &quot;what?! COM+ and .Net you must be out of your mind!&quot;, but there are some places in this world where there still are a lot of COM+ components. I was just curious if someone ever faced this and if you figured out how to make this work.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1328" OwnerDisplayName="bubbassauro" LastEditorUserId="2432317" LastEditorDisplayName="bubbassauro" LastEditDate="2013-12-02T13:37:38.003" LastActivityDate="2013-12-02T13:37:38.003" Title="Is it possible to share a transaction between a .Net application and a COM+ object?" Tags="&lt;.net&gt;&lt;database&gt;&lt;transactions&gt;&lt;com+&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="21593" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-22T01:40:06.993" Score="2796" ViewCount="745667" Body="&lt;p&gt;In the C and C++ programming languages, what is the difference between using angle brackets and using quotes in an &lt;code&gt;include&lt;/code&gt; statement, as follows?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;#include &amp;lt;filename&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; &lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;#include &quot;filename&quot;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2399" LastEditorUserId="1307905" LastEditDate="2016-03-25T11:04:00.810" LastActivityDate="2021-09-20T16:13:06.127" Title="What is the difference between #include &lt;filename&gt; and #include &quot;filename&quot;?" Tags="&lt;c++&gt;&lt;c&gt;&lt;include&gt;&lt;header-files&gt;&lt;c-preprocessor&gt;" AnswerCount="31" CommentCount="4" FavoriteCount="684" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="21635" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="21726" CreationDate="2008-08-22T02:13:38.363" Score="3" ViewCount="987" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a product which has been traditionally shipped as an MSI file.  It is deployed through some sort of SMS push to thousands of desktops by our various clients.  The software we use to create these installers is getting long in the tooth and we are looking to replace it.  We have already standardized on InstallAnywhere for most of our products as we support many operating systems.  Unfortunately InstallAnywhere cannot produce MSI files.  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I am wondering if it is required that SMS use MSI files or if it can handle other installer types (.exe).  If not, are there any open source programmes for creating MSI files?  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="361" OwnerDisplayName="stimms" LastActivityDate="2012-08-11T15:54:00.660" Title="Pushing out MSI files" Tags="&lt;deployment&gt;&lt;installation&gt;&lt;windows-installer&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="21640" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="22361" CreationDate="2008-08-22T02:18:28.307" Score="253" ViewCount="176976" Body="&lt;p&gt;Is there a simple way in .NET to quickly get the current protocol, host, and port? For example, if I'm on the following URL:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;http://www.mywebsite.com:80/pages/page1.aspx&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I need to return:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;http://www.mywebsite.com:80&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I know I can use &lt;code&gt;Request.Url.AbsoluteUri&lt;/code&gt; to get the complete URL, and I know I can use &lt;code&gt;Request.Url.Authority&lt;/code&gt; to get the host and port, but I'm not sure of the best way to get the protocol without parsing out the URL string.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Any suggestions?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2076253" OwnerDisplayName="Jeremy Kratz" LastEditorUserId="133" LastEditDate="2018-07-19T08:57:52.063" LastActivityDate="2021-10-22T10:17:34.187" Title=".NET - Get protocol, host, and port" Tags="&lt;.net&gt;&lt;asp.net&gt;&lt;url&gt;&lt;uri&gt;&lt;authority&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="41" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="21647" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="21656" CreationDate="2008-08-22T02:21:07.440" Score="124" ViewCount="328842" Body="&lt;p&gt;The following &lt;strong&gt;C++&lt;/strong&gt; code uses a &lt;strong&gt;ifstream&lt;/strong&gt; object to read integers from a text file (which has one number per line) until it hits &lt;strong&gt;EOF&lt;/strong&gt;. Why does it read the integer on the last line twice? How to fix this?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Code:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;#include &amp;lt;iostream&amp;gt;&#xA;#include &amp;lt;fstream&amp;gt;&#xA;using namespace std;&#xA;&#xA;int main()&#xA;{&#xA;    ifstream iFile(&quot;input.txt&quot;);    // input.txt has integers, one per line&#xA;&#xA;    while (!iFile.eof())&#xA;    {&#xA;        int x;&#xA;        iFile &amp;gt;&amp;gt; x;&#xA;        cerr &amp;lt;&amp;lt; x &amp;lt;&amp;lt; endl;&#xA;    }&#xA;&#xA;    return 0;&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;input.txt&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;10  &#xA;20  &#xA;30&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Output&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;10  &#xA;20  &#xA;30  &#xA;30&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Note&lt;/strong&gt;: I've skipped all error checking code to keep the code snippet small. The above behaviour is seen on Windows (Visual C++), cygwin (gcc) and Linux (gcc).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1630" OwnerDisplayName="Ash" LastEditorUserId="560648" LastEditorDisplayName="Ash" LastEditDate="2011-07-09T22:22:34.747" LastActivityDate="2016-09-07T10:54:42.500" Title="Reading from text file until EOF repeats last line" Tags="&lt;c++&gt;&lt;iostream&gt;&lt;fstream&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="72" ClosedDate="2018-05-13T12:49:27.157" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
