  <row Id="21817" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="22497" CreationDate="2008-08-22T05:16:43.837" Score="153" ViewCount="105449" Body="&lt;p&gt;The topic says the most of it - what is the reason for the fact that static methods can't be declared in an interface?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;public interface ITest {&#xA;    public static String test();&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The code above gives me the following error (in Eclipse, at least): &quot;Illegal modifier for the interface method ITest.test(); only public &amp;amp; abstract are permitted&quot;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2238" OwnerDisplayName="wolfie" LastEditorUserId="21234" LastEditorDisplayName="mark" LastEditDate="2010-12-11T21:19:01.360" LastActivityDate="2019-02-21T11:20:23.583" Title="Why can't I declare static methods in an interface?" Tags="&lt;java&gt;&lt;interface&gt;&lt;methods&gt;&lt;static&gt;" AnswerCount="14" CommentCount="7" FavoriteCount="41" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="21830" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="26398" CreationDate="2008-08-22T05:22:39.610" Score="38" ViewCount="18075" Body="&lt;p&gt;From what information I could find, they both solve the same problems - more esoteric operations like array containment and intersection (&amp;amp;&amp;amp;, @&gt;, &amp;lt;@, etc). However I would be interested in advice about when to use one or the other (or neither possibly).&lt;br&gt;&#xA;The &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/static/textsearch-indexes.html&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;PostgreSQL documentation&lt;/a&gt; has some information about this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;GIN index lookups are about three times faster than GiST&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;GIN indexes take about three times longer to build than GiST&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;GIN indexes are about ten times slower to update than GiST&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;GIN indexes are two-to-three times larger than GiST&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;However I would be particularly interested to know if there is a performance impact when the memory to index size ration starts getting small (ie. the index size becomes much bigger than the available memory)? I've been told on the #postgresql IRC channel that GIN needs to keep all the index in memory, otherwise it won't be effective, because, unlike B-Tree, it doesn't know which part to read in from disk for a particular query? The question would be: is this true (because I've also been told the opposite of this)? Does GiST have the same restrictions? Are there other restrictions I should be aware of while using one of these indexing algorithms?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1265" OwnerDisplayName="Cd-MaN" LastEditorUserId="168868" LastEditorDisplayName="jsight" LastEditDate="2014-03-29T03:31:52.273" LastActivityDate="2017-06-07T04:49:44.357" Title="PostgreSQL: GIN or GiST indexes?" Tags="&lt;postgresql&gt;&lt;indexing&gt;&lt;gwt-gin&gt;&lt;gist-index&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="10" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="21848" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="21932" CreationDate="2008-08-22T05:39:54.843" Score="105" ViewCount="46257" Body="&lt;p&gt;There may be more than one way to ask this question, so here's a desciption of the problem. I was working on master and committed some stuff and then decided I wanted to put that work on hold. I backed up a few commits and then branched from before I started my crap work. Practically this works fine, I just now have a different branch as my main development branch. I'm wondering how I could change things around so I'm working on master again but it doesn't have my junk work and said work is on a different branch.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Some ways this could be asked/solved:&#xA;How do I rename my master branch to something else and then rename something else to master?&#xA;How do I back up master and then cause all commits I've backed up past to be on a different branch?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Thanks for all the (quick) answers! They're all good.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2427" OwnerDisplayName="Daniel" LastEditorUserId="2427" LastEditorDisplayName="Daniel" LastEditDate="2008-08-22T09:36:03.840" LastActivityDate="2012-08-18T18:28:45.933" Title="Switch branch names in git" Tags="&lt;git&gt;&lt;branch&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="43" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="21869" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="21872" CreationDate="2008-08-22T06:04:43.690" Score="3" ViewCount="522" Body="&lt;p&gt;When you to develop web applications from scratch, what are the skills needed, to produce usable and interaction-rich products?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Do you do UI Prototype first? &lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Do you use User Stories?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Some agile methodology or best practice you'd recommend or actively advocate?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In short: &lt;strong&gt;What skills make you transcend from requirements gathering to AmazingWebApp™?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Finally: Any books you'd recommend?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="861" LastEditorUserId="861" LastEditorDisplayName="Camilo DR" LastEditDate="2008-09-18T20:13:58.293" LastActivityDate="2021-04-12T13:31:16.787" Title="What skills do you need for proper UI/Interaction/Functional design in Web Apps?" Tags="&lt;language-agnostic&gt;&lt;project-management&gt;&lt;web-applications&gt;" AnswerCount="9" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ClosedDate="2019-07-09T22:45:46.647" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="21870" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="984076" CreationDate="2008-08-22T06:07:29.487" Score="29" ViewCount="13344" Body="&lt;p&gt;For a .NET component that will be used in both web applications and rich client applications, there seem to be two obvious options for caching: System.Web.Caching or the Ent. Lib. Caching Block.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;What do you use?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Why?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;h2&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/system.web.caching.aspx&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;System.Web.Caching&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is this safe to use outside of web apps?  I've seen mixed information, but I think the answer is maybe-kind-of-not-really.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;http://support.microsoft.com/kb/917411&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;a KB article warning against 1.0 and 1.1 non web app use&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;The 2.0 page has a &lt;em&gt;comment&lt;/em&gt; that indicates it's OK: &lt;a href=&quot;http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/system.web.caching.cache(VS.80).aspx&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/system.web.caching.cache(VS.80).aspx&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.hanselman.com/blog/UsingTheASPNETCacheOutsideOfASPNET.aspx&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;Scott Hanselman is creeped out by the notion&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/system.web.caching.cache.aspx&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;The 3.5 page&lt;/a&gt; includes a warning against such use&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Rob Howard &lt;a href=&quot;http://weblogs.asp.net/cschittko/archive/2004/07/04/172684.aspx&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;encouraged use outside of web apps&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I don't expect to use one of its highlights, &lt;a href=&quot;http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/system.web.caching.sqlcachedependency.aspx&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;SqlCacheDependency&lt;/a&gt;, but the addition of &lt;a href=&quot;http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/system.web.caching.cacheitemupdatecallback.aspx&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;CacheItemUpdateCallback&lt;/a&gt; in .NET 3.5 seems like a Really Good Thing.  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;h2&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/aa480453.aspx&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;Enterprise Library Caching Application Block&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;other blocks are already in use so the dependency already exists&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;cache persistence isn't necessary; regenerating the cache on restart is OK&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Some cache items should always be available, but be refreshed periodically.  For these items, getting a callback &lt;em&gt;after&lt;/em&gt; an item has been removed is not very convenient.  It looks like a client will have to just sleep and poll until the cache item is repopulated.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;h2&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;http://jehiah.cz/projects/memcached-win32/&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;Memcached for Win32&lt;/a&gt; + &lt;a href=&quot;http://sourceforge.net/projects/memcacheddotnet/&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;.NET client&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What are the pros and cons when you don't need a &lt;em&gt;distributed&lt;/em&gt; cache?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="150" OwnerDisplayName="ESV" LastEditorUserId="305" LastEditorDisplayName="Mark Biek" LastEditDate="2008-08-31T00:28:23.770" LastActivityDate="2013-06-14T14:44:02.807" Title="System.Web.Caching vs. Enterprise Library Caching Block" Tags="&lt;caching&gt;&lt;.net-2.0&gt;&lt;memcached&gt;&lt;enterprise-library&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="27" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="21877" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="21887" CreationDate="2008-08-22T06:14:06.600" Score="13" ViewCount="30910" Body="&lt;p&gt;What I want to achieve is this. I want to give the user the ability to upload an image file, store the image in BLOB in SQL Server, and then use this image as a logo in other pages of the site. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I have done this by using  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;   Response.Clear();&#xA;   Response.ContentType = &quot;image/pjpeg&quot;;&#xA;   Response.BinaryWrite(imageConents);&#xA;   Response.End();&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;but to do this, I use a User control in the place where I want to show the image. I want to do it if possible using an asp:Image control, or even a pure old html image control. Is this possible?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="932" OwnerDisplayName="niksteiak" LastEditorUserId="2761106" LastEditDate="2018-09-17T13:44:50.087" LastActivityDate="2018-09-17T13:44:50.087" Title="Dynamically Rendering asp:Image from BLOB entry in ASP.NET" Tags="&lt;asp.net&gt;" AnswerCount="6" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="10" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="21879" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="21962" CreationDate="2008-08-22T06:15:39.717" Score="12" ViewCount="4512" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm trying to reteach myself some long forgotten math skills. This is part of a much larger project to effectively &quot;teach myself software development&quot; from the ground up (the details are &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.appscanadian.ca/archives/cs-101-introduction-to-computer-science/&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;here&lt;/a&gt; if you're interested in helping out).  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;My biggest stumbling block so far has been math - how can I learn about algorithms and asymptotic notation without it??&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What I'm looking for is some sort of &quot;dependency tree&quot; showing what I need to know. Is calculus required before discrete? What do I need to know before calculus (read: components to the general &quot;pre-calculus&quot; topic)? What can I cut out to fast track the project (&quot;what can I go back for later&quot;)?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Thank!&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1588" OwnerDisplayName="AgentConundrum" LastActivityDate="2015-06-18T00:53:15.307" Title="Where can I find a &quot;Math topic dependency tree&quot; to assist my self-guided refresher on the subject?" Tags="&lt;math&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="3" ClosedDate="2011-08-18T13:15:20.580" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="21908" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-22T06:59:56.490" Score="45" ViewCount="75472" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm working on a web application that needs to prints silently -- that is without user involvement.  What's the best way to accomplish this?  It doesn't like it can be done with strictly with Javascript, nor Flash and/or AIR.  The closest I've seen involves a Java applet.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I can understand why it would a Bad Idea for just any website to be able to do this.  This specific instance is for an internal application, and it's perfectly acceptable if the user needs to add the URL to a trusted site list, install an addon, etc.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="181" LastEditorUserId="1839439" LastEditDate="2020-02-15T19:16:45.700" LastActivityDate="2020-02-15T19:16:45.700" Title="&quot;Silent&quot; Printing in a Web Application" Tags="&lt;browser&gt;&lt;web-applications&gt;&lt;printing&gt;" AnswerCount="11" CommentCount="4" FavoriteCount="39" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="2626567" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="2626576" CreationDate="2010-04-13T02:17:18.207" Score="23" ViewCount="21621" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm trying to collapse a data frame by removing all but one row from each group of rows with identical values in a particular column. In other words, the first row from each group.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;For example, I'd like to convert this&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; d = data.frame(x=c(1,1,2,4),y=c(10,11,12,13),z=c(20,19,18,17))&#xA;&amp;gt; d&#xA;  x  y  z&#xA;1 1 10 20&#xA;2 1 11 19&#xA;3 2 12 18&#xA;4 4 13 17&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Into this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;    x  y  z&#xA;1   1 11 19&#xA;2   2 12 18&#xA;3   4 13 17&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I'm using aggregate to do this currently, but the performance is unacceptable with more data:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; d.ordered = d[order(-d$y),]&#xA;&amp;gt; aggregate(d.ordered,by=list(key=d.ordered$x),FUN=function(x){x[1]})&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I've tried split/unsplit with the same function argument as here, but unsplit complains about duplicate row numbers.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is rle a possibility? Is there an R idiom to convert rle's length vector into the indices of the rows that start each run, which I can then use to pluck those rows out of the data frame?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="315062" LastEditorUserId="5414452" LastEditDate="2016-01-23T10:35:32.623" LastActivityDate="2019-03-20T11:47:11.117" Title="Collapsing data frame by selecting one row per group" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;dataframe&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="8" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="21912" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="21921" CreationDate="2008-08-22T07:07:17.643" Score="2" ViewCount="1637" Body="&lt;p&gt;Reading an article called &lt;em&gt;&quot;Increase LINQ Query Performance&quot;&lt;/em&gt; in July's MSDN magazine, the author states that using an &lt;code&gt;Imports&lt;/code&gt; in VB providing a path to schema in the current project will turn IntelliSense on for XElement. In the code provided, he uses statements like &lt;code&gt;xelement.@name&lt;/code&gt; to retreive attributes values and so on.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I did not try this out myself in VB but I would like to use that in C#. This really looks like LINQ to XSD.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there any equivalent in C#? It seems that it is not possible to use a namespace inside C# code, there is no &lt;code&gt;using&lt;/code&gt; equivalent to this &lt;code&gt;Import&lt;/code&gt; statement.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="920" OwnerDisplayName="Philippe" LastEditorUserId="298281" LastEditorDisplayName="John Sheehan" LastEditDate="2012-05-02T18:26:32.620" LastActivityDate="2012-05-02T18:26:32.620" Title="IntelliSense for XElement objects with XML schema" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;linq&gt;&lt;xsd&gt;&lt;linq-to-xml&gt;&lt;vb.net-to-c#&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="21934" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="21964" CreationDate="2008-08-22T07:35:26.703" Score="62" ViewCount="19727" Body="&lt;p&gt;Python uses the reference count method to handle object life time. So an object that has no more use will be immediately destroyed.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;But, in Java, the GC(garbage collector) destroys objects which are no longer used at a specific time.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Why does Java choose this strategy and what is the benefit from this?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is this better than the Python approach?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1556" OwnerDisplayName="popopome" LastEditorUserId="1337136" LastEditorDisplayName="Scott Dorman" LastEditDate="2013-03-10T23:27:26.813" LastActivityDate="2014-02-02T23:15:20.800" Title="Why Java and Python garbage collection methods are different?" Tags="&lt;java&gt;&lt;python&gt;&lt;garbage-collection&gt;" AnswerCount="9" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="27" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="21938" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="21950" CreationDate="2008-08-22T07:41:13.223" Score="67" ViewCount="54184" Body="&lt;p&gt;Whilst analysing some legacy code with FXCop, it occurred to me is it really that bad to catch a general exception error within a try block or should you be looking for a specific exception.  Thoughts on a postcard please.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1731" OwnerDisplayName="Triss" LastActivityDate="2019-02-20T03:18:10.290" Title="Is it really that bad to catch a general exception?" Tags="&lt;exception&gt;" AnswerCount="15" CommentCount="4" FavoriteCount="24" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="2627431" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2010-04-13T06:31:06.677" Score="0" ViewCount="2035" Body="&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; D &amp;lt;- read.csv(&quot;sample1.csv&quot;, header = FALSE, sep = &quot;,&quot;)&#xA;&#xA;&amp;gt; D&#xA;        V1     V2     V3     V4&#xA;1 20100316 109825 352120 239065&#xA;2 20100317 108625 352020 239000&#xA;3 20100318 109125 352324 241065&#xA;&#xA;&amp;gt; D[,1]&#xA;[1] 20100316 20100317 20100318&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In the above example how do I get the data in &lt;code&gt;D[,1]&lt;/code&gt; to be read, and stored as date values: 2010-03-16, 2010-03-17, 2010-03-18? I have lots of data files in this format.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;TIA,&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="313558" LastEditorUserId="168747" LastEditDate="2010-09-14T08:43:05.867" LastActivityDate="2010-09-14T08:43:05.867" Title="Reading numeric Date value from CSV file to data.frame in &quot;R&quot;" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;csv&gt;&lt;formatting&gt;&lt;dataframe&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="21956" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="22256" CreationDate="2008-08-22T09:00:26.250" Score="4" ViewCount="4750" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have two arrays of &lt;code&gt;System.Data.DataRow&lt;/code&gt; objects which I want to compare. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The rows have two columns A and B. Column A is a key and I want to find out which rows have had their B column changed and which rows have been added or deleted. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How do I do this in PowerShell?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="966" OwnerDisplayName="Manga Lee" LastEditorUserId="4311889" LastEditDate="2016-07-05T05:21:25.147" LastActivityDate="2018-01-27T01:59:29.213" Title="How do I compare two arrays of DataRow objects in PowerShell?" Tags="&lt;arrays&gt;&lt;powershell&gt;&lt;comparison&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
