  <row Id="22269" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-22T12:57:41.407" Score="2" ViewCount="1806" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm trying to build a C# console application to automate grabbing certain files from our website, mostly to save myself clicks and - frankly - just to have done it.  But I've hit a snag that for which I've been unable to find a working solution.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The website I'm trying to which I'm trying to connect uses ASP.Net forms authorization, and I cannot figure out how to authenticate myself with it.  This application is a complete hack so I can hard code my username and password or any other needed auth info, and the solution itself doesn't need to be something that is viable enough to release to general users.  In other words, if the only possible solution is a hack, I'm fine with that.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Basically, I'm trying to use HttpWebRequest to pull the site that has the list of files, iterating through that list and then downloading what I need.  So the actual work on the site is fairly trivial once I can get the website to consider me authorized.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="111" OwnerDisplayName="saalon" LastEditorUserId="1477076" LastEditDate="2012-08-11T16:16:23.403" LastActivityDate="2013-09-01T16:11:48.570" Title="Authenticate on an ASP.Net Forms Authorization website from a console app" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;asp.net&gt;&lt;authentication&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="22278" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-22T13:04:29.487" Score="5" ViewCount="1558" Body="&lt;p&gt;Most of my experience is on the MSFT stack, but I am now working on a side project, helping someone with a personal site with cheap hosting that is built on the LAMP stack.  My options for installing extras are limited, so I'm wondering about how to write my data access code without embedding raw queries in the .php files.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I like to keep things simple, even with .NET.  I generally write stored procedures for everything, and I have a helper class that wraps all calls to execute procedures and return data sets.  I'm not looking for a full-blown ORM, but it might be the way to go and others who view this question might be looking for that.  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Remember that I'm on a $7/month GoDaddy account, so I'm limited to what's already installed in their basic package.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Edit:&lt;/strong&gt; Thanks rix0rr, Alan, Anders, dragon, I will check all of those out. I edited the question to be more open to ORM solutions, since they are so popular.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1219" OwnerDisplayName="Eric Z Beard" LastEditorUserId="367456" LastEditorDisplayName="Eric Z Beard" LastEditDate="2011-11-13T14:09:26.860" LastActivityDate="2011-11-13T14:09:26.860" Title="What's a good way to encapsulate data access with PHP/MySQL?" Tags="&lt;php&gt;&lt;mysql&gt;&lt;database&gt;&lt;lamp&gt;" AnswerCount="6" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="5" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="22306" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-22T13:15:52.157" Score="0" ViewCount="2732" Body="&lt;p&gt;Just recently started having issues with an SQL Server Agent Job that contains an SSIS package to extract production data and summarize it into a separate reporting database. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I &lt;em&gt;think&lt;/em&gt; that some of the Alerts/Notifications settings I tried playing with caused the problem as the job had been running to completion unattended for the previous two weeks.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So... Where's a good place to start reading up on SQL Agent Alerts and Notifications? &lt;br /&gt;I want to enable some sort of alert/notification so that I'm always informed: &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;That the job completes successfully (as a check to ensure that it's always executed), or &lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;That the job ran into some sort of error, which should include enough info (such as error number) that I can diagnose the cause of the error&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;As always, any help will be greatly appreciated!&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2292" OwnerDisplayName="CrazyOnYou" LastEditorUserId="155077" LastEditorDisplayName="user756519" LastEditDate="2015-08-24T08:58:46.180" LastActivityDate="2015-08-24T08:58:46.180" Title="Where do I begin to learn about SQL Server alerts or notifications?" Tags="&lt;sql-server&gt;&lt;ssis&gt;&lt;notifications&gt;&lt;alert&gt;&lt;etl&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="22309" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="28847" CreationDate="2008-08-22T13:17:48.317" Score="2" ViewCount="6965" Body="&lt;p&gt;Does anyone know of a disassembler for &lt;em&gt;ARMV4i&lt;/em&gt; executables and DLLs?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I've got a plug-in DLL I'm writing with a very rare &lt;code&gt;data abort&lt;/code&gt; (&amp;lt;5% of the time) that I have narrowed down to a specific function (via dumpbin and the address output by the &lt;code&gt;data abort&lt;/code&gt;).  However, it is a fairly large function and I would like to narrow it down a little.  I know it's happening in a &lt;code&gt;memset()&lt;/code&gt; call, but that particular function has about 35 of them, so I was hoping that by looking at the disassembly I could figure out where about the problem actually is.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="194" OwnerDisplayName="Adam Haile" LastEditorUserId="1880339" LastEditDate="2013-03-01T04:58:51.023" LastActivityDate="2013-03-01T04:58:51.023" Title="ARMV4i (Windows Mobile 6) Native Code disassembler" Tags="&lt;windows-mobile&gt;&lt;arm&gt;&lt;disassembly&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="22318" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="429683" CreationDate="2008-08-22T13:21:18.807" Score="0" ViewCount="575" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm developing a website which will be used in the corporate intranet which uses JCIFS and NTLM to automatically authenticate the users without asking them to log on.  Everything seems to be working in IE 6, but some users are being prompted for their passwords in IE 7.  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;We've had some success by getting the users to change their Logon option to &quot;Automatic logon using current username and password&quot; (Tools &gt; Internet Options &gt; Securty Tab &gt; Select Local Intranet &gt; Custom Level &gt; All the way at the bottom User Authentication &gt; Logon), but we are still having a few users who are getting username/password prompts.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Also, we've had some users report they can just click cancel when the prompt comes up and the page will come up correctly.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If anyone has any other suggestions, I'd greatly appreciate them.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="466" OwnerDisplayName="qulup" LastEditorUserId="466" LastEditorDisplayName="qulup" LastEditDate="2008-08-22T19:18:29.540" LastActivityDate="2009-01-09T20:49:58.537" Title="IE Securty Zone Issues" Tags="&lt;internet-explorer&gt;&lt;ntlm&gt;&lt;intranet&gt;&lt;jcifs&gt;&lt;security-zone&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="22319" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="22693" CreationDate="2008-08-22T13:22:31.050" Score="3" ViewCount="912" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am writing a program that needs to send out an email every hour on the hour, but at a time local to the user.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Say I have 2 users in different time zones. John is in New York and Fred is in Los Angeles. The server is in Chicago. If I want to send an email at 6 PM local to each user, I'd have to send the email to John at 7 PM Server time and Fred at 4 PM Server time.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What's a good approach to this in .NET / Sql Server? I have found an xml file with all of the time zone information, so I am considering writing a script to import it into the database, then querying off of it.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Edit:&lt;/strong&gt; I used “t4znet.dll” and did all comparisons on the .NET side.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="26" OwnerDisplayName="modesty" LastEditorUserId="182371" LastEditorDisplayName="modesty" LastEditDate="2016-08-03T16:25:55.810" LastActivityDate="2016-08-03T16:25:55.810" Title="How to send out email at a user's local time in .NET / Sql Server?" Tags="&lt;sql&gt;&lt;.net&gt;&lt;sql-server&gt;&lt;timezone&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="22321" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="160441" CreationDate="2008-08-22T13:22:38.867" Score="4" ViewCount="3909" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a client/server application that communicates with .Net remoting. I need my clients to be able to find the server(s) on the network without requiring client-side configuration. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;As far as I know, there is no support for discovery in remoting. I am prepared to implement a UDP solution where the clients will be able to find the servers via broadcast messages. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I want to hear the collective SO wisdom before I go ahead. Is this the best way? Any other suggestions?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="227" OwnerDisplayName="Ishmaeel" LastEditorUserId="227" LastEditorDisplayName="Ishmaeel" LastEditDate="2008-08-22T13:34:20.303" LastActivityDate="2009-03-25T06:43:42.930" Title="Remoting server auto-discovery. Broadcast or not?" Tags="&lt;.net&gt;&lt;networking&gt;&lt;remoting&gt;&lt;udp&gt;&lt;broadcast&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="22322" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="479664" CreationDate="2008-08-22T13:23:15.773" Score="17" ViewCount="4244" Body="&lt;p&gt;I've got a problem similar to,but subtly different from, that described &lt;a href=&quot;https://stackoverflow.com/questions/22012/loading-assemblies-and-its-dependencies&quot;&gt;here&lt;/a&gt; (Loading assemblies and their dependencies).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I have a C++ DLL for 3D rendering that is what we sell to customers. For .NET users we will have a CLR wrapper around it. The C++ DLL can be built in both 32 and 64bit versions, but I think this means we need to have two CLR wrappers since the CLR binds to a specific DLL? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Say now our customer has a .NET app that can be either 32 or 64bit, and that it being a pure .NET app it leaves the CLR to work it out from a single set of assemblies. The question is how can the app code dynamically choose between our 32 and 64bit CLR/DLL combinations at run-time?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Even more specifically, is the suggested answer to the aforementioned question applicable here too (i.e. create a ResolveEvent handler)?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2102" OwnerDisplayName="Greg Whitfield" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditorDisplayName="Greg Whitfield" LastEditDate="2017-05-23T10:27:19.503" LastActivityDate="2015-11-20T11:39:42.673" Title="How to late bind 32bit/64 bit libs at runtime" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;.net&gt;&lt;64-bit&gt;&lt;clr&gt;&lt;x86-64&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="6" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="22326" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="27425" CreationDate="2008-08-22T13:24:24.697" Score="11" ViewCount="6586" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am trying to &lt;strong&gt;replace the current selection in Word (2003/2007)&lt;/strong&gt; by some &lt;strong&gt;RTF string&lt;/strong&gt; stored in a variable.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Here is the current code:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;Clipboard.SetText(strRTFString, TextDataFormat.Rtf)&#xA;oWord.ActiveDocument.ActiveWindow.Selection.PasteAndFormat(0)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there any way to do the same thing without going through the clipboard. Or is there any way to push the clipboard data to a safe place and restore it after?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1508" OwnerDisplayName="Vincent" LastEditorUserId="100297" LastEditorDisplayName="Joel Spolsky" LastEditDate="2020-07-05T12:04:44.867" LastActivityDate="2020-07-05T12:04:44.867" Title="Word Automation: Write RTF text without going through clipboard" Tags="&lt;vba&gt;&lt;automation&gt;&lt;ms-word&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="5" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
