  <row Id="24622" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="24631" CreationDate="2008-08-23T21:39:54.793" Score="19" ViewCount="24170" Body="&lt;p&gt;I can set the PHP include path in the &lt;code&gt;php.ini&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;include_path = /path/to/site/includes/&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;But then other websites are affected so that is no good.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I can set the PHP include in the start of every file:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;$path = '/path/to/site/includes/';&#xA;set_include_path(get_include_path() . PATH_SEPARATOR . $path);&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;But that seems like bad practice and clutters things up. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So I can make an include of that and then include it into every file:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;include 'includes/config.php';&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;or&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;include '../includes/config.php';&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This is what I'm doing right now, but the include path of &lt;code&gt;config.php&lt;/code&gt; will change depending on what is including it. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there a better way? Does it matter?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2118" OwnerDisplayName="Annan" LastEditorUserId="214668" LastEditDate="2011-11-28T02:56:16.477" LastActivityDate="2018-04-03T15:46:56.710" Title="Setting PHP Include Path on a per site basis?" Tags="&lt;php&gt;&lt;include&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="2759394" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="2759436" CreationDate="2010-05-03T16:05:57.153" Score="12" ViewCount="22298" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a big dataframe with columns such as:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;ID, time, OS, IP&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Each row of that dataframe corresponds to one entry. Within that dataframe for some &lt;b&gt;IDs&lt;/b&gt; several entries (rows) exist. I would like to get rid of those multiple rows (obviously the other attributes will differ for the same ID). Or put different: I only want one single entry (row) for each ID.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;When I use &lt;code&gt;unique&lt;/code&gt; on the ID column, I only receive the levels (or each unique ID), but I want to keep the other attributes as well.&#xA;I have tried to use &lt;code&gt;apply(x,2,unique(data$ID))&lt;/code&gt;, but this does not work either.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="262085" LastEditorUserId="759866" LastEditDate="2014-02-02T14:08:10.303" LastActivityDate="2014-02-02T14:08:10.303" Title="Get rid of rows with duplicate attributes in R" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;duplicates&gt;&lt;dataframe&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="7" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="24623" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-23T21:40:10.797" Score="1" ViewCount="2045" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have seen two ways of implementing DAO-DVO design.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;1) DVO are objects and DAOs are instantiated using factories i.e DAOs are also objects&#xA;2) DVOs are again objects but in this case, DAOs are classes which contain only static methods which accept DVOs to perform tasks.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I was wondering which way is better and more scalable.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerDisplayName="Vibhu" LastEditorUserId="472792" LastEditDate="2013-01-16T20:42:14.597" LastActivityDate="2013-01-16T20:42:14.597" Title="Java EE - DAO DVO" Tags="&lt;java&gt;&lt;jakarta-ee&gt;&lt;dao&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="24626" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="24748" CreationDate="2008-08-23T21:41:49.670" Score="178" ViewCount="154270" Body="&lt;p&gt;Can you tell me what is the difference between &lt;strong&gt;abstraction&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;information hiding&lt;/strong&gt; in software development?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I am confused. Abstraction hides detail implementation and&#xA;information hiding abstracts whole details of something.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Update:&lt;/strong&gt; I found a good answer for these three concepts. &lt;a href=&quot;https://stackoverflow.com/a/8694874/240733&quot;&gt;See the separate answer below&lt;/a&gt; for several citations taken from &lt;a href=&quot;http://web.archive.org/web/20080906224409/http://www.itmweb.com/essay550.htm&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;there&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1556" OwnerDisplayName="popopome" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditorDisplayName="popopome" LastEditDate="2017-05-23T11:47:30.917" LastActivityDate="2020-05-20T15:18:53.500" Title="Abstraction VS Information Hiding VS Encapsulation" Tags="&lt;encapsulation&gt;&lt;abstraction&gt;&lt;glossary&gt;&lt;ooad&gt;&lt;information-hiding&gt;" AnswerCount="22" CommentCount="3" FavoriteCount="177" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="24642" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="24701" CreationDate="2008-08-23T21:56:15.707" Score="19" ViewCount="7201" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm a moderately skilled programmer using JavaScript but I am no guru. I know you can do some pretty powerful things with it, I just haven't seen much other than fairly basic DOM manipulation. I'm wondering if people could provide some examples of traditional design pattern concepts such as Factory Method, Singleton, etc using JavaScript. In what cases would these patterns be used for on the web?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2628" OwnerDisplayName="Kamikaze Mercenary" LastEditorUserId="20984" LastEditorDisplayName="Kamikaze Mercenary" LastEditDate="2012-05-04T08:37:44.737" LastActivityDate="2017-04-29T06:39:11.537" Title="What Are Some Examples of Design Pattern Implementations Using JavaScript?" Tags="&lt;javascript&gt;&lt;design-patterns&gt;&lt;singleton&gt;&lt;strategy-pattern&gt;&lt;factory-method&gt;" AnswerCount="10" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="15" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
