  <row Id="26079" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="26097" CreationDate="2008-08-25T14:11:10.160" Score="40" ViewCount="26341" Body="&lt;p&gt;Let's say I'm writing a PHP (&gt;= 5.0) class that's meant to be a singleton.  All of the docs I've read say to make the class constructor private so the class can't be directly instantiated.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So if I have something like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;class SillyDB&#xA;{&#xA;  private function __construct()&#xA;  {&#xA;&#xA;  }&#xA;&#xA;  public static function getConnection()&#xA;  {&#xA;&#xA;  }&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Are there any cases where __construct() is called other than if I'm doing a &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;new SillyDB() &#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;call inside the class itself?  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;And why am I allowed to instantiate SillyDB from inside itself at all?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="305" OwnerDisplayName="Mark Biek" LastEditorUserId="305" LastEditorDisplayName="Mark Biek" LastEditDate="2008-08-25T14:19:27.220" LastActivityDate="2018-09-26T05:36:19.867" Title="In a PHP5 class, when does a private constructor get called?" Tags="&lt;php&gt;&lt;oop&gt;&lt;constructor&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="8" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
