  <row Id="26173" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-25T14:57:02.450" Score="15" ViewCount="36337" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am looking for some JavaScript based component to be used as a course scheduler which would be a cross between Google Calendar and the login time. I do not know if the right term for this is &lt;i&gt;Course Scheduler&lt;/i&gt; but I shall describe this in more detail here.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Course Scheduler&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;The widget would be used to enter date and times of a course, as an example if I run a programming course 3 days a week on Mon, Tue and Wed every 7:00 am to 9:00am, 2 hours every day from 1st September to 30th November. I could answer various questions and the course data would be displayed in the calendar. It would also allow for non pattern based timings where each week is different from the other week etc. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Question&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;So would I end up creating something from scratch? Would it be sensible to use Google Calendar API for this? I did a Google search for some widgets, but I believe I need better keywords, as I could not find anything close to what I am looking for. Any tips? Commercial libraries would also work for me. Thanks.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="370899" OwnerDisplayName="Ravi Chhabra" LastEditorUserId="542251" LastEditorDisplayName="Greg Hurlman" LastEditDate="2021-02-23T14:31:00.250" LastActivityDate="2021-02-23T14:31:00.250" Title="Is there a Scheduler/Calendar JS Widget library?" Tags="&lt;javascript&gt;&lt;calendar&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="4" FavoriteCount="9" ClosedDate="2014-05-19T05:14:08.510" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="2851015" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="2851213" CreationDate="2010-05-17T16:52:02.603" Score="382" ViewCount="708321" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a data frame. Let's call him &lt;code&gt;bob&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; head(bob)&#xA;                 phenotype                         exclusion&#xA;GSM399350 3- 4- 8- 25- 44+ 11b- 11c- 19- NK1.1- Gr1- TER119-&#xA;GSM399351 3- 4- 8- 25- 44+ 11b- 11c- 19- NK1.1- Gr1- TER119-&#xA;GSM399352 3- 4- 8- 25- 44+ 11b- 11c- 19- NK1.1- Gr1- TER119-&#xA;GSM399353 3- 4- 8- 25+ 44+ 11b- 11c- 19- NK1.1- Gr1- TER119-&#xA;GSM399354 3- 4- 8- 25+ 44+ 11b- 11c- 19- NK1.1- Gr1- TER119-&#xA;GSM399355 3- 4- 8- 25+ 44+ 11b- 11c- 19- NK1.1- Gr1- TER119-&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I'd like to concatenate the rows of this data frame (this will be another question). But look:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; class(bob$phenotype)&#xA;[1] &quot;factor&quot;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;Bob&lt;/code&gt;'s columns are factors. So, for example:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; as.character(head(bob))&#xA;[1] &quot;c(3, 3, 3, 6, 6, 6)&quot;       &quot;c(3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3)&quot;      &#xA;[3] &quot;c(29, 29, 29, 30, 30, 30)&quot;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I don't begin to understand this, but I guess these are indices into the levels of the factors of the columns (of the court of king caractacus) of &lt;code&gt;bob&lt;/code&gt;? Not what I need.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Strangely I can go through the columns of &lt;code&gt;bob&lt;/code&gt; by hand, and do&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;bob$phenotype &amp;lt;- as.character(bob$phenotype)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;which works fine. And, after some typing, I can get a data.frame whose columns are characters rather than factors. So my question is: how can I do this automatically? How do I convert a data.frame with factor columns into a data.frame with character columns without having to manually go through each column? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Bonus question: why does the manual approach work?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="270572" LastEditorUserId="967840" LastEditDate="2013-01-10T22:26:57.347" LastActivityDate="2020-08-20T10:13:49.193" Title="Convert data.frame columns from factors to characters" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;dataframe&gt;" AnswerCount="18" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="156" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="2851327" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="49017065" CreationDate="2010-05-17T17:38:24.907" Score="433" ViewCount="276388" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have code that at one place ends up with a list of data frames which I really want to convert to a single big data frame. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I got some pointers from an &lt;a href=&quot;https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1652522/rbind-dataframes-in-a-list-of-lists&quot;&gt;earlier question&lt;/a&gt; which was trying to do something similar but more complex. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Here's an example of what I am starting with (this is grossly simplified for illustration):&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;listOfDataFrames &amp;lt;- vector(mode = &quot;list&quot;, length = 100)&#xA;&#xA;for (i in 1:100) {&#xA;    listOfDataFrames[[i]] &amp;lt;- data.frame(a=sample(letters, 500, rep=T),&#xA;                             b=rnorm(500), c=rnorm(500))&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I am currently using this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;  df &amp;lt;- do.call(&quot;rbind&quot;, listOfDataFrames)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="37751" LastEditorUserId="1851712" LastEditDate="2021-02-24T16:53:48.180" LastActivityDate="2021-11-18T21:53:11.253" Title="Combine a list of data frames into one data frame by row" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;list&gt;&lt;dataframe&gt;&lt;r-faq&gt;" AnswerCount="9" CommentCount="4" FavoriteCount="177" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="26176" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="26270" CreationDate="2008-08-25T14:59:49.853" Score="25" ViewCount="16856" Body="&lt;p&gt;Does your work environment use Harvest SCM? I've used this now at two different locations and find it appalling. In one situation I wrote a conversion script so I could use CVS locally and then daily import changes to the Harvest system while I was sleeping. The corp was fanatic about using Harvest, despite 80% of the programmers crying for something different. It was needlessly complicated, slow and heavy. It is now a job requirement for me that Harvest is not in use where I work.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Has anyone else used Harvest before? What's your experience? As bad as mine? Did you employ other, different workarounds? Why is this product still purchased today?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2836" OwnerDisplayName="Mike Caron" LastEditorUserId="182690" LastEditorDisplayName="Mark Biek" LastEditDate="2010-03-16T12:46:04.590" LastActivityDate="2012-11-22T14:04:18.570" Title="Why is Harvest being purchased at all?" Tags="&lt;version-control&gt;&lt;harvest&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="6" ClosedDate="2012-11-23T07:29:03.113" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="26195" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="26708" CreationDate="2008-08-25T15:10:27.380" Score="6" ViewCount="2453" Body="&lt;p&gt;I've always wanted to be able to get a reasonably elegant way of getting vimdiff to work with a CVS controlled file. I've found numerous (somewhat hacky) scripts around the internet (best example &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.vim.org/tips/tip.php?tip_id=390&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;) that basically check out the file you are editing from CVS to a temp file, and vimdiff the two. None of these take into account branches, and always assume you're working from MAIN, which for me is completely useless. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So, my question is this: has anyone out there found a decent solution for this that does more than this script?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Or failing that, does anyone have any ideas of how they would implement this, or suggestions for what features you would consider vital for something that does this? My intention is that, if no one can suggest an already built solution to either use or build from, we start building one from here. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="24" OwnerDisplayName="braklet" LastActivityDate="2015-08-20T22:17:52.987" Title="vimdiff and CVS integration" Tags="&lt;vim&gt;&lt;cvs&gt;&lt;vimdiff&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
