  <row Id="2906855" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="2906999" CreationDate="2010-05-25T17:20:04.033" Score="9" ViewCount="2444" Body="&lt;p&gt;When you choose a log scale, ggplot2 formats the breaks like 10^x.  I'd like it to not do that.  For example, the code below should display a graph with ticks at 1, 2, 5 etc, not 10^0, 10^0.3, 10^0.69 etc.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;library(ggplot2)&#xA;dfr &amp;lt;- data.frame(x = 1:100, y = rlnorm(100))&#xA;breaks &amp;lt;- as.vector(c(1, 2, 5) %o% 10^(-1:1))&#xA;p1 &amp;lt;- ggplot(dfr, aes(x, y)) + geom_point() + scale_y_log10(breaks = breaks)&#xA;print(p1)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I guess that adding a &lt;code&gt;formatter&lt;/code&gt; argument to &lt;code&gt;scale_y_log10&lt;/code&gt; would do the trick, but I'm not sure what to put in the argument, or where the options might be documented.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="134830" LastActivityDate="2012-07-23T16:10:43.510" Title="How to override ggplot2's axis formatting?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;ggplot2&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="4" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
