  <row Id="2913145" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="4770287" CreationDate="2010-05-26T13:03:34.773" Score="0" ViewCount="232" Body="&lt;p&gt;How can I easily create a fractional y-value when using ggplot?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;t &amp;lt;- as.factor(test=sample(seq(0,100,10),1000,rep=T))&#xA;d &amp;lt;- as.factor(sample(c(0,1),1000,rep=T)&#xA;p &amp;lt;- data.frame(t,d)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;My best shot was:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;ggplot(p,aes(x=t,y=prop.table(table(t,d),1)[,1])) + geom_point()&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;However this doesnt work and I guess there is an easier way around this...&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="338745" LastEditorUserId="172261" LastEditDate="2010-05-26T14:58:42.237" LastActivityDate="2011-01-22T20:13:47.413" Title="Fractional y-var in ggplot" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;graph&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="27206" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="27344" CreationDate="2008-08-26T01:29:27.423" Score="2" ViewCount="600" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm comparing between two techniques to create partitioned tables in SQL 2005.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Use partitioned views with a standard version of SQL 2005 (described &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.sqlservercentral.com/articles/SQL+Server+2005/61676/&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Use the built in partition in the Enterprise edition of SQL 2005 (described &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.sqlservercentral.com/articles/SQL+Server+2005/61765/&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Given that the enterprise edition is much more expensive, I would like to know what are the main benefits of the newer enterprise built-in implementation. Is it just an time saver for the implementation itself. Or will I gain real performance on large DBs?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I know i can adjust the constraints in the first option to keep a sliding window into the partitions. Can I do it with the built in version?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1363" OwnerDisplayName="harlev" LastEditorUserId="21234" LastEditorDisplayName="Kevin Fairchild" LastEditDate="2011-03-09T11:22:06.027" LastActivityDate="2011-04-07T17:33:10.663" Title="What are the benefits of using partitions with the Enterprise edition of SQL 2005" Tags="&lt;sql&gt;&lt;sql-server-2005&gt;&lt;partitioning&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="27219" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="27259" CreationDate="2008-08-26T01:46:27.273" Score="8" ViewCount="13406" Body="&lt;p&gt;Given a select with multiple option's in jQuery. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;$select = $(&quot;&amp;lt;select&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/select&amp;gt;&quot;);&#xA;$select.append(&quot;&amp;lt;option&amp;gt;Jason&amp;lt;/option&amp;gt;&quot;) //Key = 1&#xA;       .append(&quot;&amp;lt;option&amp;gt;John&amp;lt;/option&amp;gt;&quot;) //Key = 32&#xA;       .append(&quot;&amp;lt;option&amp;gt;Paul&amp;lt;/option&amp;gt;&quot;) //Key = 423&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How should the key be stored and retrieved?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The ID may be an OK place but would not be guaranteed unique if I had multiple select's sharing values (and other scenarios).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Thanks&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;and in the spirit of TMTOWTDI.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;$option = $(&quot;&amp;lt;option&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/option&amp;gt;&quot;);&#xA;$select = $(&quot;&amp;lt;select&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/select&amp;gt;&quot;);&#xA;$select.addOption = function(value,text){&#xA;    $(this).append($(&quot;&amp;lt;option/&amp;gt;&quot;).val(value).text(text));&#xA;};&#xA;&#xA;$select.append($option.val(1).text(&quot;Jason&quot;).clone())&#xA;       .append(&quot;&amp;lt;option value=32&amp;gt;John&amp;lt;/option&amp;gt;&quot;)&#xA;       .append($(&quot;&amp;lt;option/&amp;gt;&quot;).val(423).text(&quot;Paul&quot;))&#xA;       .addOption(&quot;321&quot;,&quot;Lenny&quot;);&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1293" OwnerDisplayName="Jason" LastEditorUserId="7079025" LastEditorDisplayName="Jason" LastEditDate="2019-03-22T15:07:38.433" LastActivityDate="2019-03-22T15:07:38.433" Title="Keeping key value pairs together in HTML &lt;select/&gt; with jQuery?" Tags="&lt;javascript&gt;&lt;jquery&gt;&lt;html&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="2914792" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="2917205" CreationDate="2010-05-26T16:15:25.327" Score="10" ViewCount="12311" Body="&lt;p&gt;I know I can use ls() and rm() to see and remove objects that exist in my environment.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;However, when dealing with &quot;old&quot; .RData file, one needs to sometimes pick an environment a part to find what to keep and what to leave out.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What I would like to do, is to have a GUI like interface to allow me to see the objects, sort them (for example, by there size), and remove the ones I don't need (for example, by a check-box interface).  Since I imagine such a system is not currently implemented in R, what ways do exist?  What do you use for cleaning old .RData files?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Thanks,&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Tal&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="256662" LastActivityDate="2019-03-20T18:05:34.650" Title="What ways are there for cleaning an R environment from objects?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;" AnswerCount="8" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="9" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
