  <row Id="27850" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="28217" CreationDate="2008-08-26T12:11:05.010" Score="5" ViewCount="256" Body="&lt;p&gt;So far I have encountered adjacency list, nested sets and nested intervals as models for storing tree structures in a database. I know these well enough and have migrated trees from one to another.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What are other popular models? What are their characteristics? What are good resources (books, web, etc) on this topic?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I'm not only looking for db storage but would like to expand my knowledge on trees in general. For example, I understand that nested sets/intervals are especially favorable for relational database storage and have asked myself, are they actually a &lt;i&gt;bad&lt;/i&gt; choice in other contexts?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2077" OwnerDisplayName="Hanno" LastEditorUserId="2077" LastEditorDisplayName="Hanno" LastEditDate="2008-09-12T12:39:11.227" LastActivityDate="2008-09-27T19:54:54.780" Title="What are models for storing tree structures and what are their characteristics?" Tags="&lt;data-structures&gt;&lt;modeling&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="2953641" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3023265" CreationDate="2010-06-01T21:54:05.773" Score="5" ViewCount="2132" Body="&lt;p&gt;Anyone knows how to take advantage of ggplot or lattice in doing survival analysis? It would be nice to do a trellis or facet-like survival graphs.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So in the end I played around and sort of found a solution for a Kaplan-Meier plot. I apologize for the messy code in taking the list elements into a dataframe, but I couldnt figure out another way. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Note: It only works with two levels of strata. If anyone know how I can use &lt;code&gt;x&amp;lt;-length(stratum)&lt;/code&gt; to do this please let me know (in Stata I could append to a macro-unsure how this works in R).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;ggkm&amp;lt;-function(time,event,stratum) {&#xA;&#xA;    m2s&amp;lt;-Surv(time,as.numeric(event))&#xA;&#xA;    fit &amp;lt;- survfit(m2s ~ stratum)&#xA;&#xA;    f$time &amp;lt;- fit$time&#xA;&#xA;    f$surv &amp;lt;- fit$surv&#xA;&#xA;    f$strata &amp;lt;- c(rep(names(fit$strata[1]),fit$strata[1]),&#xA;            rep(names(fit$strata[2]),fit$strata[2])) &#xA;&#xA;    f$upper &amp;lt;- fit$upper&#xA;&#xA;    f$lower &amp;lt;- fit$lower&#xA;&#xA;    r &amp;lt;- ggplot (f, aes(x=time, y=surv, fill=strata, group=strata))&#xA;        +geom_line()+geom_ribbon(aes(ymin=lower,ymax=upper),alpha=0.3)&#xA;&#xA;    return(r)&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="338745" LastEditorDisplayName="user1685185" LastEditDate="2014-01-16T13:01:53.430" LastActivityDate="2014-01-16T13:01:53.430" Title="Utilise Surv object in ggplot or lattice" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;graph&gt;&lt;analysis&gt;&lt;ggplot2&gt;&lt;lattice&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="4" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="27853" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="245175" CreationDate="2008-08-26T12:12:23.603" Score="0" ViewCount="2065" Body="&lt;p&gt;Whenever I show a ModalPopupExtender on my Sharepoint site, the popup shown creates both horizontal and vertical scrollbars.  If you scroll all the way to the end of the page, the scrollbar refreshes, and there is more page to scroll through.  Basically, I think the popup is setting its bounds beyond the end of the page.  Has anyone run into this?  Searching Google, it seems this may be a known problem, but I haven't found a good solution that doesn't include recompiling AJAX, which my boss will not allow.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3018" OwnerDisplayName="Dana" LastEditorUserId="3018" LastEditorDisplayName="Dana" LastEditDate="2008-08-26T13:09:27.443" LastActivityDate="2008-10-28T22:54:00.910" Title="ModalPopupExtender adding scrollbars in SharePoint" Tags="&lt;sharepoint&gt;&lt;modalpopupextender&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
