  <row Id="27889" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="28346" CreationDate="2008-08-26T12:26:10.620" Score="0" ViewCount="371" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am trying to get SpecUnit to run in a continuous integration build using Nant. At the moment the files are in the correct place but no output is generated from SpecUnit.Report.exe. Here is the relevant task from the nant build script:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;echo message=&quot;**** Starting SpecUnit report generation ****&quot; /&amp;gt;&#xA;&amp;lt;copy file=&quot;${specunit.exe}&quot; tofile=&quot;${output.dir}SpecUnit.Report.exe&quot; /&amp;gt;&#xA;&amp;lt;exec program=&quot;${output.dir}SpecUnit.Report.exe&quot; failonerror=&quot;false&quot;&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;arg value=&quot;${acceptance.tests.assembly}&quot; /&amp;gt;&#xA;&amp;lt;/exec&amp;gt;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Please note:  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;${specunit.exe}&lt;/code&gt; is the full path to where “SpecUnit.Report.exe” is located.  &lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;${output.dir}&lt;/code&gt; is the teamcity output directory for the current build agent.  &lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;${acceptance.tests.assembly}&lt;/code&gt; is &quot;AcceptanceTests.dll&quot;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Anyone tried this before?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2993" OwnerDisplayName="Sean Chambers" LastEditorUserId="2432317" LastEditDate="2013-12-02T12:50:12.490" LastActivityDate="2013-12-02T12:50:12.490" Title="How to get SpecUnit to run within a TeamCity CI build" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;nant&gt;&lt;teamcity&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="27894" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="64891" CreationDate="2008-08-26T12:27:30.337" Score="411" ViewCount="387497" Body="&lt;p&gt;In SQL Server 2005, we can create temp tables one of two ways:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;declare @tmp table (Col1 int, Col2 int);&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;or&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;create table #tmp (Col1 int, Col2 int);&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What are the differences between these two?  I have read conflicting opinions on whether @tmp still uses tempdb, or if everything happens in memory.  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In which scenarios does one out-perform the other? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1219" OwnerDisplayName="Eric Z Beard" LastEditorUserId="3682162" LastEditorDisplayName="LFSR Consulting" LastEditDate="2016-09-28T12:58:23.280" LastActivityDate="2021-11-19T10:56:41.337" Title="What's the difference between a temp table and table variable in SQL Server?" Tags="&lt;sql-server&gt;&lt;temp-tables&gt;&lt;table-variable&gt;" AnswerCount="13" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="172" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="27899" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="5040105" CreationDate="2008-08-26T12:30:19.253" Score="34" ViewCount="24518" Body="&lt;p&gt;Is there a way to make S3 default to an index.html page?  E.g.: My bucket object listing:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;/index.html&#xA;/favicon.ico&#xA;/images/logo.gif&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;A call to &lt;strong&gt;www.example.com/&lt;em&gt;index.html&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; works great!  But if one were to call &lt;strong&gt;www.example.com/&lt;/strong&gt; we'd either get a 403 or a REST object listing XML document depending on how bucket-level ACL was configured.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So, the question: Is there a way to have index.html functionality with content hosted on S3?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2961" OwnerDisplayName="Stu Thompson" LastEditorUserId="1033581" LastEditorDisplayName="Stu Thompson" LastEditDate="2017-07-18T16:00:53.777" LastActivityDate="2019-09-17T07:51:26.657" Title="Is there a way to have index.html functionality with content hosted on S3?" Tags="&lt;amazon-s3&gt;&lt;amazon-web-services&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="11" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="2954005" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="2954610" CreationDate="2010-06-01T23:15:13.040" Score="84" ViewCount="88813" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm trying to create a ggplot2 plot with the legend beneath the plot.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The ggplot2 book says on p 112 &quot;The position and justification of legends are controlled by the theme setting legend.position, and the value can be right, left, top, bottom, none (no legend), or a numeric position&quot;. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The following code works (since &quot;right&quot; it is the default), and it also works with &quot;none&quot; as the legend position, but &quot;left&quot;, &quot;top&quot;, &quot;bottom&quot;, all fail with &quot;Error in grid.Call.graphics(&quot;L_setviewport&quot;, pvp, TRUE) :  Non-finite location and/or size for viewport&quot;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;library(ggplot2)&#xA;(myDat &amp;lt;- data.frame(cbind(VarX=10:1, VarY=runif(10)), &#xA;    Descrip=sample(LETTERS[1:3], 10, replace=TRUE)))&#xA;qplot(VarX,VarY, data=myDat, shape=Descrip) + &#xA;    opts(legend.position=&quot;right&quot;)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What am I doing wrong? Re-positioning a legend must be incredibly common, so I figure it's me.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="23929" LastEditorUserId="23929" LastEditDate="2010-06-01T23:21:48.500" LastActivityDate="2021-11-12T00:11:18.577" Title="How to move or position a legend in ggplot2" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;ggplot2&gt;&lt;legend&gt;&lt;legend-properties&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="9" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="27910" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="10324802" CreationDate="2008-08-26T12:37:33.387" Score="55" ViewCount="12654" Body="&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href=&quot;http://doi.org/&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;DOI&lt;/a&gt; system places basically no useful limitations on what constitutes &lt;a href=&quot;http://doi.org/handbook_2000/enumeration.html#2.2&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;a reasonable identifier&lt;/a&gt;. However, being able to pull DOIs out of PDFs, web pages, etc. is quite useful for citation information, etc.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there a reliable way to identify a DOI in a block of text without assuming the 'doi:' prefix? (any language acceptable, regexes preferred, and avoiding false positives a must)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2963" OwnerDisplayName="Kai" LastActivityDate="2020-10-31T16:47:24.333" Title="Finding a DOI in a document or page" Tags="&lt;regex&gt;&lt;doi&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="31" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
