  <row Id="2956672" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="2956727" CreationDate="2010-06-02T10:11:34.623" Score="1" ViewCount="7937" Body="&lt;p&gt;I tried to pass a filepath to a function in R, but I failed =/ I hope someone here can help me.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt;heat &amp;lt;- function(filepath) &#xA;{ chicks &amp;lt;- read.table(file=filepath, dec=&quot;,&quot;, header=TRUE, sep=&quot;\t&quot;)&#xA;...&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;When I call the function, nothing happens...&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt;heat(&quot;/home/.../file.txt&quot;)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;... and &quot;chicks&quot; is not found&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt;chicks&#xA;Error: Object 'chicks' not found&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What is the correct way to pass a path to a function?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="356315" LastEditorUserId="3848765" LastEditDate="2017-09-05T15:32:37.690" LastActivityDate="2017-09-05T15:32:37.690" Title="Passing a filepath to a R function?" Tags="&lt;function&gt;&lt;r&gt;&lt;path&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="27998" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="28289" CreationDate="2008-08-26T13:18:30.480" Score="28" ViewCount="39011" Body="&lt;p&gt;Most of my C/C++ development involves monolithic module files and absolutely no classes whatsoever, so usually when I need to make a &lt;strong&gt;DLL&lt;/strong&gt; with accessible functions I just export them using the standard &lt;code&gt;__declspec(dllexport)&lt;/code&gt; directive.  Then access them either dynamically via &lt;code&gt;LoadLibrary()&lt;/code&gt; or at compile time with a header and lib file. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How do you do this when you want to export an entire class (and all it's public methods and properties)?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is it possible to dynamically load that class at runtime and if so, how?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How would you do it with a header and lib for compile time linking?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="194" OwnerDisplayName="Adam Haile" LastEditorUserId="1332690" LastEditorDisplayName="Adam Haile" LastEditDate="2012-04-26T01:59:40.010" LastActivityDate="2015-02-03T10:30:57.460" Title="Exporting a C++ class from a DLL" Tags="&lt;c++&gt;&lt;windows&gt;&lt;dll&gt;" AnswerCount="6" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="14" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="28002" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1255015" CreationDate="2008-08-26T13:20:55.357" Score="1920" ViewCount="806112" Body="&lt;p&gt;I've been writing C and C++ code for almost twenty years, but there's one aspect of these languages that I've never really understood. I've obviously used regular casts i.e.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;MyClass *m = (MyClass *)ptr;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;all over the place, but there seem to be two other types of casts, and I don't know the difference. What's the difference between the following lines of code?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;MyClass *m = (MyClass *)ptr;&#xA;MyClass *m = static_cast&amp;lt;MyClass *&amp;gt;(ptr);&#xA;MyClass *m = dynamic_cast&amp;lt;MyClass *&amp;gt;(ptr);&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1821" OwnerDisplayName="Graeme" LastEditorUserId="46642" LastEditDate="2014-05-15T15:15:29.143" LastActivityDate="2021-09-13T13:37:26.223" Title="Regular cast vs. static_cast vs. dynamic_cast" Tags="&lt;c++&gt;&lt;pointers&gt;&lt;casting&gt;" AnswerCount="8" CommentCount="4" FavoriteCount="955" ClosedDate="2014-05-15T15:13:05.190" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
