  <row Id="28965" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="29084" CreationDate="2008-08-26T19:51:32.187" Score="17" ViewCount="1913" Body="&lt;p&gt;Watching SO come online has been quite an education for me.  I'd like to make a checklist of various vunerabilities and exploits used against web sites, and what programming techniques can be used to defend against them.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;What categories of  vunerabilities?&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;crashing site&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;breaking into server&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;breaking into other people's logins&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;spam&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet_sock_puppet&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;sockpuppeting&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet_sock_puppet#Meatpuppet&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;meatpuppeting&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;etc...&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;What kind of defensive programming techniques?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;etc...&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="116" OwnerDisplayName="Mark Harrison" LastEditorUserId="3218692" LastEditorDisplayName="Jeff Atwood" LastEditDate="2015-07-14T07:49:37.057" LastActivityDate="2015-07-14T07:49:37.057" Title="Checklist for Web Site Programming Vulnerabilities" Tags="&lt;security&gt;&lt;defensive-programming&gt;" AnswerCount="9" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="10" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="28975" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-26T19:55:29.510" Score="59" ViewCount="12613" Body="&lt;p&gt;I've followed the &lt;a href=&quot;http://incubator.apache.org/couchdb/index.html&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;CouchDB&lt;/a&gt; project with interest over the last couple of years, and see it is now an Apache Incubator project. Prior to that, the CouchDB web site was full of &lt;em&gt;do not use for production code&lt;/em&gt; type disclaimers, so I'd done no more than keep an eye on it. I'd be interested to know your experiences if you've been using CouchDB either for a live project, or a technology pilot. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2745" OwnerDisplayName="reefnet_alex" LastEditorUserId="832" LastEditorDisplayName="Rob Cooper" LastEditDate="2008-08-26T20:01:41.337" LastActivityDate="2013-04-09T22:33:46.250" Title="Anyone using CouchDB?" Tags="&lt;database&gt;&lt;couchdb&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="18" ClosedDate="2015-10-02T14:05:33.040" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="3019473" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2010-06-11T00:34:52.007" Score="0" ViewCount="143" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am having some problems with my clipboard contents when using R. When I run my scripts/commands in tinn-r, very often I would get something that I had ran earlier pasted onto my R Console instead of the command that I have just selected. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;To get over this, I would go to my clipboard and delete its content(text/syntax). However, the same text/syntax that I had just deleted would re-appear on my clipboard and would again appear on my R Console when trying to run a different syntax from my tinn-r. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="345634" LastEditorUserId="169095" LastEditDate="2014-10-01T21:02:23.277" LastActivityDate="2014-10-01T21:02:23.277" Title="Preventing what was/were previously copied on to my clipboard from appearing on R Console when using R" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;clipboard-interaction&gt;&lt;tinn-r&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="3019834" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3067286" CreationDate="2010-06-11T02:27:32.277" Score="4" ViewCount="1194" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm creating a plot and adding a basic loess smooth line to it.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;qplot(Age.GTS2004., X.d18O,data=deepsea, geom=c('point')) + &#xA;geom_smooth(method=&quot;loess&quot;,se=T,span=0.01, alpha=.5, fill='light blue',color='navy')&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The problem is that the line is coming out really choppy.  I need more evaluation point for the curve in certain areas. Is there a way to increase the number of evaluation points without having to reconstruct geom_smooth?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="65148" LastActivityDate="2011-02-23T10:56:16.780" Title="How do I increase the number of evaluation points in geom_smooth for ggplot2 in R" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;ggplot2&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="3" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="28982" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-26T19:58:57.927" Score="173" ViewCount="73948" Body="&lt;p&gt;Related to my &lt;a href=&quot;https://stackoverflow.com/questions/28975/anyone-using-couchdb&quot;&gt;CouchDB&lt;/a&gt; question.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Can anyone explain MapReduce in terms a numbnuts could understand?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2745" OwnerDisplayName="reefnet_alex" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditorDisplayName="Jay Bazuzi" LastEditDate="2017-05-23T11:55:03.767" LastActivityDate="2017-10-08T11:28:27.200" Title="Simple explanation of MapReduce?" Tags="&lt;frameworks&gt;&lt;mapreduce&gt;&lt;glossary&gt;" AnswerCount="8" CommentCount="3" FavoriteCount="92" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="29004" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="29032" CreationDate="2008-08-26T20:09:40.303" Score="13" ViewCount="47549" Body="&lt;p&gt;Sometimes I need to quickly extract some arbitrary data from XML files to put into a CSV format. What's your best practices for doing this in the Unix terminal? I would love some code examples, so for instance how can I get the following problem solved?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Example XML input:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre class=&quot;lang-html prettyprint-override&quot;&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;root&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;myel name=&amp;quot;Foo&amp;quot; /&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;myel name=&amp;quot;Bar&amp;quot; /&amp;gt;&#xA;&amp;lt;/root&amp;gt;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;My desired CSV output:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;Foo,&#xA;Bar,&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="261" OwnerDisplayName="Mattias" LastEditorUserId="5123537" LastEditorDisplayName="Rob Cooper" LastEditDate="2021-01-19T08:19:39.093" LastActivityDate="2021-02-12T13:05:44.047" Title="Parsing XML using unix terminal" Tags="&lt;xml&gt;&lt;unix&gt;&lt;parsing&gt;&lt;shell&gt;&lt;csv&gt;" AnswerCount="8" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="4" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="29011" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="30719" CreationDate="2008-08-26T20:13:46.020" Score="7" ViewCount="11329" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;class Foo &amp;lt; ActiveRecord::Base&#xA;  named_scope :a, lambda { |a| :conditions =&amp;gt; { :a =&amp;gt; a } }&#xA;  named_scope :b, lambda { |b| :conditions =&amp;gt; { :b =&amp;gt; b } }&#xA;end&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I'd like&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;class Foo &amp;lt; ActiveRecord::Base&#xA;  named_scope :ab, lambda { |a,b| :conditions =&amp;gt; { :a =&amp;gt; a, :b =&amp;gt; b } }&#xA;end&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;but I'd prefer to do it in a DRY fashion.  I can get the same effect by using&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt; Foo.a(something).b(something_else)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;but it's not particularly lovely.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1190" OwnerDisplayName="Gaius" LastEditorUserId="2262149" LastEditorDisplayName="Rob Cooper" LastEditDate="2018-08-16T21:00:49.980" LastActivityDate="2018-08-16T21:00:49.980" Title="Is there a way to combine named scopes into a new named scope?" Tags="&lt;ruby-on-rails&gt;&lt;rails-activerecord&gt;&lt;named-scope&gt;" AnswerCount="6" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="3024060" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3035592" CreationDate="2010-06-11T15:25:43.903" Score="0" ViewCount="472" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have been getting into R recently and have been using the StatET plugin for Eclipse.  When I search for help, it prints the 'contents' of the help to the console.  I am hoping to have the help open up in a new window, similar to what happens when in R itself.  I am hoping this is just a setting.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Any help will be greatly appreciated!&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Brock&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="155406" LastActivityDate="2010-06-14T07:28:52.053" Title="Help Window using StatET in Eclipse" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;eclipse-plugin&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="29030" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="30691" CreationDate="2008-08-26T21:47:22.817" Score="1" ViewCount="3757" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a custom SharePoint application page deployed to the _layouts folder.  It's a custom &quot;new form&quot; for a custom content type.  During my interactions with this page, I will need to add an item to my list.  When the page first loads, I can use SPContext.Current.List to see the current list I'm working with.  But after I fill in my form and the form posts back onto itself and IsPostBack is true, then SPContext.Current.List is null so I can't find the list that I need to add my stuff into.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is this expected?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How should I retain some info about my context list across the postback?  Should I just populate some asp:hidden control with my list's guid and then just pull it back from that on the postback?  That seems safe, I guess.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;FWIW, this is the MOSS 2007 Standard version.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="404" OwnerDisplayName="Chris Farmer" LastActivityDate="2016-06-17T09:50:55.513" Title="SharePoint SPContext.List in a custom application page" Tags="&lt;sharepoint&gt;&lt;spcontext&gt;&lt;applicationpage&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
