  <row Id="30066" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-27T13:19:17.100" Score="10" ViewCount="19911" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm looking for a way to redirect all the stderr streams in interactive bash (ideally to its calling parent process).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I don't want to redirect stderr stream from each individual command, which I could do by appending &lt;code&gt;2&amp;gt; a_file&lt;/code&gt; to each command.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;By default, these stderr streams are redirected to the stdout of an interactive bash. I would like to get them on the stderr of this interactive bash process in order to prevent my stdout to be polluted by error messages and be able to treat them separatly.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Any ideas?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I still haven't found an answer ... But maybe it's actually a tty parameter. Does anybody knows something about tty/interactive shell responsibility for handling stderr ?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1044" OwnerDisplayName="Grégoire" LastEditorUserId="2284" LastEditorDisplayName="Grégoire" LastEditDate="2009-04-08T19:35:12.253" LastActivityDate="2013-08-07T02:04:45.327" Title="How to redirect all stderr in bash?" Tags="&lt;linux&gt;&lt;bash&gt;&lt;shell&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="30067" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="52893" CreationDate="2008-08-27T13:20:15.287" Score="52" ViewCount="9707" Body="&lt;p&gt;I just listened to the StackOverflow team's 17th podcast, and they talked so highly of &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.asp.net/mvc/&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;ASP.NET MVC&lt;/a&gt; that I decided to check it out.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;But first, I want to be sure it's worth it. I already created a base web application (for other developers to build on) for a project that's starting in a few days and wanted to know, based on your experience, if I should take the time to learn the basics of MVC and re-create the base web application with this model.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Are there really big pros that'd make it worthwhile?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;EDIT: It's not an existing project, it's a project about to start, so if I'm going to do it it should be now...&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I just found this&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;It does not, however, use the existing post-back model for interactions back to the server.  Instead, you'll route all end-user interactions to a Controller class instead - which helps ensure clean separation of concerns and testability (&lt;strong&gt;it also means no viewstate or page lifecycle with MVC based views&lt;/strong&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How would that work? No viewstate? No events?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1782" OwnerDisplayName="Juan Manuel" LastEditorUserId="727208" LastEditDate="2012-07-13T06:40:24.413" LastActivityDate="2012-07-13T06:40:24.413" Title="Should I migrate to ASP.NET MVC?" Tags="&lt;asp.net-mvc&gt;" AnswerCount="20" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="23" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="3088931" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2010-06-21T22:27:10.927" Score="0" ViewCount="1068" Body="&lt;p&gt;I recently started experimenting with the biganalytics package for R. I ran into a problem however...&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I am trying to run &lt;code&gt;bigkmeans&lt;/code&gt; with a cluster number of about 2000 e.g &lt;code&gt;clust &amp;lt;- bigkmeans(mymatrix, centers=2000)&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;However, I get the following error:&lt;br&gt;&#xA;&lt;code&gt;Error in 1:(10 + 2^k) : result would be too long a vector&lt;/code&gt; &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Can someone maybe give me a hint what I am doing wrong here?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="210944" LastEditorUserId="97160" LastEditDate="2012-07-02T12:46:45.763" LastActivityDate="2012-07-02T12:46:45.763" Title="Clustering with bigkmeans from bigmemory package in R?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;cluster-analysis&gt;&lt;k-means&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="30074" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="30090" CreationDate="2008-08-27T13:23:35.763" Score="12" ViewCount="5132" Body="&lt;p&gt;We have several .NET applications that monitor a directory for new files, using FileSystemWatcher. The files are copied from another location, uploaded via FTP, etc. When they come in, the files are processed in one way or another. However, one problem that I have never seen a satisfactory answer for is: for large files, how does one know when the files being monitored are still being written to? Obviously, we need to wait until the files are complete and closed before we begin processing them. The event args in the FileSystemWatcher events do not seem to address this.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2144" OwnerDisplayName="glaxaco" LastActivityDate="2009-09-16T08:33:53.320" Title="Monitoring files - how to know when a file is complete" Tags="&lt;.net&gt;&lt;filesystems&gt;" AnswerCount="9" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
