  <row Id="3097598" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3097799" CreationDate="2010-06-22T22:23:44.077" Score="19" ViewCount="11939" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm currently working on several projects on my own (at least the developing part is done only by me :). Using Eclipse with different Java, R, SQL and other source files I'm wondering what version control system would be best for me.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;At the time the history of Eclipse IDE seems to be enough, but I'm not sure if this will be true in a month/year...&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What solution would you recommend and why?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;To be more precise: I'm quite sure to use SVN or git if I decide to use a full version control system. But I'm just not sure if it is necessary...&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;small update: has the release of Eclipse Helios added new opinions?&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="370095" LastEditorUserId="370095" LastEditDate="2010-06-23T18:09:48.023" LastActivityDate="2010-06-23T18:09:48.023" Title="version control for one-man project using eclipse?" Tags="&lt;java&gt;&lt;eclipse&gt;&lt;version-control&gt;&lt;r&gt;&lt;project-management&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="8" FavoriteCount="9" ClosedDate="2019-06-22T08:05:27.820" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="30354" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="30679" CreationDate="2008-08-27T14:54:52.423" Score="10" ViewCount="20914" Body="&lt;p&gt;In one of my VB6 forms, I create several other Form objects and store them in member variables.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;Private m_frm1 as MyForm&#xA;Private m_frm2 as MyForm&#xA;&#xA;// Later...&#xA;Set m_frm1 = New MyForm&#xA;Set m_frm2 = New MyForm&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I notice that I'm leaking memory whenever this (parent) form is created and destroyed.  Is it necessary for me to assign these member variables to &lt;code&gt;Nothing&lt;/code&gt; in &lt;code&gt;Form_Unload()&lt;/code&gt;?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In general, when is that required?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;SOLVED: This particular memory leak was fixed when I did an &lt;code&gt;Unload&lt;/code&gt; on the forms in question, not when I set the form to &lt;code&gt;Nothing&lt;/code&gt;.  I managed to remove a few other memory leaks by explicitly setting some instances of Class Modules to &lt;code&gt;Nothing&lt;/code&gt;, as well.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="863" OwnerDisplayName="Matt Dillard" LastEditorUserId="863" LastEditorDisplayName="Matt Dillard" LastEditDate="2008-08-27T18:05:49.267" LastActivityDate="2019-12-16T06:23:19.943" Title="When must I set a variable to &quot;Nothing&quot; in VB6?" Tags="&lt;vb6&gt;&lt;memory-leaks&gt;" AnswerCount="8" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="30373" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="30420" CreationDate="2008-08-27T15:03:00.173" Score="75" ViewCount="17380" Body="&lt;p&gt;I remember first learning about vectors in the STL and after some time, I wanted to use a vector of bools for one of my projects. After seeing some strange behavior and doing some research, I learned that &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.informit.com/guides/content.aspx?g=cplusplus&amp;amp;seqNum=98&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;a vector of bools is not really a vector of bools&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Are there any other common pitfalls to avoid in C++?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2328" OwnerDisplayName="Craig H" LastEditorUserId="63550" LastEditDate="2014-01-22T21:14:04.110" LastActivityDate="2017-12-05T18:47:36.597" Title="What C++ pitfalls should I avoid?" Tags="&lt;c++&gt;&lt;stl&gt;" AnswerCount="29" CommentCount="4" FavoriteCount="94" ClosedDate="2011-03-29T03:44:56.750" CommunityOwnedDate="2009-12-29T04:12:17.417" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
