  <row Id="3136293" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2010-06-28T21:30:17.593" Score="58" ViewCount="88901" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am trying to learn R and want to bring in an SPSS file, which I can open in SPSS.  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I have tried using &lt;code&gt;read.spss&lt;/code&gt; from &lt;code&gt;foreign&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;spss.get&lt;/code&gt; from &lt;code&gt;Hmisc&lt;/code&gt;.  Both error messages are the same. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Here is my code:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;## install.packages(&quot;Hmisc&quot;)&#xA;library(foreign)&#xA;&#xA;## change the working directory&#xA;getwd()&#xA;setwd('C:/Documents and Settings/BTIBERT/Desktop/')&#xA;&#xA;## load in the file&#xA;## ?read.spss&#xA;asq &amp;lt;- read.spss('ASQ2010.sav', to.data.frame=T)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;And the resulting error:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;Error in read.spss(&quot;ASQ2010.sav&quot;, to.data.frame = T) :    error&#xA;  reading system-file header In addition: Warning message: In&#xA;  read.spss(&quot;ASQ2010.sav&quot;, to.data.frame = T) :   ASQ2010.sav: position&#xA;  0: character `\000' (&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Also, I tried saving out the SPSS file as a SPSS 7 .sav file (was previously using SPSS 18).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;Warning messages: 1: In read.spss(&quot;ASQ2010_test.sav&quot;, to.data.frame =&#xA;  T) :   ASQ2010_test.sav: Unrecognized record type 7, subtype 14&#xA;  encountered in system file 2: In read.spss(&quot;ASQ2010_test.sav&quot;,&#xA;  to.data.frame = T) :   ASQ2010_test.sav: Unrecognized record type 7,&#xA;  subtype 18 encountered in system file&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="155406" LastEditorUserId="1737251" LastEditDate="2017-07-10T04:36:23.493" LastActivityDate="2021-03-25T21:01:19.640" Title="Read SPSS file into R" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;spss&gt;" AnswerCount="15" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="24" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="3137168" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3137187" CreationDate="2010-06-29T00:57:52.263" Score="5" ViewCount="5062" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am interested in finding the numbers that exhibit the property of having the sum of their proper divisors equal to the number. The first example is 6, where the proper divisors are 1 + 2 + 3 = 6.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I wrote the following code in R, but I feel it is pretty inefficient and can be improved upon significantly. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;propDivisor &amp;lt;- function(&#xA;    max&#xA;)&#xA;{&#xA;    n&amp;lt;-{}&#xA;    for(j in 2:max){&#xA;        m&amp;lt;-{}&#xA;        for(i in 1:(j/2+1)){&#xA;            if(j%%i==0){m&amp;lt;-c(m,i)}&#xA;        }   &#xA;        if(sum(m)==j){n&amp;lt;-c(n,j)}&#xA;    }&#xA;return(cat(&quot;The proper divisors between 1 and&quot;, max, &quot;are&quot;, n, &quot;.&quot;, sep=&quot; &quot;)    )&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Does anyone have any suggestions for improving the following code? I feel one of the apply functions should be used here. Maybe this would be a decent code golf exercise for the future? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;And as I know this comes up somewhat frequently here, this is NOT a homework problem, just something a coworker posed as an interesting coding challenger earlier today.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;UPDATE:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Thanks to everyone for your comments and thoughts on places to look for further information. Here's another solution that utilizes sapply:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;D &amp;lt;- function(n) sum((1:(n-1))[n%%1:(n-1)==0])==n&#xA;(2:9000)[sapply(2:9000,D)]&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="415635" LastEditorUserId="415635" LastEditDate="2010-06-29T14:10:53.533" LastActivityDate="2013-07-02T11:40:14.070" Title="Algorithm to determine proper divisors" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;refactoring&gt;&lt;apply&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="3137525" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3137552" CreationDate="2010-06-29T02:48:12.483" Score="2" ViewCount="411" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have been working on large datasets lately (more than 400 thousands lines). So far, I have been using XTS format, which worked fine for &quot;small&quot; datasets of a few tenth of thousands elements.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Now that the project grows, R simply crashes when retrieving the data for the database and putting it into the XTS.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;It is my understanding that R should be able to have vectors with size up to 2^32-1 elements (or 2^64-1 according the the version). Hence, I came to the conclusion that XTS might have some limitations but I could not find the answer in the doc. (maybe I was a bit overconfident about my understanding of theoretical possible vector size).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;To sum up, I would like to know if:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;XTS has indeed a size limitation&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;What do you think is the smartest way to handle large time series? (I was thinking about splitting the analysis into several smaller datasets).&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;I don't get an error message, R simply shuts down automatically. Is this a known behavior?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;SOLUTION&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;The same as R and it depends on the kind of memory being used (64bits, 32 bits). It is anyway extremely large.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Chuncking data is indeed a good idea, but it is not needed.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;This problem came from a &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;bug in R 2.11.0 which has been solved in R 2.11.1&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;. There was a problem with long dates vector (here the indexes of the XTS).&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="295307" LastEditorDisplayName="user1228" LastEditDate="2013-09-04T15:37:44.613" LastActivityDate="2013-09-04T15:37:44.613" Title="XTS size limitation" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;time&gt;&lt;time-series&gt;&lt;xts&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="31051" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="31300" CreationDate="2008-08-27T19:51:09.420" Score="3" ViewCount="517" Body="&lt;p&gt;So we're trying to set up replicated repositories using PlasticSCM, one in the US, and one in Australia and running into a bit of a snag.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The US configuration is Active Directory, the AU configuration is User/Password.  This in itself is not a big deal, I've already set up the SID translation table.  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The problem is with plasticscm's replicate command itself.  This is the command which should replicate from the US to AU, run ON the AU server.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;cm replicate br:/main@rep:default@repserver:US:8084 rep:myrep@repserver:AU:9090 --trmode=name --trtable=trans.txt --authdata=ActiveDirectory:192.168.1.3:389:john.doe@factory.com:fPBea2rPsQaagEW3pKNveA==:dc=factory,dc=com&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The part I'm stuck at is the authdata part (the above is an EXAMPLE only).   How can I generate the obscured password?  I think it's the only thing preventing these two repositories from talking to each other.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2170" OwnerDisplayName="hova" LastEditorUserId="832" LastEditorDisplayName="Rob Cooper" LastEditDate="2008-08-27T19:52:43.863" LastActivityDate="2009-04-02T14:38:51.587" Title="Setting up replicated repositories in Plastic SCM" Tags="&lt;version-control&gt;&lt;repository&gt;&lt;plasticscm&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
