  <row Id="31129" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="31153" CreationDate="2008-08-27T20:14:19.880" Score="54" ViewCount="95340" Body="&lt;p&gt;I want to return &lt;code&gt;StudentId&lt;/code&gt; to use elsewhere outside of the &lt;em&gt;scope&lt;/em&gt; of the &lt;code&gt;$.getJSON()&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;j.getJSON(url, data, function(result)&#xA;{&#xA;    var studentId = result.Something;&#xA;});&#xA;&#xA;//use studentId here&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I would imagine this has to do with scoping, but it doesn't seem to work the same way &lt;em&gt;c#&lt;/em&gt; does&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2993" OwnerDisplayName="Sean Chambers" LastEditorUserId="4939915" LastEditorDisplayName="JasonBunting" LastEditDate="2015-12-25T05:44:34.630" LastActivityDate="2018-11-16T10:24:39.177" Title="How can I return a variable from a $.getJSON function" Tags="&lt;javascript&gt;&lt;jquery&gt;&lt;ajax&gt;&lt;scope&gt;&lt;return-value&gt;" AnswerCount="6" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="19" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="3142731" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3142933" CreationDate="2010-06-29T16:33:13.720" Score="10" ViewCount="4518" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have run into a situation where I need to take all the extra arguments passed to an R function and roll them into an object for later use. I thought the &lt;a href=&quot;https://stackoverflow.com/questions/3057341/how-to-use-rs-ellipsis-feature-when-writing-your-own-function&quot;&gt;previous question about ellipses&lt;/a&gt; in functions would help me, but I still can't quite grasp how to do this. Here is a very simple example of what I would like to do:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;newmean &amp;lt;- function(X, ...){&#xA;  args &amp;lt;- as.list(substitute(list(...)))[-1L]&#xA;  return(mean(X, args))&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I've tried a number of different formulations of args in the above example and tried unlisting args in the return call. But I can't make this work. Any tips?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I realize that I could do this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;newmean &amp;lt;- function(X, ...){&#xA;    return(mean(X, ...))&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;But I need to have the ... arguments in an object which I can serialize and read back into another machine. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="37751" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditDate="2017-05-23T12:25:10.027" LastActivityDate="2010-06-29T20:55:20.270" Title="R: using a list for ellipsis arguments" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;parameters&gt;&lt;function&gt;&lt;ellipsis&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="6" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="31140" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-27T20:16:58.953" Score="1" ViewCount="476" Body="&lt;p&gt;A number of forms in my project inherit from a base form. It is easy to get at the Controls collection of the derived forms, but I have not found a simple way to access the Components collection, since VS marks this as private. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I assume this could be done with reflection, but I'm not really sure how best to go about it, not having worked with reflection before.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Right now, I'm using a sort of clunky workaround, in which I override a function GetComponents and return an array of the components I'm interested in. This is obviously prone to errors, since it's easy to forget to implement the overridden function or update it when components are added.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If anyone has any tips or can suggest a better way, I'd be glad to hear.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2899" OwnerDisplayName="Tom Juergens" LastActivityDate="2013-06-09T08:43:00.547" Title="Accessing a Component on an inherited form from the base form" Tags="&lt;.net&gt;&lt;winforms&gt;&lt;reflection&gt;&lt;inheritance&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
