  <row Id="31158" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-27T20:21:09.300" Score="12" ViewCount="8518" Body="&lt;p&gt;What are in your opinion the best resources (books or web pages) describing algorithms or techniques to use for collision detection in a 2D environment?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I'm just eager to learn different techniques to make more sophisticated and efficient games.&#xA;­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2772" OwnerDisplayName="borrego" LastEditorUserId="7275984" LastEditorDisplayName="Rob Cooper" LastEditDate="2017-08-19T14:44:33.220" LastActivityDate="2021-02-12T19:51:19.800" Title="Resources of techniques use for collision detection in 2D?" Tags="&lt;algorithm&gt;&lt;collision-detection&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="9" ClosedDate="2021-02-12T22:47:47.780" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="3142961" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3151766" CreationDate="2010-06-29T17:00:42.000" Score="5" ViewCount="1018" Body="&lt;p&gt;I want to create a function that will retry an expression if it fails.  Here's my working version:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;retry &amp;lt;- function(.FUN, max.attempts=3, sleep.seconds=1) {&#xA;  x &amp;lt;- NULL&#xA;  if(max.attempts &amp;gt; 0) {&#xA;    f &amp;lt;- substitute(.FUN)&#xA;    x &amp;lt;- try(eval(f))&#xA;    if(class(x) == &quot;try-error&quot;) {&#xA;      Sys.sleep(sleep.seconds)&#xA;      return(suppressWarnings(retry(.FUN, max.attempts-1)))&#xA;    }&#xA;  }&#xA;  x&#xA;}&#xA;&#xA;retry(stop(&quot;I'm here&quot;))&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If I remove the &lt;code&gt;suppressWarnings()&lt;/code&gt; function above, then I get a set of warnings on each recursive call.  Does anyone know what I'm doing wrong that would cause that?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Here's an example that can be run repeatedly:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;retry({ tmp &amp;lt;- function() { if(rnorm(1) &amp;lt; 0) stop(&quot;I'm here&quot;) else &quot;success&quot; }; tmp() })&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="163053" LastEditorUserId="163053" LastEditDate="2010-06-30T17:57:28.530" LastActivityDate="2010-06-30T17:57:28.530" Title="R warning message on recursive expression: If you fail, try, try again" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;recursion&gt;&lt;try-catch&gt;&lt;callstack&gt;&lt;substitution&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="3144790" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3144843" CreationDate="2010-06-29T21:13:56.693" Score="9" ViewCount="13016" Body="&lt;p&gt;So when I make plots sometimes I see the y crossing x at some offset. I generated this figure using:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;ggplot(data=d2,aes(y=log10(Nems+1),x=Time)) +&#xA;  geom_point(size=3,shape=1) +        &#xA;  geom_line(data=d2,aes(x=time_model,y=log10(value),group=variable,linetype=variable)) +&#xA;  ylim(0.001,2) + no_bg + draw_axis&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I end up manually moving the y in Illustrator. Is there a way to just do it here?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;http://img816.imageshack.us/img816/7633/testzh.png&quot;&gt;alt text http://img816.imageshack.us/img816/7633/testzh.png&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="313163" LastEditorUserId="172261" LastEditDate="2010-06-30T08:14:47.323" LastActivityDate="2010-06-30T08:14:47.323" Title="How do I make the y-axis intersect the x-axis at 0 in ggplot2?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;ggplot2&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="3" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="3144802" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2010-06-29T21:15:06.193" Score="10" ViewCount="5312" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am running R on a multiple node Linux cluster. I would like to run my analysis on R using scripts or batch mode without using parallel computing software such as MPI or snow.  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I know this can be done by dividing the input data such that each node runs different parts of the data.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;My question is how do I go about this exactly?  I am not sure how I should code my scripts.  An example would be very helpful!&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I have been running my scripts so far using PBS but it only seems to run on one node as R is a single thread program. Hence, I need to figure out how to adjust my code so it distributes labor to all of the nodes. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Here is what I have been doing so far:&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;1) command line:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; qsub myjobs.pbs&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;2) myjobs.pbs: &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; #!/bin/sh&#xA;&amp;gt; #PBS -l nodes=6:ppn=2&#xA;&amp;gt; #PBS -l walltime=00:05:00&#xA;&amp;gt; #PBS -l arch=x86_64&#xA;&amp;gt; &#xA;&amp;gt; pbsdsh -v $PBS_O_WORKDIR/myscript.sh&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;3) myscript.sh:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;#!/bin/sh&#xA;cd $PBS_O_WORKDIR&#xA;R CMD BATCH --no-save my_script.R&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;4) my_script.R:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; library(survival)&#xA;&amp;gt; ...&#xA;&amp;gt; write.table(test,&quot;TESTER.csv&quot;,&#xA;&amp;gt; sep=&quot;,&quot;, row.names=F, quote=F)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Any suggestions will be appreciated! Thank you!&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;-CC&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="379465" LastEditorUserId="37751" LastEditDate="2010-06-30T