  <row Id="31326" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-27T23:34:19.010" Score="27" ViewCount="43945" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a few internal .net web application here that require users to &quot;log out&quot; of them. I know this may seem moot on an Intranet application, but nonetheless it is there.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;We are using Windows authentication for our Intranet apps, so we tie in to our Active Directory with Basic Authentication and the credentials get stored in the browser cache, as opposed to a cookie when using .net forms authentication.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In IE6+ you can leverage a special JavaScript function they created by doing the following:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;document.execCommand(&quot;ClearAuthenticationCache&quot;, &quot;false&quot;)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;However, for the other browsers that are to be supported (namely Firefox at the moment, but I strive for multi-browser support), I simply display message to the user that they need to close their browser to log out of the application, which effectively flushes the application cache.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Does anybody know of some commands/hacks/etc. that I can use in other browsers to flush the authentication cache?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="71" OwnerDisplayName="Dillie-O" LastEditorUserId="2134" LastEditorDisplayName="Chris" LastEditDate="2008-09-26T19:56:43.130" LastActivityDate="2012-02-25T15:55:04.017" Title="Is there a browser equivalent to IE's ClearAuthenticationCache?" Tags="&lt;asp.net&gt;&lt;authentication&gt;&lt;cross-browser&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="16" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="31340" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="31398" CreationDate="2008-08-27T23:44:47.843" Score="88" ViewCount="43190" Body="&lt;p&gt;I've been trying to wrap my head around how threads work in Python, and it's hard to find good information on how they operate. I may just be missing a link or something, but it seems like the official documentation isn't very thorough on the subject, and I haven't been able to find a good write-up.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;From what I can tell, only one thread can be running at once, and the active thread switches every 10 instructions or so?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Where is there a good explanation, or can you provide one? It would also be very nice to be aware of common problems that you run into while using threads with Python.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="242853" OwnerDisplayName="jdd" LastEditorUserId="63550" LastEditDate="2010-09-11T20:31:26.950" LastActivityDate="2018-03-30T11:47:11.123" Title="How do threads work in Python, and what are common Python-threading specific pitfalls?" Tags="&lt;python&gt;&lt;multithreading&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="33" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
