  <row Id="3162286" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3162661" CreationDate="2010-07-01T23:02:45.237" Score="1" ViewCount="1518" Body="&lt;p&gt;R's qplot function has a nifty alpha parameter for shading coincident points in a scatter plot darker.  Here it is in action:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.decisionsciencenews.com/2010/07/01/maps-without-map-packages&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;http://www.decisionsciencenews.com/2010/07/01/maps-without-map-packages&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I'm wondering how to do the same in Mathematica.&#xA;Here's code to grab the data from the above article and plot it, without the nifty shading:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;data = Rest@Import[&#xA;  &quot;http://www.decisionsciencenews.com/wp-content/uploads/2010/07/latlong.zip&quot;, &#xA;  &quot;latlong.csv&quot;]&#xA;&#xA;ListPlot[data, PlotRange -&amp;gt; {{-130, -65}, {23.5, 50.5}}, Frame -&amp;gt; True]&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The question is, how to add the shading of the points?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="4234" LastActivityDate="2010-07-02T01:09:54.767" Title="Scatter plot with indication of the density of points" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;map&gt;&lt;plot&gt;&lt;wolfram-mathematica&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
