  <row Id="32243" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="32302" CreationDate="2008-08-28T13:23:33.320" Score="109" ViewCount="98725" Body="&lt;p&gt;The following PHP code snippet uses GD to resize a browser-uploaded PNG to 128x128. It works great, except that the transparent areas in the original image are being replaced with a solid color- black in my case. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Even though &lt;code&gt;imagesavealpha&lt;/code&gt; is set, something isn't quite right.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What's the best way to preserve the transparency in the resampled image?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;$uploadTempFile = $myField[ 'tmp_name' ]&#xA;list( $uploadWidth, $uploadHeight, $uploadType ) &#xA;  = getimagesize( $uploadTempFile );&#xA;&#xA;$srcImage = imagecreatefrompng( $uploadTempFile );    &#xA;imagesavealpha( $targetImage, true );&#xA;&#xA;$targetImage = imagecreatetruecolor( 128, 128 );&#xA;imagecopyresampled( $targetImage, $srcImage, &#xA;                    0, 0, &#xA;                    0, 0, &#xA;                    128, 128, &#xA;                    $uploadWidth, $uploadHeight );&#xA;&#xA;imagepng(  $targetImage, 'out.png', 9 );&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1820" OwnerDisplayName="Cheekysoft" LastEditorUserId="1612146" LastEditorDisplayName="travis" LastEditDate="2013-04-04T15:34:49.957" LastActivityDate="2018-08-12T16:59:03.817" Title="Can PNG image transparency be preserved when using PHP's GDlib imagecopyresampled?" Tags="&lt;php&gt;&lt;png&gt;&lt;transparency&gt;&lt;gd&gt;&lt;alpha&gt;" AnswerCount="10" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="21" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="32246" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="32263" CreationDate="2008-08-28T13:24:09.013" Score="0" ViewCount="218" Body="&lt;p&gt;OK. This problem is doing my head in. And I don't know if there even IS a definitive answer.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;We have a website, lets call it &lt;em&gt;mycompany.com&lt;/em&gt;. It's a UK-based site, with UK based content. Google knows about it, and we have done a load of SEO on it. All is well.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Except&lt;/strong&gt;, we are about to relaunch &lt;strong&gt;my company, the GLOBAL brand&lt;/strong&gt;, so we now need mycompany.com/uk, mycompany.com/us, and mycompany.com/au, for the various countries local content. We are using GEOIP, so if someone from the US loads mycompany.com, they get redirected to mycompany.com/us etc. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If someone isn't in one of those three countries (US, Australia, or UK) they get the UK site.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This is all well and good, but we dont want to lose the rather large amount of Google juice we have on mycompany.com! And worse, the Google bot appears to be 100% based in the US, so the US site (which is pretty much out LEAST important one of the three) will appear to be the main one.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;We have thought about detecting the bot&lt;/strong&gt;, and serving UK content, but it appears Google may smack us for that.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Has anyone else come across this situation, and have a solution?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2947" OwnerDisplayName="Nic Wise" LastEditorUserId="3393505" LastEditDate="2015-12-03T19:46:09.913" LastActivityDate="2015-12-03T19:46:09.913" Title="how to get the googlebot to get the correct GEOIPed content?" Tags="&lt;seo&gt;&lt;bots&gt;&lt;google-search&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" ClosedDate="2013-05-03T15:48:01.323" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="32260" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="32336" CreationDate="2008-08-28T13:28:38.147" Score="931" ViewCount="617805" Body="&lt;p&gt;Instead of relying on my host to send an email, I was thinking of sending the email messages using my &lt;strong&gt;Gmail&lt;/strong&gt; account. The emails are personalized emails to the bands I play on my show. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is it possible to do it?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2535" OwnerDisplayName="Mike Wills" LastEditorUserId="806202" LastEditDate="2020-04-19T17:29:39.007" LastActivityDate="2021-07-14T14:55:29.687" Title="Sending email in .NET through Gmail" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;.net&gt;&lt;email&gt;&lt;smtp&gt;&lt;gmail&gt;" AnswerCount="22" CommentCount="8" FavoriteCount="379" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="32280" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="33552" CreationDate="2008-08-28T13:34:40.313" Score="20" ViewCount="42816" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am in the middle of reading the excellent &lt;a href=&quot;https://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/com/0132350882&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;Clean Code&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;One discussion is regarding passing nulls into a method.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;public class MetricsCalculator {&#xA;    public double xProjection(Point p1, Point p2) {&#xA;        return (p2.x - p1.x) * 1.5;&#xA;    }&#xA;}&#xA;...&#xA;calculator.xProjection(null, new Point(12,13));&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;It represents different ways of handling this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;public double xProjection(Point p1, Point p2) {&#xA;    if (p1 == null || p2 == null) {&#xA;        throw new IllegalArgumentException(&quot;Invalid argument for xProjection&quot;);&#xA;    }&#xA;    return (p2.x - p1.x) * 1.5;&#xA;}&#xA;&#xA;public double xProjection(Point p1, Point p2) {&#xA;    assert p1 != null : &quot;p1 should not be null&quot;;&#xA;    assert p2 != null : &quot;p2 should not be null&quot;;&#xA;    return (p2.x - p1.x) * 1.5;&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I prefer the &lt;a href=&quot;http://java.sun.com/j2se/1.4.2/docs/guide/lang/assert.html&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;assertions&lt;/a&gt; approach, but I don't like the fact that assertions are turned off by default.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The book finally states:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;In most programming languages there is no good way to deal with a null that is passed by a caller accidentally. Because this is the case, the rational approach is to forbid passing null by default.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;It doesn't really go into how you would enforce this restriction?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Do any of you have strong opinions either way.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3295" OwnerDisplayName="toolkit" LastActivityDate="2018-12-20T12:55:18.797" Title="Passing null to a method" Tags="&lt;java&gt;&lt;null&gt;&lt;assert&gt;" AnswerCount="16" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="9" ClosedDate="2012-01-26T13:34:32.597" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="32282" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="32290" CreationDate="2008-08-28T13:34:55.510" Score="85" ViewCount="48994" Body="&lt;p&gt;How can I test the same regex against different regular expression engines?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2820" OwnerDisplayName="Onorio Catenacci" LastEditorUserId="1269037" LastEditDate="2014-02-26T04:55:13.727" LastActivityDate="2014-06-16T10:06:01.133" Title="How can test I regular expressions using multiple RE engines?" Tags="&lt;regex&gt;&lt;testing&gt;" AnswerCount="30" CommentCount="9" FavoriteCount="63" ClosedDate="2014-07-25T16:27:31.670" CommunityOwnedDate="2009-11-19T19:46:21.670" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="3197999" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3198753" CreationDate="2010-07-07T19:09:52.897" Score="7" ViewCount="4164" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm trying to order this dataframe by population and date, so I'm using the &lt;code&gt;order()&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;rank()&lt;/code&gt; functions:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; df &amp;lt;- data.frame(idgeoville = c(5, 8, 4, 3, 4, 5, 8, 8),&#xA;                   date       = c(rep(1950, 4), rep(2000, 4)),&#xA;                   population = c(500, 450, 350, 350, 650, 500, 500, 450))&#xA;&amp;gt; df&#xA;   idgeoville date    population&#xA;1  5          1950     500&#xA;2  8          1950     450&#xA;3  4          1950     350&#xA;4  3          1950     350&#xA;5  4          2000     650&#xA;6  5          2000     500&#xA;7  8          2000     500&#xA;8  8          2000     450&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;With &lt;code&gt;ties.method = &quot;first&quot;&lt;/code&gt; I have no problem, finally I'm producing this dataframe:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;   idgeoville date    population  rank&#xA;1  5          1950     500        1&#xA;2  8          1950     450        2&#xA;3  4          1950     350        3&#xA;4  3          1950     350        4&#xA;5  4          2000     650        1&#xA;6  5          2000     500        2&#xA;7  8          2000     500        3&#xA;8  8          2000     450        4&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;But in fact, I want a dataframe with &lt;strong&gt;equal ranking for equal population rank&lt;/strong&gt;, like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;   idgeoville date    population  rank&#xA;1  5          1950     500        1&#xA;2  8          1950     450        2&#xA;3  4          1950     350        3&#xA;4  3          1950     350        3&#xA;5  4          2000     650        1&#xA;6  5          2000     500        2&#xA;7  8          2000     500        2&#xA;8  8          2000     450        3&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How can I resolve this problem with R? With a custom &lt;code&gt;ties.method()&lt;/code&gt; or another R tricks?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="385881" LastEditorUserId="1169233" LastEditDate="2014-04-24T13:35:10.460" LastActivityDate="2016-05-10T00:58:11.710" Title="How I can create a new ties.method with the R rank() function?" Tags="&lt;sorting&gt;&lt;select&gt;&lt;r&gt;&lt;ranking&gt;&lt;dataframe&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="3" FavoriteCount="2" ClosedDate="2017-05-12T08:31:21.280" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="32332" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="37542" CreationDate="2008-08-28T14:07:00.207" Score="36" ViewCount="26639" Body="&lt;p&gt;This isn't a design question, really, though it may seem like it. (Well, okay, it's kind of a design question). What I'm wondering is why the C++ &lt;code&gt;std::fstream&lt;/code&gt; classes don't take a &lt;code&gt;std::string&lt;/code&gt; in their constructor or open methods. Everyone loves code examples so:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;#include &amp;lt;iostream&amp;gt;&#xA;#include &amp;lt;fstream&amp;gt;&#xA;#include &amp;lt;string&amp;gt;&#xA;&#xA;int main()&#xA;{&#xA;    std::string filename = &quot;testfile&quot;;      &#xA;    std::ifstream fin;&#xA;&#xA;    fin.open(filename.c_str()); // Works just fine.&#xA;    fin.close();&#xA;&#xA;    //fin.open(filename); // Error: no such method.&#xA;    //fin.close();&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This gets me all the time when working with files. Surely the C++ library would use &lt;code&gt;std::string&lt;/code&gt; wherever possible?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="61" OwnerDisplayName="Bernard" LastEditorUserId="3153" LastEditorDisplayName="Brian R. Bondy" LastEditDate="2008-11-02T03:16:53.383" LastActivityDate="2017-01-09T18:44:33.457" Title="Why don't the std::fstream classes take a std::string?" Tags="&lt;c++&gt;&lt;stl&gt;&lt;file-io&gt;&lt;stdstring&gt;" AnswerCount="10" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="14" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="32333" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="161268" CreationDate="2008-08-28T14:07:15.197" Score="5" ViewCount="1066" Body="&lt;p&gt;Here's a perfect example of the problem: &lt;a href=&quot;http://blog.teksol.info/2009/03/27/argumenterror-on-number-sum-when-using-classifier-bayes.html&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;Classifier gem breaks Rails&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;** Original question: **&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;One thing that concerns me as a security professional is that Ruby doesn't have a parallel of Java's package-privacy.  That is, this isn't valid Ruby:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;public module Foo&#xA;  public module Bar&#xA;    # factory method for new Bar implementations&#xA;    def self.new(...)&#xA;      SimpleBarImplementation.new(...)&#xA;    end&#xA;    def baz&#xA;      raise NotImplementedError.new('Implementing Classes MUST redefine #baz')&#xA;    end&#xA;  end&#xA;&#xA;  private class SimpleBarImplementation&#xA;    include Bar&#xA;    def baz&#xA;      ...&#xA;    end&#xA;  end&#xA;end&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;It'd be really nice to be able to prevent monkey-patching of Foo::BarImpl.  That way, people who rely on the library know that nobody has messed with it.  Imagine if somebody changed the implementation of MD5 or SHA1 on you!  I can call &lt;code&gt;freeze&lt;/code&gt; on these classes, but I have to do it on a class-by-class basis, and other scripts might modify them before I finish securing my application if I'm not &lt;strong&gt;very&lt;/strong&gt; careful about load order.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Java provides lots of other tools for defensive programming, many of which are not possible in Ruby.  (See Josh Bloch's book for a good list.)  Is this really a concern?  Should I just stop complaining and use Ruby for lightweight things and not hope for &quot;enterprise-ready&quot; solutions?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;(And no, core classes are not frozen by default in Ruby.  See below:)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;require 'md5'&#xA;# =&amp;gt; true&#xA;MD5.frozen?&#xA;# =&amp;gt; false&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1190" OwnerDisplayName="Gaius" LastEditorUserId="1190" LastEditDate="2009-04-08T13:48:11.633" LastActivityDate="2009-04-08T13:48:11.633" Title="How can I program defensively in Ruby?" Tags="&lt;ruby&gt;&lt;security&gt;&lt;defensive-programming&gt;" AnswerCount="9" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="32338" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="32670" CreationDate="2008-08-28T14:09:25.880" Score="4" ViewCount="255" Body="&lt;p&gt;I just saw &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.codeplex.com/CloneDetectiveVS&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;Clone Detective&lt;/a&gt; linked on YCombinator news, and the idea heavily appeals to me. It seems like it would be useful for many languages, not just C#, but I haven't seen anything similar elsewhere.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Edit: For those who don't want to follow the link, Clone Detective scans the codebase for duplicate code that may warrant refactoring to minimize duplication.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3326" OwnerDisplayName="Runevault" LastEditorUserId="1810429" LastEditorDisplayName="travis" LastEditDate="2014-12-13T08:27:19.127" LastActivityDate="2014-12-13T08:27:19.127" Title="Are there similar tools to Clone Detective for other languages/IDEs?" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;language-agnostic&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="32341" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-28T14:10:52.100" Score="-1" ViewCount="1289" Body="&lt;p&gt;I may just be missing this functionality, but does anyone know if there is a widget available:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I need to list the subject for all the entries that are associated with a given tag.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;For example: I have 5 articles tagged with &quot;Tutorial&quot;, I'd like to see a list as follows:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Tutorial 1: Installing the app&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Tutorial 2: Customizing&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Tutorial 3: Advanced edits&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Tutorial 4: User managment&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Does functionality like this exists in wordpress allready?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2490" OwnerDisplayName="Markus" LastEditorUserId="1332690" LastEditDate="2012-04-25T22:08:20.933" LastActivityDate="2019-10-08T08:24:54.233" Title="How do I list all Entries with a certain tag in Wordpress?" Tags="&lt;wordpress&gt;&lt;widget&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="3198111" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3198566" CreationDate="2010-07-07T19:24:46.847" Score="4" ViewCount="1942" Body="&lt;p&gt;I want to use Random forests for attribute reduction. One problem I have in my data is that I don't have discrete class - only continuous, which indicates how example differs from 'normal'. This class attribute is a kind of distance from zero to infinity. &#xA;Is there any way to use Random forest for such data? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="296427" LastEditorUserId="172261" LastEditDate="2011-11-08T20:58:59.673" LastActivityDate="2011-11-08T20:58:59.673" Title="How to use R Random forests to reduce attributes having no discrete classes?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;statistics&gt;&lt;machine-learning&gt;&lt;feature-selection&gt;&lt;random-forest&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="32343" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="32348" CreationDate="2008-08-28T14:11:20.523" Score="65" ViewCount="62440" Body="&lt;p&gt;Let's say I had a program in C# that did something computationally expensive, like encoding a list of WAV files into MP3s. Ordinarily I would encode the files one at a time, but let's say I wanted the program to figure out how many CPU cores I had and spin up an encoding thread on each core. So, when I run the program on a quad core CPU, the program figures out it's a quad core CPU, figures out there are four cores to work with, then spawns four threads for the encoding, each of which is running on its own separate CPU. How would I do this?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;And would this be any different if the cores were spread out across multiple physical CPUs? As in, if I had a machine with two quad core CPUs on it, are there any special considerations or are the eight cores across the two dies considered equal in Windows?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2577" OwnerDisplayName="Schnapple" LastEditorUserId="33311" LastEditDate="2016-05-18T12:01:17.233" LastActivityDate="2016-05-18T12:01:17.233" Title="How do I spawn threads on different CPU cores?" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;.net&gt;&lt;windows&gt;&lt;multithreading&gt;" AnswerCount="10" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="33" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="32360" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="32394" CreationDate="2008-08-28T14:16:15.520" Score="9" ViewCount="8916" Body="&lt;p&gt;How do you specify that a foreign key constraint should be a 1:1 relationship in transact sql? Is declaring the column UNIQUE enough? Below is my existing code.!&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;CREATE TABLE [dbo].MyTable(&#xA;    [MyTablekey] INT IDENTITY(1,1) NOT FOR REPLICATION NOT NULL,&#xA;    [OtherTableKey] INT NOT NULL UNIQUE&#xA;        CONSTRAINT [FK_MyTable_OtherTable] FOREIGN KEY REFERENCES [dbo].[OtherTable]([OtherTableKey]),&#xA;    ...&#xA;    CONSTRAINT [PK_MyTable] PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED &#xA;    (&#xA;        [MyTableKey] ASC&#xA;    ) WITH (PAD_INDEX  = OFF, STATISTICS_NORECOMPUTE  = OFF, IGNORE_DUP_KEY = OFF, ALLOW_ROW_LOCKS  = ON, ALLOW_PAGE_LOCKS  = ON) ON [PRIMARY]&#xA;) ON [PRIMARY]&#xA;GO&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3400" OwnerDisplayName="homeskillet" LastEditorUserId="1274572" LastEditDate="2016-08-12T19:25:39.643" LastActivityDate="2016-08-12T19:25:39.643" Title="1:1 Foreign Key Constraints" Tags="&lt;sql&gt;&lt;sql-server&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
