  <row Id="3238242" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3238446" CreationDate="2010-07-13T14:36:52.733" Score="4" ViewCount="8083" Body="&lt;p&gt;Given a matrix A (not neccessarily square) with independent columns, I was able to apply Gram-Schmidt iteration and produce an orthonormal basis for its columnspace (in the form of an orthogonal matrix Q) using Matlab's function &lt;code&gt;qr&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;A=[1,1;1,0;1,2]&#xA;&#xA;[Q,R] = qr(A)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;and then&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt;&amp;gt; Q(:,1:size(A,2))&#xA;ans =&#xA;  -0.577350269189626  -0.000000000000000&#xA;  -0.577350269189626  -0.707106781186547&#xA;  -0.577350269189626   0.707106781186547&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;You can verify that the columns are orthonormal &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;Q(:,1)'*Q(:,2) equals zero and&#xA;&#xA;norm(Q(:,1)) equals norm(Q(:,2)) equals 1&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Given a matrix that has independent columns (like A), is there a function in R that produces the (Gram-Schmidt) orthogonal matrix Q ?. R's &lt;code&gt;qr&lt;/code&gt; function doesn't produce an orthogonal Q.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="170792" LastActivityDate="2013-03-20T02:28:37.087" Title="Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;linear-algebra&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
