00" LastEditorUserId="393200" LastEditDate="2010-07-16T17:59:02.550" LastActivityDate="2010-07-17T09:48:44.087" Title="issue running a test in Python, via rpy2" Tags="&lt;python&gt;&lt;r&gt;&lt;rpy2&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="3267312" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3267379" CreationDate="2010-07-16T17:13:36.783" Score="7" ViewCount="39430" Body="&lt;p&gt;In R, I have 600,000 categorical variables, each of which is classified as &amp;quot;0&amp;quot;, &amp;quot;1&amp;quot;, or &amp;quot;2&amp;quot;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What I would like to do is collapse &amp;quot;1&amp;quot; and &amp;quot;2&amp;quot; and leave &amp;quot;0&amp;quot; by itself, such that after re-categorizing &amp;quot;0&amp;quot; = &amp;quot;0&amp;quot;; &amp;quot;1&amp;quot; = &amp;quot;1&amp;quot; and &amp;quot;2&amp;quot; = &amp;quot;1&amp;quot;. In the end I only want &amp;quot;0&amp;quot; and &amp;quot;1&amp;quot; as categories for each of the variables.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Also, if possible, I would rather not create 600,000 new variables, if I can replace the existing variables with the new values that would be great!&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What would be the best way to do this?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="394139" LastEditorUserId="466862" LastEditDate="2021-11-05T18:51:08.173" LastActivityDate="2021-11-05T18:51:08.173" Title="How to collapse categories or recategorize variables?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;categories&gt;&lt;collapse&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="4" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="33469" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="33795" CreationDate="2008-08-28T21:43:16.163" Score="15" ViewCount="4312" Body="&lt;p&gt;So I just fixed a bug in a framework I'm developing. The pseudo-pseudocode looks like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;myoldObject = new MyObject { someValue = &quot;old value&quot; };&#xA;cache.Insert(&quot;myObjectKey&quot;, myoldObject);&#xA;myNewObject = cache.Get(&quot;myObjectKey&quot;);&#xA;myNewObject.someValue = &quot;new value&quot;;&#xA;if(myObject.someValue != cache.Get(&quot;myObjectKey&quot;).someValue)&#xA;     myObject.SaveToDatabase();&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So, essentially, I was getting an object from the cache, and then later on comparing the original object to the cached object to see if I need to save it to the database in case it's changed. The problem arose because the original object is a reference...so changing someValue also changed the referenced cached object, so it'd never save back to the database. I fixed it by cloning the object off of the cached version, severing the reference and allowing me to compare the new object against the cached one.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;My question is: &lt;strong&gt;is there a better way to do this, some pattern, that you could recommend?&lt;/strong&gt; I can't be the only person that's done this before :)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1212" OwnerDisplayName="Chris" LastActivityDate="2012-02-23T14:50:29.303" Title="Caching Patterns in ASP.NET" Tags="&lt;asp.net&gt;&lt;caching&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="15" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
