  <row Id="33969" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="1318059" CreationDate="2008-08-29T04:50:50.410" Score="222" ViewCount="57006" Body="&lt;p&gt;We're experimenting with various ways to throttle user actions in a &lt;strong&gt;given time period&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Limit question/answer posts&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Limit edits&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Limit feed retrievals&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;For the time being, we're using the Cache to simply insert a record of user activity - if that record exists if/when the user does the same activity, we throttle.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Using the Cache automatically gives us stale data cleaning and sliding activity windows of users, but how it will scale could be a problem.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What are some other ways of ensuring that requests/user actions can be effectively throttled (emphasis on stability)?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3" OwnerDisplayName="Jarrod Dixon" LastEditorUserId="3" LastEditDate="2009-08-23T08:25:28.323" LastActivityDate="2018-10-21T08:07:59.350" Title="Best way to implement request throttling in ASP.NET MVC?" Tags="&lt;asp.net-mvc&gt;&lt;throttling&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="3" FavoriteCount="241" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
