  <row Id="34151" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-29T08:02:03.663" Score="10" ViewCount="4118" Body="&lt;p&gt;Is the following safe?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I am new to threading and I want to delegate a time consuming process to a separate thread in my C++ program.&#xA;Using the boost libraries I have written code something like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;thrd = new boost::thread(boost::bind(&amp;amp;myclass::mymethod, this, &amp;amp;finished_flag);&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Where finished_flag is a boolean member of my class. When the thread is finished it sets the value and the main loop of my program checks for a change in that value.&#xA;I assume that this is okay because I only ever start one thread, and that thread is the only thing that changes the value (except for when it is initialised  before I start the thread)&#xA;So is this okay, or am I missing something, and need to use locks and mutexes, etc&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerDisplayName="Hamish" LastEditorUserId="95735" LastEditDate="2009-07-11T19:18:14.093" LastActivityDate="2012-07-03T14:52:21.750" Title="C++ Thread question - setting a value to indicate the thread has finished" Tags="&lt;c++&gt;&lt;multithreading&gt;&lt;boost-thread&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="34155" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="34387" CreationDate="2008-08-29T08:07:58.687" Score="21" ViewCount="2972" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm trying to grasp the concept of continuations and I found several small teaching examples like this one from the &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Continuation&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;Wikipedia article&lt;/a&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;(define the-continuation #f)&#xA;&#xA;(define (test)&#xA;  (let ((i 0))&#xA;    ; call/cc calls its first function argument, passing &#xA;    ; a continuation variable representing this point in&#xA;    ; the program as the argument to that function. &#xA;    ;&#xA;    ; In this case, the function argument assigns that&#xA;    ; continuation to the variable the-continuation. &#xA;    ;&#xA;    (call/cc (lambda (k) (set! the-continuation k)))&#xA;    ;&#xA;    ; The next time the-continuation is called, we start here.&#xA;    (set! i (+ i 1))&#xA;    i))&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I understand what this little function does, but I can't see any obvious application of it. While I don't expect to use continuations all over my code anytime soon, I wish I knew a few cases where they can be appropriate. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So I'm looking for more explicitely usefull code samples of what continuations can offer me as a programmer.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Cheers!&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2797" OwnerDisplayName="Seb" LastEditorUserId="2797" LastEditorDisplayName="S&amp;#233;bastien Rocca-Serra" LastEditDate="2008-09-10T09:27:42.593" LastActivityDate="2010-05-18T06:47:24.493" Title="Looking for examples of &quot;real&quot; uses of continuations" Tags="&lt;ruby&gt;&lt;language-agnostic&gt;&lt;scheme&gt;&lt;smalltalk&gt;&lt;continuations&gt;" AnswerCount="12" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="5" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
