  <row Id="35047" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-29T19:02:34.170" Score="14" ViewCount="3940" Body="&lt;p&gt;Perhaps the biggest risk in pushing new functionality to live lies with the database modifications required by the new code. In Rails, I believe they have 'migrations', in which you can programmatically make changes to your development host, and then make the same changes live along with the code that uses the revised schema. And roll both backs if needs be, in a synchronized fashion.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Has anyone come across a similar toolset for PHP/MySQL? Would love to hear about it, or any programmatic or process solutions to help make this less risky...&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="137" OwnerDisplayName="Flubba" LastEditorUserId="567854" LastEditDate="2019-01-18T11:03:49.643" LastActivityDate="2019-01-18T11:03:49.643" Title="Migrating database changes from development to live" Tags="&lt;php&gt;&lt;mysql&gt;&lt;schema&gt;&lt;migration&gt;" AnswerCount="10" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="6" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
