  <row Id="3367393" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3367858" CreationDate="2010-07-29T22:14:08.687" Score="8" ViewCount="10566" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am using ggplot2 (respectively qplot) to generate a report with Sweave. Now I need some help with the adjustment of the size of the plot. I use the following Sweave code to include it.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;\begin{figure}[htbp]&#xA;\begin{center}&#xA;&amp;lt;&amp;lt;fig=true,echo=false&amp;gt;&amp;gt;=&#xA;print(mygraph)&#xA;@&#xA;\caption{MyCaption}&#xA;\end{center}&#xA;\end{figure}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If I add a width argument (like shown below) to plot is squeezed down, but not really scaled down. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;&amp;lt;fig=true,echo=false,width=3&amp;gt;&amp;gt;=&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If I use ggsave() instead, I could use a scale argument and influence the size of the resulting .pdf file. Is there a way to influence the dimensions of the plot without saving it (since the .pdf is generated by Sweave anyway) ? Is there anything I need to add to my qplot code?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;mygraph=qplot(date,value,data=graph1,geom=&quot;line&quot;,colour=variable,xlab=&quot;&quot;,ylab=&quot;&quot;)&#xA;+ scale_y_continuous(limits = c(-0.3,0.3))&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Thx for any suggestions in advance!&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="366256" LastActivityDate="2010-07-30T06:04:50.797" Title="How to control the dimension / size of a plot with ggplot2" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;ggplot2&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="3" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="35743" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-30T06:48:47.873" Score="19" ViewCount="46178" Body="&lt;p&gt;According to &lt;a href=&quot;http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/bb386454.aspx&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;Microsoft&lt;/a&gt; the FileUpload control is not compatible with an AJAX UpdatePanel. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I am aware that a PostBackTrigger can be added to the submit button of the form like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;Triggers&amp;gt;&#xA;  &amp;lt;asp:PostBackTrigger ControlID=&quot;Button1&quot; /&amp;gt;&#xA;&amp;lt;/Triggers&amp;gt;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The problem is that this forces the form to perform a full post-back which voids out the whole point of using the UpdatePanel in the first place. Is there a workaround to this issue that does not cause the whole page to refresh?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3677" OwnerDisplayName="JohnMcCon" LastEditorUserId="811" LastEditorDisplayName="Shog9" LastEditDate="2008-09-19T22:42:34.313" LastActivityDate="2014-04-22T07:20:31.547" Title="FileUpload control inside an UpdatePanel without refreshing the whole page?" Tags="&lt;.net&gt;&lt;asp.net&gt;&lt;asp.net-ajax&gt;&lt;file-upload&gt;&lt;updatepanel&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="4" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
