  <row Id="35798" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="35803" CreationDate="2008-08-30T08:55:43.543" Score="3" ViewCount="1930" Body="&lt;p&gt;What techniques can be applied to detect &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_virus#Polymorphic_code&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;Polymorphic&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_virus#Metamorphic_code&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;Metamorphic&lt;/a&gt; viruses?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How difficult is to implement these techniques? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Are these techniques being applied in modern day anti-virus softwares?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="184" OwnerDisplayName="Niyaz" LastActivityDate="2011-04-29T06:54:57.713" Title="Techniques to detect Polymorphic and Metamorphic viruses?" Tags="&lt;antivirus&gt;&lt;virus&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ClosedDate="2015-01-03T20:14:55.347" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="35805" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="35823" CreationDate="2008-08-30T09:12:41.367" Score="37" ViewCount="5192" Body="&lt;p&gt;If I create a class &lt;code&gt;A&lt;/code&gt; as follows:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;class A:&#xA;    def __init__(self):&#xA;        self.name = 'A'&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Inspecting the &lt;code&gt;__dict__&lt;/code&gt; member looks like &lt;code&gt;{'name': 'A'}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If however I create a class &lt;code&gt;B&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;class B:&#xA;    name = 'B'&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;__dict__&lt;/code&gt; is empty.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What is the difference between the two, and why doesn't &lt;code&gt;name&lt;/code&gt; show up in &lt;code&gt;B&lt;/code&gt;'s &lt;code&gt;__dict__&lt;/code&gt;?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3154" OwnerDisplayName="David Sykes" LastEditorUserId="91962" LastEditorDisplayName="zacherates" LastEditDate="2011-08-12T06:33:50.663" LastActivityDate="2013-02-05T05:28:27.900" Title="Why is my instance variable not in __dict__?" Tags="&lt;python&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="5" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="3369600" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3374675" CreationDate="2010-07-30T07:55:16.830" Score="4" ViewCount="1275" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a script where I'm using ddply, as in the following example:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;ddply(df, .(col),&#xA;function(x) data.frame(&#xA;col1=some_function(x$y),&#xA;col2=some_other_function(x$y)&#xA;)&#xA;)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Within ddply, is it possible to reuse col1 without calling the entire function again?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;For example: &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;ddply(df, .(col),&#xA;function(x) data.frame(&#xA;col1=some_function(x$y),&#xA;col2=some_other_function(x$y)&#xA;col3=col1*col2&#xA;)&#xA;)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="170352" LastEditorUserId="1385941" LastEditDate="2013-03-28T01:57:12.623" LastActivityDate="2013-03-28T01:57:12.623" Title="Is it possible to reuse generated columns in ddply?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;plyr&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="5" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="3369959" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3370115" CreationDate="2010-07-30T08:52:42.197" Score="71" ViewCount="107617" Body="&lt;p&gt;Is there a method for moving a column from one position in a data.frame to the next - without typing an entirely new data.frame()&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;For example: &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;a &amp;lt;- b &amp;lt;- c &amp;lt;- d &amp;lt;- e &amp;lt;- f &amp;lt;- g &amp;lt;- 1:100&#xA;df &amp;lt;- data.frame(a,b,c,d,e,f,g)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Now let's say I wanted &quot;g&quot; in front of &quot;a&quot;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I could retype it, as &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;df &amp;lt;- data.frame(g,a,b,c,d,e,f)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;But is there not a quicker way? (Imagine 1500+ columns)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="170352" LastActivityDate="2020-05-14T02:26:48.343" Title="Moving columns within a data.frame() without retyping" Tags="&lt;r&gt;" AnswerCount="17" CommentCount="3" FavoriteCount="32" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="35817" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="35857" CreationDate="2008-08-30T09:27:24.640" Score="135" ViewCount="105196" Body="&lt;p&gt;When using os.system() it's often necessary to escape filenames and other arguments passed as parameters to commands.  How can I do this?  Preferably something that would work on multiple operating systems/shells but in particular for bash.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I'm currently doing the following, but am sure there must be a library function for this, or at least a more elegant/robust/efficient option:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;def sh_escape(s):&#xA;   return s.replace(&quot;(&quot;,&quot;\\(&quot;).replace(&quot;)&quot;,&quot;\\)&quot;).replace(&quot; &quot;,&quot;\\ &quot;)&#xA;&#xA;os.system(&quot;cat %s | grep something | sort &amp;gt; %s&quot; &#xA;          % (sh_escape(in_filename), &#xA;             sh_escape(out_filename)))&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Edit:&lt;/em&gt; I've accepted the simple answer of using quotes, don't know why I didn't think of that; I guess because I came from Windows where ' and &quot; behave a little differently.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Regarding security, I understand the concern, but, in this case, I'm interested in a quick and easy solution which os.system() provides, and the source of the strings is either not user-generated or at least entered by a trusted user (me).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3715" LastEditorUserId="355230" LastEditorDisplayName="Roger Pate" LastEditDate="2017-12-17T15:26:56.927" LastActivityDate="2022-01-11T15:36:01.220" Title="How to escape os.system() calls?" Tags="&lt;python&gt;&lt;shell&gt;&lt;escaping&gt;" AnswerCount="9" CommentCount="5" FavoriteCount="27" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="35834" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-30T09:46:06.840" Score="3" ViewCount="394" Body="&lt;p&gt;Without resorting to PInvoke, is there a way in .net to find out what windows are open?  This is slightly different than asking what applications are running in memory.  For example, Firefox could be running, but could be more than one window.  Basically, I just want to be privy to the same information that the taskbar (and &lt;code&gt;alt-tab&lt;/code&gt;?) is.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Also, once I have a reference to a window, is there any way to programatically give it focus?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there any way to do this with managed code?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1493" OwnerDisplayName="Burton" LastEditorUserId="1836618" LastEditDate="2017-08-09T20:15:32.350" LastActivityDate="2017-08-09T20:15:32.350" Title="Get a list of current windows, and give one of them focus, in .Net" Tags="&lt;.net&gt;&lt;windows&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
