  <row Id="36344" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-08-30T21:08:18.200" Score="8" ViewCount="835" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm trying to &quot;install SGML::Parser::OpenSP&quot; from the cpan shell, but it fails on the first &quot;make test&quot;. I also get the same error if I go into the build directory and run make test.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I believe this bit of the output below is the relevant part.  Note the Symbol not found when perl gets to the &quot;use&quot; line for the new library. The file listed there exists and is readable.  When I run the unix command &quot;nm&quot; it &lt;em&gt;does&lt;/em&gt; show the symbol.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I don't know what to make of the symbol not found error.  I'm not running as admin/root if that matters.  This is on a mac, 10.4.11 My googling turned up some hints that this can happen if gcc is called instead of g++, but I believe that is set up correctly.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What else could it be, and how can I try to fix?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Here's the excerpt from running make test:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;PERL_DL_NONLAZY=1 /usr/bin/perl &quot;-MExtUtils::Command::MM&quot; &quot;-e&quot; &quot;test_harness(0, 'blib/lib', 'blib/arch')&quot; t/*.t&#xA;t/01basic...........1/4&#xA;#   Failed test 'use SGML::Parser::OpenSP;'&#xA;#   at t/01basic.t line 14.&#xA;#     Tried to use 'SGML::Parser::OpenSP'.&#xA;#     Error:  Can't load '/Users/joshgold/.cpan/build/SGML-Parser-OpenSP-0.994/blib/arch/auto/SGML/Parser/OpenSP/OpenSP.bundle' for module SGML::Parser::OpenSP: dlopen(/Users/joshgold/.cpan/build/SGML-Parser-OpenSP-0.994/blib/arch/auto/SGML/Parser/OpenSP/OpenSP.bundle, 2): Symbol not found: __ZTI15SGMLApplication&#xA;#   Referenced from: /Users/joshgold/.cpan/build/SGML-Parser-OpenSP-0.994/blib/arch/auto/SGML/Parser/OpenSP/OpenSP.bundle&#xA;#   Expected in: dynamic lookup&#xA;#  at (eval 3) line 2&#xA;# Compilation failed in require at (eval 3) line 2.&#xA;# BEGIN failed--compilation aborted at (eval 3) line 2.&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerDisplayName="Josh" LastEditorUserId="13051" LastEditorDisplayName="JesperE" LastEditDate="2008-11-17T11:10:27.690" LastActivityDate="2010-01-09T23:28:23.463" Title="Why can't SGML::Parser::OpenSP find the symbol __ZTI15SGMLApplication?" Tags="&lt;perl&gt;&lt;cpan&gt;&lt;sgml&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="3388323" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3388358" CreationDate="2010-08-02T13:43:01.173" Score="10" ViewCount="610" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am interested in (functional) vector manipulation in &lt;code&gt;R&lt;/code&gt;. Specifically, what are &lt;code&gt;R&lt;/code&gt;'s equivalents to Perl's &lt;a href=&quot;http://perldoc.perl.org/functions/map.html&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;&lt;code&gt;map&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&quot;http://perldoc.perl.org/functions/grep.html&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;&lt;code&gt;grep&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt;? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The following Perl script greps the even array elements and multiplies them by 2:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;@a1=(1..8); &#xA;@a2 = map {$_ * 2} grep {$_ % 2 == 0} @a1;&#xA;print join(&quot; &quot;, @a2)&#xA;# 4 8 12 16&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How can I do that in &lt;code&gt;R&lt;/code&gt;? I got this far, using &lt;code&gt;sapply&lt;/code&gt; for Perl's &lt;code&gt;map&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; a1 &amp;lt;- c(1:8)&#xA;&amp;gt; sapply(a1, function(x){x * 2})&#xA;[1]  2  4  6  8 10 12 14 16&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Where can I read more about such functional array manipulations in &lt;code&gt;R&lt;/code&gt;?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Also, is there a &lt;code&gt;Perl&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;R&lt;/code&gt; phrase book, similar to the &lt;a href=&quot;http://wiki.python.org/moin/PerlPhrasebook&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;Perl Python Phrasebook&lt;/a&gt;? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="60628" LastEditorUserId="10248678" LastEditDate="2020-08-08T13:53:32.097" LastActivityDate="2020-08-08T13:53:32.097" Title="What are R's equivalents to Perl's map and grep?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;perl&gt;&lt;dictionary&gt;&lt;functional-programming&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
