  <row Id="36656" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="36658" CreationDate="2008-08-31T05:55:12.330" Score="33" ViewCount="47779" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm parsing text from a file and storing it in a string.  The problem is that some of the text in the original files contains &lt;code&gt;ASCII art&lt;/code&gt; and whatnot that I would like to preserve.  When I print out the string on the &lt;code&gt;HTML page&lt;/code&gt;, even if it does have the same formatting and everything since it is in &lt;code&gt;HTML&lt;/code&gt;, the spacing and line breaks are not preserved.  What is the best way to print out the text in &lt;code&gt;HTML&lt;/code&gt; exactly as it was in the original text file?&lt;br&gt;&#xA;I would like to give an example, but unfortunately, I was not able to get it to display correctly in this markdown editor :P&lt;br&gt;&#xA;Basically, I would like suggestions on how to display &lt;code&gt;ASCII art in HTML&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3831" OwnerDisplayName="Steven Oxley" LastEditorUserId="567854" LastEditorDisplayName="Rob Cooper" LastEditDate="2019-01-18T11:00:15.583" LastActivityDate="2020-01-21T23:17:32.880" Title="How do I keep whitespace formatting using PHP/HTML?" Tags="&lt;php&gt;&lt;html&gt;&lt;ascii&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="3" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="36682" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="36689" CreationDate="2008-08-31T08:08:35.570" Score="4" ViewCount="393" Body="&lt;p&gt;Learning WPF nowadays. Found something new today with .Net dependency properties. What they bring to the table is &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Support for Callbacks (Validation, Change, etc)  &lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Property inheritance&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Attached properties  &lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;among others.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;But my question here is why do they need to be declared as static in the containing class? The recommmended way is to then add instance 'wrapper' property for them. Why ?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;edit:&#xA;@Matt, but doesn't that also mandate that the property value is also shared across instances - unless of course it is a derived value ?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1695" OwnerDisplayName="Gishu" LastEditorUserId="1695" LastEditorDisplayName="Gishu" LastEditDate="2008-08-31T13:37:02.127" LastActivityDate="2012-06-13T06:53:05.180" Title="Why do .Net WPF DependencyProperties have to be static members of the class" Tags="&lt;.net&gt;&lt;wpf&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="36693" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="155600" CreationDate="2008-08-31T08:42:17.797" Score="12" ViewCount="15430" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a dynamically created image that I am saving to a stream so that I can display it on a ReportViewer surface.  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Setup:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Windows Client application (not WebForms)&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Report datasource is an object datasource, with a dynamically generated stream as a property (CustomImage)&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Report.EnableExternalImages = true&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Image.Source = Database&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Image.MIMEType = image/png&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Image.Value = =Fields!CustomImage.Value&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This is not working, but is not reporting any errors, just showing an empty image icon on the report surface.  All other fields are displaying correctly.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Does anyone have a working code sample of this scenario?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2918" OwnerDisplayName="Ben Laan" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditDate="2008-09-10T00:13:43.690" LastActivityDate="2015-01-01T18:07:11.883" Title="How can I render a PNG image (as a memory stream) onto a .NET ReportViewer report surface" Tags="&lt;.net&gt;&lt;image&gt;&lt;reportviewer&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="8" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="3396166" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3396218" CreationDate="2010-08-03T11:50:05.810" Score="1" ViewCount="491" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm trying to multicore a function (in Windows), which, at one point, calls another workhorse function (function within function). Here is a minimal working example. You will need &lt;code&gt;doSMP&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;revoIPC&lt;/code&gt; packages (to get them, see Tal's post &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.r-statistics.com/2010/04/parallel-multicore-processing-with-r-on-windows/&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;func1 &amp;lt;- function(x) {sqrt(x)}&#xA;func2 &amp;lt;- function(y) {&#xA;    func1(y)&#xA;}&#xA;&#xA;library(doSMP)&#xA;wrk &amp;lt;- startWorkers(workerCount = 4) #I have 4 cores, so adjust to your specs&#xA;registerDoSMP(wrk)&#xA;&#xA;obj.result &amp;lt;- foreach(i = 1:10000) %dopar% func2(i)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The above routine doesn't work, but if I nest &lt;code&gt;func1&lt;/code&gt; within &lt;code&gt;func2&lt;/code&gt; like so&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;func2 &amp;lt;- function(y) {&#xA;func1 &amp;lt;- function(x) {sqrt(x)}&#xA;    func1(y)&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;the process goes through smoothly (as far as I can tell).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How can I call functions from outside with &lt;code&gt;%dopar%&lt;/code&gt;?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="322912" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditDate="2020-06-20T09:12:55.060" LastActivityDate="2014-06-11T17:11:06.250" Title="parallel computing of a function with doSMP on Windows" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;foreach&gt;&lt;parallel-processing&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="5" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="36701" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="36734" CreationDate="2008-08-31T09:17:01.737" Score="197" ViewCount="354023" Body="&lt;p&gt;Is it completely against the Java way to create struct like objects?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;class SomeData1 {&#xA;    public int x;&#xA;    public int y;&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I can see a class with accessors and mutators being more Java like.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;class SomeData2 {&#xA;    int getX();&#xA;    void setX(int x);&#xA;&#xA;    int getY();&#xA;    void setY(int y);&#xA;&#xA;    private int x;&#xA;    private int y;&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The class from the first example is notationally convenient.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;// a function in a class&#xA;public int f(SomeData1 d) {&#xA;    return (3 * d.x) / d.y;&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This is not as convenient.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;// a function in a class&#xA;public int f(SomeData2 d) {&#xA;    return (3 * d.getX()) / d.getY();&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3836" OwnerDisplayName="cdv" LastEditorUserId="4076315" LastEditorDisplayName="Cheekysoft" LastEditDate="2018-11-02T07:32:22.853" LastActivityDate="2019-01-11T04:31:00.327" Title="Struct like objects in Java" Tags="&lt;java&gt;&lt;oop&gt;&lt;struct&gt;" AnswerCount="20" CommentCount="5" FavoriteCount="39" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
