  <row Id="3433981" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2010-08-08T10:38:15.387" Score="2" ViewCount="277" Body="&lt;p&gt;There are 2 eigenvectors corresponding to 1 eigenvalue (with multiplicity 4) for the following example. However, R returns 4 distinct eigenvectors. It looks like pairs of them are approximately the same only differing in machine floating point error (epsilon). Can you please check and confirm?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; B&#xA; [,1] [,2] [,3] [,4]&#xA; [1,]    2    0    0    0&#xA; [2,]    1    2    0    0&#xA; [3,]    0    1    2    0&#xA; [4,]    0    0    1    2&#xA;&amp;gt; eigen(B)&#xA;$values&#xA;[1] 2 2 2 2&#xA;&#xA;$vectors&#xA;[,1]          [,2]          [,3]          [,4]&#xA;[1,]    0  0.000000e+00  0.000000e+00  8.758115e-47&#xA;[2,]    0  0.000000e+00  1.972152e-31 -1.972152e-31&#xA;[3,]    0  4.440892e-16 -4.440892e-16  4.440892e-16&#xA;[4,]    1 -1.000000e+00  1.000000e+00 -1.000000e+00&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="236215" LastEditorUserId="236215" LastEditDate="2010-08-08T10:56:06.357" LastActivityDate="2010-08-08T13:02:15.183" Title="number of distinct eigenvectors in R" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;linear-algebra&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
