  <row Id="3443576" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="5068939" CreationDate="2010-08-09T19:43:05.600" Score="48" ViewCount="26206" Body="&lt;p&gt;I was surprised to see that R will coerce factors into a number when concatenating vectors. This happens even when the levels are the same. For example:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; facs &amp;lt;- as.factor(c(&quot;i&quot;, &quot;want&quot;, &quot;to&quot;, &quot;be&quot;, &quot;a&quot;, &quot;factor&quot;, &quot;not&quot;, &quot;an&quot;, &quot;integer&quot;))&#xA;&amp;gt; facs&#xA;[1] i       want    to      be      a       factor  not     an      integer&#xA;Levels: a an be factor i integer not to want&#xA;&amp;gt; c(facs[1 : 3], facs[4 : 5])&#xA;[1] 5 9 8 3 1&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;what is the idiomatic way to do this in R (in my case these vectors can be pretty large)? Thank you.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="160015" LastEditorUserId="563532" LastEditDate="2017-01-16T03:22:18.580" LastActivityDate="2021-07-20T00:29:34.127" Title="How to concatenate factors, without them being converted to integer level?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;concatenation&gt;&lt;r-factor&gt;" AnswerCount="9" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="9" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
