  <row Id="38308" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="320969" CreationDate="2008-09-01T19:13:10.767" Score="2" ViewCount="1387" Body="&lt;p&gt;Drawing a parallelgram is nicely supported with Graphics.DrawImage:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;Bitmap destImage = new Bitmap(srcImage.Width, srcImage.Height);&#xA;using (Graphics gr = new Graphics.FromImage(destImage))&#xA;{&#xA;    Point[] destPts = new Point[] { new PointF(x1, y1), &#xA;        new PointF(x2, y2), new PointF(x4, y4)};&#xA;    gr.DrawImage(srcImage, destPts);&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How, do you do 4 points (obviously the following is not supported, but this is what is wanted):&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;Bitmap destImage = new Bitmap(srcImage.Width, srcImage.Height);&#xA;using (Graphics gr = new Graphics.FromImage(destImage))&#xA;{&#xA;    Point[] destPts = new Point[] { new PointF(x1, y1), new PointF(x2, y2),&#xA;        new PointF(x3, y3), new PointF(x4, y4)};&#xA;    gr.DrawImage(srcImage, destPts);&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3798" OwnerDisplayName="Kris Erickson" LastActivityDate="2019-02-07T17:53:00.093" Title="Is there any way to draw an image to use 4 points rather than 3 (perspective warp)" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;.net&gt;&lt;gdi+&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="3463837" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3464107" CreationDate="2010-08-12T00:14:49.547" Score="11" ViewCount="2396" Body="&lt;p&gt;I've started to use R a little while ago and am not sure how often to update the installed packages (at this time, I'm using mostly ggplot2 and rattle). One one hand it's the typical geek impulse to have the latest version :-) On the other, updates can break functionality and, as an R beginner, I don't want to waste time looking into package incompatibilities and reinstalling libraries, it's almost certain I wouldn't notice any difference with an improved package.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;With other applications I have a sense developed from experience on how often to upgrade, how much to wait between the release of an upgrade and installing it and so on. But I'm in the dark with regards to R.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;And to be clear: I'm not talking about R itself, but its libraries.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Thanks.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="277434" LastEditorUserId="1033808" LastEditDate="2012-01-10T17:45:09.870" LastActivityDate="2016-01-04T22:32:05.603" Title="Is it good practice to update R packages often?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;" AnswerCount="6" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ClosedDate="2016-01-05T12:54:42.640" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
