  <row Id="40273" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="40961" CreationDate="2008-09-02T18:46:09.060" Score="91" ViewCount="86954" Body="&lt;p&gt;A client of mine has asked me to integrate a 3rd party API into their Rails app.  The only problem is that the API uses SOAP.  Ruby has basically dropped SOAP in favor of REST.  They provide a Java adapter that apparently works with the Java-Ruby bridge, but we'd like to keep it all in Ruby, if possible.  I looked into soap4r, but it seems to have a slightly bad reputation.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So what's the best way to integrate SOAP calls into a Rails app?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2884" OwnerDisplayName="jcoby" LastActivityDate="2018-04-09T21:41:55.810" Title="What's the best way to use SOAP with Ruby?" Tags="&lt;ruby-on-rails&gt;&lt;ruby&gt;&lt;soap&gt;" AnswerCount="10" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="44" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="40295" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-09-02T18:54:31.990" Score="8" ViewCount="4126" Body="&lt;p&gt;I regularly access Windows domains that have been set up to use a domain under the &lt;code&gt;.local&lt;/code&gt; top level name. This conflicts with Bonjour/Zeroconf which reserves &lt;code&gt;.local&lt;/code&gt; for it's own use. A number of platforms support Bonjour out of the box (including Mac OS, iPhone, and Ubuntu) and there's numerous name resolution issues when this confict occurs.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I have a manual (per workstation) workaround in place for Mac OS by creating an &lt;code&gt;/etc/resolver/ntdomain.local&lt;/code&gt; as per &lt;a href=&quot;https://web.archive.org/web/20081010191427/http://developer.apple.com/documentation/Darwin/Reference/ManPages/man5/resolver.5.html&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;&lt;code&gt;resolver(5)&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt; which works well. Unfortunately this requires manual changes on every workstation and does not work on the iPhone.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What I'm looking for is a way to redirect requests for &lt;code&gt;*.ntdomain.local&lt;/code&gt; coming in via mDNS to a specific unicast DNS server. I don't mind writing some code if required. I can deploy on either preferably Debian or alternatively Windows 2003. It looks like &lt;a href=&quot;http://avahi.org/&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;Avahi&lt;/a&gt; may be the library I'm looking for.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Can this be done without registering every address in the subdomain or is it possible to register a single NS record of &lt;code&gt;ntdomain.local&lt;/code&gt; that points to the Windows DNS server?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3736" OwnerDisplayName="Jason W" LastEditorUserId="6761181" LastEditorDisplayName="Orion Edwards" LastEditDate="2017-09-13T06:13:51.667" LastActivityDate="2017-09-13T07:13:04.070" Title="Redirecting &quot;.local&quot; subdomain to unicast DNS" Tags="&lt;dns&gt;&lt;bonjour&gt;&lt;zeroconf&gt;&lt;mdns&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="3545559" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3546155" CreationDate="2010-08-23T07:40:51.077" Score="4" ViewCount="2392" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am using a Pentium 4 HT machine at office for running R, some of the code requires plyr package, which I usually need to wait for 6-7 minutes for the script to finish running, while I saw my processor is only half utilized.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I have heard of using Multicore package in R for better utilizing the multicore processor, is my case suitable for this?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Thanks!&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="373908" LastActivityDate="2010-08-23T16:28:25.080" Title="Using Multicore in R for a pentium 4 HT machine" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;multicore&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="40302" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="40321" CreationDate="2008-09-02T18:56:36.837" Score="4" ViewCount="2549" Body="&lt;p&gt;Let's say I'm building a data access layer for an application.  Typically I have a class definition for a each kind of object that is stored in the database.  Of course, the actual data access retrieves data in the form of a datareader, typed or untyped dataset, or similar, usually with the data needed to create one object per row in the results.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How would you go about creating your object instances in the data layer?  Would have a constructor that accepts a datarow?  If so, how would you make that type-safe?  Or would you have your constructor list out one parameter for each field you want to instantiate, even if there could be many fields?  Would you mark this constructor 'internal'?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3043" OwnerDisplayName="Joel Coehoorn" LastEditorUserId="3043" LastEditorDisplayName="Joel Coehoorn" LastEditDate="2008-09-02T19:02:52.873" LastActivityDate="2014-07-11T12:09:11.007" Title="Create object from database row" Tags="&lt;.net&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="40311" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="40327" CreationDate="2008-09-02T19:01:34.950" Score="5" ViewCount="4543" Body="&lt;p&gt;I added a custom install action to my installer to add one of my installation directories to the System PATH environment variable. After I run the installer, the PATH variable reflects the changes (when I access it through the Control Panel::System applet), but when I start a new command shell, the PATH variable does not reflect the changes. Is there something I'm failing to do, that causes this?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3114" OwnerDisplayName="Brian" LastEditorUserId="14444" LastEditorDisplayName="moocha" LastEditDate="2008-10-04T16:55:52.130" LastActivityDate="2008-11-19T18:57:59.320" Title="Installer changes PATH variable, changes don't show up in Command Shell" Tags="&lt;windows-installer&gt;&lt;environment&gt;&lt;path&gt;&lt;custom-action&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="40317" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="40320" CreationDate="2008-09-02T19:04:44.290" Score="163" ViewCount="381384" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have an NFS-mounted directory on a Linux machine that has hung. I've tried to force an unmount, but it doesn't seem to work:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;$ umount -f /mnt/data&#xA;$ umount2: Device or resource busy&#xA;$ umount: /mnt/data: device is busy&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If I type &quot;&lt;code&gt;mount&lt;/code&gt;&quot;, it appears that the directory is no longer mounted, but it hangs if I do &quot;&lt;code&gt;ls /mnt/data&lt;/code&gt;&quot;, and if I try to remove the mountpoint, I get:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;$ rmdir /mnt/data&#xA;rmdir: /mnt/data: Device or resource busy&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there anything I can do other than reboot the machine?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="742" OwnerDisplayName="lorin" LastEditorUserId="149841" LastEditDate="2012-01-27T02:53:26.467" LastActivityDate="2016-08-03T02:18:53.810" Title="Force unmount of NFS-mounted directory" Tags="&lt;linux&gt;&lt;mount&gt;&lt;nfs&gt;&lt;umount&gt;&lt;nfsclient&gt;" AnswerCount="6" CommentCount="5" FavoriteCount="77" ClosedDate="2013-07-18T05:58:39.750" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="40322" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="41132" CreationDate="2008-09-02T19:06:09.980" Score="14" ViewCount="23856" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am a member of all the roles (Browser, Content Manager, My Reports, Publisher, Report Builder). If I login with a Local Administrator account, I can see and use it fine. Any ideas?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1287" OwnerDisplayName="vitule" LastEditorUserId="1287" LastEditorDisplayName="vitule" LastEditDate="2008-09-03T15:38:13.933" LastActivityDate="2013-08-07T10:53:45.997" Title="I can't see &quot;Report Builder&quot; button in the Report Manager on SQL Server 2005" Tags="&lt;sql-server&gt;&lt;reporting-services&gt;&lt;reportbuilder&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="40335" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-09-02T19:14:23.267" Score="11" ViewCount="122657" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm need to find a method to programmatically determine which disk drive Windows is using to boot. In other words, I need a way from Windows to determine which drive the BIOS is using to boot the whole system. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Does Windows expose an interface to discover this? With how big the Windows API is, I'm hoping there is something buried in there that might do the trick.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Terry&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;p.s. Just reading the first sectors of the hard disk isn't reveling anything. On my dev box I have two hard disks, and when I look at the contents of the first couple of sectors on either of the hard disks I have a standard boiler plate MBR.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Edit to clarify a few things.&#xA;The way I want to identify the device is with a string which will identify a physical disk drive (as opposed to a logical disk drive). Physical disk drives are of the form &quot;\\.\PHYSICALDRIVEx&quot; where x is a number. On the other hand, a logical drive is identified by a string of the form, &quot;\\.\x&quot; where x is a drive letter.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Edit to discuss a few of the ideas that were thrown out.&#xA;Knowing which logical volume Windows used to boot doesn't help me here. Here is the reason. Assume that C: is using a mirrored RAID setup. Now, that means we have at least two physical drives. Now, I get the mapping from Logical Drive to Physical Drive and I discover that there are two physical drives used by that volume. Which one did Windows use to boot? Of course, this is assuming that the physical drive Windows used to boot is the same physical drive that contains the MBR. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2171" LastEditorUserId="2171" LastEditDate="2008-09-02T20:31:00.353" LastActivityDate="2021-06-15T07:16:37.473" Title="How to tell which disk Windows Used to Boot" Tags="&lt;windows&gt;&lt;mbr&gt;" AnswerCount="10" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="5" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="40344" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="47361" CreationDate="2008-09-02T19:17:27.097" Score="5" ViewCount="1220" Body="&lt;p&gt;We have a SQL server database. To manipulate the data non-programmatically, I can use SQL Server Management Studio by right-clicking a table and selecting &quot;Open Table&quot;. However this is slow for very large tables and sorting and filtering is cumbersome.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Typically what we have done until now is to create an Access database containing linked tables which point to the SQL Server tables and views. Opening a large table is much faster this way, and Access has easy-to-use right-click filtering and sorting.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;However, since Access 2007, sorting in particular has been quite slow when working with large tables. The Access database can also inadvertently lock the database tables, blocking other processes that may need to access the data. Creating the Access database in the first place, and updating it when new tables are added to SQL Server, is also tedious.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there a better way to work with the data that offers the usability of Access without its drawbacks?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3012" OwnerDisplayName="Phillip Wells" LastEditorUserId="3043" LastEditorDisplayName="Joel Coehoorn" LastEditDate="2008-10-24T17:21:08.940" LastActivityDate="2009-03-10T04:19:44.183" Title="What's the best way to work with SQL Server data non-programmatically?" Tags="&lt;sql-server&gt;&lt;ms-access&gt;&lt;ssms&gt;" AnswerCount="6" CommentCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
