  <row Id="3548263" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3552084" CreationDate="2010-08-23T14:00:46.123" Score="12" ViewCount="2662" Body="&lt;p&gt;Say I have an R list like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; summary(data.list)&#xA;                                 Length Class      Mode&#xA;aug9104AP                        18     data.frame list&#xA;Aug17-10_acon_7pt_dil_series_01  18     data.frame list&#xA;Aug17-10_Picro_7pt_dil_series_01 18     data.frame list&#xA;Aug17-10_PTZ_7pt_dil_series_01   18     data.frame list&#xA;Aug17-10_Verat_7pt_dil_series_01 18     data.frame list&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I want to process each data.frame in the list using &lt;code&gt;l_ply&lt;/code&gt;, but I also need the name (e.g. aug9104AP) to be passed into the processing function along with the data.frame.  Something like:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;l_ply(data.list,function(df,...) {&#xA;&#xA;    cli.name&amp;lt;- arg_to_access_current_list_item_name&#xA;&#xA;    #make plots with df, use cli.name in plot titles&#xA;    #save results in a file called cli.name&#xA;&#xA;  }, arg_to_access_current_list_item_name&#xA;)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What should &lt;code&gt;arg_to_access_current_list_item_name&lt;/code&gt; be?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="128245" LastActivityDate="2010-08-25T00:28:30.817" Title="l_ply: how to pass the list's name attribute into the function?" Tags="&lt;list&gt;&lt;r&gt;&lt;plyr&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="3" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="40480" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-09-02T20:14:29.270" Score="7355" ViewCount="2379480" Body="&lt;p&gt;I always thought Java uses &lt;strong&gt;pass-by-reference&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;However, I've seen &lt;a href=&quot;http://javadude.com/articles/passbyvalue.htm&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;a blog post&lt;/a&gt; that claims that Java uses &lt;strong&gt;pass-by-value&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I don't think I understand the distinction they're making.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What is the explanation?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="4315" LastEditorUserId="4267244" LastEditorDisplayName="user4642421" LastEditDate="2021-09-05T13:31:34.037" LastActivityDate="2022-01-22T11:49:55.263" Title="Is Java &quot;pass-by-reference&quot; or &quot;pass-by-value&quot;?" Tags="&lt;java&gt;&lt;methods&gt;&lt;parameter-passing&gt;&lt;pass-by-reference&gt;&lt;pass-by-value&gt;" AnswerCount="92" CommentCount="12" FavoriteCount="3326" CommunityOwnedDate="2012-09-14T18:22:55.293" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="3548600" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3548854" CreationDate="2010-08-23T14:39:41.520" Score="0" ViewCount="342" Body="&lt;p&gt;First I read-in a csv and create an xts object.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;require(quantmod)&#xA;&#xA;sugar  &amp;lt;- as.xts(read.zoo(&quot;SUGAR.CSV&quot;, sep=&quot;,&quot;, format =&quot;%m/%d/%Y&quot;, header=TRUE))&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Then I create a new series of RSI values using TTR (loads with quantmod)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;sugarRSI &amp;lt;- RSI(sugar)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Now I'd like to get a new series that only includes the value of the last day of each month. There is a last() function in xts, but not clear on how to deploy it efficiently. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="296155" LastEditorUserId="271616" LastEditDate="2011-08-13T17:54:28.230" LastActivityDate="2011-08-13T17:54:28.230" Title="HT index RSI value at end of each month in an xts time series object" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;xts&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="40485" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-09-02T20:15:13.527" Score="42" ViewCount="41923" Body="&lt;p&gt;To experiment, I've (long ago) implemented Conway's &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conway&amp;#39;s_Game_of_Life&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;Game of Life&lt;/a&gt; (and I'm aware of &lt;a href=&quot;https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1823/writing-a-conways-game-of-life-program&quot;&gt;this&lt;/a&gt; related question!).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;My implementation worked by keeping 2 arrays of booleans, representing the 'last state', and the 'state being updated' (the 2 arrays being swapped at each iteration). While this is reasonably fast, I've often wondered about how to optimize this.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;One idea, for example, would be to precompute at iteration N the zones that &lt;em&gt;could&lt;/em&gt; be modified at iteration (N+1) (so that if a cell does not belong to such a zone, it won't even be considered for modification at iteration (N+1)). I'm aware that this is very vague, and I never took time to go into the details...&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Do you have any ideas (or experience!) of how to go about optimizing (for speed) Game of Life iterations?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2638" OwnerDisplayName="OysterD" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditDate="2017-05-23T11:54:26.267" LastActivityDate="2021-11-26T13:56:41.710" Title="Optimizing Conway's 'Game of Life'" Tags="&lt;algorithm&gt;&lt;performance&gt;&lt;language-agnostic&gt;&lt;conways-game-of-life&gt;" AnswerCount="12" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="24" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
