  <row Id="3558592" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3559850" CreationDate="2010-08-24T16:18:28.767" Score="2" ViewCount="1796" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am having trouble finding clear documentation on how to set up a batch file for a Sweave document on Windows XP.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I am using the batch  files that are found &lt;a href=&quot;http://cran.r-project.org/contrib/extra/batchfiles/&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I have created a batch file names &lt;code&gt;run.bat&lt;/code&gt; which contains the following:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;Sweave myFile.Rnw&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The first thing I do in my Sweave file after setting the wd is read in a dataset using the &lt;code&gt;RODBC&lt;/code&gt; package:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;library(RODBC)&#xA;fetch &amp;lt;- odbcConnect(&quot;myDatabase&quot;)&#xA;myData &amp;lt;- &quot;select * from myTable&quot;&#xA;x &amp;lt;- sqlQuery(fetch, myData)&#xA;odbcCloseAll()&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;When I run my batch file, I receive the following error:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;Error: chunk 2&#xA;Error in library(RODBC) : there is no package called RODBC&#xA;In addition: Warning message:&#xA;In library(package, lib.loc = lib.loc, character.only = TRUE, logical.return = TRUE, :&#xA;    there is no package called 'xtable'&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Obviously these packages do exist and are functional, but something isn't right in the permissions or it isn't finding the right directories. Any thoughts?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Related question &lt;a href=&quot;https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1188544/getting-windows-to-start-r-in-batch-mode-using-the-start-command&quot;&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="415635" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditDate="2017-05-23T12:19:50.280" LastActivityDate="2010-08-24T18:40:35.183" Title="Windows batch R Sweave error loading packages" Tags="&lt;windows&gt;&lt;r&gt;&lt;batch-file&gt;&lt;sweave&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="40703" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="40714" CreationDate="2008-09-02T21:19:39.627" Score="159" ViewCount="70315" Body="&lt;p&gt;It will be important for developers wanting to develop for the chrome browser to be able to review existing bugs (to avoid too much pulling-out of hair), and to add new ones (to improve the thing).  Yet I can't seem to find the bug tracking for this project.  It &lt;em&gt;is&lt;/em&gt; open source, right?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1190" OwnerDisplayName="Gaius" LastEditorUserId="1037" LastEditorDisplayName="onnodb" LastEditDate="2008-09-03T09:11:21.507" LastActivityDate="2020-04-08T22:59:24.113" Title="Where can I find and submit bug reports on Google's Chrome browser?" Tags="&lt;google-chrome&gt;&lt;bug-tracking&gt;" AnswerCount="8" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="18" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="40705" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="40715" CreationDate="2008-09-02T21:21:14.383" Score="13" ViewCount="4692" Body="&lt;p&gt;At the beginning of all my executable Python scripts I put the &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shebang_(Unix)&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;shebang&lt;/a&gt; line:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;#!/usr/bin/env python&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I'm running these scripts on a system where &lt;code&gt;env python&lt;/code&gt; yields a Python 2.2 environment. My scripts quickly fail because I have a manual check for a compatible Python version:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;if sys.version_info &amp;lt; (2, 4):&#xA;    raise ImportError(&quot;Cannot run with Python version &amp;lt; 2.4&quot;)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I don't want to have to change the shebang line on every executable file, if it's possible; however, I don't have administrative access to the machine to change the result of &lt;code&gt;env python&lt;/code&gt; and I don't want to force a particular version, as in:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;#!/usr/bin/env python2.4&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I'd like to avoid this because system may have a newer version than Python 2.4, or may have Python 2.5 but no Python 2.4.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What's the elegant solution?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;[Edit:] I wasn't specific enough in posing the question -- I'd like to let users execute the scripts without manual configuration (e.g. path alteration or symlinking in &lt;code&gt;~/bin&lt;/code&gt; and ensuring your PATH has &lt;code&gt;~/bin&lt;/code&gt; before the Python 2.2 path). Maybe some distribution utility is required to prevent the manual tweaks?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3594" OwnerDisplayName="cdleary" LastEditorUserId="410" LastEditorDisplayName="cynicalman" LastEditDate="2008-09-25T02:19:15.280" LastActivityDate="2013-10-04T18:27:17.367" Title="Python deployment and /usr/bin/env portability" Tags="&lt;python&gt;&lt;executable&gt;&lt;environment&gt;&lt;shebang&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="40716" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-09-02T21:25:49.753" Score="3" ViewCount="1870" Body="&lt;p&gt;Although the general case is undecidable, many people still do solve problems that are equivilent well enough for day to day use.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In cohen's phd thesis on computer viruses, he showed how virus scanning is equivilent to the halting problem, yet we have an entire industry based around this challenge.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I also have seen microsoft's terminator project -  &lt;a href=&quot;http://research.microsoft.com/Terminator/&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;http://research.microsoft.com/Terminator/&lt;/a&gt; &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Which leads me to ask - is the halting problem overrated - do we need to worry about the general case?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Will types become turing complete over time - dependant types do seem like a good development?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Or, to look the other way, will we begin to use non turing complete languages to gain the benefits of static analysis ?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="4319" OwnerDisplayName="1729" LastEditorUserId="792066" LastEditorDisplayName="Mike Stone" LastEditDate="2021-11-02T17:04:22.057" LastActivityDate="2021-11-02T17:04:22.057" Title="Is solving the halting problem easier than people think?" Tags="&lt;language-agnostic&gt;" AnswerCount="8" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
