  <row Id="3605364" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3605654" CreationDate="2010-08-31T01:05:50.383" Score="1" ViewCount="195" Body="&lt;p&gt;I've been put in charge of automating the graph production at work and need a specific way of displaying the scale on our bar plots. currently what we have is i.imgur.com/bWjk9.png&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;and they would like the value of each of the scales listed below. When i change the scale it comes out like this.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;i.imgur.com/1D9up.png     &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;And the way they want it is like the way don't know (9) is displayed&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Here's my code for the graph which looks up the scale elsewhere using barplot2&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;plot.graph &amp;lt;- function(data, px, py, scale_lab){&#xA;  par(mar=c(5.1, 4, 1, 2))&#xA;  #Plotting the Barplot&#xA; barplot2(summary(data),&#xA;  #main = strwrap(heading, width = 50),&#xA;  xlab = &quot;Response&quot;,&#xA;  ylab = &quot;Number of Responses&quot;,&#xA;  prcol = &quot;#FAF4E6&quot;,&#xA;  col = &quot;#800000&quot;,&#xA;  space = 1.5,&#xA;  ylim = c(0, max(summary(data))+2),&#xA;  names.arg = sapply(scale_lab,wordwrap,USE.NAMES=TRUE),&#xA;&#xA;  plot.grid = TRUE)&#xA; points(px, py, pch=21, cex=4, col=&quot;black&quot;, bg=&quot;yellow&quot;, lwd=1)&#xA; par(mar=c(5, 4, 4, 2) + 0.1)&#xA; }&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Any help would be greatly appreciated.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Cameron.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="435473" LastActivityDate="2010-08-31T02:28:10.460" Title="How to define next line in a list for label" Tags="&lt;r&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="41715" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="190743" CreationDate="2008-09-03T13:54:39.643" Score="1" ViewCount="3371" Body="&lt;p&gt;Right now I'm working with an ASP.NET website that automatically generates images and stores them in a temporary folder. When working on my local system these go going into a temporary folder that gets picked up by Visual Source Safe which then wants to check them in. As such, I am wondering if there is a way to just exclude that particular folder from source control?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I've done a bit of reading and found that there are ways to do this for &lt;a href=&quot;http://forums.msdn.microsoft.com/en-US/vssourcecontrol/thread/6cc4aab0-e7bc-44e8-baa3-045c9cd82e9a/&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;individual files&lt;/a&gt;, but I haven't found anything yet about an entire folder.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1185" OwnerDisplayName="Rob" LastEditorUserId="2354564" LastEditorDisplayName="Rob" LastEditDate="2014-07-14T11:50:01.623" LastActivityDate="2014-07-14T11:51:24.047" Title="Excluding a folder from source control in an ASP.NET website?" Tags="&lt;asp.net&gt;&lt;visual-sourcesafe&gt;" AnswerCount="6" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="41717" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="41722" CreationDate="2008-09-03T13:55:15.257" Score="11" ViewCount="4729" Body="&lt;p&gt;Like many others on this site I am considering a move to ASP.NET MVC for future projects.  Currently my sites are running the traditional ASP.NET 2.0 Web Forms, and it works OK for us, so my other option is just to stick with what I know and make the move to ASP.NET 3.5 with the integrated AJAX stuff.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I'm wondering about how user controls work in ASP.NET MVC. We have tons of &lt;code&gt;.ASCX&lt;/code&gt; controls, and a few composite controls.  When I work with web designers it is very easy to get them to use ASCX controls effectively, even without any programming knowledge, so that's a definite plus.  But then of course the downsides are the page life cycle, which can be maddening, and the fact that ASCX controls are hard to share between different projects.  Composite controls are share-able, but basically a black box to a designer.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What's the model in ASP.NET MVC?  Is there a way to create controls that solves the problems we've dealt with using ASCX and composite controls?  Allowing easy access for web designers without having to worry about code being broken is an important consideration.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1219" OwnerDisplayName="Eric Z Beard" LastEditorUserId="2354564" LastEditDate="2014-07-14T11:44:59.963" LastActivityDate="2014-07-14T11:48:23.853" Title="What's the best way to implement user controls in ASP.NET MVC?" Tags="&lt;asp.net-mvc&gt;&lt;user-controls&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="3" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="41724" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="41743" CreationDate="2008-09-03T14:00:51.623" Score="24" ViewCount="4996" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm hearing more and more about domain specific languages being thrown about and how they change the way you treat business logic, and I've seen &lt;a href=&quot;http://ayende.com/blog/tags/domain-specific-languages&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;Ayende's blog posts&lt;/a&gt; and things, but I've never really gotten exactly why I would take my business logic away from the methods and situations I'm using in my provider.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If you've got some background using these things, any chance you could put it in real laymans terms:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;What exactly building DSLs means?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;What languages are you using?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Where using a DSL makes sense?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;What is the benefit of using DSLs?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3717" OwnerDisplayName="crucible" LastEditorUserId="11343" LastEditDate="2011-10-20T08:58:59.190" LastActivityDate="2014-07-14T11:43:16.833" Title="What is a DSL and where should I use it?" Tags="&lt;theory&gt;&lt;dsl&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="7" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
