  <row Id="41781" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-09-03T14:25:04.110" Score="21" ViewCount="44654" Body="&lt;p&gt;I've worked with T-SQL for years but I've just moved to an organisation that is going to require writing some Oracle stuff, probably just simple CRUD operations at least until I find my feet. I'm not going to be migrating databases from one to the other simply interacting with existing Oracle databases from an Application Development perspective. Is there are tool or utility available to easily translate T-SQL into Oracle SQL, a keyword mapper is the sort of thing I'm looking for.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;P.S. I'm too lazy to RTFM, besides it's not going to be a big part of my role so I just want something to get me up to speed a little faster.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="4403" LastEditorUserId="330315" LastEditorDisplayName="Michael Prewecki" LastEditDate="2018-10-09T16:32:18.257" LastActivityDate="2020-12-29T15:31:44.390" Title="Microsoft T-SQL to Oracle SQL translation" Tags="&lt;sql&gt;&lt;oracle&gt;&lt;tsql&gt;" AnswerCount="12" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="11" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="41785" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-09-03T14:25:57.757" Score="31" ViewCount="8791" Body="&lt;p&gt;I've been wanting to play around with writing my own language for a while now (ostensibly for the learning experience) and as such need to be relatively grounded in the construction of Parsers, Interpreters, and Compilers. So:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Does anyone know of any good resources on constructing Parsers, Interpreters, and Compilers?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;EDIT: I'm not looking for compiler-compilers/parser-compilers such as Lex, Yacc and Bison...&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="145" OwnerDisplayName="akdom" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditDate="2008-09-03T16:13:09.123" LastActivityDate="2013-06-25T02:52:57.473" Title="Learning Resources on Parsers, Interpreters, and Compilers" Tags="&lt;compiler-construction&gt;&lt;interpreter&gt;&lt;parsing&gt;&lt;compiler-theory&gt;&lt;language-theory&gt;" AnswerCount="12" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="40" ClosedDate="2013-11-27T09:35:50.230" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="3606697" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3606798" CreationDate="2010-08-31T07:03:24.510" Score="402" ViewCount="647313" Body="&lt;p&gt;I plot the following:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;library(ggplot2)    &#xA;&#xA;carrots &amp;lt;- data.frame(length = rnorm(500000, 10000, 10000))&#xA;cukes &amp;lt;- data.frame(length = rnorm(50000, 10000, 20000))&#xA;carrots$veg &amp;lt;- 'carrot'&#xA;cukes$veg &amp;lt;- 'cuke'&#xA;vegLengths &amp;lt;- rbind(carrots, cukes)&#xA;&#xA;ggplot(vegLengths, aes(length, fill = veg)) +&#xA; geom_density(alpha = 0.2)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Now say, I only want to plot the region between &lt;code&gt;x=-5000&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;5000&lt;/code&gt;, instead of the entire range.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How can I do that?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="377031" LastEditorUserId="15293191" LastEditDate="2022-01-05T21:27:31.477" LastActivityDate="2022-01-05T21:27:31.477" Title="How to set limits for axes in ggplot2 R plots?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;plot&gt;&lt;ggplot2&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="105" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="41792" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="41820" CreationDate="2008-09-03T14:28:57.423" Score="5" ViewCount="3950" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am re-factoring some code and am wondering about the use of a &lt;code&gt;lock&lt;/code&gt; in the instance constructor.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;public class MyClass {&#xA;&#xA;    private static Int32 counter = 0;&#xA;    private Int32 myCount;&#xA;&#xA;    public MyClass() {&#xA;&#xA;        lock(this) {&#xA;            counter++;&#xA;            myCount = counter;&#xA;        }&#xA;    }&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Please confirm&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Instance constructors are thread-safe.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;The lock statement prevents access to that code block, not to the static 'counter' member.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If the intent of the original programmer were to have each instance know its 'count', how would I synchronize access to the 'counter' member to ensure that another thread isn't new'ing a &lt;code&gt;MyClass&lt;/code&gt; and changing the count before this one sets its count?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;FYI - This class is not a singleton. Instances must simply be aware of their number.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3619" OwnerDisplayName="ajmastrean" LastEditorUserId="3641067" LastEditorDisplayName="ajmastrean" LastEditDate="2017-12-14T03:09:12.067" LastActivityDate="2017-12-14T03:11:10.570" Title="Instance constructor sets a static member, is it thread safe?" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;.net&gt;&lt;multithreading&gt;&lt;thread-safety&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
