  <row Id="42182" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="42195" CreationDate="2008-09-03T17:41:06.430" Score="92" ViewCount="85492" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm trying to write a blog post which includes a code segment inside a &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;pre&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; tag. The code segment includes a generic type and uses &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; to define that type. This is what the segment looks like:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;pre&amp;gt;&#xA;    PrimeCalc calc = new PrimeCalc();&#xA;    Func&amp;lt;int, int&amp;gt; del = calc.GetNextPrime;&#xA;&amp;lt;/pre&amp;gt;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The resulting HTML removes the &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; and ends up like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;PrimeCalc calc = new PrimeCalc();&#xA;Func del = calc.GetNextPrime;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How do I escape the &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; so they show up in the HTML?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="373" OwnerDisplayName="urini" LastEditorUserId="1016716" LastEditDate="2016-09-22T18:01:40.873" LastActivityDate="2019-12-04T21:00:15.243" Title="How to escape &lt; and &gt; inside &lt;pre&gt; tags" Tags="&lt;html&gt;&lt;escaping&gt;&lt;markup&gt;&lt;blogger&gt;" AnswerCount="8" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="15" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="42187" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="43557" CreationDate="2008-09-03T17:42:41.967" Score="18" ViewCount="8023" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have read about partial methods in the latest &lt;a href=&quot;http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/vcsharp/aa336809.aspx&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;C# language specification&lt;/a&gt;, so I understand the principles, but I'm wondering how people are actually using them.  Is there a particular design pattern that benefits from partial methods?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1219" OwnerDisplayName="Eric Z Beard" LastEditorUserId="905" LastEditorDisplayName="Keith" LastEditDate="2008-09-04T12:00:35.040" LastActivityDate="2010-05-24T05:18:39.810" Title="How are partial methods used in C# 3.0?" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;design-patterns&gt;&lt;.net-3.5&gt;&lt;partial-methods&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="4" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="3621963" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3622255" CreationDate="2010-09-01T20:35:18.087" Score="24" ViewCount="21118" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm trying to group one variable of my data by another and then plot a line through the means. It works fine when both variables are numbers. However, I'm having a problem when the grouping variable is a factor. I have a mock up below. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;x &amp;lt;- sample(1:3, 40, replace=T)&#xA;y &amp;lt;- rnorm(40)&#xA;&#xA;df1 &amp;lt;- data.frame(x, y)&#xA;qplot(x, y, data=df1) + stat_summary(fun.y=mean, colour=&quot;red&quot;, geom=&quot;line&quot;)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://i.stack.imgur.com/MvrbB.png&quot; alt=&quot;alt text&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This is looks great. However if the x variable is a factor I don't get the line. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;df2 &amp;lt;- data.frame(x=as.factor(x), y)&#xA;qplot(x, y, data=df2) + stat_summary(fun.y=mean, colour=&quot;red&quot;, geom=&quot;line&quot;)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://i.stack.imgur.com/Sanw8.png&quot; alt=&quot;alt text&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there something I can do to get this line displayed?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Ps. geom=&quot;point&quot; works but not geom=&quot;line&quot;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;qplot(x, y, data=df2) + stat_summary(fun.y=mean, colour=&quot;red&quot;, geom=&quot;point&quot;)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://i.stack.imgur.com/7RoA5.png&quot; alt=&quot;alt text&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="342362" LastEditorUserId="5325862" LastEditDate="2019-12-27T16:53:26.707" LastActivityDate="2019-12-27T16:53:26.707" Title="ggplot2: line connecting the means of grouped data" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;ggplot2&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="10" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="42197" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="42285" CreationDate="2008-09-03T17:46:21.363" Score="28" ViewCount="74767" Body="&lt;p&gt;The company I work for makes hardware that communicates to the computer though a serial port. Third party companies write software that communicates with our hardware. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;There are times when I need to diagnose our hardware. However, a third party software app connects to the serial port when Windows starts up, blocking any other connection. I don't know the name of this application/service and it's not always the same one.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there any way to either:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Find the name/pid of the app/service that is currently using a given serial port or&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Steal the serial port connection from another app.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;vb.net preferably, but I'll take a language agnostic answer as well.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="30" OwnerDisplayName="Grant" LastActivityDate="2012-08-27T17:50:42.307" Title="How do I determine which process is using a serial port?" Tags="&lt;vb.net&gt;&lt;serial-port&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="4" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="42200" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-09-03T17:46:50.350" Score="7" ViewCount="2574" Body="&lt;p&gt;This question is addressed to a degree in this question on &lt;a href=&quot;https://stackoverflow.com/questions/33664/best-practices-for-managing-linq-to-sql-dbml-files&quot;&gt;LINQ to SQL .dbml best practices&lt;/a&gt;, but I am not sure how to add to a question.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;One of our applications uses LINQ to SQL and we have currently have one .dbml file for the entire database which is becoming difficult to manage.  We are looking at refactoring it a bit into separate files that are more module/functionality specific, but one problem is that many of the high level classes would have to be duplicated in several .dbml files as the associations can't be used across .dbml files (as far as I know), with the additional partial class code as well.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Has anyone grappled with this problem and what recommendations would you make?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="4299" OwnerDisplayName="Scott Nichols" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditDate="2017-05-23T12:13:33.373" LastActivityDate="2013-08-24T16:06:08.970" Title="Managing LINQ to SQL .dbml model complexity" Tags="&lt;linq&gt;&lt;linq-to-sql&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
