  <row Id="3638773" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3638931" CreationDate="2010-09-03T19:03:55.240" Score="12" ViewCount="14886" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm trying to output 6 figures into one image, in a 3x2 layout. I'd like to place extra space between the top row and the bottom two rows.  Is this possible using R? I've looked through the documentation for par and plot and can't seem to find an appropriate option.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Here's some example code:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;a = rnorm(100,100,10)&#xA;b = rnorm(100,100,10)&#xA;&#xA;par(mfrow=c(3,2), oma=c(1,1,1,1), mar=c(2,2,2,2))&#xA;hist(a)&#xA;hist(b)&#xA;plot(a,b)&#xA;plot(a,b)&#xA;plot(a,b)&#xA;plot(a,b)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Here's what that code outputs:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://i.stack.imgur.com/coEMA.png&quot; alt=&quot;alt text&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Here's what I'd like it to output (I modified this image in an external editor). Note the extra space between the top row and bottom rows.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://i.stack.imgur.com/SyBtg.png&quot; alt=&quot;alt text&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="152159" LastEditorUserId="429846" LastEditDate="2013-01-02T09:49:06.540" LastActivityDate="2013-01-02T09:49:06.540" Title="Add extra spacing between a subset of plots" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;graphics&gt;&lt;plot&gt;&lt;spacing&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="42357" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="42651" CreationDate="2008-09-03T19:28:30.837" Score="8" ViewCount="4157" Body="&lt;p&gt;I've seen a few attempted SQL injection attacks on one of my web sites. It comes in the form of a query string that includes the &quot;cast&quot; keyword and a bunch of hex characters which when &quot;decoded&quot; are an injection of banner adverts into the DB.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;My solution is to scan the full URL (and params) and search for the presence of &quot;cast(0x&quot; and if it's there to redirect to a static page.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How do you check your URL's for SQL Injection attacks?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1463" OwnerDisplayName="Guy" LastActivityDate="2010-01-02T06:21:20.833" Title="How do you check your URL for SQL Injection Attacks?" Tags="&lt;sql&gt;&lt;sql-server&gt;" AnswerCount="10" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
