  <row Id="42437" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="42448" CreationDate="2008-09-03T19:55:05.500" Score="6" ViewCount="4375" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm writing a C# POS (point of sale) system that takes input from a keyboard wedge magcard reader. This means that any data it reads off of a mag stripe is entered as if it were typed on the keyboard very quickly. Currently I'm handling this by attaching to the KeyPress event and looking for a series of very fast key presses that contain the card swipe sentinel characters.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there a better way to deal with this sort of input? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Edit: The device does simply present the data as keystrokes and doesn't interface through some other driver. Also We use a wide range of these types of devices so ideally a method should work independent of the specific model of wedge being used. However if there is no other option I'll have to make do.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2191" OwnerDisplayName="Mykroft" LastEditorUserId="213269" LastEditorDisplayName="Mykroft" LastEditDate="2010-04-06T17:49:58.157" LastActivityDate="2010-04-06T17:49:58.157" Title="Best way to handle input from a keyboard &quot;wedge&quot;" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;point-of-sale&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="3" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
