  <row Id="42438" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-09-03T19:55:13.973" Score="5" ViewCount="7699" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have some code that uses SMO to populate a list of available SQL Servers and databases.  While we no longer support SQL Server 2000, it's possible that the code could get run on a machine that SQL Server 2000 and not have the SMO library installed.  I would perfer to check for SMO first and degrade the functionality gracefully instead of blowing up in the user's face.  What is best way to detect whether or not SMO is available on a machine?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Every example that I have seen through a quick Google scan was a variation of &quot;look for C:\Program Files\Microsoft SQL Server\90\SDK\Assemblies\Microsoft.SqlServer.Smo.dll&quot;.  The problem with that approach is that it only works with SQL Server 2005.  If SQL Server 2008 is the only SQL Server installed then the path will be different.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="206" OwnerDisplayName="Chris Miller" LastActivityDate="2016-01-13T15:30:49.907" Title="What's the best way to detect the presence of SMO?" Tags="&lt;sql-server&gt;&lt;smo&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
