  <row Id="42833" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="42838" CreationDate="2008-09-03T23:30:48.230" Score="28" ViewCount="4028" Body="&lt;p&gt;In the web-application I'm developing I currently use a naive solution when connecting to the database:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;Connection c = DriverManager.getConnection(&quot;url&quot;, &quot;username&quot;, &quot;password&quot;);&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This is pretty unsafe. If an attacker gains access to the sourcecode he also gains access to the database itself. How can my web-application connect to the database without storing the database-password in plaintext in the sourcecode?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="4464" OwnerDisplayName="runaros" LastEditorUserId="15168" LastEditorDisplayName="Dave L." LastEditDate="2015-05-03T02:56:37.950" LastActivityDate="2015-05-03T02:56:37.950" Title="How do I avoid having the database password stored in plaintext in sourcecode?" Tags="&lt;language-agnostic&gt;&lt;passwords&gt;&lt;database-connection&gt;" AnswerCount="6" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="3" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="3661302" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3662267" CreationDate="2010-09-07T17:57:27.553" Score="8" ViewCount="5963" Body="&lt;p&gt;In the statistics programming language R, the following formula (e.g. in &lt;code&gt;lm()&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;glm()&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;z ~ (x+y)^2  &#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;is equivalent to&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;z ~ x + y + x:y&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Assuming, I only have continuous predictors, is there a concise way to obtain&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;z ~ I(x^2) + I(y^2) + I(x) + I(y) + I(x*y)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;A formula that does the right thing for factor predictors is a plus.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;One possible solution is&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;z ~ (poly(x,2) + poly(y,2))^2&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I am looking for something more elegant.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="411038" LastEditorUserId="5436877" LastEditDate="2019-05-06T13:54:15.177" LastActivityDate="2019-05-06T13:54:15.177" Title="Formula for all first and second order predictors including interactions in R" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;formula&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="6" FavoriteCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
