  <row Id="43267" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-09-04T07:13:40.223" Score="9" ViewCount="2735" Body="&lt;p&gt;For certain programs nothing beats the command line. Unfortunately, I have never seen good documentation or examples on how to write console applications that go beyond &quot;Hello World&quot;.  I'm interested in making console apps like &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vim_(text_editor)&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;Vim&lt;/a&gt; or &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emacs&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;Emacs&lt;/a&gt;.  Well not exactly like Vim or Emacs but one that takes over the entire command prompt while it is in use and then after you exit it leaves no trace behind. I know that on Unix there is the &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curses_(programming_library)&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;curses library&lt;/a&gt; but for Windows? ...&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="4491" LastEditorUserId="63550" LastEditorDisplayName="minty" LastEditDate="2009-08-29T17:12:17.283" LastActivityDate="2015-08-30T06:40:09.920" Title="Good resources for writing console style applications for Windows?" Tags="&lt;windows&gt;&lt;command-line&gt;&lt;console&gt;&lt;cmd&gt;" AnswerCount="10" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="3" CommunityOwnedDate="2008-09-04T07:25:15.640" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="43289" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="8808245" CreationDate="2008-09-04T07:33:25.387" Score="601" ViewCount="374776" Body="&lt;p&gt;How can I do this fast?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Sure I can do this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;static bool ByteArrayCompare(byte[] a1, byte[] a2)&#xA;{&#xA;    if (a1.Length != a2.Length)&#xA;        return false;&#xA;&#xA;    for (int i=0; i&amp;lt;a1.Length; i++)&#xA;        if (a1[i]!=a2[i])&#xA;            return false;&#xA;&#xA;    return true;&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;But I'm looking for either a &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Standard_Libraries_%28CLI%29#Base_Class_Library&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;BCL&lt;/a&gt; function or some highly optimized proven way to do this.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;java.util.Arrays.equals((sbyte[])(Array)a1, (sbyte[])(Array)a2);&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;works nicely, but it doesn't look like that would work for x64.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Note my super-fast answer &lt;a href=&quot;https://stackoverflow.com/a/8808245/4489&quot;&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="4489" OwnerDisplayName="Hafthor" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditorDisplayName="Daniel Jennings" LastEditDate="2017-05-23T12:34:45.403" LastActivityDate="2021-12-26T17:44:56.390" Title="Comparing two byte arrays in .NET" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;.net&gt;&lt;arrays&gt;&lt;performance&gt;&lt;j#&gt;" AnswerCount="28" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="132" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
