  <row Id="43743" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="46200" CreationDate="2008-09-04T13:20:03.057" Score="102" ViewCount="30248" Body="&lt;p&gt;I found some wild remarks that ASP.NET MVC is 30x faster than ASP.NET WebForms. What real performance difference is there, has this been measured and what are the performance benefits.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This is to help me consider moving from ASP.NET WebForms to ASP.NET MVC.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="383" OwnerDisplayName="GateKiller" LastEditorUserId="727208" LastEditDate="2012-06-18T17:20:53.017" LastActivityDate="2018-09-18T12:34:27.700" Title="ASP.NET MVC Performance" Tags="&lt;asp.net&gt;&lt;asp.net-mvc&gt;&lt;performance&gt;&lt;webforms&gt;" AnswerCount="14" CommentCount="5" FavoriteCount="32" CommunityOwnedDate="2009-02-12T10:52:50.487" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="43764" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="79273" CreationDate="2008-09-04T13:31:53.590" Score="1" ViewCount="7320" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am working on a project converting a &quot;spreadsheet application&quot; to a database solution.  A macro was written that takes screen shots of each page and pastes them into a PowerPoint presentation.  Because of the nice formatting options in Excel, the presentation looks very pretty.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The problem I'm having is that I haven't ever seen an Access report that would be pretty enough to display to upper management.  I think the output still has to be a PowerPoint presentation.  It needs to look as close as possible to the original output.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I am currently trying to write some code to use a .pot (presentation template) and fill in the data programmatically.  Putting the data into a PowerPoint table has been tricky because the tables are not easy to manipulate.  For example, if a particular description is too long, I need to break into the next cell down (word-wrap isn't allowed because I can only have &lt;em&gt;n&lt;/em&gt; lines per page).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there a way to make an Access report pretty, am I headed down the right path, or should I just try to programmatically fill in the Excel spreadsheet and use the code that already exists there to produce the presentation?  (I'd still need to figure out how to know when to break a line when using a non-monospaced font, as the users are currently doing that manually when they enter the data in the spreadsheet)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Jason Z:&#xA;If I set it to wrap, and I already have &lt;em&gt;n&lt;/em&gt; lines, it would make &lt;em&gt;n&lt;/em&gt;+1 or 2 lines on the slide, which is unacceptable.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Dennis:&#xA;That article looks very good, I should be able to glean something from it.  Thanks!&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2878" OwnerDisplayName="Knobloch" LastEditorUserId="2878" LastEditorDisplayName="Knobloch" LastEditDate="2008-09-24T13:13:57.227" LastActivityDate="2011-03-24T17:28:32.223" Title="MS Access Reporting - can it be pretty?" Tags="&lt;ms-access&gt;&lt;reporting&gt;&lt;powerpoint&gt;" AnswerCount="10" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="3692988" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3693205" CreationDate="2010-09-11T22:52:04.580" Score="12" ViewCount="1583" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have used Stata and gVim on Windows for a while now. Recently I have switched to Linux, and I am planning to also change from Stata to R.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;A friend of mine is using R and Emacs ESS which seems to work perfect, however i'd rather like to keep using vim. I have installed the vim-r-plugin2, however, i can only send code to a seperate terminal running R. I would much rather split my screen into a buffer running R and one buffer with my .R file, and then send code from one to the other. With ESS in Emacs this seems to work, you can run a terminal/R in a buffer without a problem. I haven't found a way to make this work. The R plugin for vim uses screen, and the only way to open a buffer running a shell I could find is the Conque Shell plugin.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I know that unlike Emacs, Vim is designed to be a simple text editor. However, having R run in a buffer seems just so much more practical.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I hope my problem is understandable, please bear with me I only recently switched to Linux and know virtually nothing about programming besides statistics. Thanks!&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="278616" LastEditorUserId="967840" LastEditDate="2013-08-25T18:02:34.180" LastActivityDate="2013-08-25T18:02:34.180" Title="Running R inside a buffer in Vim" Tags="&lt;vim&gt;&lt;plugins&gt;&lt;shell&gt;&lt;r&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="43765" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="65992" CreationDate="2008-09-04T13:31:57.893" Score="24" ViewCount="5696" Body="&lt;p&gt;For my day job, I live in Emacs.  Utterly.  I also have become pretty dependent on CScope to help me find things in the code.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Normally, I have 2 windows in a split (C-x 3):&#xA;&lt;a href=&quot;http://bitthicket.com/files/emacs-2split.JPG&quot;&gt;alt text http://bitthicket.com/files/emacs-2split.JPG&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;And I use the right window for code buffers and the left window for the CScope search buffer.  When you do a CScope search and select a result, it automatically updates the right-side window to show the buffer referred to by the result.  This is all well and good, except that it causes me to lose my place in some other buffer that I was studying.  Sometimes this is no biggie, because [C-s u] gets me back to where I was.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What would be better, though, is to have 3 split windows like this ([C-x 2] in the left window):&#xA;&lt;a href=&quot;http://bitthicket.com/files/emacs-3split.jpg&quot;&gt;alt text http://bitthicket.com/files/emacs-3split.jpg&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;And have the bottom left window contain the CScope search buffer, and the top left window be the only buffer that CScope ever updates.  That way, I can see my CScope searches and navigate around the code without losing the buffer I'm focused on.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Anyone know how I can do that?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3279" OwnerDisplayName="Ben Collins" LastEditorUserId="692432" LastEditDate="2011-04-06T07:05:22.030" LastActivityDate="2011-04-06T07:25:17.883" Title="Pin Emacs buffers to windows (for cscope)" Tags="&lt;emacs&gt;&lt;cscope&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="16" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="43766" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-09-04T13:32:05.773" Score="2" ViewCount="225" Body="&lt;p&gt;We have a build box that uses CruiseControl.Net and has been building VB.Net and C# projects using msbuild.  All I have installed on the box as far as .Net is concerned is .Net 2.0 SDK (I'm trying to keep the box as clean as possible).  We are now trying to get a C++ app building on this box.  The problem we are running into is that the header files (e.g. windows.h) are not installed with the SDK.  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Do I have to install VS 2005 to get this to work?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Edit:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;As a couple people have answered, I had actually downloaded the 3.5 Platform SDK, but the applications built on this box MUST run on boxes that do not have 3.5 installed.  By installing the 3.5 SDK on my 2.0 build box, am I compromising my build box?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Edit:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I'm going to leave this as unanswered, but thought I would add that I went ahead and installed Visual Studio on the box and all is well.  I hate having to do that, but didn't want to run the risk of having a 3.5 SDK on my 2.0 build box.  I would still love to hear a better solution.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="4231" OwnerDisplayName="Mike Schall" LastEditorUserId="4231" LastEditorDisplayName="Mike Schall" LastEditDate="2008-09-12T00:20:25.060" LastActivityDate="2008-09-19T14:55:02.737" Title="VS.Net 2005 required on Build box with .Net 2.0 C++ Projects?" Tags="&lt;c++&gt;&lt;msbuild&gt;&lt;build-automation&gt;&lt;cruisecontrol.net&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="43768" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="43771" CreationDate="2008-09-04T13:32:36.130" Score="8" ViewCount="505" Body="&lt;p&gt;What is a good (and preferably simple) way to test the rendering performance of WPF custom controls?  I have several complex controls in which rendering performance is highly crucial.  I want to be able to make sure that I can have lots of them drawwing out in a designer with a minimal impact on performance.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="93" OwnerDisplayName="MojoFilter" LastEditorUserId="1196" LastEditorDisplayName="aku" LastEditDate="2008-09-04T13:35:02.583" LastActivityDate="2008-09-04T13:35:02.583" Title="WPF control performance" Tags="&lt;.net&gt;&lt;wpf&gt;&lt;performance&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="3693043" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3693048" CreationDate="2010-09-11T23:08:38.697" Score="2" ViewCount="4028" Body="&lt;p&gt;Imagine I have a data frame with data like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt; A | B | C&#xA;---+---+---&#xA; 1 | 2 | a&#xA; 1 | 2 | b&#xA; 5 | 5 | a&#xA; 5 | 5 | b&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I want to take only columns A and B, and I want to remove any rows that have become duplicates as a result of eliminating all other columns (that is, column C). So my desied result for the table above would be:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt; A | B&#xA;---+---&#xA; 1 | 2 &#xA; 5 | 5 &#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What is the best way to do this?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="125921" LastActivityDate="2010-09-11T23:09:57.140" Title="In R, how can I take a subset of columns of a data frame and then eliminate duplicate rows?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;duplicates&gt;&lt;subset&gt;&lt;dataframe&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
