  <row Id="44078" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="44124" CreationDate="2008-09-04T16:04:57.870" Score="27" ViewCount="18751" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am trying to write a regular expression to strip all HTML with the exception of links (the &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;a href&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;/a&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; tags respectively. It does not have to be 100% secure (I am not worried about injection attacks or anything as I am parsing content that has already been approved and published into a &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SWF&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;SWF&lt;/a&gt; movie).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The original &quot;strip tags&quot; regular expression I'm using was &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;(.|\n)+?&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;, and I tried to modify it to &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;([^a]|\n)+?&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;, but that of course will allow any tag that has an &lt;strong&gt;a&lt;/strong&gt; in it rather than one that has it in the beginning, with a space.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Not that it should really matter, but in case anyone cares to know I am writing this in &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ActionScript#ActionScript_3.0&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;ActionScript 3.0&lt;/a&gt; for a &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adobe_Flash&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;Flash&lt;/a&gt; movie.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1306" OwnerDisplayName="Jeff Winkworth" LastEditorUserId="63550" LastEditorDisplayName="Jeff Winkworth" LastEditDate="2009-12-28T21:15:13.097" LastActivityDate="2021-04-26T12:33:23.183" Title="Strip all HTML tags except links" Tags="&lt;html&gt;&lt;regex&gt;&lt;actionscript-3&gt;&lt;string&gt;&lt;tags&gt;" AnswerCount="6" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="12" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="3716764" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3716824" CreationDate="2010-09-15T10:31:17.060" Score="15" ViewCount="16848" Body="&lt;p&gt;Is it possible to reset an instance of R?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Eg. if I have used the commands&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;x &amp;lt;- 1:10&#xA;plot(x, -x)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;And thus polluted the system with the x variable. In this state can I then revert back to a clean state without shutting R down and launching it again?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="185475" LastActivityDate="2018-01-10T12:43:55.607" Title="Reset R instance" Tags="&lt;r&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="5" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="44080" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="44137" CreationDate="2008-09-04T16:05:57.930" Score="2" ViewCount="1226" Body="&lt;p&gt;We've been using &quot;Drip&quot; to try and identify why pages with UpdatePanels in them tend to use a lot of client-side memory.  With a page with a regular postback, we are seeing 0 leaks detected by Drip.  However, when we add an update panel to the mix, every single DOM object that is inside of the update panel appears to leak (according to Drip).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I am not certain is Drip is reliable enough to report these kinds of things - the reported leaks do seem to indicate Drip is modifying the page slightly.  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Does anyone have any experience with this?  Should I panic and stop using Microsoft Ajax?  I'm not above doubting Microsoft, but it seems fishy to me that it could be &lt;em&gt;this&lt;/em&gt; bad.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Also, if you know of a tool that is better than Drip, that would be helpful as well.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3244" LastEditorUserId="811" LastEditorDisplayName="Shog9" LastEditDate="2008-09-19T22:27:05.163" LastActivityDate="2009-03-30T17:18:52.630" Title="Does Microsoft ASP.NET Ajax Cause DOM Object Leaks?" Tags="&lt;asp.net&gt;&lt;dom&gt;&lt;asp.net-ajax&gt;&lt;memory-leaks&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="44084" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="44095" CreationDate="2008-09-04T16:06:48.957" Score="76" ViewCount="56761" Body="&lt;p&gt;That's it. If you want to document a function or a class, you put a string just after the definition. For instance:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;def foo():&#xA;    &quot;&quot;&quot;This function does nothing.&quot;&quot;&quot;&#xA;    pass&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;But what about a module? How can I document what a &lt;em&gt;file.py&lt;/em&gt; does?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1679" OwnerDisplayName="Auron" LastEditorUserId="1337" LastEditorDisplayName="Brad Gilbert" LastEditDate="2008-09-16T17:06:56.573" LastActivityDate="2019-08-21T21:24:19.503" Title="How do I document a module in Python?" Tags="&lt;python&gt;&lt;documentation&gt;&lt;python-module&gt;" AnswerCount="6" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="6" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="44087" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-09-04T16:08:43.947" Score="0" ViewCount="405" Body="&lt;p&gt;With an XML prolog like &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;? xml version=&quot;1.0&quot; encoding=&quot;iso-8859-1&quot;? &amp;gt;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;and a Doctype like &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;!DOCTYPE html PUBLIC &quot;-//W3C//DTD XHTML 1.0 Frameset//EN&quot;&#xA;   &quot;http://www.w3.org/TR/xhtml1/DTD/xhtml1-frameset.dtd&quot;&amp;gt;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I can get my page to render as expected. However, in IE7 the same page does not render correctly. (a span inside a div does not align vertically) Articles on the web suggest that XML prolog + doctype will throw IE6 into quirks mode. However &lt;a href=&quot;http://blogs.msdn.com/ie/archive/2005/09/15/467901.aspx&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;this article&lt;/a&gt; seems to suggest otherwise, although it does not mention the version (is it 6 or 7) it applies to, though the article is dated sep 2005 which makes me believe it applies to IE6&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Does XML Prolog + doc type throw IE6 into quirks mode? What about IE7? Any recommendations on for or against using the prolog + doctype?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3635" OwnerDisplayName="rams" LastEditorUserId="2581872" LastEditorDisplayName="rams" LastEditDate="2014-12-15T22:04:01.090" LastActivityDate="2014-12-15T22:04:01.090" Title="IE6 and XML prolog" Tags="&lt;internet-explorer-6&gt;&lt;standards&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="44089" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="44676" CreationDate="2008-09-04T16:09:20.777" Score="16" ViewCount="66375" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have connected to a server via SFTP using FileZilla and accepted adding the server's SSH key to the key cache in FileZilla.  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How can I extract this cached key to a keyfile so that may use it through other SFTP applications that require a keyfile be made available?  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I have not been able to find anything in the FileZilla documentation related to this.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="4311" OwnerDisplayName="Dougman" LastActivityDate="2018-04-16T14:32:29.797" Title="How To Extract SFTP SSH Key From Key Cache in FileZilla FTP Client" Tags="&lt;ftp&gt;&lt;sftp&gt;&lt;filezilla&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="44100" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="44106" CreationDate="2008-09-04T16:15:13.650" Score="3" ViewCount="1753" Body="&lt;p&gt;This is a fairly trivial matter, but I'm curious to hear people's opinions on it.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If I have a Dictionary which I'm access through properties, which of these formats would you prefer for the property?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;/// &amp;lt;summary&amp;gt;&#xA;/// This class's FirstProperty property&#xA;/// &amp;lt;/summary&amp;gt;&#xA;[DefaultValue(&quot;myValue&quot;)]&#xA;public string FirstProperty {&#xA;    get {&#xA;        return Dictionary[&quot;myKey&quot;];&#xA;    }&#xA;    set {&#xA;        Dictionary[&quot;myKey&quot;] = value;&#xA;    }&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This is probably the typical way of doing it. It's fairly efficient, easy to understand, etc. The only disadvantage is with a longer or more complex key it would be possible to misspell it or change only one instance or something, leading me to this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;/// &amp;lt;summary&amp;gt;&#xA;/// This class's SecondProperty property&#xA;/// &amp;lt;/summary&amp;gt;&#xA;[DefaultValue(&quot;myValue&quot;)]&#xA;private const string DICT_MYKEY = &quot;myKey&quot;&#xA;public string SecondProperty {&#xA;    get {&#xA;        return Dictionary[DICT_MYKEY];&#xA;    }&#xA;    set {&#xA;        Dictionary[DICT_MYKEY] = value;&#xA;    }&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Which is marginally more complicated, but seems to offer additional safety, and is closer to what I would think of as the &quot;Code Complete&quot; solution. The downside is that when you also have a ///  block and a [DefaultValue()] block above the property already, it starts getting a bit crowded up there.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So which do you like better, and why? Does anybody have any better ideas?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1512" OwnerDisplayName="Lawrence Johnston" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditDate="2008-09-04T17:12:54.167" LastActivityDate="2009-09-19T00:57:26.220" Title="Best way to use a property to reference a Key-Value pair in a dictionary" Tags="&lt;.net&gt;&lt;dictionary&gt;&lt;properties&gt;&lt;constants&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="0" ClosedDate="2012-08-30T20:55:22.930" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="3717734" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3719142" CreationDate="2010-09-15T12:53:12.267" Score="3" ViewCount="10014" Body="&lt;p&gt;merge is a very nice function: It merges matrices and data.frames, and returns a data.frame.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;Having rather big character matrices,&#xA;  is there another good way to merge -&#xA;  without data.frame conversion?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Comment 1:&#xA;A small function to merge a named vector with a matrix or data.frame. Elements of the vector can link to multiple entries in the matrix:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;expand &amp;lt;- function(v,m,by.m,v.name='v',...) {&#xA;  df &amp;lt;- do.call(rbind,lapply(names(v),function(x) {&#xA;    pos &amp;lt;- which(m[,by.m] %in% v[x])&#xA;    cbind(x,m[pos,],...)&#xA;  }))&#xA;  colnames(df)[1] &amp;lt;- v.name&#xA;  df&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Example:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;v &amp;lt;- rep(letters,each=3)[seq_along(letters)]&#xA;names(v) &amp;lt;- letters&#xA;m &amp;lt;- data.frame(a=unique(v),b=seq_along(unique(v)),stringsAsFactors=F)&#xA;expand(v,m,'a')&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="299878" LastEditorUserId="299878" LastEditDate="2010-09-16T19:32:14.420" LastActivityDate="2016-08-04T17:02:33.390" Title="R: merging matrices (not data.frames)" Tags="&lt;r&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="3" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
