  <row Id="44270" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-09-04T17:37:04.327" Score="1" ViewCount="3763" Body="&lt;p&gt;What is the best algorithm to take array like below:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;A {0,1,2,3}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I expected to order it like array below:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;B {3,1,0,2}&lt;/code&gt;  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Any ideas?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="4541" OwnerDisplayName="JC Grubbs" LastEditorUserId="7840345" LastEditDate="2017-10-17T14:50:01.673" LastActivityDate="2017-10-17T14:50:01.673" Title="Order an Array like another Array in C#" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;arrays&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="44272" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-09-04T17:38:40.627" Score="4" ViewCount="236" Body="&lt;p&gt;This is a php example, but an algorithm for any language would do. What I specifically want to do is bubble up the United States and Canada to the top of the list. Here is an example of the array shortened for brevity. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;array(&#xA;  0 =&amp;gt; '-- SELECT --',&#xA;  1 =&amp;gt; 'Afghanistan',&#xA;  2 =&amp;gt; 'Albania',&#xA;  3 =&amp;gt; 'Algeria',&#xA;  4 =&amp;gt; 'American Samoa',&#xA;  5 =&amp;gt; 'Andorra',)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The id's need to stay intact. So making them -1 or -2 will unfortunately not work.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1797" OwnerDisplayName="mk" LastEditorUserId="367456" LastEditorDisplayName="mk" LastEditDate="2011-11-13T14:21:50.637" LastActivityDate="2019-05-22T06:43:57.193" Title="Given that I have a hash of id(key) and countries(values) sorted alphabetically, what is the best way to bubble up an entry to the top of the stack?" Tags="&lt;php&gt;&lt;arrays&gt;&lt;algorithm&gt;&lt;hash&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="44281" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="44454" CreationDate="2008-09-04T17:42:13.330" Score="24" ViewCount="5755" Body="&lt;p&gt;Does anyone know of papers/books/etc. that document patterns for databases?  For example, one common rule of thumb is that every table should have a primary key and that the key should be &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surrogate_key&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;devoid of information content&lt;/a&gt;.  So I was wondering if anyone had written a book or published papers regarding design patterns for designing relational databases?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;@Gaius,&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;That is the question that a database designer needs to weigh--what is the probable stability of the database structure?  Given a long-enough horizon nothing is stable. Or to say the converse, given a long-enough horizon, everything is subject to change.  A surrogate key (in theory) should never change its meaning because it never had meaning to begin with.  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I guess the other thing to consider in that particular design scenario is who is it that will be seeing the primary key?  If the primary key is something that end-users will actually need to refer to then it makes sense to make it something they can understand.  But I can't think of many cases where an end-user needs to see a primary key; usually the primary key is present to allow the DB engine to speed up certain operations.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;My original thought in asking the question was to find design patterns for database design that were codified by more experienced database designers than myself so as to, hopefully, avoid some easily avoidable errors.  It would be interesting reading if anyone had ever codified database design anti-patterns. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2820" OwnerDisplayName="Onorio Catenacci" LastEditorUserId="50776" LastEditDate="2012-10-30T13:12:52.120" LastActivityDate="2012-10-30T14:06:03.513" Title="Database Patterns" Tags="&lt;design-patterns&gt;&lt;database-design&gt;" AnswerCount="6" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="16" ClosedDate="2016-07-04T09:51:29.300" CommunityOwnedDate="2010-05-06T21:25:11.393" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="44286" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="45018" CreationDate="2008-09-04T17:42:59.727" Score="13" ViewCount="19590" Body="&lt;p&gt;Are there any good programs for dealing with reading large CSV files?  Some of the datafiles I deal with are in the 1 GB range. They have too many lines for Excel to even deal with. Using Access can be a little slow, as you have to actually import them into a database to work with them directly.  Is there a program that can open large CSV files and give you a simple spreadsheet layout to help you easily and quickly scan through the data?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1862" OwnerDisplayName="Kibbee" LastEditorUserId="4370109" LastEditDate="2015-10-19T10:14:43.930" LastActivityDate="2015-10-19T10:14:43.930" Title="Program for working with large CSV Files" Tags="&lt;csv&gt;&lt;load-data-infile&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="5" ClosedDate="2012-09-19T22:28:57.047" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="44288" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="44373" CreationDate="2008-09-04T17:44:10.440" Score="280" ViewCount="199265" Body="&lt;p&gt;Comparing string in C# is pretty simple.  In fact there are several ways to do it.  I have listed some in the block below.  What I am curious about are the differences between them and when one should be used over the others?  Should one be avoided at all costs?  Are there more I haven't listed?  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;string testString = &quot;Test&quot;;&#xA;string anotherString = &quot;Another&quot;;&#xA;&#xA;if (testString.CompareTo(anotherString) == 0) {}&#xA;if (testString.Equals(anotherString)) {}&#xA;if (testString == anotherString) {}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;(Note: I am looking for equality in this example, not less than or greater than but feel free to comment on that as well)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2894" OwnerDisplayName="Craig" LastEditorUserId="182542" LastEditDate="2014-07-15T08:46:26.583" LastActivityDate="2018-04-23T06:27:50.607" Title="Differences in string compare methods in C#" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;string&gt;&lt;comparison&gt;" AnswerCount="11" CommentCount="4" FavoriteCount="81" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="44294" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="46226" CreationDate="2008-09-04T17:45:59.577" Score="2" ViewCount="1341" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have the vmware server with this error, anyone knows how to fix it?&lt;a href=&quot;http://soporte.cardinalsystems.com.ar/errorvmwareserver.jpg&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;VMware Server Error http://soporte.cardinalsystems.com.ar/errorvmwareserver.jpg&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1154" OwnerDisplayName="Jedi Master Spooky" LastActivityDate="2008-09-08T17:09:07.370" Title="Enabled Brigded Network in Vmware Server" Tags="&lt;vmware&gt;&lt;virtualization&gt;&lt;vmware-server&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" ClosedDate="2017-06-01T20:27:02.317" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="44298" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="47663" CreationDate="2008-09-04T17:47:48.700" Score="1" ViewCount="2715" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a databound TextBox in my application like so: (The type of &lt;code&gt;Height&lt;/code&gt; is &lt;code&gt;decimal?&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre class=&quot;lang-xml prettyprint-override&quot;&gt;&lt;code&gt;    &amp;lt;TextBox Text=&amp;quot;{Binding Height, UpdateSourceTrigger=PropertyChanged, &#xA;                            ValidatesOnExceptions=True, &#xA;                            Converter={StaticResource NullConverter}}&amp;quot; /&amp;gt;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre class=&quot;lang-cs prettyprint-override&quot;&gt;&lt;code&gt;    public class NullableConverter : IValueConverter {&#xA;        public object Convert(object o, Type type, object parameter, CultureInfo culture) {&#xA;            return o;&#xA;        }&#xA;&#xA;        public object ConvertBack(object o, Type type, object parameter, CultureInfo culture) {&#xA;            if (o as string == null || (o as string).Trim() == string.Empty)&#xA;                return null;&#xA;            return o;&#xA;        }&#xA;    }&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Configured this way, any non-empty strings which cannot be converted to decimal result in a validation error which will immediately highlight the textbox. However, the TextBox can still lose focus and remain in an invalid state. What I would like to do is either:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Not allow the TextBox to lose focus until it contains a valid value.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Revert the value in the TextBox to the last valid value.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What is the best way to do this?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Update:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I've found a way to do #2. I don't love it, but it works:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;private void TextBox_LostKeyboardFocus(object sender, RoutedEventArgs e) {&#xA;    var box = sender as TextBox;&#xA;    var binding = box.GetBindingExpression(TextBox.TextProperty);&#xA;    if (binding.HasError)&#xA;        binding.UpdateTarget();&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Does anyone know how to do this better? (Or do #1.)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="317" OwnerDisplayName="Joseph Sturtevant" LastEditorUserId="1379871" LastEditorDisplayName="Joseph Sturtevant" LastEditDate="2020-07-20T04:38:52.273" LastActivityDate="2020-07-20T04:38:52.273" Title="Strong Validation in WPF" Tags="&lt;wpf&gt;&lt;validation&gt;&lt;data-binding&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="44337" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="44579" CreationDate="2008-09-04T17:59:52.073" Score="3" ViewCount="885" Body="&lt;p&gt;As an exercise for myself, I was translating a sample program into various languages.   Starting in C#, I had a visitor-pattern interface like so:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;interface Visitor&#xA;    {&#xA;        void Accept(Bedroom x);&#xA;        void Accept(Bathroom x);&#xA;        void Accept(Kitchen x);&#xA;        void Accept(LivingRoom x);&#xA;    }&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Moving to ruby (or python, or whatever), I no longer get dispatch from the compiler based on type.  Should I do the dispatch in the visitor itself?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;class Cleaner&#xA;&#xA;  def accept(x)&#xA;    acceptBedroom(x) if Bedroom === x&#xA;    acceptBathroom(x) if Bathroom === x&#xA;    acceptKitchen(x) if Kitchen===x&#xA;    acceptLivingRoom(x) if LivingRoom===x&#xA;  end&#xA;&#xA;  ...&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Or should I do the dispatch in the different specializaions of the room:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;class Bathroom&amp;lt;Room&#xA;  def initialize(name)&#xA;    super(name)&#xA;  end&#xA;  def accept(visitor)&#xA;    visitor.acceptBathroom(self)&#xA;  end&#xA;end&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Or is there another preferred idiom that is used in dynamic languages?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="4540" LastEditorUserId="4540" LastEditDate="2010-06-18T15:33:08.890" LastActivityDate="2010-06-18T15:33:08.890" Title="preferred way to implement visitor pattern in dynamic languages?" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;ruby&gt;&lt;design-patterns&gt;&lt;dynamic-languages&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" CommunityOwnedDate="2010-06-18T15:33:08.890" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="3729421" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3733651" CreationDate="2010-09-16T18:04:21.043" Score="1" ViewCount="658" Body="&lt;p&gt;What would be a good way to create a timer in R. One that would require little system resources.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So far I have a simple delay:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;t1=as.numeric(format(Sys.time(), &quot;%s&quot;));&#xA;t2=t1; &#xA;while (t2-t1&amp;lt;5) t2=as.numeric(format(Sys.time(), &quot;%s&quot;));&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;And the corresponding timer:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;t1=as.numeric(format(Sys.time(), &quot;%s&quot;));t2=t1;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;[Event]&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;t2=as.numeric(format(Sys.time(), &quot;%s&quot;));&#xA;time=t2-t1;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Thanks for reading.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="409828" LastActivityDate="2010-09-17T08:13:59.110" Title="R - How to 'time' delays as well as user input" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;timing&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
