  <row Id="3770589" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3770714" CreationDate="2010-09-22T15:02:34.057" Score="11" ViewCount="5503" Body="&lt;p&gt;R newbie question here. I have a list called &lt;code&gt;dbdata&lt;/code&gt;. Normally I use it like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;myresults &amp;lt;- rlm(V001 ~ V002+V003, data=dbdata)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;However, I would like to make these references dynamic. I need to have something like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;var1 &amp;lt;- &quot;V001&quot;&#xA;var2 &amp;lt;- &quot;V002&quot;&#xA;var3 &amp;lt;- &quot;V003&quot;&#xA;myresults &amp;lt;- rlm(var1 ~ var2+var3, data=dbdata)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How would I reference the variables in the context of &lt;code&gt;rlm()&lt;/code&gt;? I thought perhaps something like &lt;code&gt;eval()&lt;/code&gt;, but that didn't work.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Thanks!&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="283763" LastEditorUserId="445565" LastEditorDisplayName="user4275591" LastEditDate="2021-04-15T11:27:12.953" LastActivityDate="2021-04-15T11:27:12.953" Title="Strings as variable references in an R formula" Tags="&lt;r&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="5" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="3770737" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2010-09-22T15:14:30.970" Score="2" ViewCount="249" Body="&lt;p&gt;I use a photoelectric cell to time an event in a response time experiment. It works well in Matlab, but I wanted to control it in R. Would any of you be able to help me translate the Matlab code to R?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;global AI ch0&#xA;AI = analoginput('nidaq', 1);&#xA;AI.InputType = 'SingleEnded';&#xA;ch0 = addchannel(AI,0);&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Thanks!&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="409828" LastActivityDate="2010-09-23T19:45:31.613" Title="How read an analogue input in R? / or how to translate an existing Matlab code" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;matlab&gt;&lt;input&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="4" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="3771877" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3773070" CreationDate="2010-09-22T17:19:11.700" Score="2" ViewCount="1247" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have two matrices (of approximately 300 x 100) and I would like to plot a graph to see the parts of the first one that are higher than those of the second.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I can do, for instance:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;# Calculate the matrices and put them into m1 and m2&#xA;# Note that the values are between -1 and 1&#xA;par(mfrow=c(1,3))&#xA;image(m1, zlim=c(-1,1))&#xA;image(m2, zlim=c(-1,1))&#xA;image(m1-m2, zlim=c(0,1))&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This will plot only the desired regions in the 3rd plot but I would like to do something a bit different, like putting a line around those areas over the first plot in order to highlight them directly there.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Any idea how I can do that?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Thank you&#xA;nico&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="176923" LastActivityDate="2010-09-23T08:11:11.700" Title="Graph to compare two matrices in R" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;matrix&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="44903" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="44908" CreationDate="2008-09-04T22:32:51.573" Score="9" ViewCount="1451" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have multiple selects:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;select id=&quot;one&quot;&amp;gt;&#xA;  &amp;lt;option value=&quot;1&quot;&amp;gt;one&amp;lt;/option&amp;gt;&#xA;  &amp;lt;option value=&quot;2&quot;&amp;gt;two&amp;lt;/option&amp;gt;&#xA;  &amp;lt;option value=&quot;3&quot;&amp;gt;three&amp;lt;/option&amp;gt;&#xA;&amp;lt;/select&amp;gt;&#xA;&amp;lt;select id=&quot;two&quot;&amp;gt;&#xA;  &amp;lt;option value=&quot;1&quot;&amp;gt;one&amp;lt;/option&amp;gt;&#xA;  &amp;lt;option value=&quot;2&quot;&amp;gt;two&amp;lt;/option&amp;gt;&#xA;  &amp;lt;option value=&quot;3&quot;&amp;gt;three&amp;lt;/option&amp;gt;&#xA;&amp;lt;/select&amp;gt;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What I want is to select &quot;one&quot; from the first select, then have that option be removed from the second one.&#xA;Then if you select &quot;two&quot; from the second one, I want that one removed from the first one.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Here's the JS I have currently:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;$(function () {&#xA;    var $one = $(&quot;#one&quot;);&#xA;    var $two = $(&quot;#two&quot;);&#xA;&#xA;    var selectOptions = [];&#xA;    $(&quot;select&quot;).each(function (index) {&#xA;        selectOptions[index] = [];&#xA;        for (var i = 0; i &amp;lt; this.options.length; i++) {&#xA;            selectOptions[index][i] = this.options[i];&#xA;        }&#xA;    });&#xA;&#xA;    $one.change(function () {&#xA;        var selectedValue = $(&quot;option:selected&quot;, this).val();&#xA;        for (var i = 0; i &amp;lt; selectOptions[1].length; i++) {&#xA;            var exists = false;&#xA;            for (var x = 0; x &amp;lt; $two[0].options.length; x++) {&#xA;                if ($two[0].options[x].value == selectOptions[1][i].value)&#xA;                    exists = true;&#xA;            }&#xA;            if (!exists)&#xA;                $two.append(selectOptions[1][i]);&#xA;        }&#xA;&#xA;        $(&quot;option[value='&quot; + selectedValue + &quot;']&quot;, $two).remove();&#xA;    });&#xA;    $two.change(function () {&#xA;        var selectedValue = $(&quot;option:selected&quot;, this).val();&#xA;        for (var i = 0; i &amp;lt; selectOptions[0].length; i++) {&#xA;            var exists = false;&#xA;            for (var x = 0; x &amp;lt; $one[0].options.length; x++) {&#xA;                if ($one[0].options[x].value == selectOptions[0][i].value)&#xA;                    exists = true;&#xA;            }&#xA;            if (!exists)&#xA;                $one.append(selectOptions[0][i]);&#xA;        }&#xA;&#xA;        $(&quot;option[value='&quot; + selectedValue + &quot;']&quot;, $one).remove();&#xA;    });&#xA;});&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;But when the elements get repopulated, it fires the change event in the select whose options are changing. I tried just setting the &lt;code&gt;disabled&lt;/code&gt; attribute on the option I want to remove, but that doesn't work with IE6.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1414" OwnerDisplayName="travis" LastActivityDate="2009-09-22T03:06:55.880" Title="Is there any way to repopulate an Html Select's Options without firing the Change event (using jQuery)?" Tags="&lt;javascript&gt;&lt;jquery&gt;&lt;dom&gt;&lt;html-select&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="3773969" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3774173" CreationDate="2010-09-22T22:04:52.073" Score="0" ViewCount="2968" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a fairly simple data.frame dput(x), below: &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;x &amp;lt;- structure(list(variable = c(&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;, &quot;c&quot;, &quot;d&quot;, &quot;e&quot;, &quot;f&quot;, &quot;g&quot;, &#xA;&quot;h&quot;, &quot;i&quot;, &quot;j&quot;, &quot;k&quot;, &quot;l&quot;, &quot;m&quot;, &quot;n&quot;, &quot;o&quot;, &quot;p&quot;), top2 = c(0.51, &#xA;0.24, 0.55, 0.36, 0.56, 0.67, 0.36, 0.22, 0.48, 0.6, 0.59, 0.06, &#xA;0.04, 0.2, 0.26, 0.25), bottom = c(0.03, 0.05, 0.03, 0.01, 0.02, &#xA;0.03, 0.01, 0.05, 0.03, 0, 0.03, 0.2, 0.11, 0.06, 0.16, 0.07)), .Names = c(&quot;variable&quot;, &#xA;&quot;top2&quot;, &quot;bottom&quot;), row.names = c(NA, -16L), class = &quot;data.frame&quot;)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What I'd like to do is create a graph that shows top2 and bottom beside each other for each &quot;variable&quot;. I'm trying to implement position=&quot;dodge&quot; but so far it seems to be largely ignored by ggplot2&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;ggplot() + geom_bar(aes(variable, top2), data=x, position=&quot;dodge&quot;) +&#xA;geom_bar(aes(x$variable, x$bottom), data=x, position=&quot;dodge&quot;, fill=&quot;pink&quot;) + &#xA;coord_flip()&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The pink isn't staying :P&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="170352" LastActivityDate="2010-09-24T13:43:13.477" Title="ggplot: position=&quot;dodge&quot; question" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;ggplot2&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="44905" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="44921" CreationDate="2008-09-04T22:34:47.030" Score="148" ViewCount="116228" Body="&lt;p&gt;When writing a switch statement, there appears to be two limitations on what you can switch on in case statements.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;For example (and yes, I know, if you're doing this sort of thing it probably means your &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Object-oriented_programming&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;object-oriented&lt;/a&gt; (OO) architecture is iffy - this is just a contrived example!),&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;  Type t = typeof(int);&#xA;&#xA;  switch (t) {&#xA;&#xA;    case typeof(int):&#xA;      Console.WriteLine(&quot;int!&quot;);&#xA;      break;&#xA;&#xA;    case typeof(string):&#xA;      Console.WriteLine(&quot;string!&quot;);&#xA;      break;&#xA;&#xA;    default:&#xA;      Console.WriteLine(&quot;unknown!&quot;);&#xA;      break;&#xA;  }&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Here the switch() statement fails with 'A value of an integral type expected' and the case statements fail with 'A constant value is expected'.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Why are these restrictions in place, and what is the underlying justification? I don't see any reason why the switch statement &lt;em&gt;has&lt;/em&gt; to succumb to static analysis only, and why the value being switched on has to be integral (that is,  primitive). What is the justification?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3394" OwnerDisplayName="kronoz" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditorDisplayName="ecleel" LastEditDate="2014-12-17T19:27:28.463" LastActivityDate="2020-02-01T09:18:33.557" Title="C# switch statement limitations - why?" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;switch-statement&gt;" AnswerCount="17" CommentCount="3" FavoriteCount="35" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
