  <row Id="45235" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-09-05T04:37:15.380" Score="1" ViewCount="167" Body="&lt;p&gt;I work on a large Visual C++ (MFC) project in VS.NET 2005.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I have noticed strange behavior in Visual C++ 2005. Often, I will change one line of code in a .CPP file but many .CPP files will compile as a result of this. I have never seen this until VS.NET 2005. Theortically, changing a line of code in a .CPP file should simply require recompilation of that .CPP file and re-linking of the PE file.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What am I not understanding about the build process.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2768" LastEditorUserId="14444" LastEditorDisplayName="moocha" LastEditDate="2009-03-23T09:18:38.317" LastActivityDate="2009-03-23T09:18:38.317" Title="Strange Dependency Behavior in VS.NET 2005 (Unnecessary .CPP Compilation)" Tags="&lt;visual-c++&gt;&lt;visual-studio-2005&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="45239" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="679997" CreationDate="2008-09-05T04:43:43.350" Score="10" ViewCount="1339" Body="&lt;p&gt;Short version: What is the cleanest and most maintainable technique for consistant presentation and AJAX function across all browsers used by both web developers and web developers' end-users?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;IE 6, 7, 8&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Firefox 2, 3&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Safari&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Google Chrome&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Opera&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Long version: I wrote a &lt;a href=&quot;http://con.appspot.com&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;web app aimed at other web developers&lt;/a&gt;. I want my app to support the major web browsers (plus Google Chrome) in both presentation and AJAX behavior.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I began on Firefox/Firebug, then added conditional comments for a consistent styling under IE 6 and 7.  Next, to my amazement, I discovered that jQuery does not behave identically in IE; so I &lt;a href=&quot;http://github.com/jhs/app-engine-console/commit/4fe7741ad1856208b565eeab4260a64933929c01&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;changed my Javascript to be portable on FF and IE&lt;/a&gt; using conditionals and less pure jQuery.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Today, I started testing on Webkit and Google Chrome and discovered that, not only are the styles inconsistant with both FF and IE, but Javascript is not executing at all, probably due to a syntax or parse error.  I expected some CSS work, but now I have more Javascript debugging to do!  At this point, I want to step back and think before writing piles of special cases for all situations.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I am &lt;b&gt;not looking for a silver bullet, just best practices&lt;/b&gt; to keep things as understandable and maintainable as possible.  I prefer if this works with no server-side intelligence; however if there is a advantage to, for example, check the user-agent and then return different files to different browsers, that is fine if the total comprehensibility and maintainability of the web app is lower.  Thank you all very much!&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2938" OwnerDisplayName="jhs" LastEditorUserId="2938" LastEditorDisplayName="jhs" LastEditDate="2008-09-06T10:19:31.473" LastActivityDate="2009-03-29T22:19:02.370" Title="What is the best technique for consistent form, function between all web browsers (including Google Chrome)?" Tags="&lt;css&gt;&lt;google-chrome&gt;&lt;portability&gt;" AnswerCount="11" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="5" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="3788188" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3788324" CreationDate="2010-09-24T14:45:38.770" Score="1" ViewCount="114" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have some irregularly spaced data, say table A. The frequency is every 2-5 days. I have another data set, table B, which has entries for every weekday. I want to run the following regression:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;A_{t} = alpha + beta1 * B_{t-2 months} + error&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;where, when I lag B, if there isn't something that isn't exactly 60 days ago, e.g. if 60 days ago was a Sunday, then just pick the next Monday. I can of course construct this w/ a for loop, but what is the R way. Currently, the data are store in MySQL tables and I am using RMySQL to access.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Thanks for the help.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="318976" LastActivityDate="2010-09-24T15:13:10.497" Title="correctly lagging with irregularly spaced data" Tags="&lt;r&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
