  <row Id="3800130" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3801037" CreationDate="2010-09-26T22:43:15.007" Score="6" ViewCount="2398" Body="&lt;p&gt;The discussion in &lt;a href=&quot;https://stackoverflow.com/questions/3787231/r-and-spss-difference&quot;&gt;this question&lt;/a&gt; is the direct cause for me asking this question. The more general reason is the fact that I often have to explain R use to people that are only familiar with SPSS. I know most of the basics of SPSS, as we still use it in the base course statistics. But as I'm more of an R guy, it's difficult to know how SPSS users experience the first meeting with R.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I know there is the book &lt;a href=&quot;http://rforsasandspssusers.com/&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;R for SAS and SPSS users&lt;/a&gt; and that contains already some information. Yet, I would like to know what the more difficult parts are when you switch from SPSS to R. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Or in other words : if you would have to explain R in one day to SPSS users, which topics would you focus on? This is &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; a hypothetical question by the way (yeah, I know, it's not because one get paid for it that it always makes sense...).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="428790" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditDate="2017-05-23T12:09:11.783" LastActivityDate="2013-08-26T14:12:33.000" Title="How did you experience the transition from SPSS to R?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;spss&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="7" ClosedDate="2013-08-26T17:16:01.243" CommunityOwnedDate="2010-09-26T23:00:59.027" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
