  <row Id="45705" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="45731" CreationDate="2008-09-05T12:48:42.433" Score="9" ViewCount="3352" Body="&lt;p&gt;I've created a simple desktop application in C# 3.0 to learn some C#, wpf and .Net 3.5.&#xA;My application essentially reads data from a csv file and stores it in a SQL server CE database. I use sqlmetal to generate the ORM code for the database.&#xA;My first iteration of this app is ugly as hell and I'm in the process of refactoring it.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Which brings me to my question. How would you architect a desktop database app in C#?&#xA;What are the best practices?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Do you create a Database Abstraction Layer (DAL) which uses the sqlmetal generated code? Or is the generated code enough of an abstraction?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If you use DAL pattern, do you make it a singleton or a static member?&#xA;Do you use the View-Model-ModelView pattern with the DAL pattern?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Apologies if this seems like a long open ended question, but I have been giving this a lot of thought recently.&#xA;I see a lot of examples on how to architect an enterprise n-tier app in C# but not that many on architecting standalone desktop apps.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="87645" OwnerDisplayName="Asif" LastEditorUserId="4761806" LastEditorDisplayName="Asif" LastEditDate="2016-04-18T07:25:18.287" LastActivityDate="2016-04-18T07:25:18.287" Title="How would you architect a desktop application in C# 3.0" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;wpf&gt;&lt;architecture&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="7" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="45716" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="226949" CreationDate="2008-09-05T12:53:40.270" Score="4" ViewCount="4289" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am working in Visual Basic 6 and need to sort by multiple columns in a ListView.  For example, sorting a list of music tracks by artist, then album, then track number.  As far as I know, VB6 does not support this out of the box.  Here are the suggestions I have already heard:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Sort the data in a SQL table first and display the data in the resulting order&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Sort the data in an ADO recordset object in memory&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Sort by the primary column and then perform a sort algorithm on the items, moving them around into the correct positions manually&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Does anyone have experience with multiple-column sorting in VB6 who could lend advice?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1214" OwnerDisplayName="atoumey" LastActivityDate="2008-10-22T18:15:01.823" Title="How to do multi-column sorting on a Visual Basic 6 ListView?" Tags="&lt;sql-server&gt;&lt;vb6&gt;&lt;sorting&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
