  <row Id="3843112" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3843180" CreationDate="2010-10-01T21:19:23.547" Score="0" ViewCount="111" Body="&lt;p&gt;Frequently, I use functions to shape or numerically alter data that I'm passing to ggplot, so that I don't have to alter my data before putting it in.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If I've saved a plot to a variable. Is there a way to see the actual data that I'm plotting? It's a sanity check. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;For example: &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;c &amp;lt;- ggplot(mtcars, aes(factor(cyl))) + geom_bar()&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If I type &lt;code&gt;str(c)&lt;/code&gt;, I'm presented with $data at the top, but I would like to see it in tabular form. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="170352" LastActivityDate="2010-10-01T21:32:14.090" Title="ggplot2: Viewing data generated for a plot" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;ggplot2&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
