  <row Id="3844112" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3844222" CreationDate="2010-10-02T02:27:19.823" Score="5" ViewCount="3637" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am plotting a simple histogram of data sampled at, say, 10%.  I want counts on the y-axis, but since the data are sampled, I want them to be scaled appropriately.  If I were using base graphics, I'd do something like &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;foo &amp;lt;- rnorm(50)&#xA;foo.hist &amp;lt;- hist(foo,plot=F)&#xA;foo.hist$counts &amp;lt;- foo.hist$counts * 10&#xA;plot(foo.hist)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there an easy way to accomplish this with ggplot2?..  There are all sorts of &quot;canned&quot; y-axis transformations (scale_y_log(), etc); is there something more general-purpose?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="133234" LastActivityDate="2012-08-30T14:52:56.113" Title="Scale y-axis (counts) in ggplot2 histogram" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;ggplot2&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
