  <row Id="46918" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="47743" CreationDate="2008-09-05T21:20:47.040" Score="62" ViewCount="64044" Body="&lt;p&gt;What is the proper way to minimize a WinForms app to the system tray? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Note: minimize to &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;system tray&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;; on the right side of the taskbar by the clock. I'm not asking about minimizing to taskbar, which is what happens when you hit the &quot;minus&quot; button on the window.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I've seen hackish solutions like, &quot;minimize, set ShowInTaskbar = false, then show your NotifyIcon.&quot;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Solutions like that are hackish because the app doesn't appear to minimize to the tray like other apps, the code has to detect when to set ShowInTaskbar = true, among other issues.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What's the proper way to do this?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="536" OwnerDisplayName="Judah Himango" LastEditorUserId="536" LastEditDate="2016-04-12T19:18:16.617" LastActivityDate="2021-06-23T09:45:45.253" Title="What's the proper way to minimize to tray a C# WinForms app?" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;winforms&gt;&lt;trayicon&gt;" AnswerCount="9" CommentCount="5" FavoriteCount="17" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="46924" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="47002" CreationDate="2008-09-05T21:26:51.970" Score="4" ViewCount="775" Body="&lt;p&gt;Suppose I want to implement a reasonably efficient 'keyword recognition algorithm', that is first given a list of keyword, and must then answer if another given word was in the list. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In an imperative language, I would store the keywords in a tree (one node per character). Then, when receiving a word to test, I would scan my tree to test if the word is a keyword.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I'd like to understand how such an algorithm would be coded in a functional language.  How does one get the benefits of 'stateless' programming while keeping the efficiency of 'imperative' algorithms.  Isn't it necessary to store the tree somewhere between the lookups if you don't want to rebuild it each time? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="4177" OwnerDisplayName="Xavier Nodet" LastActivityDate="2010-11-02T15:20:51.277" Title="How can a simple tree algorithm be coded in a functional language?" Tags="&lt;algorithm&gt;&lt;functional-programming&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
