  <row Id="3989987" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3990646" CreationDate="2010-10-21T17:00:13.930" Score="24" ViewCount="32353" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a boxplot showing multiple boxes. I want to connect the mean for each box together with a line. The boxplot does not display the mean  by default, instead the middle line only indicates the median. I tried &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;ggplot(data, aes(x=xData, y=yData, group=g)) &#xA;    + geom_boxplot() &#xA;    + stat_summary(fun.y=mean, geom=&quot;line&quot;)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This does not work.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Interestingly enough, doing &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;stat_summary(fun.y=mean, geom=&quot;point&quot;) &#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;draws the median point in each box. Why would &quot;line&quot; not work?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Something like this but using ggplot2, &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.aliquote.org/articles/tech/RMB/c4_sols/plot45.png&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;http://www.aliquote.org/articles/tech/RMB/c4_sols/plot45.png&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="229378" LastEditorUserId="558647" LastEditDate="2012-11-20T22:38:04.400" LastActivityDate="2021-04-15T07:53:41.287" Title="Joining means on a boxplot with a line (ggplot2)" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;ggplot2&gt;&lt;boxplot&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="3" FavoriteCount="17" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="48550" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="48553" CreationDate="2008-09-07T16:15:01.750" Score="19" ViewCount="24897" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am trying to publish an Asp.net MVC web application locally using the NAnt and MSBuild. This is what I am using for my NAnt target;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;target name=&quot;publish-artifacts-to-build&quot;&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;msbuild project=&quot;my-solution.sln&quot; target=&quot;Publish&quot;&amp;gt;&#xA;      &amp;lt;property name=&quot;Configuration&quot; value=&quot;debug&quot; /&amp;gt;&#xA;      &amp;lt;property name=&quot;OutDir&quot; value=&quot;builds\&quot; /&amp;gt;&#xA;      &amp;lt;arg line=&quot;/m:2 /tv:3.5&quot; /&amp;gt;&#xA;    &amp;lt;/msbuild&amp;gt;&#xA;&amp;lt;/target&amp;gt;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;and all I get is this as a response;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;[msbuild]          Skipping unpublishable project.&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is it possible to publish web applications via the command line in this way?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="4642" OwnerDisplayName="Xian" LastEditorUserId="76337" LastEditDate="2013-06-23T07:33:59.693" LastActivityDate="2013-06-23T07:33:59.693" Title="How do I publish a Asp.NET web application using MSBuild?" Tags="&lt;asp.net&gt;&lt;.net&gt;&lt;deployment&gt;&lt;msbuild&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="5" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="48555" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-09-07T16:23:12.167" Score="10" ViewCount="5310" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a few sites on a shared host that is running Apache 2. I would like to compress the HTML, CSS and Javascript that is delivered to the browser. The host has disabled mod_deflate and mod_gzip, so these options are out. I do have PHP 5 at my disposal, though, so I could use the gzip component of that.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I am currently placing the following in my .htaccess file:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;php_value output_handler ob_gzhandler&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;However, this only compresses the HTML and leaves out the CSS and JS.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there a reliable way of transparently compressing the output of the CSS and JS without having to change every page? I have searched Google and a number of solutions are presented, but I've yet to get one to work. If anyone could suggest a solution that they know to work, that would be very gratefully received.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Note, &lt;strong&gt;Method 2&lt;/strong&gt; in &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.fiftyfoureleven.com/weblog/web-development/css/the-definitive-css-gzip-method&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;The Definitive Post on Gzipping your CSS&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; looks like a good solution, but I couldn't get it working. Has anyone else succeeded using this method?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1944" OwnerDisplayName="charlesroper" LastActivityDate="2017-07-26T14:31:50.233" Title="Best way to compress HTML, CSS &amp; JS with mod_deflate and mod_gzip disabled" Tags="&lt;php&gt;&lt;html&gt;&lt;apache&gt;&lt;compression&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="48562" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="50507" CreationDate="2008-09-07T16:32:55.653" Score="8" ViewCount="4171" Body="&lt;p&gt;I was wondering if anyone here had some experience writing this type of script and if they could give me some pointers.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I would like to modify this &lt;a href=&quot;http://wordaligned.org/articles/a-subversion-pre-commit-hook&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;script&lt;/a&gt; to validate that the check-in file does not have a Carriage Return in the EOL formatting. The EOL format is CR LF in Windows and LF in Unix. When a User checks-in code with the Windows format. It does not compile in Unix anymore. I know this can be done on the client side but I need to have this validation done on the server side. To achieve this, I need to do the following:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;1) Make sure the file I check is not a binary, I dont know how to do this with svnlook, should I check the mime:type of the file? The &lt;a href=&quot;http://svnbook.red-bean.com/nightly/en/svn.reposadmin.create.html&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;Red Book&lt;/a&gt; does not indicate this clearly or I must have not seen it.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;2) I would like to run the &lt;a href=&quot;http://linux.about.com/od/commands/l/blcmdl1_dos2uni.htm&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;dos2unix&lt;/a&gt; command to validate that the file has the correct EOL format. I would compare the output of the dos2unix command against the original file. If there is a diff between both, I give an error message to the client and cancel the check-in.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I would like your comments/feedback on this approach.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="5060" OwnerDisplayName="Julien Nephtali" LastEditorUserId="2666859" LastEditDate="2016-07-31T09:56:05.313" LastActivityDate="2016-07-31T09:56:05.313" Title="How do I implement a pre-commit hook script in SVN that calls dos2unix to validate checked-in file" Tags="&lt;python&gt;&lt;svn&gt;&lt;dos2unix&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="3990155" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="3990529" CreationDate="2010-10-21T17:20:36.387" Score="15" ViewCount="17253" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm trying to make sense of how to sort one data.frame based on multiple columns in another.  This question &lt;a href=&quot;https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1568511/how-do-i-sort-one-vector-based-on-values-of-another&quot;&gt;does this with vectors&lt;/a&gt;.  Can someone suggest a way to do the equivalent with data.frames?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Here's some sample data.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;x1 &amp;lt;- data.frame(a=1:5, b=letters[1:5], c=rnorm(5))&#xA;x2 &amp;lt;- data.frame(a=c(4,4,2), b=c(&quot;d&quot;, &quot;d&quot;, &quot;b&quot;), d=rnorm(3))&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So I want to sort x2 by the first two columns of x1.  My actual data is much more complicated, but this replicates the idea...&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="171249" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditDate="2017-05-23T11:58:39.697" LastActivityDate="2017-11-28T20:00:51.940" Title="R: Sort multiple columns by another data.frame?" Tags="&lt;sorting&gt;&lt;r&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="3" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
