  <row Id="4022046" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2010-10-26T08:53:06.380" Score="2" ViewCount="741" Body="&lt;p&gt;I wonder if there is a better way to put two data.frames into one, treating the rownames as if it was a column and then merge by this column with some other data.frame. I know I could do the following&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt; df1$rn &amp;lt;- row(df1) &#xA; all &amp;lt;- merge(df1,df2, by.x=&quot;rn&quot;, by.y=&quot;some_column&quot;)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I mean this produces redundant data (rownames as column) which is not needed at all. So what´s the smarter way to do it?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="366256" LastEditorDisplayName="Roger Pate" LastEditDate="2010-10-26T13:21:38.157" LastActivityDate="2010-10-26T13:21:38.157" Title="How to merge on a rowname by some column from another data.frame?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="3" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="4022195" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="4022321" CreationDate="2010-10-26T09:12:52.133" Score="6" ViewCount="13289" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm trying to transform a 3D array into a matrix. I want the third dimension of the array to form the first row in the matrix, and this third dimension should be read by row (i.e., row 1, then row 2 etc... of dimension 3 should make up the first row of the matrix). I've given an example below, where the array has dimensions of 4, 3, and 5, and the resulting matrix has 5 rows and 12 columns. I have a solution below that achieves what I want, but it seems very cumbersome for large arrays (it first creates vectors from the elements of the array (by row), and then rbinds these to form the matrix). Is there a more elegant way to do this? Thanks in advance for any suggestions.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;dat &amp;lt;- array( rnorm(60), dim=c(4, 3, 5) )   &#xA;&#xA;results &amp;lt;- list(1:5)            &#xA;for (i in 1:5) {  &#xA;    vec &amp;lt;- c( t(dat[, , i]) )  &#xA;    results[[i]] &amp;lt;- vec  &#xA;    }&#xA;&#xA;datNew &amp;lt;- rbind( results[[1]], results[[2]], results[[3]], results[[4]], results[[5]] )  &#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="230850" LastEditorUserId="230850" LastEditDate="2010-10-26T09:44:18.910" LastActivityDate="2020-02-06T16:52:53.023" Title="Transform a 3D array into a matrix in R" Tags="&lt;r&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="3" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
