ount="7" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="49214" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="49218" CreationDate="2008-09-08T05:45:34.000" Score="80" ViewCount="39326" Body="&lt;p&gt;I need a list of integers from 1 to x where x is set by the user.  I could build it with a for loop eg assuming x is an integer set previously:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;List&amp;lt;int&amp;gt; iList = new List&amp;lt;int&amp;gt;();&#xA;for (int i = 1; i &amp;lt;= x; i++)&#xA;{&#xA;    iList.Add(i);&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This seems dumb, surely there's a more elegant way to do this, something like the &lt;a href=&quot;http://au2.php.net/manual/en/function.range.php&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;PHP range method&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2975" OwnerDisplayName="Glenn Slaven" LastActivityDate="2016-04-06T21:04:08.247" Title="Populating a list of integers in .NET" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;.net&gt;&lt;list&gt;&lt;integer&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="3" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="49220" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="49221" CreationDate="2008-09-08T05:51:35.483" Score="2" ViewCount="2463" Body="&lt;p&gt;I've got two tables in my database: Articles and Tags&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The Tags tables consist of ArticleID (foreign key) and a Tag (varchar).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Now I need to map an articles tags into a readonly collection on Article entity, either using IList Tags or ReadOnlyCollection Tags.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I've consulted the NHibernate reference material, but I can't seem to figure when to use Set, Bag and the other Nhibernate collections. I've seen examples using the ISet collection, but I really don't like to tie my entity classes to a NHibernate type.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How can I do the mapping in NHibernate?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;edit: I ended up using a &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;bag&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; instead, as it doesn't require an index:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;bag name=&quot;Tags&quot; table=&quot;Tag&quot; access=&quot;nosetter.camelcase&quot; lazy=&quot;false&quot;&amp;gt;&#xA;  &amp;lt;key column=&quot;ArticleId&quot; /&amp;gt;&#xA;  &amp;lt;element column=&quot;Tag&quot; type=&quot;System.String&quot; /&amp;gt;&#xA;&amp;lt;/bag&amp;gt;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerDisplayName="Lindholm" LastEditorUserId="3817004" LastEditDate="2017-12-14T10:06:03.960" LastActivityDate="2017-12-14T10:06:03.960" Title="How can I map a list of strings to my entity using NHibernate?" Tags="&lt;nhibernate&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="49226" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="49256" CreationDate="2008-09-08T06:13:19.863" Score="5" ViewCount="9601" Body="&lt;p&gt;I need to store some sensitive data by encrypting it with atleast 128 bit key. I investigated into javax.crypto package and found that there are certain Cipher names, like &#xA;PBEWithMD5AndDES or PBEWithSHA1AndDESede which provides encryption upto 56 bit and 80 bit (&lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DESede&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DESede&lt;/a&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I referred other guys posts but those are mainly using RSA and in my understanding RSA is generally suitable for encrypting the communication data (with private-public key pair). My need is different, I just want to store the data and retrieve it back by decrypting it. Therefore I don't need any private-public key pairs.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Please let me know if you have any idea about this.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="959" OwnerDisplayName="jatanp" LastEditorUserId="256196" LastEditorDisplayName="jatanp" LastEditDate="2014-12-23T15:46:46.153" LastActivityDate="2014-12-23T15:46:46.153" Title="128 bit data encryption using Java" Tags="&lt;java&gt;&lt;cryptography&gt;" AnswerCount="6" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="3" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="4027004" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="4027340" CreationDate="2010-10-26T18:55:11.203" Score="12" ViewCount="14132" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm using geom_density to plot densities with very thin tails.  I want to restrict the y-axis range (so the top of the distribution will be off screen and the tail is more clearly visible) but it's throwing away data that is off-screen when it calculate the density, rather than just not showing what is off screen.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;E.g.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This plots the full distribution,&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;testData = data.frame(counts=c(rep(1,5), 1:10))&#xA;ggplot(testData, aes(x=testData$counts))+geom_density()&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;but when the y range is restricted, it looks as though the distribution has smaller support.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;ggplot(testData, aes(x=testData$counts))+geom_density()+scale_y_continuous(limits=c(0,0.1))&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How can I &quot;zoom in&quot; on the y axis without throwing away data?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="160356" LastActivityDate="2010-10-26T19:36:26.113" Title="Restrict y-axis range on ggplot+geom_density" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;ggplot2&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
