  <row Id="4049026" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2010-10-29T04:41:14.787" Score="1" ViewCount="508" Body="&lt;p&gt;I would like to create a function within a package with a NAMESPACE that will &lt;code&gt;save&lt;/code&gt; some variables.  The problem is that when &lt;code&gt;load&lt;/code&gt; is called on the .Rdata file it &#xA;tries to load the namespace of the package that contained the function that created the .Rdata file, but this package need not be loaded.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This example function is in a package in a namespace :&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;create.global.function &amp;lt;- function(x, FUN, ...) {&#xA; environment(FUN) &amp;lt;- .GlobalEnv&#xA; assign(&quot;.GLOBAL.FUN&quot;, function(x) { FUN(x, ...) }, env=.GlobalEnv)&#xA; environment(.GLOBAL.FUN) &amp;lt;- .GlobalEnv&#xA; save(list = ls(envir = .GlobalEnv, all.names = TRUE),&#xA;      file = &quot;/tmp/.Rdata&quot;,&#xA;      envir = .GlobalEnv)&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;environment(.GLOBAL.FUN) &amp;lt;- .GlobalEnv&lt;/code&gt; calls are not sufficient and attaching gdb to the R process confirms it is serializing a &lt;code&gt;NAMESPACESXP&lt;/code&gt; here with the name of the package namespace and the &lt;code&gt;load&lt;/code&gt; fails because it is unable to load this.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is it possible to fully strip the namespace out of the &lt;code&gt;.GLOBAL.FUN&lt;/code&gt; before I &lt;code&gt;save&lt;/code&gt; it such that it can be loaded into other R instances without trying to load the namespace?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="490884" LastEditorUserId="271616" LastEditDate="2010-10-29T06:35:04.077" LastActivityDate="2010-10-31T10:06:06.150" Title="Creating and serializing / saving global variable from within a NAMESPACE in R" Tags="&lt;r&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="4" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="4049674" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="4049974" CreationDate="2010-10-29T07:00:16.540" Score="31" ViewCount="85476" Body="&lt;p&gt;A way to draw the curve corresponding to a given function is this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;fun1 &amp;lt;- function(x) sin(cos(x)*exp(-x/2))&#xA;plot (fun1, -8, 5)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How can I add another function's curve (e.g. fun2, which is also defined by its mathematical formula) in the same plot?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="299077" LastActivityDate="2013-10-07T18:42:07.600" Title="Draw more than one function curves in the same plot" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;plot&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="14" ClosedDate="2019-04-17T06:59:23.043" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="4050702" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="4051211" CreationDate="2010-10-29T09:43:43.870" Score="0" ViewCount="2375" Body="&lt;p&gt;Imagine I've got 100 numeric matrixes with 5 columns each.&#xA;I keep the names of that matrixes in a vector or list:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;Mat &amp;lt;- c(&quot;GON1EU&quot;, &quot;GON2EU&quot;, &quot;GON3EU&quot;, &quot;NEW4&quot;, ....)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I also have a vector of coefficients &quot;coef&quot;,&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;coef &amp;lt;- c(1, 2, 2, 1, ...)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;And I want to calculate a resulting vector in this way:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;coef[1]*GON1EU[,1]+coef[2]*GON2EU[,1]+coef[3]*GON3EU[,1]+coef[4]*NEW4[,1]+.....&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How can I do it in a compact way, using the the vector of names?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Something like:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;coef*(Object(Mat)[,1])&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I think the key is how to call an object from a string with his name and use and vectorial notation. But I don't know how.  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="425895" LastEditorUserId="21234" LastEditDate="2010-10-29T10:01:47.740" LastActivityDate="2010-10-29T15:52:40.293" Title="R: Call matrixes from a vector of string names?" Tags="&lt;string&gt;&lt;r&gt;&lt;object&gt;&lt;matrix&gt;&lt;call&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="49465" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="49467" CreationDate="2008-09-08T11:00:05.477" Score="3" ViewCount="1644" Body="&lt;p&gt;When I was in China my company's website was blocked for about 24 hours.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I assume it was the &quot;Great Chinese Firewall&quot; but I was wondering if there is any way that I can find out exactly where a packet or TCP/IP connection gets blocked.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I was able to verify that it wasn't being blocked at our end(I used the local host file to point to the backup server inside of China) or at the end of our server (Other people could still connect to both ISPs).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I tried tracert but only port 80 was being redirected. I could ssh into the server without any problems.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The other problem is that most of the routers in China just drop the packets and don't respond to ping etc so you can't find out their IP addresses.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In the future are there any tools that can track down where packets are being blocked?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="5177" OwnerDisplayName="Adinobro" LastEditorUserId="4964373" LastEditorDisplayName="Adinobro" LastEditDate="2019-06-13T14:40:30.987" LastActivityDate="2019-06-13T14:40:30.987" Title="Track down where packets are being blocked/dropped" Tags="&lt;networking&gt;&lt;tcp&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
