  <row Id="49511" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="49523" CreationDate="2008-09-08T11:32:49.517" Score="7" ViewCount="1363" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have played with the idea of using a wiki (MediaWiki) to centralize all project information for a development project.  This was done using extensions that pull information from SVN (using &lt;a href=&quot;http://svnkit.com/&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;SVNKit&lt;/a&gt;) and by linking to Bugzilla to extract work assigned to a developer or work remaining for a release.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Examples:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;bugzilla type=&quot;summary&quot; user=&quot;richard.tasker@gmail.com&quot; /&amp;gt;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;would return a summary&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://i.stack.imgur.com/rfJjy.png&quot; alt=&quot;Bugzilla Summary&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;bugzilla type=&quot;status&quot; status=&quot;ASSIGNED&quot; product=&quot;SCM BEPPI&quot; /&amp;gt;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;would return&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://i.stack.imgur.com/YSV0t.png&quot; alt=&quot;Bugzilla Status&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Do you think that this would be useful?  If so then what other integrations would you think would be valuable?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2939" OwnerDisplayName="Richard Tasker" LastEditorUserId="1219121" LastEditDate="2012-04-02T08:32:45.380" LastActivityDate="2012-04-02T08:40:41.757" Title="Using a wiki as a central development project repository" Tags="&lt;svn&gt;&lt;integration&gt;&lt;wiki&gt;&lt;projects&gt;&lt;bugzilla&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="49536" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="29188477" CreationDate="2008-09-08T11:57:08.133" Score="87" ViewCount="85849" Body="&lt;p&gt;You find plenty of tutorials on menu bars in HTML, but for this specific (though IMHO generic) case, I haven't found any decent solution:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;#  THE MENU ITEMS    SHOULD BE    JUSTIFIED     JUST AS    PLAIN TEXT     WOULD BE  #&#xA;#  ^                                                                             ^  #&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;There's an varying number of text-only menu items and the page layout is fluid.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;The first menu item should be left-aligned, the last menu item should be right-aligned.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;The remaining items should be spread optimally on the menu bar.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;The number is varying,so there's no chance to pre-calculate the optimal widths.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Note that a TABLE won't work here as well:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;If you center all TDs, the first and the last item aren’t aligned correctly.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;If you left-align and right-align the first resp. the last items, the spacing will be sub-optimal.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Isn’t it strange that there is no obvious way to implement this in a clean way by using HTML and CSS?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3377" OwnerDisplayName="flight" LastEditorUserId="20578" LastEditDate="2010-03-14T23:10:18.703" LastActivityDate="2016-12-03T18:52:55.673" Title="How do I *really* justify a horizontal menu in HTML+CSS?" Tags="&lt;html&gt;&lt;css&gt;" AnswerCount="13" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="52" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="49547" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="2068407" CreationDate="2008-09-08T12:08:49.213" Score="1765" ViewCount="1122404" Body="&lt;p&gt;Our investigations have shown us that not all browsers respect the HTTP cache directives in a uniform manner.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;For security reasons we do not want certain pages in our application to be cached, &lt;strong&gt;ever,&lt;/strong&gt; by the web browser. This must work for at least the following browsers:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Internet Explorer 6+&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Firefox 1.5+&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Safari 3+&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Opera 9+&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Chrome&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Our requirement came from a security test. After logging out from our website you could press the back button and view cached pages.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="5182" OwnerDisplayName="Edward Wilde" LastEditorUserId="5377805" LastEditorDisplayName="Edward Wilde" LastEditDate="2021-03-22T07:20:12.643" LastActivityDate="2021-08-21T08:32:17.027" Title="How do we control web page caching, across all browsers?" Tags="&lt;http&gt;&lt;caching&gt;&lt;https&gt;&lt;http-headers&gt;&lt;cross-browser&gt;" AnswerCount="29" CommentCount="7" FavoriteCount="1150" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
