  <row Id="49594" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-09-08T12:44:07.163" Score="14" ViewCount="736" Body="&lt;p&gt;In my company each developer is given a project to work on his own, so there is hardly any teamwork. I've been building software like this, without the discipline of a good methodology of development, for a year. The results were ok but I would like to change and start using a more serious development approach for my next projects.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What would you consider the best practices to develop software on your own? What methodologies could I use in order to avoid common pitfalls in software development? What models of software development (waterfall -I'm joking-, extreme, agile, etc) are best suited for me?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I'd be very happy if you point me to some resources or tutorials where I could learn how to be a better developer :)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Thanks.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1679" OwnerDisplayName="Auron" LastActivityDate="2009-05-14T16:24:12.123" Title="Developing on your own" Tags="&lt;methodology&gt;" AnswerCount="16" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="3" ClosedDate="2011-11-09T13:35:20.763" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="4058145" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="4058389" CreationDate="2010-10-30T09:57:23.247" Score="1" ViewCount="684" Body="&lt;p&gt;I just came by the Ruby function&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;ncbi.esearch_count&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Used in a recent post &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.r-bloggers.com/findings-increasingly-novel-scientists-say%E2%80%A6/&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow&quot;&gt;here (regarding the use of the word noval in publications)&lt;/a&gt;, used to search in &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow&quot;&gt;pubmed&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;And I wonder how can it be done using R (via maybe Rcurl, or other library(XML) functions)?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Thanks.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="256662" LastActivityDate="2010-11-01T13:34:18.753" Title="How to search through pubmed using R, as is possible with Ruby?" Tags="&lt;ruby&gt;&lt;r&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
