  <row Id="4174185" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="4174272" CreationDate="2010-11-13T19:03:40.503" Score="4" ViewCount="1274" Body="&lt;p&gt;Using this dummy code saved in a file named &lt;strong&gt;foo.txt&lt;/strong&gt;...&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;COG,station1,station2,station3,station4,station5&#xA;COG000Z,0.019393497,0.183122497,0.089911227,0.283250444,0.074110521&#xA;COG0002,0.044632051,0.019118032,0.034625785,0.069892277,0.034073709&#xA;COG0001,0.033066112,0,0,0,0&#xA;COG0004,0.115086472,0.098805295,0.148167492,0.040019101,0.043982814&#xA;COG0005,0.064613057,0.03924007,0.105262559,0.076839235,0.031070155&#xA;COG0006,0.079920475,0.188586049,0.123607421,0.27101229,0.274806929&#xA;COG0007,0.051727492,0.066311584,0.080655401,0.027024185,0.059156417&#xA;COG0008,0.126254841,0.108478559,0.139106704,0.056430812,0.099823028&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I made a heatmap in ggplot2 with the accompanying code from &lt;a href=&quot;http://biostar.stackexchange.com/questions/921/how-to-draw-a-csv-data-file-as-a-heatmap-using-numpy-and-matplotlib/928#928&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow&quot;&gt;following this answer on stackexchange&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; library(ggplot2)&#xA;&amp;gt; foo = read.table('foo.txt', header=T, sep=',')&#xA;&amp;gt; foomelt = melt(foo)&#xA;Using COG as id variables&#xA;&amp;gt; ggplot(foomelt, aes(x=COG, y=variable, fill=value)) + geom_tile() + scale_fill_gradient(low='white', high='steelblue')&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;It produces a really nice heatmap, but I'm really just after the color codes of each tile (basically the original foo but with color codes in place of each variable). Any idea how to go about this?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="485216" LastActivityDate="2010-11-14T17:34:33.087" Title="Extract color information from ggplot2?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;colors&gt;&lt;ggplot2&gt;&lt;heatmap&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="4174288" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="4174332" CreationDate="2010-11-13T19:30:02.080" Score="11" ViewCount="1679" Body="&lt;p&gt;I was studying some functions of the package &lt;code&gt;rimage&lt;/code&gt;. If you want to see, for example, the code for the &lt;code&gt;sobel.h&lt;/code&gt; function, you obtain:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; library(rimage)&#xA;&amp;gt; sobel.h&#xA;function (img) &#xA;{&#xA;    w &amp;lt;- dim(img)[2]&#xA;    h &amp;lt;- dim(img)[1]&#xA;    imagematrix(abs(matrix(.C(&quot;sobel_h&quot;, as.double(img), as.integer(w), &#xA;        as.integer(h), eimg = double(w * h), PACKAGE = &quot;rimage&quot;)$eimg, &#xA;        nrow = h, ncol = w)), noclipping = TRUE)&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So the &lt;code&gt;sobel.h&lt;/code&gt; function uses the C routine called &lt;code&gt;sobel_h&lt;/code&gt;, (which is (I think) stored in the file &lt;code&gt;rimage.dll&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there any way to see the C code of the &lt;code&gt;sobel_h&lt;/code&gt; function?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;(I speak about the package &lt;code&gt;rimage&lt;/code&gt; for a practical example; but the answer would of course generalised to all packages that uses .C routines).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="477851" LastEditorUserId="1043380" LastEditDate="2015-07-22T14:19:39.007" LastActivityDate="2015-07-22T14:19:39.007" Title="How to display the code of a .C routine used by R function?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="51210" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-09-09T04:08:54.427" Score="2" ViewCount="2024" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm playing with the routing.rb code in Rails 2.1, and trying to to get it to the point where I can do something useful with the RoutingError exception that is thrown when it can't find the appropriate path.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This is a somewhat tricky problem, because there are some class of URLs which are just plain BAD: the /azenv.php bot attacks, the people typing /bar/foo/baz into the URL, etc... we don't want that.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Then there's subtle routing problems, where we do want to be notified:  /artists/ for example, or ///.  In these situations, we may want an error being thrown, or not... or we get Google sending us URLs which used to be valid but are no longer because people deleted them.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In each of these situations, I want a way to contain, analyze and filter the path that we get back, or at least some Railsy way to manage routing past the normal 'fallback catchall' url.  Does this exist?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;EDIT:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So the code here is: &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;# File vendor/rails/actionpack/lib/action_controller/rescue.rb, line 141&#xA;&#xA;def rescue_action_without_handler(exception)&#xA; log_error(exception) if logger&#xA; erase_results if performed?&#xA;&#xA; # Let the exception alter the response if it wants.&#xA; # For example, MethodNotAllowed sets the Allow header.&#xA; if exception.respond_to?(:handle_response!)&#xA;   exception.handle_response!(response)&#xA; end&#xA;&#xA; if consider_all_requests_local || local_request?&#xA;   rescue_action_locally(exception)&#xA; else&#xA;  rescue_action_in_public(exception)&#xA; end&#xA;end&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So our best option is to override log_error(exception) so that we can filter down the exceptions according to the exception.  So in ApplicationController&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;def log_error(exception)&#xA;    message = '...'&#xA;    if should_log_exception_as_debug?(exception)&#xA;      logger.debug(message)&#xA;    else&#xA;      logger.error(message)&#xA;    end&#xA;end&#xA;&#xA;def should_log_exception_as_debug?(exception)&#xA;   return (ActionController::RoutingError === exception)&#xA;end&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Salt for additional logic where we want different controller logic, routes, etc.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="5266" OwnerDisplayName="Will Sargent" LastEditorUserId="249341" LastEditDate="2015-03-06T04:34:49.470" LastActivityDate="2015-03-06T04:34:49.470" Title="Best way to deal with RoutingError in Rails 2.1.x?" Tags="&lt;ruby-on-rails&gt;&lt;ruby&gt;&lt;routing&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="51212" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="51239" CreationDate="2008-09-09T04:09:15.503" Score="57" ViewCount="31345" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am writing a little application to download files over http (as, for example, described &lt;a href=&quot;https://stackoverflow.com/questions/22676/how-do-i-download-a-file-over-http-using-python#22776&quot;&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I also want to include a little download progress indicator showing the percentage of the download progress.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Here is what I came up with:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&#xA;    sys.stdout.write(rem_file + &quot;...&quot;)    &#xA;    urllib.urlretrieve(rem_file, loc_file, reporthook=dlProgress)&#xA;&#xA;    def dlProgress(count, blockSize, totalSize):&#xA;      percent = int(count*blockSize*100/totalSize)&#xA;      sys.stdout.write(&quot;%2d%%&quot; % percent)&#xA;      sys.stdout.write(&quot;\b\b\b&quot;)&#xA;      sys.stdout.flush()&#xA;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Output: MyFileName... 9%&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Any other ideas or recommendations to do this? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;One thing that's somewhat annoying is the blinking cursor in the terminal on the first digit of the percentage. Is there a way to prevent this? Is there a way to hide the cursor?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;EDIT:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Here a better alternative using a global variable for the filename in dlProgress and the '\r' code:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&#xA;    global rem_file # global variable to be used in dlProgress&#xA;&#xA;    urllib.urlretrieve(rem_file, loc_file, reporthook=dlProgress)&#xA;&#xA;    def dlProgress(count, blockSize, totalSize):&#xA;      percent = int(count*blockSize*100/totalSize)&#xA;      sys.stdout.write(&quot;\r&quot; + rem_file + &quot;...%d%%&quot; % percent)&#xA;      sys.stdout.flush()&#xA;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Output: MyFileName...9% &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;And the cursor shows up at the END of the line. Much better.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2386" OwnerDisplayName="Schroeder" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditorDisplayName="Stu Thompson" LastEditDate="2017-05-23T11:33:13.693" LastActivityDate="2022-01-22T00:37:14.267" Title="How to write a download progress indicator in Python?" Tags="&lt;python&gt;&lt;http&gt;" AnswerCount="10" CommentCount="3" FavoriteCount="27" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
