  <row Id="51475" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-09-09T09:41:14.613" Score="1" ViewCount="3272" Body="&lt;p&gt;The system default polygon fill mode in current device context is &lt;code&gt;ALTERNATE&lt;/code&gt; (as I've learned from the Petzold book on Windows programming) and this one is used in &lt;a href=&quot;http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/ms533274(VS.85).aspx&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;&lt;code&gt;Polygon&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt; Win32 function unless you change the mode with &lt;a href=&quot;http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/ms536642(VS.85).aspx&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;&lt;code&gt;SetPolyFillMode&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;My question is&lt;/strong&gt;:&#xA;Does the GDI+ &lt;a href=&quot;http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/ms535958(VS.85).aspx&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;&lt;code&gt;Graphics::FillPolygon&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt; (without the &lt;code&gt;FillMode&lt;/code&gt; parameter in its signature) method also use the current device context fill mode or sets the well-known-default and then sets back the mode set before it was called?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Thanks!&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="5049" OwnerDisplayName="macbirdie" LastActivityDate="2011-02-26T21:31:53.187" Title="Polygon fill modes in GDI and GDI+" Tags="&lt;winapi&gt;&lt;gdi+&gt;&lt;gdi&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="4200979" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="4203345" CreationDate="2010-11-17T02:36:26.090" Score="7" ViewCount="6700" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'd like to add background grid to the center of the plot and then hide the standard gridlines.  The corner points of the grid are stored in the pts data frame and I've tried using geom_tile, but it doesn't appear to use the specified points.  Thanks in advance for your help.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;library(ggplot2)  &#xA;pts &amp;lt;- data.frame(&#xA;        x=c(170,170,170,177.5,177.5,177.5,185,185,185), &#xA;        y=c(-35,-25,-15,-35,-25,-15,-35,-25,-15))  &#xA;ggplot(quakes, aes(long, lat)) + &#xA;    geom_point(shape = 1) + &#xA;    geom_tile(data=pts,aes(x=x,y=y),fill=&quot;transparent&quot;,colour=&quot;black&quot;) +&#xA;    opts(&#xA;        panel.grid.major=theme_blank(),&#xA;        panel.grid.minor=theme_blank()&#xA;    )&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="338714" LastEditorUserId="614735" LastEditDate="2011-09-17T12:27:10.023" LastActivityDate="2011-09-17T12:27:10.023" Title="How can I add a background grid using ggplot2?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;ggplot2&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="51492" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="2567924" CreationDate="2008-09-09T09:54:41.383" Score="28" ViewCount="27713" Body="&lt;p&gt;For me &lt;strong&gt;usable&lt;/strong&gt; means that:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;it's being used in real-wold&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;it has tools support. (at least some simple editor)&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;it has human readable syntax (no angle brackets please) &lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Also I want it to be as close to XML as possible, i.e. there must be support for attributes as well as for properties. So, no &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/YAML&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;YAML&lt;/a&gt; please. Currently, only one matching language comes to my mind - &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JSON&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;JSON&lt;/a&gt;. Do you know any other alternatives?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1196" OwnerDisplayName="aku" LastEditorUserId="40614" LastEditorDisplayName="aku" LastEditDate="2017-12-18T09:12:55.510" LastActivityDate="2018-12-25T20:51:00.897" Title="What usable alternatives to XML syntax do you know?" Tags="&lt;xml&gt;&lt;syntax&gt;&lt;yaml&gt;" AnswerCount="14" CommentCount="4" FavoriteCount="7" ClosedDate="2017-11-05T09:59:47.513" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
