  <row Id="4214469" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="4228323" CreationDate="2010-11-18T11:57:11.083" Score="2" ViewCount="1577" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a file with many rows of this kind&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;2010-01-12 19:40   1021.00000   0.00001     1.00&#xA;2010-01-12 19:50   1031.00000   0.00000     -1.00&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In order to read it as zoo I use&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;tmp &amp;lt;- read.table(&quot;myfile&quot;)&#xA;GOEMD &amp;lt;- zoo(tmp[,3], as.chron(paste(tmp[,1],tmp[,2]), format=&quot;%Y-%m-%d %H:%M&quot;))&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;that works properly&#xA;But I'd like to use &lt;code&gt;read.zoo()&lt;/code&gt; instead.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I've tried&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;f &amp;lt;- function(x)  as.chron(paste(tmp[,1],tmp[,2]))&#xA;tmp &amp;lt;- read.zoo(&quot;myfile&quot;, index = 1:2, sep=&quot; &quot;, FUN  = f)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;and even specifying &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;colClasses= c(&quot;character&quot;,&quot;character&quot;,&quot;numeric&quot;,&quot;numeric&quot;,&quot;numeric&quot;)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;but it doesn't work;&#xA;it says: &#xA;line 136 (the one I've pasted above) doesn't have 14 elements.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I've also tried:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;tmp &amp;lt;- read.zoo(&quot;myfile&quot;, index = 1:2, sep=&quot; &quot;, FUN  = as.chron)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="425895" LastEditorUserId="4370109" LastEditDate="2015-12-03T21:07:02.313" LastActivityDate="2015-12-03T21:07:02.313" Title="Using read.zoo instead of read.table and zoo()?" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;time-series&gt;&lt;zoo&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="51781" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="51802" CreationDate="2008-09-09T12:51:05.017" Score="5" ViewCount="4574" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a variable of type &lt;code&gt;Dynamic&lt;/code&gt; and I know for sure one of its fields, lets call it &lt;code&gt;a&lt;/code&gt;, actually is an array. But when I'm writing &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;var d : Dynamic = getDynamic();&#xA;for (t in d.a) {&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I get a compilation error on line two:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;You can't iterate on a Dynamic value, please specify Iterator or Iterable&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How can I make this compilable?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerDisplayName="Vadim" LastEditorUserId="2631715" LastEditDate="2019-02-02T14:47:47.307" LastActivityDate="2019-02-02T14:49:44.467" Title="Haxe iteration on Dynamic" Tags="&lt;arrays&gt;&lt;for-loop&gt;&lt;loops&gt;&lt;haxe&gt;&lt;iterable&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="4214504" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2010-11-18T12:01:11.563" Score="9" ViewCount="33210" Body="&lt;p&gt;I need to analyze some data about internet sessions for a DSL Line. I wanted to have a look at how the session durations are distributed. I figured a simple way to do this would be to begin by making a probability density plot of the duration of all the sessions. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I have loaded the data in R and used the &lt;code&gt;density()&lt;/code&gt; function. So, it was something like this&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;plot(density(data$duration), type = &quot;l&quot;, col = &quot;blue&quot;, main = &quot;Density Plot of Duration&quot;,&#xA;     xlab = &quot;duration(h)&quot;, ylab = &quot;probability density&quot;)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I am new to R and this kind of analysis. This was what I found from going through google. I got a plot but I was left with some questions. Is this the right function to do what I am trying to do or is there something else?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In the plot I found that the Y-axis scale was from 0...1.5. I don't get how it can be 1.5, shouldn't it be from 0...1?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Also, I would like to get a smoother curve. Since, the data set is really large the lines are really jagged. It would be nicer to have them smoothed out when I am presenting this. How would I go about doing that?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="213334" LastEditorUserId="4895725" LastEditDate="2017-04-04T12:16:27.513" LastActivityDate="2017-04-04T12:16:27.513" Title="Getting Probability Density of Data" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;plot&gt;&lt;probability&gt;&lt;distribution&gt;&lt;data-analysis&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="4" FavoriteCount="12" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="51782" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="51790" CreationDate="2008-09-09T12:53:47.553" Score="20" ViewCount="21151" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have always made a point of writing nice code comments for classes and methods with the C# xml syntax. I always expected to easily be able to export them later on.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Today I actually have to do so, but am having trouble finding out how. Is there something I'm missing? I want to go &lt;em&gt;Menu-&gt;Build-&gt;Build Code Documentation&lt;/em&gt;, but there is no option to do that, there.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2260" OwnerDisplayName="Daren Thomas" LastEditorUserId="1677912" LastEditorDisplayName="hop" LastEditDate="2018-01-17T17:32:36.517" LastActivityDate="2019-06-18T17:56:43.347" Title="How do I export the code documentation in C# / VisualStudio 2008?" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;.net&gt;&lt;visual-studio-2008&gt;&lt;xml-documentation&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="5" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="51783" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="51816" CreationDate="2008-09-09T12:54:05.087" Score="31" ViewCount="20042" Body="&lt;p&gt;Flat files and relational databases give us a mechanism to serialize structured data. XML is superb for serializing un-structured tree-like data.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;But many problems are best represented by graphs. A thermal simulation program will, for instance, work with temperature nodes connected to each others through resistive edges.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So what is the best way to serialize a graph structure? I know XML can, to some extent, do it---in the same way that a relational database can serialize a complex web of objects: it usually works but can easily get ugly.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I know about the dot language used by the graphviz program, but I'm not sure this is the best way to do it. This question is probably the sort of thing academia might be working on and I'd love to have references to any papers discussing this.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1428" OwnerDisplayName="lindelof" LastEditorUserId="1571709" LastEditDate="2017-03-23T08:55:59.570" LastActivityDate="2017-03-23T08:55:59.570" Title="How to serialize a graph structure?" Tags="&lt;serialization&gt;&lt;graph-theory&gt;" AnswerCount="6" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="7" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="4215154" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="4215196" CreationDate="2010-11-18T13:18:48.057" Score="170" ViewCount="523093" Body="&lt;p&gt;Let's say I have:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;v = rep(c(1,2, 2, 2), 25)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Now, I want to count the number of times each unique value appears. &lt;code&gt;unique(v)&lt;/code&gt; returns what the unique values are, but not how many they are. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; unique(v)&#xA;[1] 1 2&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I want something that gives me &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;length(v[v==1])&#xA;[1] 25&#xA;length(v[v==2])&#xA;[1] 75&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;but as a more general one-liner :) Something close (but not quite) like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;#&amp;lt;doesn't work right&amp;gt; length(v[v==unique(v)])&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="377562" LastEditorUserId="1851712" LastEditDate="2020-06-17T10:40:03.720" LastActivityDate="2020-09-22T15:14:47.567" Title="Count number of occurences for each unique value" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;count&gt;&lt;unique&gt;" AnswerCount="13" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="55" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="4216298" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="4218258" CreationDate="2010-11-18T15:13:53.643" Score="0" ViewCount="264" Body="&lt;p&gt;We are looking to train R with structures like:&#xA;age, data1, data2, ... dataN, actions&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;where N depends on the amount of data we have about a person.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Our goal is to determine how likely is it that another person would generate actions by querying on all the data we have him/her. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;age, data1, data2, ...dataM where M could be bigger or smaller than N.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;With complete data-sets we could have used binary logistic regression. But we need to use partial sets.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What's the best way to calculate the likelihood that a person performs actions by asking with partial data sets? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="247707" LastEditorUserId="247707" LastEditDate="2010-11-18T22:15:13.460" LastActivityDate="2010-11-18T22:15:13.460" Title="Partial data sets in R" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;statistics&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="2" ClosedDate="2010-11-18T19:11:57.773" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="4216753" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="4216864" CreationDate="2010-11-18T15:53:45.480" Score="466" ViewCount="343089" Body="&lt;p&gt;I often find myself writing R scripts that generate a lot of output. I find it cleaner to put this output into it's own directory(s). What I've written below will check for the existence of a directory and move into it, or create the directory and then move into it. Is there a better way to approach this?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;mainDir &amp;lt;- &quot;c:/path/to/main/dir&quot;&#xA;subDir &amp;lt;- &quot;outputDirectory&quot;&#xA;&#xA;if (file.exists(subDir)){&#xA;    setwd(file.path(mainDir, subDir))&#xA;} else {&#xA;    dir.create(file.path(mainDir, subDir))&#xA;    setwd(file.path(mainDir, subDir))&#xA;&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="415635" LastEditorUserId="563532" LastEditDate="2016-04-28T06:24:21.043" LastActivityDate="2021-12-22T09:43:42.720" Title="Check existence of directory and create if doesn't exist" Tags="&lt;r&gt;" AnswerCount="10" CommentCount="9" FavoriteCount="101" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
