  <row Id="54142" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="54146" CreationDate="2008-09-10T14:13:39.550" Score="187" ViewCount="60243" Body="&lt;p&gt;How does the comma operator work in C++?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;For instance, if I do:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;a = b, c;  &#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Does a end up equaling b or c? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;(Yes, I know this is easy to test - just documenting on here for someone to find the answer quickly.)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Update:&lt;/strong&gt;  This question has exposed a nuance when using the comma operator.  Just to document this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;a = b, c;    // a is set to the value of b!&#xA;&#xA;a = (b, c);  // a is set to the value of c!&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This question was actually inspired by a typo in code.  What was intended to be&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;a = b;&#xA;c = d;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Turned into&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;a = b,    //  &amp;lt;-  Note comma typo!&#xA;c = d;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1541" OwnerDisplayName="Joe Schneider" LastEditorUserId="1561378" LastEditorDisplayName="Joe Schneider" LastEditDate="2012-11-12T04:47:17.817" LastActivityDate="2020-12-19T08:22:11.983" Title="How does the Comma Operator work" Tags="&lt;c++&gt;&lt;comma-operator&gt;" AnswerCount="9" CommentCount="4" FavoriteCount="62" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
