  <row Id="54536" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-09-10T16:07:58.607" Score="23" ViewCount="12532" Body="&lt;p&gt;How do I create a windows application that does the following:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;it's a regular GUI app when invoked with no command line arguments&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;specifying the optional &quot;--help&quot; command line argument causes the app to write usage text to stdout then terminate&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;it must be a single executable.  No cheating by making a console app exec a 2nd executable.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;assume the main application code is written in C/C++&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;bonus points if no GUI window is created when &quot;--help&quot; is specified.  (i.e., no flicker from a short-lived window)&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In my experience the standard visual studio template for console app has no GUI capability, and the normal win32 template does not send its stdout to the parent cmd shell.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="5429" OwnerDisplayName="JeffJ" LastActivityDate="2014-09-28T17:30:25.177" Title="win32 GUI app that writes usage text to stdout when invoked as &quot;app.exe --help&quot;" Tags="&lt;windows&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="4" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="4369334" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="4369551" CreationDate="2010-12-06T17:50:35.867" Score="4" ViewCount="1382" Body="&lt;p&gt;I see the following idiom in the .First.lib function in a lot of R packages:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;fullName &amp;lt;- paste(&quot;package&quot;, pkgname, sep=&quot;:&quot;)&#xA;myEnv &amp;lt;- as.environment(match(fullName, search()))&#xA;barepackage &amp;lt;- sub(&quot;([^-]+)_.*&quot;, &quot;\\1&quot;, pkgname)&#xA;dbbase &amp;lt;- file.path(libname, pkgname, &quot;R&quot;, barepackage)&#xA;rm(.First.lib, envir = myEnv)&#xA;lazyLoad(dbbase, myEnv)&#xA;if(exists(&quot;.First.lib&quot;, envir = myEnv, inherits = FALSE)) {&#xA;    f &amp;lt;- get(&quot;.First.lib&quot;, envir = myEnv, inherits = FALSE)&#xA;    if(is.function(f))&#xA;        f(libname, pkgname)&#xA;    else&#xA;        stop(gettextf(&quot;package '%s' has a non-function '.First.lib'&quot;,&#xA;                      pkgname),&#xA;             domain = NA)&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I understand that the .First.lib function is run when a package is loaded.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I understand that the code above defines an environment for the package and sets up a path, but I don't understand why it's looking for a .First.lib function after it explicitly deletes the .First.lib function. What makes the above idiom so common? Is it a &quot;best practice&quot; to include this in an R package? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="37751" LastEditorUserId="37751" LastEditDate="2010-12-07T15:41:22.567" LastActivityDate="2010-12-07T15:41:22.567" Title=".First.lib idiom in R packages" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;packages&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
