  <row Id="55179" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="55189" CreationDate="2008-09-10T20:05:02.983" Score="6" ViewCount="1728" Body="&lt;p&gt;I've noticed that there seems to be quite a bit of hostility towards Linq To Entities particularly from the Alt.Net folks.  I understand the resistance to more &quot;drag and drop&quot; programming, but from my understanding, Linq To Entities doesn't require it.  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;We're currently using Linq to SQL, and we are using the DBML document to define it (once you get more than a dozen or so tables, the designer is pretty useless.)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So why wouldn't the same approach work for Linq To Entities?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3268" OwnerDisplayName="Bramha Ghosh" LastActivityDate="2011-03-22T15:47:57.970" Title="Why all the Linq To Entities Hate?" Tags="&lt;linq-to-entities&gt;" AnswerCount="6" CommentCount="0" ClosedDate="2012-09-20T11:55:17.487" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
