  <row Id="55403" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="319096" CreationDate="2008-09-10T21:59:23.363" Score="19" ViewCount="3570" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am interested to know whether anyone has written an application that takes advantage of a &lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GPGPU&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;GPGPU&lt;/a&gt; by using, for example, &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.nvidia.com/object/cuda_get.html&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;nVidia CUDA&lt;/a&gt;.  If so, what issues did you find and what performance gains did you achieve compared with a standard CPU?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3305" OwnerDisplayName="John Channing" LastEditorUserId="3204551" LastEditDate="2015-02-18T17:21:33.220" LastActivityDate="2019-02-21T20:00:53.957" Title="Have you successfully used a GPGPU?" Tags="&lt;cuda&gt;&lt;gpgpu&gt;&lt;hpc&gt;" AnswerCount="10" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="17" ClosedDate="2018-12-19T09:41:38.813" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="55411" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="60353" CreationDate="2008-09-10T22:06:40.477" Score="10" ViewCount="12995" Body="&lt;p&gt;Running into a problem where on certain servers we get an error that the directory name is invalid when using Path.GetTempFileName.  Further investigation shows that it is trying to write a file to c:\Documents and Setting\computername\aspnet\local settings\temp (found by using Path.GetTempPath).  This folder exists so I'm assuming this must be a permissions issue with respect to the asp.net account.  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I've been told by some that Path.GetTempFileName should be pointing to C:\Windows\Microsoft.NET\Framework\v2.0.50727\temporaryasp.net files.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I've also been told that this problem may be due to the order in which IIS and .NET where installed on the server.  I've done the typical 'aspnet_regiis -i' and checked security on the folders etc.  At this point I'm stuck.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Can anyone shed some light on this?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;**Update:**Turns out that providing 'IUSR_ComputerName' access to the folder does the trick.  Is that the correct procedure?  I don't seem to recall doing that in the past, and obviously, want to follow best practices to maintain security.  This is, after all, part of a file upload process.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="5678" LastEditorUserId="5678" LastEditorDisplayName="Douglas Anderson" LastEditDate="2008-09-10T22:26:35.747" LastActivityDate="2017-07-25T18:00:01.183" Title="Path.GetTempFileName -- Directory name is invalid" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;asp.net&gt;&lt;iis&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
