  <row Id="57383" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="17091351" CreationDate="2008-09-11T19:17:52.090" Score="345" ViewCount="90109" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am calling, through reflection, a method which may cause an exception. How can I pass the exception to my caller without the wrapper reflection puts around it?&lt;br&gt;&#xA;I am rethrowing the InnerException, but this destroys the stack trace.&lt;br&gt;&#xA;Example code:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;public void test1()&#xA;{&#xA;    // Throw an exception for testing purposes&#xA;    throw new ArgumentException(&quot;test1&quot;);&#xA;}&#xA;&#xA;void test2()&#xA;{&#xA;    try&#xA;    {&#xA;        MethodInfo mi = typeof(Program).GetMethod(&quot;test1&quot;);&#xA;        mi.Invoke(this, null);&#xA;    }&#xA;    catch (TargetInvocationException tiex)&#xA;    {&#xA;        // Throw the new exception&#xA;        throw tiex.InnerException;&#xA;    }&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3205" OwnerDisplayName="Alberto Conne" LastEditorUserId="4519059" LastEditorDisplayName="Alberto Conne" LastEditDate="2018-09-09T11:23:15.427" LastActivityDate="2021-07-30T08:49:23.627" Title="How to rethrow InnerException without losing stack trace in C#?" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;.net&gt;&lt;exception&gt;" AnswerCount="10" CommentCount="3" FavoriteCount="146" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
