  <row Id="4568188" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="4608965" CreationDate="2010-12-31T05:05:05.833" Score="4" ViewCount="3797" Body="&lt;p&gt;As question, I am trying to create a plot using the following code:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;chart.demo.sex.age &amp;lt;- ggplot(psf10[!is.na(psf10[,&quot;age_gp&quot;]),c(&quot;sex&quot;,&quot;age_gp&quot;)], aes(x=age_gp))&#xA;chart.demo.sex.age.f &amp;lt;- chart.demo.sex.age + geom_bar(subset = .(sex ==&quot;F&quot;), fill=&quot;red&quot;)&#xA;chart.demo.sex.age.f &amp;lt;- chart.demo.sex.age.f + scale_x_discrete(expand=c(0.05,0))&#xA;chart.demo.sex.age.f &amp;lt;- chart.demo.sex.age.f + scale_y_continuous(limits=c(0,1500), expand=c(0.05,0))&#xA;chart.demo.sex.age.f &amp;lt;- chart.demo.sex.age.f + opts(axis.title.x = theme_blank(),&#xA;                                                    axis.title.y = theme_blank(),&#xA;                                                    axis.text.y  = theme_blank(),&#xA;                                                    axis.ticks = theme_blank(),&#xA;                                                    panel.border = theme_rect(colour=&quot;black&quot;),&#xA;                                                    plot.margin = unit(c(1,0,1,1),&quot;lines&quot;))&#xA;chart.demo.sex.age.f &amp;lt;- chart.demo.sex.age.f + scale_y_continuous(trans = 'reverse') + coord_flip()&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;With &lt;code&gt;scale_y_continuous(trans = 'reverse')&lt;/code&gt;, I can't use &lt;code&gt;ylimit&lt;/code&gt; (i.e. i can't see adjustment in ylimit), I don't know why. Any suggestions? Thanks.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="373908" LastActivityDate="2011-01-06T00:16:45.737" Title="cannot use ylimit in ggplot when used &quot;scale_y_continuous(trans = 'reverse')&quot;" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;ggplot2&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="57625" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="57664" CreationDate="2008-09-11T20:57:48.933" Score="13" ViewCount="29257" Body="&lt;p&gt;This one will take some explaining. What I've done is create a specific custom message queue in SQL Server 2005. I have a table with messages that contain timestamps for both acknowledgment and completion. The stored procedure that callers execute to obtain the next message in their queue also acknowledges the message. So far so good. Well, if the system is experiencing a massive amount of transactions (thousands per minute), isn't it possible for a message to be acknowledged by another execution of the stored procedure while another is prepared to so itself? Let me help by showing my SQL code in the stored proc:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;--Grab the next message id&#xA;declare @MessageId uniqueidentifier&#xA;set @MessageId = (select top(1) ActionMessageId from UnacknowledgedDemands);&#xA;&#xA;--Acknowledge the message&#xA;update ActionMessages&#xA;set AcknowledgedTime = getdate()&#xA;where ActionMessageId = @MessageId&#xA;&#xA;--Select the entire message&#xA;...&#xA;...&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In the above code, couldn't another stored procedure running at the same time obtain the same id and attempt to acknowledge it at the same time? Could I (or should I) implement some sort of locking to prevent another stored proc from acknowledging messages that another stored proc is querying? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Wow, did any of this even make sense? It's a bit difficult to put to words...&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="5469" OwnerDisplayName="Kilhoffer" LastEditorUserId="5696608" LastEditorDisplayName="Dan" LastEditDate="2010-07-13T14:32:54.007" LastActivityDate="2011-07-19T11:25:43.490" Title="How do you lock tables in SQL Server 2005, and should I even do it?" Tags="&lt;sql-server&gt;&lt;tsql&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="57652" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-09-11T21:06:21.687" Score="22" ViewCount="37134" Body="&lt;p&gt;Scenario:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;The user has two monitors.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Their browser is open on the secondary monitor.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;They click a link in the browser which calls window.open() with a specific top and left window offset.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;The popup window always opens on their primary monitor.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there any way in JavaScript to get the popup window to open on the same monitor as the initial browser window (the opener)?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1436" OwnerDisplayName="Jeremy McCollum" LastEditorUserId="1436" LastEditorDisplayName="Jeremy McCollum" LastEditDate="2008-09-15T13:11:27.117" LastActivityDate="2018-09-10T00:34:55.307" Title="How do I get JavaScript to open a popup window on the current monitor" Tags="&lt;javascript&gt;&lt;multiple-monitors&gt;" AnswerCount="8" CommentCount="4" FavoriteCount="10" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
