  <row Id="58354" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-09-12T05:17:35.470" Score="53" ViewCount="144850" Body="&lt;p&gt;What are some simple algorithm or data structure related &quot;white boarding&quot; problems that you find effective during the candidate screening process?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I have some simple ones that I use to validate problem solving skills and that can be simply expressed but have some opportunity for the application of some heuristics.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;One of the basics that I use for junior developers is:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;Write a C# method that takes a string which contains a set of words (a sentence) and rotates those words X number of places to the right.  When a word in the last position of the sentence is rotated it should show up at the front of the resulting string.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;When a candidate answers this question I look to see that they available .NET data structures and methods (string.Join, string.Split, List, etc...) to solve the problem.  I also look for them to identify special cases for optimization.  Like the number of times that the words need to be rotated isn't really X it's X % number of words.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What are some of the white board problems that you use to interview a candidate and what are some of the things you look for in an answer (do not need to post the actual answer).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3957" OwnerDisplayName="spoon16" LastEditorUserId="410" LastEditorDisplayName="cynicalman" LastEditDate="2008-09-19T15:01:23.360" LastActivityDate="2010-03-09T21:55:13.843" Title="Algorithm/Data Structure Design Interview Questions" Tags="&lt;algorithm&gt;&lt;data-structures&gt;" AnswerCount="11" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="76" ClosedDate="2011-12-03T09:02:48.770" CommunityOwnedDate="2009-01-07T00:05:13.580" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="58380" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="58387" CreationDate="2008-09-12T05:48:36.800" Score="77" ViewCount="67963" Body="&lt;p&gt;The following bit of code catches the EOS Exception&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;using (var reader = new BinaryReader(httpRequestBodyStream)) {&#xA;&#xA;    try {&#xA;        while (true) {&#xA;            bodyByteList.Add(reader.ReadByte());&#xA;        }&#xA;    } catch (EndOfStreamException) { }&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So why do I still receive first-chance exceptions in my console? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;A first chance exception of type 'System.IO.EndOfStreamException' occurred in mscorlib.dll&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there a way to hide these first chance exception messages?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="209" OwnerDisplayName="CVertex" LastActivityDate="2021-04-23T08:45:52.890" Title="Avoiding first chance exception messages when the exception is safely handled" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;.net&gt;&lt;exception&gt;" AnswerCount="9" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="8" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="4621146" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2011-01-06T23:32:38.937" Score="0" ViewCount="891" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am writing for some help in R.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I am doing a simple RCBD analysis using the following script to&#xA;compare genotypes (Name)&#xA;for the trait &quot;X&quot;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;library(stats)&#xA;data_1=read.table(file=&quot;test.txt&quot;, head=TRUE)&#xA;result_X =aov(X~Block+Name, data=data_1)&#xA;sink(&quot;result_X.txt&quot;)&#xA;summary(result_X)&#xA;sink()&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;My data has missing data (&quot;NA&quot;). So, after calculating the LSD,&#xA;I would like to compare&#xA;the genotypes in descending order. I do not think the averages&#xA;are good since some&#xA;'blocks' for some 'names' are missing.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;So, the question is, what is the script to print out the 'least&#xA;square means', which I&#xA;think are the best to compare with LSD than simple averages.&#xA;Thank you for your help,&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Oswald&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="566217" LastEditorUserId="1855677" LastEditDate="2011-01-07T00:10:45.273" LastActivityDate="2011-01-17T23:14:36.980" Title="least square means for dataset with missing data" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;least-squares&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
