  <row Id="59436" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="59453" CreationDate="2008-09-12T16:25:34.600" Score="1" ViewCount="137" Body="&lt;p&gt;As much as we would all like to say it is a benefit to programmers to be language agnostic, is it really feasible to support multiple enterprise Web applications of the same type all written in different languages? Think about how complicated a CMS or e-commerce system can be -- now imagine supporting three different CMS platforms all written in different languages. I would hate to be known as a .NET or Java or PHP shop, but I also don't want to be the vendor who says they can support a solution they have never worked with, upsetting a client who wonders why we can't get something done right on time. Can anyone speak from experience on this? Does your company usually just suck it up, and try and to learn a new platform on the fly? Do you bill up-to-speed, or eat those costs?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerDisplayName="user4903" LastEditorUserId="321731" LastEditorDisplayName="Outlaw Programmer" LastEditDate="2014-05-21T20:17:00.427" LastActivityDate="2016-06-09T18:50:12.057" Title="Is it feasible to support multiple applications of the same type that are all written in different languages?" Tags="&lt;multilingual&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
