  <row Id="59986" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="60043" CreationDate="2008-09-12T21:16:26.727" Score="2" ViewCount="3752" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a simple type that explicitly implemets an Interface.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;public interface IMessageHeader&#xA;{&#xA;    string FromAddress { get; set; }&#xA;    string ToAddress   { get; set; }&#xA;}&#xA;&#xA;[Serializable]&#xA;public class MessageHeader:IMessageHeader&#xA;{&#xA;  private string from;&#xA;  private string to;&#xA;&#xA;  [XmlAttribute(&quot;From&quot;)]&#xA;  string IMessageHeade.FromAddress&#xA;  {&#xA;    get { return this.from;}&#xA;    set { this.from = value;}&#xA;  }&#xA;&#xA; [XmlAttribute(&quot;To&quot;)]&#xA; string IMessageHeade.ToAddress&#xA; {&#xA;    get { return this.to;}&#xA;    set { this.to = value;}&#xA; }&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there a way to Serialize and Deserialize objects of type IMessageHeader??&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I got the following error when tried&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&quot;Cannot serialize interface IMessageHeader&quot;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1647" OwnerDisplayName="kudlur" LastEditorUserId="33225" LastEditorDisplayName="swilliams" LastEditDate="2010-08-20T08:32:09.123" LastActivityDate="2010-08-20T08:34:46.603" Title="XMLSerialization in C#" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;.net&gt;&lt;serialization&gt;&lt;interface&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="60000" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="2688631" CreationDate="2008-09-12T21:24:53.413" Score="50" ViewCount="25868" Body="&lt;p&gt;In C++, can member function pointers be used to point to derived (or even base) class members? &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;EDIT: &#xA;Perhaps an example will help. Suppose we have a hierarchy of three classes &lt;code&gt;X&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Y&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Z&lt;/code&gt; in order of inheritance. &#xA;&lt;code&gt;Y&lt;/code&gt; therefore has a base class &lt;code&gt;X&lt;/code&gt; and a derived class &lt;code&gt;Z&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Now we can define a member function pointer &lt;code&gt;p&lt;/code&gt; for class &lt;code&gt;Y&lt;/code&gt;. This is written as:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;void (Y::*p)();&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;(For simplicity, I'll assume we're only interested in functions with the signature &lt;code&gt;void f()&lt;/code&gt; )  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This pointer &lt;code&gt;p&lt;/code&gt; can now be used to point to member functions of class &lt;code&gt;Y&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This question (two questions, really) is then:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Can &lt;code&gt;p&lt;/code&gt; be used to point to a function in the derived class &lt;code&gt;Z&lt;/code&gt;?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Can &lt;code&gt;p&lt;/code&gt; be used to point to a function in the base class &lt;code&gt;X&lt;/code&gt;?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1077" OwnerDisplayName="Simon Hardy" LastEditorUserId="1077" LastEditorDisplayName="Simon Hardy" LastEditDate="2009-07-15T10:52:43.013" LastActivityDate="2015-02-13T18:58:03.910" Title="C++ inheritance and member function pointers" Tags="&lt;c++&gt;&lt;inheritance&gt;&lt;oop&gt;&lt;member-function-pointers&gt;" AnswerCount="8" CommentCount="3" FavoriteCount="8" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
