  <row Id="60121" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="376143" CreationDate="2008-09-12T22:58:20.600" Score="14" ViewCount="6725" Body="&lt;p&gt;Silverlight v2.0 is getting closer and closer to RTM but I have yet to hear any stats as to how many browsers are running Silverlight. If I ask Adobe (by googling &quot;Flash install base&quot;) they're &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.adobe.com/products/player_census/flashplayer/version_penetration.html&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;only too happy to tell me&lt;/a&gt; that 97.7% of browsers are running Flash player 9 or better.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Not that I believe everything I read, but &lt;strong&gt;where are these statistics from Microsoft or some other vendor about Silverlight?&lt;/strong&gt; I'm going to be making a technology choice soon and a little bit of empirical evidence would be an asset at this point...&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;All you Silverlight developers out there, show me your stats!&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="5642" OwnerDisplayName="tyler" LastActivityDate="2011-05-06T12:16:35.967" Title="Silverlight Install Base - How big is it?" Tags="&lt;apache-flex&gt;&lt;silverlight&gt;&lt;installation&gt;" AnswerCount="16" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="60122" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="60146" CreationDate="2008-09-12T22:58:20.880" Score="2" ViewCount="7673" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a dataset that has two tables in it. I want to do the following (or something like it) is it possible and is how I have it correct?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;dsTabData.Tables(&quot;FilingTabs&quot;).Select(&quot;fs_ID not in (select fsp_fsid from ParentTabs)&quot;)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;how do you reference data from other table in the same dataset?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1889" OwnerDisplayName="MikeScott8" LastEditorUserId="249341" LastEditDate="2015-03-06T04:36:24.687" LastActivityDate="2015-03-06T04:36:24.687" Title="Select rows in dataset table based on other dataset table" Tags="&lt;.net&gt;&lt;vb.net&gt;&lt;select&gt;&lt;dataset&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="60137" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="60155" CreationDate="2008-09-12T23:13:52.887" Score="2" ViewCount="468" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm considering moving a number of small client sites to an unmanaged VPS hosting provider. I haven't decided which one yet, but my understanding is that they'll give me a base OS install (I'd prefer Debian or Ubuntu), an IP address, a root account, SSH, and that's about it.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Ideally, I would like to create a complete VM image of my configured setup and just ship those bits to the provider. Has anyone had any experience with this? I've seen &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.codinghorror.com/blog/archives/000984.html&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;Jeff talk about something like this in Coding Horror&lt;/a&gt;. But I'm not sure if his experience is typical. I suppose it also depends on the type of VM server used by the host.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Also, do such hosts provide reverse-DNS? That's kinda useful for sites that send out e-mails. I know GMail tends to bounce anything originating from a server without it.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Finally, I'd probably need multiple IP addresses as at least a couple of the sites have SSL protection which doesn't work with name-based virtual hosts. Has anyone run into trouble with multiple IPs through VPS? I wouldn't think so, but I've heard whisperings to the contrary.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="726" OwnerDisplayName="yukondude" LastEditorUserId="726" LastEditorDisplayName="yukondude" LastEditDate="2008-09-18T22:27:59.733" LastActivityDate="2008-09-23T14:10:22.233" Title="Technical issues when switching to an unmanaged Virtual Private Server (VPS) hosting provider?" Tags="&lt;hosting&gt;&lt;virtualization&gt;&lt;vps&gt;&lt;reverse-dns&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="60142" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="60154" CreationDate="2008-09-12T23:17:43.253" Score="8" ViewCount="5919" Body="&lt;p&gt;How much traffic can one web server handle? What's the best way to see if we're beyond that?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I have an ASP.Net application that has a couple hundred users. Aspects of it are fairly processor intensive, but thus far we have done fine with only one server to run both SqlServer and the site. It's running Windows Server 2003, 3.4 GHz with 3.5 GB of RAM. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;But lately I've started to notice slows at various times, and I was wondering what's the best way to determine if the server is overloaded by the usage of the application or if I need to do something to fix the application (I don't really want to spend a lot of time hunting down little optimizations if I'm just expecting too much from the box).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3442" OwnerDisplayName="Jared" LastEditorUserId="4337" LastEditorDisplayName="Gulzar" LastEditDate="2008-09-13T11:45:26.857" LastActivityDate="2009-01-29T01:09:09.793" Title="Best way to determine the number of servers needed" Tags="&lt;asp.net&gt;&lt;webserver&gt;&lt;capacity-planning&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="5" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="60143" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="60166" CreationDate="2008-09-12T23:21:04.243" Score="4" ViewCount="263" Body="&lt;p&gt;The easiest way to think of my question is to think of a single, simple unix command (albeit, this is for windows) and I need progmatic access to run it. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I have a single command-line based executable that performs some unit of work. I want to call that executable with the .net process library, as I can do with any other executable.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;However, it dawned on me that there is potential for the dll to become useless or break with unintended updates to the executable or a non-existant executable.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is it possible to run the executable from the Process object in the .net framework, as I would an external executable file?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="5619" OwnerDisplayName="Saul" LastActivityDate="2017-09-13T08:43:05.350" Title="Is it possible to embed and use a portable executable in a .net DLL?" Tags="&lt;.net&gt;&lt;dll&gt;&lt;command-line&gt;&lt;resources&gt;" AnswerCount="3" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
