  <row Id="4753368" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="4753934" CreationDate="2011-01-20T22:25:21.997" Score="1" ViewCount="1184" Body="&lt;p&gt;I am trying to implement a network core-periphery measure from an article (link: &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.sciencedirect.com/science?_ob=ArticleURL&amp;amp;_udi=B6VD1-3YHG8BC-4&amp;amp;_user=10&amp;amp;_coverDate=10%2F31%2F2000&amp;amp;_rdoc=1&amp;amp;_fmt=high&amp;amp;_orig=search&amp;amp;_origin=search&amp;amp;_sort=d&amp;amp;_docanchor=&amp;amp;view=c&amp;amp;_searchStrId=1614388781&amp;amp;_rerunOrigin=scholar.google&amp;amp;_acct=C000050221&amp;amp;_version=1&amp;amp;_urlVersion=0&amp;amp;_userid=10&amp;amp;md5=413a3389b234a9c8b3751c55da66d51f&amp;amp;searchtype=a&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow&quot;&gt;Borgatti &amp;amp; Everett 2000&lt;/a&gt;) in R. The basic approach applied by the authors is to:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Arrange the rows and columns of the network matrix so that actors that are well connected to each other occupy the top left corner.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Create an ideal pattern matrix based on the row/column arrangement in step 1&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Assess the correlation between the two matrices&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;According to the authors the trick in step one is to find the row/column arrangement of the matrix that correlates the highest with its induced pattern matrix, and they recommend using a genetic algorithm to find the best row/column arrangement. I am stuck at the first steps of the algorithm:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;How do I in R create random row/column matrix arrangements that preserve the order of the column/row entries?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Once I have assessed the fit between the matrix arrangements and the patterns matrices, how do I &quot;breed&quot; new matrix arrangements based on the &quot;fittest&quot; matrices?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Thanks.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="363268" LastEditorUserId="709619" LastEditDate="2011-10-26T02:04:10.963" LastActivityDate="2011-10-26T02:04:10.963" Title="Social networks and genetic algorithms in R" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;genetic-algorithm&gt;&lt;social-networking&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="4754996" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="4755101" CreationDate="2011-01-21T03:05:49.597" Score="32" ViewCount="17222" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'd like to write a function that presents to the user a status message that shows something like the time, the percent complete, and the current status of a process. I can handle assembling the message, but I'd like to do something other than just print to the console and have it scroll up, one message after the other. I'd really like the message to change without scrolling like &lt;code&gt;message()&lt;/code&gt; and without any graphics. Is this possible with R?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="37751" LastEditorUserId="428790" LastEditDate="2011-01-21T10:09:54.930" LastActivityDate="2011-01-21T10:09:54.930" Title="showing a status message in R" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;message&gt;&lt;status&gt;&lt;statusbar&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="12" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
