  <row Id="60751" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="60790" CreationDate="2008-09-13T18:20:36.250" Score="4" ViewCount="961" Body="&lt;p&gt;Currently my app uses just Direct3D9 for graphics, however in the future I' m planning to extend this to D3D10 and possibly OpenGL. The question is how can I do this in a tidy way?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;At present there are various Render methods in my code&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;void Render(boost::function&amp;lt;void()&amp;gt; &amp;amp;Call)&#xA;{&#xA;    D3dDevice-&amp;gt;BeginScene();&#xA;    Call();&#xA;    D3dDevice-&amp;gt;EndScene();&#xA;    D3dDevice-&amp;gt;Present(0,0,0,0);&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The function passed then depends on the exact state, eg MainMenu-&gt;Render, Loading-&gt;Render, etc. These will then oftern call the methods of other objects.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;void RenderGame()&#xA;{&#xA;    for(entity::iterator it = entity::instances.begin();it != entity::instance.end(); ++it)&#xA;        (*it)-&amp;gt;Render();&#xA;    UI-&amp;gt;Render();&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;And a sample class derived from entity::Base&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;class Sprite: public Base&#xA;{&#xA;    IDirect3DTexture9 *Tex;&#xA;    Point2 Pos;&#xA;    Size2 Size;&#xA;public:&#xA;    Sprite(IDirect3DTexture9  *Tex, const Point2 &amp;amp;Pos, const Size2 &amp;amp;Size);&#xA;    virtual void Render();&#xA;};&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Each method then takes care of how best to render given the more detailed settings (eg are pixel shaders supported or not).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The problem is I'm really not sure how to extend this to be able to use one of, what may be somewhat different (D3D v OpenGL) render modes...&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="6266" OwnerDisplayName="Fire Lancer" LastEditorUserId="687389" LastEditorDisplayName="Fire Lancer" LastEditDate="2017-07-04T11:42:37.337" LastActivityDate="2017-07-04T11:42:37.337" Title="c++ Having multiple graphics options" Tags="&lt;c++&gt;&lt;opengl&gt;&lt;graphics&gt;&lt;direct3d&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="60757" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="74605" CreationDate="2008-09-13T18:34:58.510" Score="5" ViewCount="2963" Body="&lt;p&gt;What is the best way to handle user account management in a system, without having your employees who have access to a database, to have access to the accounts.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Examples:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Storing username/password in the database.  This is a bad idea because anyone that has access to a database can see the username and password.  And hence use it.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Storing username/password hash.  This is a better method, but the account can be accessed by replacing the password hash in the database with the hash of another account that you know the auth info for.  Then after access is granted reverting it back in the database. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;How does windows/*nix handle this?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3153" OwnerDisplayName="Brian R. Bondy" LastActivityDate="2010-05-08T19:58:32.887" Title="Best way to handle user account authentication and passwords" Tags="&lt;security&gt;&lt;database-design&gt;&lt;authentication&gt;&lt;authorization&gt;" AnswerCount="11" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
