  <row Id="61517" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="62680" CreationDate="2008-09-14T18:00:43.030" Score="424" ViewCount="551506" Body="&lt;p&gt;Do you know if there is a built-in function to build a dictionary from an arbitrary object? I'd like to do something like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt;&amp;gt;&amp;gt; class Foo:&#xA;...     bar = 'hello'&#xA;...     baz = 'world'&#xA;...&#xA;&amp;gt;&amp;gt;&amp;gt; f = Foo()&#xA;&amp;gt;&amp;gt;&amp;gt; props(f)&#xA;{ 'bar' : 'hello', 'baz' : 'world' }&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;NOTE:&lt;/strong&gt; It should not include methods. Only fields.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2148" OwnerDisplayName="jcarrascal" LastEditorUserId="832230" LastEditorDisplayName="Chris" LastEditDate="2016-10-30T23:54:14.487" LastActivityDate="2021-09-07T13:11:40.800" Title="Python dictionary from an object's fields" Tags="&lt;python&gt;&lt;dictionary&gt;&lt;attributes&gt;&lt;object&gt;&lt;metaprogramming&gt;" AnswerCount="16" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="91" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="61520" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="61526" CreationDate="2008-09-14T18:04:57.543" Score="10" ViewCount="4686" Body="&lt;p&gt;There is a lot of information out there on object-relational mappers and how to best avoid impedance mismatch, all of which seem to be moot points if one were to use an object database.  My question is why isn't this used more frequently?  Is it because of performance reasons or because object databases cause your data to become proprietary to your application or is it due to something else?  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="1574" OwnerDisplayName="Kevin Pang" LastEditorUserId="1600" LastEditorDisplayName="Soeren Kuklau" LastEditDate="2008-09-14T18:08:54.477" LastActivityDate="2009-09-25T20:58:32.513" Title="What are the pros and cons of object databases?" Tags="&lt;database&gt;&lt;oop&gt;&lt;rdbms&gt;&lt;relational&gt;&lt;object-oriented-database&gt;" AnswerCount="7" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="4807649" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="4810264" CreationDate="2011-01-26T17:19:47.943" Score="2" ViewCount="516" Body="&lt;p&gt;In subjective probability assessments one needs to elicit the distribution of subjects believes. It can be achieved by letting the subject &lt;em&gt;manipulate the &lt;strong&gt;relative height&lt;/strong&gt; of each frequency bin of a histogram.&lt;/em&gt; I.e. the distribution of probability, the envelope curve gets shaped maintaining the cumulative sum(P_i)=1.&#xA;How can this be done with R? Is there already a package I can build on?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Alternatively:&lt;/strong&gt; how can it be done in a spreadsheet application (excel, oo calc, google spreadsheet)?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="345913" LastEditorUserId="345913" LastEditDate="2011-01-27T09:37:55.707" LastActivityDate="2011-01-28T22:07:12.860" Title="Changing the relative height of the bins of a histogram interactively" Tags="&lt;user-interface&gt;&lt;r&gt;&lt;histogram&gt;&lt;cumulative-frequency&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="61524" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="61603" CreationDate="2008-09-14T18:09:36.317" Score="11" ViewCount="1863" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a network C++ program in Windows that I'd like to test for network disconnects at various times.  What are my options?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Currently I am:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Actually disconnecting the network wire from the back of my computer&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;using ipconfig /release&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Using the &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.nirsoft.net/utils/cports.html&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noreferrer&quot;&gt;cports&lt;/a&gt; program to close out the socket completely&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;None of these methods though are ideal for me, and I'd like to emulate network problems more easily. &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I would like for sometimes connects to fail, sometimes socket reads to fail, and sometimes socket writes to fail.  It would be great if there was some utility I could use to emulate these types of problems.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;It would also be nice to be able to build some automated unit tests while this emulated bad network is up.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="3153" OwnerDisplayName="Brian R. Bondy" LastEditorUserId="3153" LastEditDate="2009-12-01T21:25:03.607" LastActivityDate="2012-10-22T08:16:05.433" Title="Testing network interrupts in software" Tags="&lt;windows&gt;&lt;testing&gt;&lt;networking&gt;&lt;network-programming&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="4808052" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="4808258" CreationDate="2011-01-26T17:55:18.157" Score="5" ViewCount="2777" Body="&lt;p&gt;Has anyone managed to get color syntax-highlighting working in the &lt;strong&gt;output&lt;/strong&gt; of Sweave documents?  I've been able to customize the output style by adding boxes, etc. in the &lt;code&gt;Sweave.sty&lt;/code&gt; file as follows:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;\DefineVerbatimEnvironment{Sinput}{Verbatim}{fontseries=bc,frame=single}&#xA;\DefineVerbatimEnvironment{Soutput}{Verbatim}{frame=leftline}&#xA;\DefineVerbatimEnvironment{Scode}{Verbatim}{fontseries=bc}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;And I can get the &lt;code&gt;minted&lt;/code&gt; package to do syntax highlighting of verbatim-code blocks in my document like so:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;\begin{minted}{perl}&#xA;use Foo::Bar;&#xA;...&#xA;\end{minted}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;but I'm not sure how to combine the two for R input sections.  I tried the following:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;\DefineVerbatimEnvironment{Sinput}{minted}{r}&#xA;\DefineVerbatimEnvironment{Scode}{minted}{r}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Any suggestions?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="169947" LastActivityDate="2011-01-26T21:24:43.300" Title="Sweave syntax highlighting in output" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;syntax-highlighting&gt;&lt;sweave&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="4" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="4808169" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="4808574" CreationDate="2011-01-26T18:09:13.803" Score="9" ViewCount="23370" Body="&lt;p&gt;I'm having a hard time passing a filename to my R script. The file is a csv file with the batch parameters for multiple runs of the script. I am trying to include it here so that the user does not need to edit the R script in order to specify the location of that file.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;My Windows command line syntax is:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;R CMD BATCH --slave &quot;--args fn=batch.csv&quot; myscript.r output.txt&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The closest I have gotten to retrieving this in my R script is by doing:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;eval(parse(file=commandArgs()[8])))&#xA;batch_args = read.table(fn, sep=&quot;,&quot;)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I have experimented with &lt;code&gt;commandArgs(trailingOnly=TRUE)&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;parse(text=commandArgs()[8])&lt;/code&gt;, etc., with no luck. Most of the documentation that I have seen does not apply specifically to passing filenames. Can anyone think of a solution?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="483620" LastEditorUserId="483620" LastEditDate="2011-01-26T18:25:30.453" LastActivityDate="2013-01-07T19:06:13.737" Title="R command line passing a filename to script in arguments (Windows)" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;command-line&gt;&lt;batch-file&gt;&lt;arguments&gt;" AnswerCount="4" CommentCount="3" FavoriteCount="12" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
