  <row Id="4808657" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="10906532" CreationDate="2011-01-26T18:53:09.513" Score="4" ViewCount="968" Body="&lt;p&gt;Is there a way to pass a variable value in ddply/sapply directly to a function without the function (x) notation?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;E.g. Instead of:&#xA;    ddply(bu,.(trial), function (x) print(x$tangle) )&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there a way to do:&#xA;    ddply(bu,.(trial), print(tangle) )&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I am asking because with many variables this notation becomes very cumbersome.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Thanks!&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="409828" LastActivityDate="2012-06-05T23:49:27.920" Title="R question about sapply /plyr syntax: how to pass variable values to a function" Tags="&lt;function&gt;&lt;r&gt;&lt;plyr&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="1" FavoriteCount="2" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="61552" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-09-14T18:54:32.213" Score="383" ViewCount="69643" Body="&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.stackoverflow.com/questions/61088/hidden-features-of-javascript#61118&quot;&gt;Alan Storm's comments&lt;/a&gt; in response to my answer regarding the &lt;a href=&quot;https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/with&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;&lt;code&gt;with&lt;/code&gt; statement&lt;/a&gt; got me thinking. I've seldom found a reason to use this particular language feature, and had never given much thought to how it might cause trouble. Now, I'm curious as to how I might make effective use of &lt;code&gt;with&lt;/code&gt;, while avoiding its pitfalls.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Where have you found the &lt;code&gt;with&lt;/code&gt; statement useful?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="811" OwnerDisplayName="Shog9" LastEditorUserId="5969346" LastEditDate="2018-06-06T06:29:29.313" LastActivityDate="2021-02-02T12:06:14.067" Title="Are there legitimate uses for JavaScript's &quot;with&quot; statement?" Tags="&lt;javascript&gt;&lt;language-features&gt;&lt;with-statement&gt;" AnswerCount="32" CommentCount="7" FavoriteCount="153" CommunityOwnedDate="2009-02-10T16:56:47.680" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="61556" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2008-09-14T18:58:51.003" Score="0" ViewCount="236" Body="&lt;p&gt;What is the best available method for developing a spell check engine (for example, with &lt;code&gt;aspell_python&lt;/code&gt;), that works with apache &lt;code&gt;mod_python&lt;/code&gt;?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;apache 2.0.59+RHEL4+mod_python+aspell_python&lt;/code&gt; seems to crash.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Is there any alternative to using &lt;code&gt;aspell_python&lt;/code&gt;?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="6361" OwnerDisplayName="Ram Prasad" LastEditorUserId="8465023" LastEditDate="2017-09-29T15:31:14.893" LastActivityDate="2017-09-29T15:31:14.893" Title="Spell Checking Service with python using mod_python" Tags="&lt;spell-checking&gt;&lt;mod-python&gt;&lt;aspell&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="61559" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="61565" CreationDate="2008-09-14T19:00:24.460" Score="108" ViewCount="26087" Body="&lt;p&gt;How much less libraries are there for Mono than for Java?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I lack the overview over both alternatives but I have pretty much freedom of choice for my next project. I'm looking for hard technical facts in the areas of&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;performance (for example, I'm told Java is good for threading, and I hear the runtime code optimization has become very good recently for .NET)&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;i&gt;real world&lt;/i&gt; portability (it's both meant to be portable, what's Catch-22 for each?)&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;tool availability (&lt;a href=&quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Continuous_integration&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;CI&lt;/a&gt;, build automation, debugging, IDE)&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I am especially looking for what you actually experienced in your own work rather than the things I could google. My application would be a back-end service processing large amounts of data from time series.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;My main target platform would be Linux.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Edit:&lt;/strong&gt;&#xA;&lt;em&gt;To phrase my question more adequately, I am interested in the whole package (3rd party libraries etc.), not just the language. For libraries, that probably boils down to the question &quot;how much less libraries are there for Mono than for Java&quot;?&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;FYI, I have since chosen Java for this project, because it seemed just more battle-worn on the portability side and it's been around for a while on older systems, too. I'm a tiny little bit sad about it, because I'm very curious about C# and I'd love to have done some large project in it, but maybe next time. Thanks for all the advice.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="2077" OwnerDisplayName="Hanno" LastEditorUserId="63550" LastEditorDisplayName="Hanno Fietz" LastEditDate="2010-02-07T17:51:28.577" LastActivityDate="2018-08-13T00:31:26.780" Title="Is .NET/Mono or Java the better choice for cross-platform development?" Tags="&lt;java&gt;&lt;.net&gt;&lt;linux&gt;&lt;mono&gt;&lt;cross-platform&gt;" AnswerCount="15" CommentCount="5" FavoriteCount="25" ClosedDate="2012-11-02T19:56:49.560" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="4809285" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="4809491" CreationDate="2011-01-26T19:51:13.940" Score="5" ViewCount="1566" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have a data frame with 2 columns, the first containing dates, and the second containing times, in the format:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;     date         time        &#xA;[1,] &quot;2003-10-03&quot; &quot;22:32:00&quot;&#xA;[2,] &quot;2003-10-05&quot; &quot;17:43:06&quot;&#xA;[3,] &quot;2003-10-10&quot; &quot;18:45:56&quot;&#xA;[4,] &quot;2003-11-12&quot; &quot;17:07:16&quot;&#xA;[5,] &quot;2003-11-13&quot; &quot;12:48:04&quot;&#xA;[6,] &quot;2003-11-13&quot; &quot;18:17:57&quot;&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I would like to create some histograms of these data looking at the number of events per year, per months and at a specific hour of the day.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;For the year that's easy&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;hist(as.Date(df[,1]), &quot;years&quot;)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Now, to get the number of events per months (disregarding the year) I used:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt; months = c(&quot;January&quot;, &quot;February&quot;, &quot;March&quot;, &#xA;            &quot;April&quot;, &quot;May&quot;, &quot;June&quot;, &quot;July&quot;, &#xA;            &quot;August&quot;, &quot;September&quot;, &quot;October&quot;, &#xA;            &quot;November&quot;, &quot;December&quot;)&#xA; tb &amp;lt;- table(factor(months.Date(dt), levels=months)&#xA; barplot(tb)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Questions:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Is there a nicer way to do the histogram per month?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;How do I do the same thing for the time of the day (hourly bins are sufficient)?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Thanks&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="176923" LastActivityDate="2011-01-26T20:16:33.377" Title="Dealing with dates and times in R" Tags="&lt;datetime&gt;&lt;r&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="2" FavoriteCount="3" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="61598" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="62003" CreationDate="2008-09-14T19:51:48.613" Score="10" ViewCount="2086" Body="&lt;p&gt;I've got an old, legacy VB6 application that uses the DHTML editing control as an HTML editor. The Microsoft DHTML editing control, a.k.a. DHTMLEd, is probably nothing more than an IE control using IE's own native editing capability internally.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I'd like to modify the app to implement smart quotes like Word. Specifically, &lt;strong&gt;&amp;quot;&lt;/strong&gt; is replaced with &lt;strong&gt;“&lt;/strong&gt; or &lt;strong&gt;”&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;'&lt;/strong&gt; is replaced with &lt;strong&gt;‘&lt;/strong&gt; or &lt;strong&gt;’&lt;/strong&gt; as appropriate as it is typed; and if the user presses &lt;kbd&gt;Ctrl&lt;/kbd&gt;+&lt;kbd&gt;Z&lt;/kbd&gt; immediately after the replacement, it goes back to being a straight quote.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Does anyone have code that does that?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;If you don't have code for DHTML/VB6, but do have JavaScript code that works in a browser with contentEditable regions, I could use that, too&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="4" OwnerDisplayName="Joel Spolsky" LastEditorUserId="9454010" LastEditDate="2021-06-17T15:01:56.537" LastActivityDate="2021-06-17T15:01:56.537" Title="Code to make a DHTMLEd control replace straight quotes with curly quotes" Tags="&lt;html&gt;&lt;dom&gt;&lt;vb6&gt;" AnswerCount="1" CommentCount="0" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 4.0" />
  <row Id="61604" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="61612" CreationDate="2008-09-14T20:13:51.227" Score="6" ViewCount="1269" Body="&lt;p&gt;Do you often see in API documentation (as in 'javadoc of public functions' for example) the description of &quot;value limits&quot; as well as the classic documentation ?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Note:&lt;/strong&gt; I am not talking about &lt;a href=&quot;https://stackoverflow.com/questions/20922/do-you-comment-your-code&quot;&gt;comments within the code&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;By &quot;value limits&quot;, I mean:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;does a parameter can support a null value (or an empty String, or...) ?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;does a 'return value' can be null or is guaranteed to never be null (or can be &quot;empty&quot;, or...) ?&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Sample:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What I often see (without having access to source code) is:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;/**&#xA; * Get all readers name for this current Report. &amp;lt;br /&amp;gt;&#xA; * &amp;lt;b&amp;gt;Warning&amp;lt;/b&amp;gt;The Report must have been published first.&#xA; * @param aReaderNameRegexp filter in order to return only reader matching the regexp&#xA; * @return array of reader names&#xA; */&#xA; String[] getReaderNames(final String aReaderNameRegexp);&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What I &lt;em&gt;like to see&lt;/em&gt; would be:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;/**&#xA; * Get all readers name for this current Report. &amp;lt;br /&amp;gt;&#xA; * &amp;lt;b&amp;gt;Warning&amp;lt;/b&amp;gt;The Report must have been published first.&#xA; * @param aReaderNameRegexp filter in order to return only reader matching the regexp &#xA; * (can be null or empty)&#xA; * @return array of reader names &#xA; * (null if Report has not yet been published, &#xA; *  empty array if no reader match criteria, &#xA; *  reader names array matching regexp, or all readers if regexp is null or empty)&#xA; */&#xA; String[] getReaderNames(final String aReaderNameRegexp);&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;My point is:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;When I use a library with a getReaderNames() function in it, I often do not even need to read the API documentation to guess what it does. But I need to be sure &lt;em&gt;how to use it&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;My only concern when I want to use this function is: what should I expect in term of parameters and return values ? That is all I need to know to safely setup my parameters and safely test the return value, yet I almost never see that kind of information in API documentation...&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Edit:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This can influence the usage or not for &lt;em&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://stackoverflow.com/questions/27578#73355&quot;&gt;checked or unchecked exceptions&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;What do you think ? value limits and API, do they belong together or not ?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="6309" OwnerDisplayName="VonC" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditorDisplayName="VonC" LastEditDate="2017-05-23T12:25:31.263" LastActivityDate="2014-06-14T20:47:16.100" Title="api documentation and &quot;value limits&quot;: do they match?" Tags="&lt;language-agnostic&gt;&lt;documentation&gt;&lt;comments&gt;&lt;design-by-contract&gt;" AnswerCount="5" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 3.0" />
  <row Id="61605" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="61636" CreationDate="2008-09-14T20:15:19.977" Score="85" ViewCount="60011" Body="&lt;p&gt;In python, you can have a function return multiple values.  Here's a contrived example:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;def divide(x, y):&#xA;    quotient = x/y&#xA;    remainder = x % y&#xA;    return quotient, remainder  &#xA;&#xA;(q, r) = divide(22, 7)&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This seems very useful, but it looks like it can also be abused (&quot;Well..function X already computes what we need as an intermediate value.  Let's have X return that value also&quot;).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;When should you draw the line and define a different method?  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="4883" OwnerDisplayName="Tim" LastEditorUserId="364696" LastEditDate="2017-07-11T19:03:18.183" LastActivityDate="2018-08-29T00:28:37.420" Title="Is it pythonic for a function to return multiple values?" Tags="&lt;python&gt;&lt;function&gt;&lt;return-value&gt;&lt;multiple-return-values&gt;" AnswerCount="9" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="27" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="61615" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="61624" CreationDate="2008-09-14T20:28:43.050" Score="26" ViewCount="18810" Body="&lt;p&gt;C# and Java allow almost any character in class names, method names, local variables, etc..  Is it bad practice to use non-ASCII characters, testing the boundaries of poor editors and analysis tools and making it difficult for some people to read, or is American arrogance the only argument against?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="4926" OwnerDisplayName="Jason Cohen" LastActivityDate="2019-01-29T16:55:25.333" Title="Should you use international identifiers in Java/C#?" Tags="&lt;c#&gt;&lt;java&gt;&lt;coding-style&gt;" AnswerCount="11" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="1" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="4811106" PostTypeId="1" AcceptedAnswerId="4811119" CreationDate="2011-01-26T22:59:16.777" Score="26" ViewCount="15163" Body="&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Possible Duplicate:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;a href=&quot;https://stackoverflow.com/questions/2547306/generate-multiple-graphics-from-within-an-r-function&quot;&gt;Generate multiple graphics from within an R function&lt;/a&gt;  &lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Very strange thing happening to me: the following code fails to print to pdf device:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;outnames &amp;lt;- c(&quot;1.pdf&quot;, &quot;2.pdf&quot;)&#xA;for (n in outnames){&#xA;    pdf(n)&#xA;    qplot(1:10)&#xA;    dev.off()&#xA;}&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;won't print anything to pdf, even though a pdf file is generated. However,&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;pdf(outnames[2])&#xA;qplot(1:10)&#xA;dev.off()&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;will work perfectly well. Any idea why? Reproduced in R 2.11.1.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;" OwnerUserId="143813" LastEditorUserId="-1" LastEditDate="2017-05-23T11:53:15.420" LastActivityDate="2011-01-27T16:51:08.390" Title="Can't print to pdf ggplot charts" Tags="&lt;r&gt;&lt;ggplot2&gt;" AnswerCount="2" CommentCount="0" FavoriteCount="4" ClosedDate="2012-09-23T08:49:30.033" ContentLicense="CC BY-SA 2.5" />
  <row Id="4811316" PostTypeId="1" CreationDate="2011-01-26T23:24:38.977" Score="6" ViewCount="14392" Body="&lt;p&gt;I have the following data frame:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; DF&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;Year Metric MWh&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2003 Demand   498343&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2004 Demand  1250904&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2005 Demand  1665176&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2006 Demand  2317643&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2007 Demand  2455311&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2008 Demand  3557987&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2009 Demand  4268125&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2010 Demand  5403704&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2011 Demand  6596158&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2012 Demand  7814387&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2013 Demand  9008863&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2014 Demand 10291085&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2015 Demand 11796549&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2003 Actual   159677&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2004 Actual   192748&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2005 Actual   248844&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2006 Actual   372661&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2007 Actual   705656&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2008 Actual   838721&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2009 Actual  1188242&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2010 Actual  1708979&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2011 Actual        0&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2012 Actual        0&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2013 Actual        0&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2014 Actual        0&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2015 Actual        0&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2003   High        0&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;&#xA;  &lt;code&gt;2